-- Step 72: the glossary, filled out.
--
-- 183 existing terms get a full write-up (definition, summary, sections, 3 common
-- questions and a search title), 33 new terms are added (algae, pests, diseases,
-- plants), and 6 near-duplicate terms are folded into the main one. The site already
-- redirects the old addresses of the removed terms.
--
-- All or nothing: if anything fails, nothing changes. Safe to run more than once.

begin;

update public.glossary_terms g
set term = v.term, category = v.category, definition = v.definition, body = v.body,
    seo_title = v.seo_title, sections = v.sections, faq = v.faq
from (values
  ($g$bubble-nest$g$, $g$Bubble Nest$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$A floating raft of saliva-coated bubbles built at the water surface by male bettas, gouramis and a few other fish to hold their eggs and newly hatched fry.$g$, $g$A bubble nest tells you a male feels settled and is in breeding condition, even if no female is around. Knowing how nests work helps you set up a successful spawn, and it saves you from worrying when a male tears his nest down or skips building one.$g$, $g$Betta Bubble Nest: What It Means and Should You Remove It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Which fish build them", "text": "Bettas, paradise fish and most gouramis build bubble nests, along with a few catfish such as the hoplo catfish. Labyrinth fish evolved in still, warm, oxygen-poor water, and the nest keeps eggs up in the moist, oxygen-rich layer at the surface. Males usually build under a floating leaf or in a calm corner."}, {"heading": "What a nest means", "text": "A nest generally means the male is healthy, comfortable and mature. It does not mean he is lonely or needs a mate. Some healthy males rarely build nests, so a missing nest on its own is not a warning sign. Watch appetite, color and activity instead."}, {"heading": "Using a nest to breed", "text": "Breeders often lower the water to 5 or 6 inches, keep it around 80 to 82 F, turn off or baffle the filter and add floating plants. After spawning, the male catches falling eggs and spits them into the nest. Remove the female right away, since he will chase her off aggressively.\n\nEggs hatch in about 24 to 48 hours. Remove the male once the fry swim freely, usually 2 to 3 days after hatching, or he may start eating them."}, {"heading": "Nests and water changes", "text": "It is fine to disturb a nest during routine maintenance when no eggs are in it. The male will simply build a new one, often within a day. If eggs or fry are in the nest, skip large water changes and keep the surface still until the fry are free swimming."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Should I remove my betta's bubble nest?", "a": "There is no need to remove it, but breaking it during a water change is harmless if there are no eggs. He will rebuild when he is ready."}, {"q": "Does a bubble nest mean my betta is happy?", "a": "It usually means he is healthy and settled. A betta that never builds one can still be perfectly content."}, {"q": "Why does my betta keep making bubble nests?", "a": "Building nests is natural behavior for a mature male in good condition. It is driven by instinct, not a sign that he needs a female."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$conditioning$g$, $g$Conditioning$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$Conditioning is preparing fish to breed by feeding them rich, varied foods and keeping water conditions ideal until they are healthy and full of eggs or sperm.$g$, $g$Most fish will not spawn successfully on a basic flake diet alone. A few weeks of conditioning builds body condition, improves egg quality and fertility, and often triggers spawning on its own when paired with the right water changes or temperature cues.$g$, $g$How to Condition Fish for Breeding: Foods, Timing and Signs$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to condition fish", "text": "- Feed high-protein live or frozen foods such as bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia, blackworms or mysis shrimp.\n- Offer 2 or 3 small meals a day instead of one big one.\n- Condition for 1 to 3 weeks before you want a spawn.\n- Many breeders separate males and females during this time, then reunite them in the breeding tank."}, {"heading": "Keeping water clean", "text": "Heavier feeding means more waste. Do extra water changes, often 25 to 50 percent a couple of times a week, and remove uneaten food within a few minutes. Poor water quality during conditioning can stress fish enough to stop spawning altogether, which defeats the purpose. Test ammonia and nitrite a couple of times a week while feeding heavily, and cut back on food if either rises above zero."}, {"heading": "Signs fish are ready", "text": "Females look fuller and rounder in the belly when viewed from above. Males often show brighter colors, more intense fin displays and more interest in the females. Many cichlids show a small breeding tube near the vent. Pairs may start cleaning flat surfaces or claiming territory."}, {"heading": "Pairing conditioning with spawning triggers", "text": "Conditioning gets fish ready, and an environmental cue often finishes the job. Corydoras frequently spawn after a large, slightly cooler water change that mimics rain. Many soft water tetras and rasboras respond to softer, more acidic water. Some species just need a small temperature rise of 2 to 3 F. Research your species' natural rainy season cues for the best results."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the best food to condition fish for breeding?", "a": "Live and frozen foods such as bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia and blackworms work well for most species. Variety matters more than any single food."}, {"q": "How long does it take to condition fish?", "a": "Usually 1 to 3 weeks of rich feeding. Fish that were already well fed may be ready sooner."}, {"q": "Should males and females be separated during conditioning?", "a": "Many breeders do, because it lets females build up eggs without being harassed and often leads to a quick spawn when they are reunited."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$culling$g$, $g$Culling$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$Culling is selectively removing fish from a brood or breeding group, usually because of deformities, poor health, or traits that don't match the breeder's goals.$g$, $g$Every spawn produces some fish that are weaker or off-type, and keeping them all can overcrowd tanks and weaken a line over time. Culling is a normal part of serious breeding, but how you do it matters, both for the fish and for keeping your strain healthy.$g$, $g$Culling Fish Fry: What It Means and How to Do It Humanely$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why breeders cull", "text": "- Physical deformities such as bent spines, missing gill covers or misshapen fins\n- Fish that are much slower growing or weaker than their siblings\n- Colors or fin types that don't match the strain\n- Too many fish for the space available, which stunts the rest\n- Excess males in livebearer groups"}, {"heading": "Culling does not always mean killing", "text": "For many hobbyists, culling simply means removing fish from the breeding program. Healthy fish with minor color or fin faults can be kept as pets, given to other hobbyists or sold as pet quality, so long as they are labeled honestly and not used to breed the strain. Fish with serious deformities or illness that will suffer should be euthanized humanely."}, {"heading": "Doing it humanely", "text": "The method most widely recommended by hobbyists and vets is an overdose of clove oil mixed into a small container of tank water, which sedates the fish before it dies. Avoid flushing, freezing a live fish or leaving it out of water, all of which are inhumane. Feeding culls to larger fish is common but debated, and it can spread disease if the culls are sick."}, {"heading": "When to cull", "text": "Many breeders do a first pass when fry are a few weeks old and deformities become visible, then cull again as color and finnage develop. Cull early and steadily rather than waiting until a tank is overcrowded. Keep notes on what you remove and why, so you can spot which parents keep throwing faults and drop them from the breeding program."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What does culling fish mean?", "a": "It means removing fish from a group or brood, usually the weaker, deformed or off-type ones, so the rest grow better and the line stays strong."}, {"q": "Is it necessary to cull fish fry?", "a": "Not always for casual breeders, but if you breed regularly, some culling is needed to avoid overcrowding and to keep a strain healthy."}, {"q": "What is the most humane way to cull fish?", "a": "Clove oil overdose in a container of tank water is the most commonly recommended humane method. Never flush or freeze a live fish."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$egg-layer$g$, $g$Egg Layer$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$An egg layer is a fish that reproduces by releasing eggs that are fertilized and hatch outside the body, rather than giving birth to live young.$g$, $g$Most aquarium fish, from tetras to cichlids to catfish, are egg layers. Knowing which spawning style your fish uses tells you how to set up a breeding tank, how to protect eggs from hungry adults, and what the tiny fry will need to eat once they hatch.$g$, $g$Egg Laying Fish: Types of Spawners and How to Save the Eggs$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Types of egg layers", "text": "- Egg scatterers: tetras, danios, barbs and rasboras spray eggs over plants or gravel and leave them, often eating them right away.\n- Substrate spawners: many cichlids and corydoras stick eggs on rocks, glass or leaves, and some guard them.\n- Cave spawners: plecos and many dwarf cichlids lay inside caves, usually guarded by a parent.\n- Bubble nesters: bettas and gouramis place eggs in a floating nest.\n- Mouthbrooders: carry eggs in the mouth.\n- Egg buriers: annual killifish lay eggs in mud that survives drying out."}, {"heading": "Protecting the eggs", "text": "For scatterers, give eggs somewhere to fall out of reach: a layer of marbles, a mesh grid above the bottom, a spawning mop or a thick clump of moss. Remove the adults after spawning. For species that guard their eggs, it is often best to leave the parents alone in their own tank, since some pairs learn to raise fry well."}, {"heading": "Hatching and fungus", "text": "Most aquarium eggs hatch in 1 to 5 days depending on species and temperature. Unfertilized eggs turn white and fuzzy with fungus within a day or two, and fungus can spread to good eggs. Remove white eggs with a pipette, keep gentle water flow over the clutch, and consider hatching eggs in a separate container if fungus is a regular problem."}, {"heading": "Feeding the fry", "text": "Newly hatched fry live off their yolk sac for 1 to 3 days. Once they swim freely, most need very tiny first foods such as infusoria or microworms before moving on to baby brine shrimp."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do egg laying fish eat their eggs?", "a": "Many do, especially egg scatterers. Removing adults after spawning or giving eggs a protected place to fall is the usual solution."}, {"q": "How do I know if fish eggs are fertilized?", "a": "Fertilized eggs stay clear or slightly amber and you may see a developing eye spot. Unfertilized eggs turn white and fuzzy within a day or two."}, {"q": "How long do fish eggs take to hatch?", "a": "Usually 1 to 5 days for common aquarium species, faster in warmer water. Some killifish eggs take weeks or months."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fry$g$, $g$Fry$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$Fry are newly hatched or newborn baby fish, from the moment they start swimming freely until they grow into juveniles a few weeks or months later.$g$, $g$Fry are tiny, hungry and easy to lose. Most of the losses come from three things: being eaten by adults, not getting food small enough, and poor water quality. Getting those right turns a handful of survivors into a healthy batch of young fish.$g$, $g$How to Care for Fish Fry: Feeding, Water and Growing Out$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Feeding fry", "text": "Livebearer fry such as guppies and platies are big enough to eat finely crushed flakes and baby brine shrimp from day one. Most egg layer fry are much smaller and need microscopic first foods such as infusoria, vinegar eels or microworms for the first week or so, then baby brine shrimp.\n\nFeed small amounts 3 to 5 times a day. Fry have tiny stomachs and grow fastest with frequent meals."}, {"heading": "Water quality", "text": "Fry are sensitive to ammonia and nitrite, and frequent feeding fouls water fast. Small, frequent water changes work best: 10 to 20 percent daily or 25 percent every other day, with water matched closely in temperature. A cycled sponge filter is ideal because it won't suck fry in. Siphon uneaten food from the bottom every day."}, {"heading": "Keeping fry safe", "text": "- Raise them in a separate tank, breeder box or net, or give them dense cover like moss and floating plants.\n- Cover filter intakes with a sponge.\n- Keep the temperature steady.\n- A bare bottom grow out tank is easiest to keep clean."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Most fry can join adults once they are too big to fit in an adult's mouth, often after 4 to 8 weeks. Uneven growth is normal. Separating the biggest fry can help smaller siblings get enough food. Keep feeding small, frequent meals during this stage, since fry that go hungry often stay stunted for life."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What do baby fish eat?", "a": "Livebearer fry can eat crushed flakes and baby brine shrimp right away. Smaller egg layer fry need infusoria or microworms first."}, {"q": "How often should I feed fish fry?", "a": "Feed small amounts 3 to 5 times a day, and remove leftovers so the water stays clean."}, {"q": "When can fry go in the main tank?", "a": "Once they are too large to fit in the mouths of the adult fish, usually after about 4 to 8 weeks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$infusoria$g$, $g$Infusoria$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$Infusoria are tiny freshwater microorganisms, mostly protozoa such as paramecium plus rotifers, used as a first live food for very small fish fry.$g$, $g$Many egg layer fry, including bettas, gouramis and most tetras, are too small to eat baby brine shrimp for their first several days. Without a food that fits their mouths, they starve. A ready culture of infusoria is one of the easiest ways to get them through that first week.$g$, $g$How to Make Infusoria for Fry: Easy Culture Steps$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to culture infusoria", "text": "- Fill a clean jar with old aquarium water or dechlorinated tap water.\n- Add a small piece of blanched lettuce, a slice of banana peel, a few dried leaves or a pinch of crushed hay.\n- Leave it uncovered or loosely covered somewhere warm with indirect light.\n- In 3 to 7 days the water turns cloudy, then starts to clear. That clearing stage usually means infusoria numbers are high.\n\nHold the jar up to a light and you should see tiny white specks moving. A sour or rotten smell is normal at first but should fade."}, {"heading": "How to feed it", "text": "Use a dropper or turkey baster to take water from the clear area of the jar and add a few droppers to the fry tank 2 or 3 times a day. Avoid dumping the rotting plant material in. Feed as soon as fry are free swimming and continue until they can take microworms or baby brine shrimp, usually 5 to 10 days."}, {"heading": "Tips for success", "text": "Start a culture about a week before you expect fry, and keep 2 or 3 jars going on staggered schedules so one is always peaking. Top up with a fresh bit of plant matter as cultures fade. A well established planted tank with moss and biofilm also supports natural infusoria, which helps fry survive."}, {"heading": "Alternatives", "text": "Green water, liquid fry foods and very finely powdered fry food can also work. Powdered egg yolk is an old method but fouls water quickly, so use it sparingly."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does infusoria take to grow?", "a": "Usually 3 to 7 days, when the cloudy water begins to clear."}, {"q": "Can you see infusoria?", "a": "Yes, barely. Hold the jar up to a light and look for tiny white specks moving in the water."}, {"q": "Which fry need infusoria?", "a": "Very small fry such as bettas, gouramis, many tetras and rasboras. Livebearer fry are big enough to skip it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$line-breeding$g$, $g$Line Breeding$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$Line breeding is selectively mating related fish, often parents to offspring or siblings, over several generations to fix and strengthen a desired color, pattern or fin type.$g$, $g$Nearly every fancy guppy, betta, angelfish and discus color you see was created and stabilized through line breeding. Done carefully, it produces fish that breed true. Done carelessly, it concentrates hidden weaknesses that show up as deformities, low fertility and poor health.$g$, $g$Line Breeding Fish vs Inbreeding: How to Fix a Trait$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Line breeding vs inbreeding", "text": "All line breeding involves some inbreeding, but the goal is different. Inbreeding just pairs close relatives. Line breeding pairs relatives on purpose to concentrate the genes of one outstanding fish, while watching for problems and adding new blood when needed. Outcrossing means bringing in an unrelated fish of the same strain to restore vigor. Many famous strains began with a single standout fish that a breeder noticed in an ordinary brood and then built a line around over many years."}, {"heading": "A simple approach", "text": "- Pick the best male and female you have for the trait you want.\n- Raise the brood and select the few offspring that show the trait most strongly.\n- Breed those back to the best parent, or to each other.\n- Repeat for several generations, selecting hard each time.\n- Keep two or three separate lines from the same start, so you can cross them later instead of buying unrelated fish."}, {"heading": "Warning signs of too much inbreeding", "text": "Watch for shrinking brood sizes, more deformed fry, smaller adult size, shorter lifespans and weaker immune systems. When these appear, outcross to a healthy fish of the same strain from another breeder, then select back toward your goal."}, {"heading": "Keep records", "text": "Label tanks, note which fish produced which brood and photograph your keepers. Without records it is easy to lose track of relatedness, and you can't tell whether a fault came from one parent or the line as a whole."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is line breeding the same as inbreeding?", "a": "It is a planned form of inbreeding. The difference is careful selection and record keeping, with outcrosses added when health starts to slip."}, {"q": "How many generations does it take to fix a trait?", "a": "Often 4 to 6 generations of careful selection, depending on how the trait is inherited."}, {"q": "Can you breed brother and sister fish?", "a": "Yes, for a few generations, and breeders often do. Repeated sibling pairings without selection or outcrossing raises the risk of deformities."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$livebearer$g$, $g$Livebearer$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$A livebearer is a fish that gives birth to free-swimming young instead of laying eggs, such as guppies, mollies, platies, swordtails and endlers.$g$, $g$Livebearers are easy to breed, which makes them great for beginners and for anyone curious about raising fry. That same ease can quickly overwhelm a small tank, so it helps to understand how they reproduce before you bring home a mixed group.$g$, $g$Livebearer Fish: Pregnancy Signs, Birth and Fry Care$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How livebearers reproduce", "text": "Males have a modified anal fin called a gonopodium that delivers sperm. Females can store sperm for several months, so one mating can produce several broods, even with no male present. Gestation usually takes about 21 to 30 days depending on species and temperature, and a brood can range from a few fry to more than 50."}, {"heading": "Signs of pregnancy and birth", "text": "- A growing belly that becomes boxy or squared off just before birth\n- A dark gravid spot near the vent that gets larger and darker\n- Hiding, staying near the heater or bottom, or refusing food\n- Restless swimming in one corner shortly before dropping fry"}, {"heading": "Keeping things under control", "text": "Keep 1 male for every 2 or 3 females so no single female is harassed constantly. If you don't want fry, keep a single sex or keep a few hungry tankmates that will eat most of them. Dense plants like moss and floating plants help some fry survive if you do want to raise them. Mollies and platies also appreciate harder, more alkaline water, around 7.5 or higher."}, {"heading": "Fry care", "text": "Livebearer fry are born large enough to eat crushed flakes and baby brine shrimp right away. Feed small amounts several times a day and keep up with water changes. Keep the water clean with small, frequent changes, and expect most fry to reach a safe size in about 4 to 6 weeks."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long are livebearers pregnant?", "a": "Usually about 3 to 4 weeks, though cooler water can stretch it out."}, {"q": "Can a female guppy get pregnant without a male?", "a": "She can give birth without a male present by using stored sperm from an earlier mating, sometimes for several broods."}, {"q": "Do livebearers eat their babies?", "a": "Yes, adults often eat fry. Dense plants, a breeder box or a separate tank improve survival."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$mouthbrooder$g$, $g$Mouthbrooder$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$A mouthbrooder is a fish that carries its fertilized eggs, and often its young fry, inside its mouth until they are developed enough to survive on their own.$g$, $g$Mouthbrooding is common in African cichlids and some bettas, and it gives fry a much better chance of survival. If you keep these fish, knowing the signs of a holding fish helps you avoid mistaking it for illness and decide whether to let it hold or move it.$g$, $g$Mouthbrooding Fish: Signs of Holding and How Long It Lasts$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Types of mouthbrooders", "text": "- Maternal: the female carries the brood. Most Lake Malawi cichlids, including mbuna and haps, work this way.\n- Paternal: the male carries it, as in several wild betta species and arowanas.\n- Biparental: both parents share the job, seen in some tilapia and related cichlids."}, {"heading": "Signs a fish is holding", "text": "Look for a swollen, pouch-like throat, a square-looking jaw, chewing motions as she turns the eggs, refusing food and hiding. A holding Malawi female typically carries for about 21 to 28 days, depending on temperature. She eats little or nothing in that time and will lose condition, so she needs good feeding before and after."}, {"heading": "Helping a holding fish", "text": "In a busy cichlid tank, a holding female may be chased and spit early. Many keepers move her to a quiet tank or a large breeder net, moving her gently to avoid scaring her into spitting. Once she releases the fry, give her a week or two to recover before returning her to the group.\n\nSome breeders strip fry by gently opening the mouth into a container around day 14 to 21 and raising the young in an egg tumbler. It boosts fry numbers and lets the female recover sooner, but it is debated because it stresses the mother. Letting her hold to term is the gentler choice."}, {"heading": "Feeding the fry", "text": "Mouthbrooder fry are large when released and can eat crushed flakes, powdered pellets and baby brine shrimp right away."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long do cichlids hold eggs in their mouth?", "a": "Most Malawi cichlid females hold for about 3 to 4 weeks, depending on species and water temperature."}, {"q": "Do mouthbrooding fish eat while holding?", "a": "Usually very little or nothing. Some females nibble a bit near the end, which is normal."}, {"q": "Will a holding fish eat its fry?", "a": "Females sometimes swallow eggs if badly stressed. After release, the fry are on their own, and adults in a community tank will eat them."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$spawn$g$, $g$Spawn$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$To spawn is for fish to release and fertilize eggs, and the word also names the batch of eggs or young produced in one breeding event.$g$, $g$Many aquarium fish spawn at some point, often without the owner noticing until eggs appear or vanish overnight. Recognizing the signs of spawning lets you protect the eggs, separate parents when needed, or simply understand why a pair suddenly turned aggressive.$g$, $g$Fish Spawning Behavior: Signs Your Fish Are About to Spawn$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs fish are about to spawn", "text": "- Brighter or darker breeding colors\n- A male chasing a female, displaying or nudging her\n- A pair cleaning a flat rock, leaf, cave or glass\n- Females looking round and full when viewed from above\n- A small breeding tube appearing near the vent in many cichlids\n- More aggression toward tankmates as a pair claims territory"}, {"heading": "Common spawning triggers", "text": "Most fish spawn when diet, water and season line up. Conditioning with live or frozen foods is the starting point. Then a cue often sets it off: a large, slightly cooler water change for corydoras, a small temperature rise for many tropical species, or soft, acidic water for many tetras. Morning light is a frequent trigger for egg scatterers."}, {"heading": "What to do after a spawn", "text": "Decide right away whether you want to raise the young. Egg scatterers and most community fish will eat eggs within hours, so remove the eggs or the adults. Guarding species like many cichlids may raise the brood themselves if they have the tank to themselves. Remove white, unfertilized eggs to limit fungus, and prepare first foods before the fry are free swimming."}, {"heading": "Spawn size", "text": "A single spawn can range from a dozen eggs in some dwarf cichlids to hundreds in many tetras and thousands in some larger fish. Expect many to be lost in the first weeks. Not every spawn succeeds, and young pairs often eat their first few clutches before getting it right."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I know if my fish spawned?", "a": "Look for eggs on plants, glass, rocks or in a nest, or a sudden drop in a female's belly size. Pairs often guard or clean one spot afterward."}, {"q": "What time of day do fish spawn?", "a": "Many species, especially egg scatterers, spawn in the early morning as the lights come on."}, {"q": "How often do fish spawn?", "a": "Well conditioned fish may spawn every 1 to 4 weeks, depending on the species and how quickly the female rebuilds eggs."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$strain$g$, $g$Strain$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$A strain is a distinct, selectively bred variety within one fish species, defined by consistent traits such as color, pattern, fin shape or body form.$g$, $g$Strain names tell you what you're actually buying, from a halfmoon betta to a blue moscow guppy. They also matter for breeding, because mixing strains usually produces offspring that look like neither parent. Knowing a strain's background helps you pick healthy stock and keep it true.$g$, $g$Fish Strains Explained: Morphs, Varieties and Breeding True$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Strain, species and morph", "text": "A species is a natural group of fish that breeds together in the wild. A strain is a line developed by people within one species. Hobbyists often use strain, variety and morph almost interchangeably, though morph sometimes refers to a single trait like albino or long fin, while strain describes a whole stable package of traits. Strain names are not official, so the same fish can be sold under different names by different breeders and stores."}, {"heading": "Common examples", "text": "- Bettas: halfmoon, crowntail, plakat, koi and dragon scale\n- Guppies: moscow, cobra, tuxedo and mosaic\n- Bristlenose plecos: albino, calico and long fin\n- Angelfish: marble, koi, zebra and platinum\n- Shrimp: red cherry, blue dream and other color grades"}, {"heading": "Keeping a strain pure", "text": "A strain stays true only with deliberate selection every generation. Keep each strain in its own tank, breed only fish that show the strain's traits clearly, and remove off-type fry. Mixing two strains usually produces offspring with muddy or wild-type colors, which hobbyists sometimes call mutts. These fish are healthy pets but can't be sold as either strain."}, {"heading": "Health tradeoffs", "text": "Some strains carry trade-offs from the traits bred into them. Very long fins tear and get infected more easily, balloon body shapes can cause swim problems, and heavily line bred strains may be smaller or less hardy. When buying, ask about the line, look for active fish with clean fins, and expect fancier strains to need extra care with water quality."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the difference between a strain and a species?", "a": "A species is a natural group that breeds in the wild. A strain is a variety humans have selectively bred within that species."}, {"q": "Can different guppy strains breed together?", "a": "Yes, all guppy strains are the same species and will interbreed freely, but the offspring usually lose the distinct look of either strain."}, {"q": "Are fancy fish strains less hardy?", "a": "Some are. Long fins, unusual body shapes and heavy inbreeding can make certain strains more delicate than wild-type fish."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$air-pump$g$, $g$Air Pump$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$An air pump is an electric device that pushes air through airline tubing to power air stones, sponge filters and other air-driven aquarium equipment.$g$, $g$An air pump is one of the cheapest ways to improve surface movement and oxygen exchange, and it runs sponge filters that are ideal for fry, shrimp and hospital tanks. A battery model can also keep fish breathing during a power outage.$g$, $g$Aquarium Air Pump: Do You Need One and How to Choose$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it does", "text": "An air pump does not add much oxygen directly through its bubbles. The rising bubbles move water from the bottom to the surface and break up the surface, which is where oxygen enters and carbon dioxide leaves. That matters most in warm water, crowded tanks, during medication and anytime fish are gasping at the surface."}, {"heading": "Choosing the right size", "text": "Pumps are rated by tank size, but depth matters more than volume. A deeper tank needs more pressure to push air to the bottom. Size up if you run several outlets or an air stone at the bottom of a tank deeper than about 18 inches. A pump with an adjustable output, or a gang valve on the line, lets you dial in the flow and split it between several devices."}, {"heading": "Setup tips", "text": "- Place the pump above the waterline, or fit a check valve on every line.\n- Set it on a soft pad or hang it to cut down on vibration noise.\n- Leave a loop of tubing below the outlet so drips can't run back into the pump.\n- Keep a battery-powered pump on hand for outages."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Running heavy aeration in a CO2-injected planted tank drives off the CO2 you are paying for. A pump that has grown weak or noisy usually needs a new diaphragm, which is cheap and easy to replace. Clogged air stones also make a pump work harder, so replace them when bubbles shrink."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does an aquarium need an air pump?", "a": "Not always. A filter that ripples the surface often provides enough oxygen exchange, but an air pump helps in warm, crowded or medicated tanks."}, {"q": "Can I turn off my air pump at night?", "a": "Usually it is fine if your filter moves the surface, but crowded tanks can run low on oxygen at night when plants stop photosynthesizing."}, {"q": "Why is my air pump so loud?", "a": "Vibration against a hard surface is the usual cause. Set it on foam or hang it, and replace a worn diaphragm if it rattles."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$air-stone$g$, $g$Air Stone$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$An air stone is a porous stone or block attached to airline tubing that breaks air from a pump into streams of small bubbles in the aquarium.$g$, $g$Air stones are a cheap way to boost surface movement and gas exchange, especially in warm or crowded tanks and during medication. They also make decorative bubble walls. Knowing when they help, and when they hurt, keeps both fish and plants happy.$g$, $g$Aquarium Air Stone: Benefits, Setup and How to Clean It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it helps", "text": "The bubbles themselves add little oxygen. Their real job is lifting water to the surface and rippling it, which lets oxygen in and carbon dioxide out. Smaller bubbles look nicer and move water more gently, while larger bubbles move more water. Air stones also sit inside many sponge filters to make them quieter and more efficient."}, {"heading": "Types and setup", "text": "- Cylinder and ball stones: cheap and common for basic aeration.\n- Disc stones: spread a wide column of bubbles.\n- Bubble walls: long strips that create a curtain along the back glass.\n\nPlace the stone low in the tank, often behind decor, and run a check valve on the line if the pump sits below the water level."}, {"heading": "Cleaning and replacing", "text": "Air stones clog over time with minerals, biofilm and algae. You'll notice bigger, uneven bubbles or weaker flow. Soak a clogged stone in a mix of water and white vinegar, or a dilute bleach solution, then rinse well and soak it in dechlorinated water before reuse. Many keepers simply replace stones every few months since they are inexpensive."}, {"heading": "When to skip it", "text": "Heavy bubbling in a CO2-injected planted tank drives off CO2 that plants need. Fish from still water, like bettas, may find strong bubbling stressful. Run it gently, or only at night, in those setups. In those tanks, a gentle surface ripple from the filter usually provides enough gas exchange on its own."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do air stones add oxygen to the water?", "a": "Indirectly. The bubbles move water to the surface, and the surface movement is what lets oxygen dissolve in."}, {"q": "Can you have too many air stones?", "a": "In most tanks extra bubbles are harmless, but strong currents can stress small or long-finned fish and strip CO2 from planted tanks."}, {"q": "How often should I replace an air stone?", "a": "Every 2 to 6 months for most stones, or whenever bubbles get large and uneven despite cleaning."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$aquasoil$g$, $g$Aquasoil$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$Aquasoil is a baked, granular, nutrient-rich substrate made for planted aquariums that feeds roots and gently softens and acidifies the water.$g$, $g$Aquasoil makes root-feeding plants like swords, crypts and carpeting plants grow far better than plain gravel. Its softening effect also suits shrimp and many soft water fish. It does need a little care at setup and eventually wears out, so it pays to know its quirks.$g$, $g$Aquasoil for Planted Tanks: Setup, Ammonia Leach and Lifespan$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it does", "text": "Aquasoil is made from fired clay or volcanic soil packed with nutrients. It absorbs carbonate hardness, which pulls pH down, often into the 6.0 to 6.8 range, and keeps it steady. That makes it great for crystal shrimp, many tetras and plants that like soft water, and a poor fit for fish that need hard, alkaline water like African cichlids or mollies."}, {"heading": "How to set it up", "text": "- Do not rinse it. It breaks down into mud if scrubbed.\n- Spread it 2 to 3 inches deep, sloping higher toward the back.\n- Fill the tank slowly over a plate or plastic bag to avoid clouding.\n- Plant heavily from the start to use up nutrients that would otherwise feed algae."}, {"heading": "The ammonia leach", "text": "Many aquasoils release ammonia for the first 2 to 6 weeks. Do large water changes, around 50 percent every few days early on, test often and don't add fish or shrimp until ammonia and nitrite read zero. This leach can actually help kick-start the nitrogen cycle."}, {"heading": "How long it lasts", "text": "Most aquasoils hold useful nutrients for about 1 to 2 years and keep their buffering effect for a similar time. As it fades you'll see pH creep up and plants need more root tabs. The granules also slowly soften, so avoid deep digging and constant replanting, which turns the bed to mush. When it is spent, you can cap it with fresh soil, add root tabs, or replace it during a full rescape."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do you need to cycle a tank with aquasoil?", "a": "Yes. Most aquasoils leach ammonia for the first few weeks, so cycle fully and wait for zero ammonia and nitrite before adding livestock."}, {"q": "Should I rinse aquasoil before use?", "a": "No. Rinsing breaks the granules down and washes away nutrients. Fill the tank slowly instead."}, {"q": "Is aquasoil good for shrimp?", "a": "Yes for soft water shrimp like crystal and taiwan bee shrimp. Neocaridina shrimp do fine too once the leach is over."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$auto-top-off$g$, $g$Auto Top-Off (ATO)$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$An auto top-off, or ATO, is a system with a sensor and small pump that automatically adds fresh water to replace what evaporates from the aquarium.$g$, $g$Only pure water evaporates, so minerals and salt stay behind and slowly concentrate as the level drops. An ATO keeps the water level and chemistry steady every day, which matters most in reef tanks with sumps and still saves real work on large or open-top freshwater tanks.$g$, $g$Auto Top-Off for Aquariums: How an ATO Works and Is It Worth It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it works", "text": "An ATO has three parts: a reservoir of top-off water, a level sensor in the tank or sump, and a small pump or valve. When the sensor detects the water has dropped, the pump runs until the level is back up. Sensors are usually optical, which have no moving parts, or float switches, which are cheaper but can stick."}, {"heading": "Saltwater vs freshwater", "text": "In a reef tank, an ATO is close to essential because evaporation raises salinity, and corals dislike swings. Always top off saltwater tanks with fresh RO/DI water, never saltwater. In freshwater, an ATO is a convenience, and the reservoir should hold dechlorinated water. Keep doing regular water changes either way, since topping off does not remove waste or dissolved minerals."}, {"heading": "Avoiding floods and failures", "text": "- Use a reservoir that holds only a few days of evaporation, so a stuck pump can't overfill the tank badly.\n- Choose a unit with a backup sensor or a pump run-time limit.\n- Clean sensors every few weeks, since snails and algae can block them.\n- Keep the reservoir covered to keep out dust and pests."}, {"heading": "Is it worth it?", "text": "For tanks that lose more than a gallon or so a day, or anyone who travels, an ATO pays for itself in stability and saved time. For a small covered freshwater tank, topping off by hand during weekly maintenance is usually enough. Mark the normal water line with a small piece of tape so you notice quickly if the system stops working."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What water should I use for auto top-off?", "a": "RO/DI water for saltwater tanks. For freshwater, dechlorinated tap water is fine."}, {"q": "Do I need an ATO for a freshwater tank?", "a": "No, but it is handy for large, open-top or warm tanks that lose a lot of water to evaporation."}, {"q": "Can an auto top-off overflow my tank?", "a": "It can if a sensor fails. A small reservoir and a unit with a backup sensor or run-time limit greatly reduce the risk."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$canister-filter$g$, $g$Canister Filter$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A canister filter is a sealed external filter, usually kept in the cabinet under the tank, that pulls water through hoses and stacked trays of filter media.$g$, $g$Canister filters hold far more media than hang-on-back models, which means better biological filtration, more flexibility and longer gaps between cleanings. They suit bigger or heavily stocked tanks and planted setups where hidden equipment matters, but they cost more and take more care to service.$g$, $g$Canister Filter Setup: Media Order, Flow Rate and Cleaning$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Choosing a canister", "text": "Aim for a rated flow of about 4 to 6 times your tank volume per hour. Real flow is lower than the box number once media and hoses are added, so it is fine to go one size up. Look for easy priming, shutoff valves on the hoses, and media trays that lift out easily."}, {"heading": "Media order", "text": "Water should hit coarse media first and fine media last:\n\n- Coarse sponge or ceramic rings to catch large debris\n- Biological media such as porous ceramic or sintered glass\n- Fine filter floss or a polishing pad last for crystal clear water\n\nChemical media like carbon is optional and best used after medication or to remove tannins."}, {"heading": "Setup and maintenance", "text": "Place the canister below the tank on a level surface, fill it with water before starting, and check that every hose connection is snug. Clean it every 1 to 3 months, or when flow drops noticeably. Rinse sponges and bio media in removed tank water, never chlorinated tap water, and replace fine floss when it is clogged. Wipe the lid O-ring and coat it lightly with pure silicone grease to keep it sealing."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Cleaning all the media at once, which can crash the bacteria colony\n- Waiting so long that flow drops and the canister turns anaerobic\n- Loose hoses or a dry, cracked O-ring that cause slow leaks\n- Leaving the intake uncovered in a shrimp or fry tank"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How often should you clean a canister filter?", "a": "Every 1 to 3 months for most tanks, or sooner if flow drops. Heavily stocked tanks need more frequent cleaning."}, {"q": "Is a canister filter better than a hang-on-back?", "a": "For tanks over about 40 gallons or heavily stocked tanks, usually yes. For small tanks, a hang-on-back is simpler and cheaper."}, {"q": "Why is my canister filter not pulling water?", "a": "Usually it needs priming, has air trapped inside, or the intake or hoses are clogged. Check the shutoff valves too."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$check-valve$g$, $g$Check Valve$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A check valve is a small one-way valve fitted in airline tubing that lets air flow to the tank but stops water from siphoning back into the air pump.$g$, $g$If an air pump sits below the water level and the power cuts out, water can siphon down the airline, flood the pump and leak onto the floor. A check valve costs very little and prevents that, protecting both your pump and your furniture.$g$, $g$Aquarium Check Valve: Which Way It Goes and Why You Need One$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to install it", "text": "Cut the airline tubing and push each end onto the valve. Most valves have an arrow or a colored side. The arrow points in the direction air travels, from the pump toward the tank. The non-colored, or inlet, side faces the pump on most models, but follow the arrow if there is one. Blow gently through it to check: air should pass one way only."}, {"heading": "Where to put it", "text": "Install the valve outside the tank, close to the aquarium and above the floor, so any water stopped by the valve stays in the short section of tubing near the tank. Use one valve on each airline. If you split air with a gang valve, put a check valve on each line leading into the water."}, {"heading": "When you need one", "text": "- The air pump sits on the floor or in a cabinet below the waterline\n- You run airline to a sponge filter, air stone or CO2 diffuser\n- CO2 setups use a CO2-rated check valve to protect the regulator\n\nIf the pump sits above the water level, a check valve is optional but still cheap insurance."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Check valves can stick or slowly fail as minerals and grime build up. If airflow suddenly drops or bubbles sputter, the valve is a likely culprit. Replace them every year or so, and swap any that let water through when tested. A failing valve can also let water creep into the line slowly, so check the tubing for water now and then."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Which way does a check valve go on an air pump?", "a": "The arrow points away from the pump, toward the tank, in the direction the air flows."}, {"q": "Do I need a check valve if my pump is above the tank?", "a": "It is not strictly required, since water can't siphon uphill, but it is still worth adding for safety."}, {"q": "Why is no air coming through my check valve?", "a": "It is likely installed backward or stuck. Flip it to test, and replace it if air still won't pass."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$co2-diffuser$g$, $g$CO2 Diffuser$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A CO2 diffuser is a device that breaks pressurized carbon dioxide gas into very fine bubbles so it dissolves into aquarium water for plants to use.$g$, $g$Carbon is the nutrient plants use most, and injected CO2 is what lets high-tech tanks grow lush carpets and fast stem plants. The diffuser decides how much of that gas actually dissolves rather than escaping at the surface, so choosing and placing it well saves gas and improves growth.$g$, $g$CO2 Diffuser for Planted Tanks: Types, Placement and Cleaning$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Types of diffusers", "text": "- Ceramic disc diffusers: a fine porous disc in a glass or plastic holder. They make a mist of tiny bubbles and need a fair amount of pressure to push gas through.\n- Inline diffusers: fit on the canister filter outflow hose, keeping equipment out of sight.\n- Reactors: chambers where water and CO2 mix fully. Very efficient, good for large tanks.\n- Bubble ladders: simple, cheap and visible, but less efficient."}, {"heading": "Placement", "text": "Put an in-tank diffuser low in the tank under or near the filter outflow, so current carries the bubbles around before they reach the surface. The longer bubbles stay in the water, the more CO2 dissolves. Use a CO2-rated check valve on the line and confirm levels with a drop checker."}, {"heading": "Getting the dose right", "text": "Most planted tanks aim for about 30 ppm CO2, which turns a standard drop checker green. Start with a low bubble rate and raise it slowly over a week or two. Many keepers turn CO2 on 1 to 2 hours before the lights and off about an hour before lights out. Fish gasping at the surface means too much: turn it down and add aeration right away."}, {"heading": "Cleaning", "text": "Ceramic discs clog with algae and biofilm, which makes bubbles bigger. Soak the disc in a dilute bleach or hydrogen peroxide solution, rinse thoroughly, and soak it in dechlorinated water before reuse."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Where should I place my CO2 diffuser?", "a": "Low in the tank, near or under the filter outflow, so the flow spreads the fine bubbles around the tank."}, {"q": "How do I know if my CO2 diffuser is working?", "a": "You should see a fine mist of bubbles, and your drop checker should turn green within a few hours."}, {"q": "Why are my CO2 diffuser bubbles big?", "a": "The ceramic disc is likely dirty or the pressure is too low. Clean the disc and check your regulator's working pressure."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$drop-checker$g$, $g$Drop Checker$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A drop checker is a small glass or plastic indicator that sits in a planted tank and changes color to show roughly how much dissolved CO2 the water holds.$g$, $g$Too little CO2 means weak plant growth and algae, while too much can suffocate fish. A drop checker gives a simple, constant visual check, so you can tune CO2 injection safely without guessing or relying only on pH readings. It is a must for any injected CO2 setup.$g$, $g$Drop Checker Colors Explained: Blue, Green and Yellow CO2$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it works", "text": "The checker holds a few milliliters of indicator fluid separated from the tank water by a small pocket of air. CO2 passes through that air gap into the fluid and changes its pH, which changes its color. Because only gas crosses over, things like fertilizers and driftwood tannins don't throw off the reading."}, {"heading": "Reading the colors", "text": "- Blue: too little CO2, often under about 15 to 20 ppm\n- Green: the target range, around 30 ppm\n- Yellow: too much CO2, a danger to fish\n\nUse the 4 dKH reference solution, not tank water, for an accurate reading. Tank water in the checker makes the color meaningless."}, {"heading": "Setup tips", "text": "Place the drop checker low in the tank, away from the CO2 diffuser and out of any direct bubble stream, ideally on the side opposite the diffuser. Replace the fluid every 2 to 4 weeks, since it slowly drifts. Look at it against a white background to judge the color more easily."}, {"heading": "Lag time and limits", "text": "A drop checker takes about 1 to 2 hours to react to changes, so it shows the average level over the past while, not what is happening right now. Adjust your bubble rate in small steps and wait several hours before judging. For faster readings, some keepers also track the pH drop: a fall of about 1 full point from degassed water roughly equals 30 ppm."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What color should my drop checker be?", "a": "Green, ideally a lime green when lights are on. Blue means too little CO2 and yellow means too much."}, {"q": "How long does a drop checker take to change color?", "a": "About 1 to 2 hours after a change in CO2, so adjust slowly and check again later."}, {"q": "Can I use tank water in a drop checker?", "a": "Not for accurate readings. Use 4 dKH reference solution so the color reflects only CO2."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$gravel-vacuum$g$, $g$Gravel Vacuum$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A gravel vacuum is a siphon with a wide tube that pulls waste and debris out of the substrate while draining water during a water change.$g$, $g$Uneaten food, fish waste and plant debris settle into gravel and slowly break down into ammonia and nitrate. Vacuuming removes that waste before it pollutes the tank, and it combines two chores into one, so it is the most useful maintenance tool most fishkeepers own.$g$, $g$How to Use a Gravel Vacuum: Water Changes Made Easy$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to use one", "text": "- Unplug the heater and filter if the water level will drop below them.\n- Start the siphon with the built-in squeeze bulb or by shaking the tube, with a bucket set below the tank.\n- Push the wide tube straight down into the gravel, let debris swirl up, then lift it before gravel gets sucked out.\n- Work in a pattern across the bottom until you've removed 25 to 50 percent of the water.\n- Refill with dechlorinated water close to the tank's temperature."}, {"heading": "Sand and planted tanks", "text": "Over sand, hover the tube just above the surface so it lifts debris without pulling sand. Pinch the hose to slow the flow if needed. In planted tanks, vacuum the open areas and leave root zones mostly alone, since some debris there feeds plants. Aquasoil should be vacuumed very lightly, if at all."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Vacuuming the whole tank deeply every week in a brand new setup; doing half the bottom per change is gentler.\n- Sucking up fry, shrimp or snails. Cover the intake with fine mesh or a sponge.\n- Forgetting to restart the heater and filter afterward.\n- Refilling with untreated tap water."}, {"heading": "Larger tanks", "text": "For tanks over about 40 gallons, a long hose that connects to a sink faucet saves carrying buckets. Add dechlorinator for the full tank volume when refilling directly from the tap."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How often should I vacuum aquarium gravel?", "a": "Most tanks do well with a vacuum during each weekly or biweekly water change."}, {"q": "Does vacuuming gravel remove good bacteria?", "a": "Very little. Most beneficial bacteria live in the filter, and the bacteria on gravel recover quickly."}, {"q": "How do you start a gravel vacuum without your mouth?", "a": "Use the squeeze bulb if it has one, or submerge the whole tube to fill it, then cap the end and lower it into the bucket."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$heater$g$, $g$Heater$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$An aquarium heater is a thermostat-controlled device that warms the water and holds it at a steady temperature suited to the fish you keep.$g$, $g$Most tropical fish need water around 74 to 80 F, and sudden temperature swings weaken their immune systems and invite diseases like ich. A reliable, correctly sized heater keeps conditions steady day and night, and a heater that fails stuck on is one of the fastest ways to lose a tank.$g$, $g$Aquarium Heater Size Guide: Watts per Gallon and Setup Tips$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Choosing the right size", "text": "A common rule is 3 to 5 watts per gallon, using the higher end if the room is cool or the tank is uncovered. A 20 gallon tank usually needs a 75 to 100 watt heater. For tanks over about 55 gallons, two smaller heaters at each end spread heat evenly and give you a backup if one fails."}, {"heading": "Setup tips", "text": "- Place the heater near the filter outflow or another spot with good flow.\n- Mount it at an angle if fully submersible, and let it sit in the water about 15 to 30 minutes before plugging in so the glass adjusts.\n- Use a separate thermometer. The dial on many heaters is off by a few degrees.\n- Add a heater guard for large or curious fish that might get burned."}, {"heading": "Safety and backups", "text": "Heaters can fail stuck on, cooking a tank in hours, or stuck off. An external temperature controller that cuts power if water gets too hot is cheap insurance on any valuable setup. Always unplug the heater before a water change and wait several minutes before lifting it out of the water, since a hot heater in air can crack."}, {"heading": "Do you need one?", "text": "Tropical fish almost always do. Some fish, like goldfish, white cloud minnows and many hillstream loaches, prefer cooler water and often do fine at room temperature if the room stays stable."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What size heater do I need for my aquarium?", "a": "About 3 to 5 watts per gallon. Use the higher end in a cold room or for a tank without a lid."}, {"q": "What temperature should my aquarium be?", "a": "Most tropical community fish do well at 76 to 78 F, but check the needs of each species."}, {"q": "Should I turn off my aquarium heater at night?", "a": "No. Leaving it on keeps the temperature steady, which is what the heater is for."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hob-filter$g$, $g$HOB Filter$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$An HOB filter, short for hang-on-back filter, hangs on the rim of the tank, pulls water up through an intake tube and returns it as a gentle waterfall.$g$, $g$HOB filters are affordable, easy to service and handle mechanical, biological and light chemical filtration in one box. They are the most common beginner filter for good reason, and with a few simple tweaks they can work just as well as more expensive options on small and medium tanks.$g$, $g$HOB Filter Guide: Setup, Media Upgrades and Cleaning Tips$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Choosing one", "text": "Look for a rated flow of about 4 to 6 times the tank volume per hour, plus an adjustable flow knob and a media chamber that fits your own sponges and bio media, not just branded cartridges. A model sized slightly above your tank is a good choice for messy fish like goldfish."}, {"heading": "Setup tips", "text": "- Fill the media chamber with tank water before plugging it in so the motor primes.\n- Keep the tank's water level within about an inch of the outflow lip to cut splashing noise.\n- Put a prefilter sponge on the intake for shrimp, fry and small fish.\n- For bettas or long-finned fish, baffle the outflow with a sponge if the current pushes them around."}, {"heading": "Cleaning without losing bacteria", "text": "Every 2 to 4 weeks, rinse the sponge or cartridge in a bucket of removed tank water, not tap water. Replacing the whole cartridge every month throws away your beneficial bacteria. Instead, rinse cartridges until they fall apart, then swap in a reusable sponge and keep a bag of bio media in the chamber. Clean the impeller every couple of months to keep flow strong."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "A rattling or silent filter usually means air trapped in the motor or a dirty impeller. Low water levels make it noisy. Flow dropping off often just means the media needs a rinse. If the filter stops after a power outage, refill the media chamber with tank water to help it restart."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How often should I clean a hang-on-back filter?", "a": "Rinse the media in old tank water every 2 to 4 weeks, or when flow slows. Clean the impeller every couple of months."}, {"q": "Do I need to replace HOB filter cartridges every month?", "a": "No. Rinse them and reuse them as long as they hold together, and swap to reusable sponges to keep your bacteria."}, {"q": "Why is my HOB filter so loud?", "a": "The water level is often too low, so water splashes. Top off the tank and clean the impeller if it rattles."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$internal-filter$g$, $g$Internal Filter$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$An internal filter is a compact powered filter that sits fully underwater inside the tank, usually stuck to the glass with suction cups.$g$, $g$Internal filters are a good fit for small tanks, hospital and quarantine tanks, turtle setups and tanks with no room behind them for hang-on-back or canister filters. They are cheap and quiet, but their small media capacity means they need more frequent cleaning and suit lightly stocked tanks best.$g$, $g$Internal Aquarium Filter: Pros, Cons and Setup Tips$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Pros and cons", "text": "- Quiet, since there is no waterfall\n- Fits tanks with tight lids or no back clearance\n- Many models have an adjustable spray bar or an air venturi for extra surface movement\n- Takes up swimming space inside the tank\n- Smaller media capacity than external filters\n- Suction cups can loosen over time"}, {"heading": "Choosing and placing one", "text": "Pick a filter rated for at least your tank size, ideally a bit larger. Mount it in a back corner with the outflow near the surface and pointed along the glass so it moves the whole tank. Plants or decor in front can hide it. Make sure it is fully submerged, as most models burn out if run dry."}, {"heading": "Cleaning", "text": "Because the sponge is small, it clogs faster than in larger filters. Rinse it in removed tank water every 1 to 3 weeks, or whenever flow drops. Don't replace all the media at once, and clean the impeller occasionally. Lift the filter out carefully, since debris trapped in the sponge can spill into the tank."}, {"heading": "Internal filter vs sponge filter", "text": "Sponge filters are air powered rather than motor powered, gentler, and safer for fry and shrimp. Internal power filters move more water and do better mechanical cleaning. Many keepers use an internal filter in display tanks and a sponge filter in breeding or quarantine tanks. Both can share the same tank, and a spare sponge kept in a running tank gives you an instant cycled filter for emergencies."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are internal filters good for aquariums?", "a": "Yes for small or lightly stocked tanks. Larger or heavily stocked tanks usually do better with a canister or hang-on-back filter."}, {"q": "Should an internal filter be fully submerged?", "a": "Yes. Most models are built to run underwater and can overheat or burn out if run dry."}, {"q": "How often should I clean an internal filter?", "a": "Rinse the sponge in old tank water every 1 to 3 weeks, or whenever flow slows."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$light-timer$g$, $g$Light Timer$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A light timer is a device that switches aquarium lights on and off automatically at the same times every day, either as a plug-in timer or built into the light.$g$, $g$Consistent day length keeps fish on a healthy rhythm and is one of the simplest ways to control algae. Plants and algae both react to how long the lights stay on, and a timer removes the guesswork of remembering, especially on busy days or when you travel.$g$, $g$Aquarium Light Timer: Best Schedule for Plants and Algae$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How long to run lights", "text": "- Planted tanks: 6 to 8 hours a day is a common starting point.\n- Fish-only tanks: 8 to 10 hours is usually plenty.\n- New planted tanks: start near 6 hours and increase slowly once plants are growing well.\n\nIf algae takes over, cutting an hour or two off the photoperiod is often the first fix."}, {"heading": "Types of timers", "text": "Mechanical timers with pins are cheap and simple but can drift and get noisy. Digital timers are more precise and remember settings after outages on most models. Many LED lights now include built-in programs with sunrise and sunset ramping, which eases fish into the day and avoids the startle of lights snapping on in a dark room. Whatever type you choose, set it once and leave it, since constant schedule changes do more harm than any single choice."}, {"heading": "Setting a good schedule", "text": "Time the lights for when you actually watch the tank, often afternoon and evening. If you inject CO2, set it to start 1 to 2 hours before the lights. Some keepers use a midday break, called a siesta, to fight algae. It is debated, and many tanks do fine with one steady block of light."}, {"heading": "Safety tips", "text": "Use a timer rated for the light's power draw, plug it into a grounded outlet, and leave a drip loop in every cord so water can't run into the plug. Check the clock after power outages, as some timers reset."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How many hours should aquarium lights be on?", "a": "About 6 to 8 hours for planted tanks and 8 to 10 hours for fish-only tanks."}, {"q": "Is it bad to leave aquarium lights on 24/7?", "a": "Yes. Fish need darkness to rest, and constant light causes heavy algae growth."}, {"q": "Should aquarium lights be on during the day or night?", "a": "Whenever you'll enjoy the tank most. Fish adjust to the schedule as long as it stays consistent."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$lighting$g$, $g$Lighting$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$Aquarium lighting is the light fixture over a tank, used to show off fish, set a day and night rhythm, and power photosynthesis in live plants.$g$, $g$The right light depends on what's in the tank. Fish-only tanks just need enough light to view and a steady schedule, while planted tanks need the right intensity and spectrum. Too much light for your plants, nutrients or CO2 is the most common cause of algae trouble.$g$, $g$Aquarium Lighting Guide: Choosing a Light for Fish and Plants$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Light for plants", "text": "Intensity is measured as PAR at the substrate. Rough guidelines:\n\n- Low light: under about 30 PAR. Java fern, anubias, crypts and mosses do well.\n- Medium light: 30 to 50 PAR. Most stem plants and swords.\n- High light: over 50 PAR. Carpeting plants and red stems, usually with CO2 injection.\n\nMany manufacturers list PAR at certain depths, which is more useful than wattage."}, {"heading": "Spectrum and color", "text": "Plants use mostly red and blue light, but a full spectrum light around 6,500 K looks natural to our eyes and grows plants well. Lights with extra red make plants and fish colors pop. Very blue lights look dramatic but can wash out colors in freshwater tanks."}, {"heading": "Balancing light and algae", "text": "More light means plants need more fertilizer and carbon to keep up. If they can't, algae takes advantage. Start with a moderate setting and 6 to 8 hours a day, then increase slowly as plants grow. Keep tanks out of direct sunlight, which is impossible to control and a classic algae trigger."}, {"heading": "Choosing a fixture", "text": "LED lights are now standard: efficient, cool running and long lasting. Pick one that covers the full length of the tank, and consider a dimmable model so you can tune intensity. For deep tanks over about 20 inches, plan on a stronger light to reach the bottom. Raising the light a few inches or lowering its brightness is an easy fix if plants melt or algae appears soon after an upgrade."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What kind of light is best for an aquarium?", "a": "A full spectrum LED around 6,500 K suits most fish and plant tanks. Planted tanks need one with enough PAR for the plants you grow."}, {"q": "Do fish need light at night?", "a": "No. Fish need darkness to rest. A dim moonlight setting is fine but not necessary."}, {"q": "Can too much light cause algae?", "a": "Yes. Light beyond what plants can use, or lights left on too long, is a leading cause of algae."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$powerhead$g$, $g$Powerhead$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A powerhead is a small submersible pump that pushes water around the tank to improve circulation, remove dead spots and increase surface movement.$g$, $g$Good circulation spreads heat, oxygen, fertilizer and CO2 evenly and keeps debris moving toward the filter instead of settling in corners. Powerheads are essential in reef tanks and useful in large, densely planted or river-style freshwater tanks, as long as the flow matches your fish.$g$, $g$Aquarium Powerhead: Do You Need One and Where to Place It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "When to use one", "text": "- Large tanks where the filter can't move water end to end\n- Densely planted tanks with dead spots where debris and algae collect\n- High-tech planted tanks, to spread CO2 and nutrients\n- River and stream setups for hillstream loaches and other fast water fish\n- Reef tanks, where corals rely on strong, changing flow"}, {"heading": "How much flow", "text": "Most freshwater tanks do well with total circulation, filter and powerhead combined, of about 5 to 10 times the tank volume per hour. Reef tanks often run 20 to 50 times. Wavemakers, a type of controllable powerhead, vary the flow to mimic natural currents."}, {"heading": "Placement tips", "text": "Mount the powerhead near the top or middle of the tank, often on a side wall, aimed along the length of the tank rather than straight across. Aim to create a gentle circular current that brushes the plants and the surface. Leave a calm area where fish can rest out of the flow. Watch your fish for a day after adding one, and adjust the angle if they avoid part of the tank."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Blasting fish that prefer calm water, like bettas, fancy goldfish and many gouramis\n- Leaving the intake bare in tanks with shrimp, fry or small fish; cover it with a sponge\n- Letting the impeller clog, which weakens flow; clean it every few months\n- Weak suction cup mounts; magnetic mounts hold far better"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do I need a powerhead in a freshwater tank?", "a": "Not always. A good filter is enough for most small tanks, but large, planted or river-style tanks benefit from extra flow."}, {"q": "Is a powerhead the same as a wavemaker?", "a": "A wavemaker is a type of powerhead that can vary its flow, usually with a controller, to mimic natural currents."}, {"q": "Can a powerhead be too strong for fish?", "a": "Yes. If fish struggle to swim or hide constantly, reduce the flow or redirect it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$protein-skimmer$g$, $g$Protein Skimmer$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A protein skimmer is a saltwater filter device that uses a column of fine bubbles to pull dissolved organic waste out of the water before it breaks down.$g$, $g$If you keep freshwater fish, you will not need one, but the term comes up constantly in reef discussions and gear listings. Knowing why skimmers fail in freshwater saves you from buying the wrong equipment and points you toward what actually removes organics in a freshwater tank.$g$, $g$Protein Skimmer Explained: Do Freshwater Tanks Need One?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How a skimmer works", "text": "A pump injects air into a tall chamber, creating a dense cloud of tiny bubbles. Proteins, fats and other dissolved organics cling to the bubble surfaces and ride them to the top, where they build up as a thick, brown foam. That foam spills into a collection cup, which you empty and rinse every few days.\n\nBecause waste leaves the system before bacteria turn it into ammonia and then nitrate, a skimmer lowers the nutrient load that feeds algae and keeps reef water clear."}, {"heading": "Why it does not work in freshwater", "text": "Salt water has the right surface tension and chemistry for stable foam. In fresh water, bubbles pop almost instantly and very little waste sticks to them, so a skimmer produces little or no skimmate. A few keepers try them on heavily stocked freshwater systems, but results are poor and inconsistent.\n\nFreshwater keepers get the same benefit in other ways:\n\n- Regular water changes of 25 to 50 percent a week\n- Mechanical filtration you rinse often so trapped food does not rot\n- Fast-growing plants that soak up nitrate\n- Activated carbon or resin media for dissolved organics and tannins"}, {"heading": "Choosing one for a marine tank", "text": "Skimmers are rated by tank size, but those ratings assume light stocking. Many reefers pick a model rated for about 1.5 to 2 times their actual volume. Most sit in a sump, though hang-on models exist for small tanks. Expect a break-in period of one to two weeks before foam forms steadily, and adjust the water level so the foam is wet but not overflowing."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do I need a protein skimmer for a freshwater tank?", "a": "No. Skimmers rely on salt water to form stable foam, so they remove almost nothing in fresh water. Water changes, good mechanical filtration and plants do the job instead."}, {"q": "Is a protein skimmer necessary for a reef tank?", "a": "It is not strictly required, and some small nano reefs run without one using frequent water changes. On most reef tanks over about 40 gallons it is considered standard equipment."}, {"q": "Why is my protein skimmer not making foam?", "a": "New skimmers need a week or two to break in, and a freshly cleaned tank or recent feeding change can reduce foam. Also check the water level setting, air intake and pump for clogs."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$sponge-filter$g$, $g$Sponge Filter$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A sponge filter is a simple, air-powered filter where rising bubbles pull tank water through a porous foam sponge that traps debris and grows beneficial bacteria.$g$, $g$Sponge filters are cheap, nearly impossible to break and gentle enough for fry, shrimp and bettas that struggle in strong current. Because the sponge is a huge home for nitrifying bacteria, many keepers run one in every tank so they always have a seeded filter ready for quarantine or emergencies.$g$, $g$What Is a Sponge Filter? How It Works and When to Use One$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it works", "text": "An air pump pushes air down a length of airline tubing into the filter's lift tube. As bubbles rise up the tube, they drag water with them, which pulls tank water in through the sponge. Debris gets caught in the foam, and bacteria living on the sponge's surface convert ammonia and nitrite. There is no motor in the water and no intake that can suck up baby fish or shrimp."}, {"heading": "Choosing and setting one up", "text": "- Pick a sponge rated for your tank size or larger. Two medium sponges often beat one big one.\n- Coarse foam clogs less; fine foam polishes water better and is safest for tiny shrimp.\n- Use an air pump strong enough for the depth of your tank, plus a check valve so water cannot siphon back into the pump during a power cut.\n- An airline control valve lets you turn flow down for bettas or fry.\n- Before adding fish, squeeze a seeded sponge from an established tank into it, or let it run through a full cycle."}, {"heading": "Cleaning without losing bacteria", "text": "Clean when flow slows noticeably, usually every two to four weeks. Pull the sponge, squeeze it a few times in a bucket of old tank water and put it back. Never rinse it under chlorinated tap water or replace the sponge; it holds most of your bacteria. A sponge can last for years."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Running it alone in a heavily stocked or large tank where it cannot keep up\n- Forgetting the check valve\n- Over-cleaning until it looks new\n- Expecting crystal-clear water; sponges are mainly biological filters"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is a sponge filter enough for a tank?", "a": "For lightly stocked tanks up to about 20 to 30 gallons, yes, as long as it is sized properly. Bigger or heavily stocked tanks usually pair one with another filter."}, {"q": "Do sponge filters need an air pump?", "a": "Most do. A few models run off a small powerhead instead, but the standard design needs an air pump and airline tubing."}, {"q": "How often should I clean a sponge filter?", "a": "Squeeze it in old tank water whenever flow drops, typically every two to four weeks. Light, infrequent cleaning protects the bacteria living in it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$substrate$g$, $g$Substrate$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$Substrate is the layer of material on the bottom of an aquarium, such as gravel, sand, aquasoil or bare glass, that fish, plants and bacteria live on.$g$, $g$Your substrate choice affects water chemistry, plant growth, cleaning and the health of the fish that root along the bottom. It is also the hardest thing to change once a tank is running, so it is worth picking the right one before you fill the tank.$g$, $g$Aquarium Substrate Guide: Gravel vs Sand vs Aquasoil$g$, $g$[{"heading": "The main types", "text": "- Gravel: easy to vacuum and lets water flow through it. Choose smooth, rounded pieces if you keep bottom feeders.\n- Sand: natural look, great for corydoras, loaches and other fish that sift. Debris sits on top, so it is easy to siphon from the surface.\n- Aquasoil: baked clay granules packed with nutrients for planted tanks. It softens water and lowers pH, and usually breaks down over one to three years.\n- Crushed coral or aragonite: raises pH and hardness, useful for African cichlids and livebearers in soft-water areas.\n- Bare bottom: easiest to keep spotless, common in fry, quarantine and grow-out tanks."}, {"heading": "How much to use", "text": "Plan on 1 to 2 inches for fish-only gravel or sand and 2 to 3 inches for planted tanks, sloping deeper toward the back. A rough rule is 1 to 1.5 pounds of gravel per gallon for a 1.5 to 2 inch layer, but check the bag; aquasoil is lighter and sold by volume."}, {"heading": "Setup tips", "text": "Rinse gravel and sand in a bucket until the water runs mostly clear. Do not rinse aquasoil; it falls apart. Many aquasoils release ammonia for the first few weeks, so cycle the tank fully and do frequent water changes before adding livestock. In inert gravel or sand, add root tabs near heavy root feeders like swords and crypts."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Using sharp, jagged gravel with catfish and loaches\n- Laying sand very deep without plants or snails to stir it\n- Never vacuuming, which lets waste build up\n- Adding pH-raising rock or coral substrate to a soft-water tank by accident"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the best substrate for a beginner aquarium?", "a": "Medium, smooth gravel or aquarium sand. Both are inert, cheap and easy to clean, and you can add root tabs later if you want plants."}, {"q": "Is sand or gravel better for an aquarium?", "a": "Neither is better overall. Sand suits sifting fish and a natural look, while gravel is easier to vacuum and lets water move through it."}, {"q": "Do I need to replace aquarium substrate?", "a": "Gravel and sand last indefinitely with regular vacuuming. Aquasoil slowly breaks down and loses nutrients, so planted tanks often redo or cap it after a few years."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$sump$g$, $g$Sump$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A sump is a second tank, usually hidden in the stand below the display, that receives water by gravity, holds filtration and equipment, then pumps clean water back up.$g$, $g$A sump adds water volume, which makes temperature and water chemistry more stable, and it gets heaters, filter media and probes out of sight. The tradeoff is plumbing: a sump that is sized or set up wrong can flood the floor when the power goes out.$g$, $g$Aquarium Sump Setup: How a Sump Works and Do You Need One$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How a sump works", "text": "Water leaves the display tank through an overflow, either a drilled hole with an internal box or a hang-on siphon overflow. It drains down into the sump, passes through filter socks or sponges, then through biological media chambers. A return pump in the last chamber sends water back to the display. Heaters, skimmers, reactors and monitoring probes sit in the sump where they are easy to reach."}, {"heading": "Freshwater vs saltwater sumps", "text": "Sumps are standard on reef tanks, but they work well in freshwater too, especially on big tanks with large fish like cichlids, arowanas or goldfish that produce a lot of waste. Freshwater sumps often hold large volumes of sponge, ceramic media and sometimes a plant refugium. Planted tank keepers tend to avoid them because the splashing drains release CO2."}, {"heading": "Setting one up safely", "text": "- Size the sump so it can hold all the water that drains down when the return pump stops, with space to spare. Test this before you trust it.\n- Mark the normal water line and top off evaporation with fresh water only.\n- Pick a return pump that turns over about 3 to 5 times the display volume per hour after head height loss.\n- Drill a small anti-siphon hole in the return line just below the surface so it cannot siphon water backward.\n- Use a backup drain or emergency overflow on drilled tanks."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Overfilling the sump, skipping the power-outage test and letting filter socks clog until the overflow backs up are the usual causes of floods. Change or rinse socks every few days."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do I need a sump for a freshwater aquarium?", "a": "No. Most freshwater tanks run fine on canister, hang-on-back or sponge filters. A sump is worth it mainly on large tanks or heavy waste producers."}, {"q": "How big should a sump be?", "a": "Many keepers aim for about 20 to 30 percent of the display volume, but the real rule is that it must hold the drain-back water during a power outage without overflowing."}, {"q": "Can a sump overflow?", "a": "Yes, if it is filled too high or the display drains more than expected when the pump stops. Running a power-off test before leaving it unattended prevents this."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$thermometer$g$, $g$Thermometer$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$An aquarium thermometer is a gauge that shows the actual water temperature in your tank, so you can confirm your heater is keeping fish in their safe range.$g$, $g$Heater dials are often off by several degrees, and a stuck heater can cook or chill a tank overnight. A thermometer you glance at every day is the cheapest way to catch a problem before fish show stress, ich outbreaks or worse.$g$, $g$Aquarium Thermometer Guide: Types, Placement and Accuracy$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Types of thermometers", "text": "- Glass stick-in: floats or sticks to the glass inside the tank. Cheap and fairly accurate, but can break.\n- Stick-on strip: an LCD strip on the outside of the glass. Easy to read, but it is affected by room temperature and can be off by a few degrees.\n- Digital with probe: a probe sits in the water and the display mounts outside. Usually the most accurate and easiest to read.\n- Controllers and smart monitors: digital units that can cut power to a heater or send alerts if the temperature drifts."}, {"heading": "Where to put it", "text": "Place it on the opposite side of the tank from the heater, about midway down, so you read the overall water temperature rather than the warm spot next to the heater. In tanks over about 75 gallons, two thermometers at each end show whether heat is spreading evenly."}, {"heading": "Checking accuracy", "text": "Compare it with a second thermometer, or test it in a glass of ice water, which should read 32°F. If two thermometers disagree by more than a degree or two, trust the digital probe and replace the odd one out."}, {"heading": "What the numbers mean", "text": "Most tropical community fish do well at 74 to 80°F. Goldfish and many coldwater species prefer the mid 60s to low 70s. Discus and some wild-type fish like 82 to 86°F. What matters most is stability; swings of more than 2 to 3°F in a day stress fish."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do I really need an aquarium thermometer?", "a": "Yes. Heaters fail and their dials are often inaccurate, so a thermometer is the only way to know the real temperature."}, {"q": "Are stick-on aquarium thermometers accurate?", "a": "They are fine for a rough reading but can be off by a few degrees because room air affects the glass. A digital probe or glass thermometer inside the water is more reliable."}, {"q": "What temperature should my aquarium be?", "a": "For most tropical fish, 74 to 80°F. Check the needs of your specific species, since goldfish, discus and hillstream fish all differ."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$uv-sterilizer$g$, $g$UV Sterilizer$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A UV sterilizer is an aquarium device that pumps water past an ultraviolet bulb, killing or disabling free-floating algae, bacteria and parasites as they pass through.$g$, $g$A UV unit is one of the fastest fixes for green water and can lower the spread of some free-swimming pathogens between fish. It only works on what flows through it, though, so it supports good water quality rather than replacing filtration, water changes or quarantine.$g$, $g$UV Sterilizer for Aquariums: Does It Work and Is It Worth It?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it can and cannot do", "text": "UV light damages the DNA of tiny organisms drifting in the water column. That makes it very effective against the single-celled algae that cause green water, and it can reduce free-floating bacteria and the swimming stage of some parasites like ich.\n\nIt does nothing for algae growing on glass, rocks or plants, and it does not harm the beneficial bacteria living in your filter and substrate. Parasites already on a fish are untouched, so it is not a cure for sick fish."}, {"heading": "Choosing and sizing one", "text": "- Match the wattage to your tank size and pump flow. Slower flow means longer exposure, which kills more organisms.\n- For green water, a lower exposure is enough. To target parasites, the water needs to move through much more slowly, so check the manufacturer's flow chart.\n- Inline units plumb into a canister filter's outlet hose. Hang-on and internal units suit smaller tanks."}, {"heading": "Running it well", "text": "Replace the bulb about every 12 months even if it still glows, because UV output fades. Clean the quartz sleeve when you change the bulb so film does not block the light. Turn it off while dosing medication, since UV can break down some treatments, and many planted tank keepers note it can break down chelated iron in fertilizer."}, {"heading": "Safety", "text": "Never look at a lit UV bulb outside its housing; it can damage eyes and skin. Unplug the unit before opening it."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Will a UV sterilizer clear green water?", "a": "Yes, usually within a few days to a week. Fix the cause too, such as too much light or excess nutrients, or the bloom can return once you remove it."}, {"q": "Does a UV sterilizer kill beneficial bacteria?", "a": "No. Beneficial bacteria live on surfaces in the filter and substrate, not free in the water, so the UV never reaches them."}, {"q": "Can I run a UV sterilizer all the time?", "a": "Yes, many keepers run it continuously. Turn it off during medication treatments and remember to replace the bulb yearly."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$wavemaker$g$, $g$Wavemaker$g$, $g$Equipment$g$, $g$A wavemaker is a small submersible pump or controller that pushes water around the tank to create strong or changing currents, rather than filtering it.$g$, $g$Good flow keeps waste moving toward the filter, removes dead spots where algae and detritus build up, and spreads oxygen and heat evenly. In freshwater it lets you build a river or hillstream setup for fish that love current, but too much flow will exhaust long-finned and slow-swimming fish.$g$, $g$Wavemaker for Freshwater Aquariums: Do You Need One?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it does", "text": "A wavemaker, also called a powerhead or circulation pump, moves a broad stream of water without any filter media. Controllable models can pulse, ramp up and down, or alternate between two pumps to mimic natural water movement. They are standard in reef tanks, where corals rely on flow to bring food and carry waste away."}, {"heading": "Freshwater uses", "text": "- River and hillstream tanks for loaches, rainbowfish, danios and some barbs\n- Large planted tanks, where flow spreads CO2 and nutrients to every leaf\n- Big cichlid or goldfish tanks with heavy waste that settles in back corners\n- Keeping detritus suspended long enough for the filter to catch it"}, {"heading": "Choosing and placing one", "text": "Total flow in a busy freshwater tank often lands around 5 to 10 times the tank volume per hour, counting the filter. Hillstream tanks run higher, while calm community tanks need little or no extra. Aim the wavemaker along the length of the tank or across the front glass, and angle it slightly toward the surface for gas exchange. Put a foam guard over the intake so small fish and shrimp cannot be pulled in."}, {"heading": "Fish that dislike strong flow", "text": "Bettas, fancy goldfish, angelfish, discus, and most long-finned or round-bodied fish tire quickly in strong current. If fish hide behind decor, struggle to swim, or hang near the surface out of the stream, turn the flow down or redirect it. Always leave calm zones behind rocks or plants where fish can rest."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do freshwater tanks need a wavemaker?", "a": "Most community tanks do not, since the filter provides enough flow. They help in large tanks, planted tanks and river-style setups."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a wavemaker and a powerhead?", "a": "The terms are often used interchangeably. Strictly, a powerhead is the pump, and a wavemaker is a pump or controller that varies the flow to create wave-like currents."}, {"q": "Can a wavemaker be too strong for fish?", "a": "Yes. If fish struggle to hold position or constantly hide, the flow is too strong for them. Reduce it or give them sheltered areas."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$barbels$g$, $g$Barbels$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Barbels are slender, whisker-like sensory feelers around the mouth of fish such as catfish, loaches, corydoras and goldfish, used to touch and taste for food.$g$, $g$Barbels are covered in taste buds, so they are how bottom feeders find food in sand and dim water. Short, worn or missing barbels are a common sign of a substrate or water quality problem, and catching that early lets them grow back.$g$, $g$Fish Barbels: What They Do and Why They Erode$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What barbels do", "text": "Barbels are packed with taste receptors and touch-sensitive nerves. Fish sweep them across the bottom to locate worms, crumbs and sinking food, often in murky water or at night when eyesight is less useful. Species range from the short paired barbels of corydoras and many barbs to the long sweeping barbels of pictus catfish and the six barbels of kuhli loaches."}, {"heading": "Barbel erosion", "text": "Barbels that look short, red, ragged or are missing entirely are a sign of trouble. The cause is debated in the hobby. Many keepers blame sharp or jagged gravel, while others point to bacterial infection from dirty substrate full of rotting food and waste. In practice the two often go together, and fixing both works best:\n\n- Switch to smooth sand or rounded fine gravel for bottom feeders\n- Vacuum the substrate regularly, especially under decor\n- Keep ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm and nitrate low with steady water changes\n- Avoid overfeeding so food does not rot in the substrate"}, {"heading": "Will they grow back?", "text": "Usually yes. Once conditions improve, barbels regrow over several weeks to a few months. Severe infections that reach the jaw need a broad-spectrum antibacterial treatment in a hospital tank. Barbels that never regrow after months of good care may have been damaged too far."}, {"heading": "Handling tips", "text": "Use a soft net and avoid pinning bottom feeders against rough decor. Many catfish also have sharp fin spines that tangle in nets, so a cup or container is gentler for moving them."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do fish whiskers grow back?", "a": "Yes, in most cases barbels regrow over weeks to months once the substrate and water quality are fixed."}, {"q": "Can catfish hurt you with their barbels?", "a": "No. Barbels are soft sensory organs. The sharp parts on catfish are the fin spines, which can poke you if you handle them."}, {"q": "Why are my corydoras barbels gone?", "a": "Usually from rough gravel, dirty substrate or poor water quality, often a mix of these. Switch to sand, clean the bottom and keep up with water changes."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$bottom-dweller$g$, $g$Bottom Dweller$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$A bottom dweller is a fish or invertebrate that spends most of its time on or just above the tank floor, feeding and resting along the substrate.$g$, $g$Bottom dwellers fill a part of the tank most fish ignore, but they are not a cleanup crew that lives on leftovers. They need their own food, the right substrate and enough floor space, which matters when you plan stocking around footprint rather than just gallons.$g$, $g$Bottom Dwelling Fish: Care, Feeding and Best Species$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common examples", "text": "- Corydoras catfish, which sift sand in groups\n- Loaches such as kuhli, clown and hillstream loaches\n- Plecos and other suckermouth catfish\n- Gobies, some cichlids like kribensis, and many shrimp and snails\n\nMany are social, so corydoras and kuhli loaches should be kept in groups of at least six."}, {"heading": "What they need", "text": "Soft sand or smooth rounded gravel protects barbels and bellies. Caves, driftwood, leaf litter and plant cover give them places to hide, which makes them more confident and more visible over time. Floor space matters more than height for these fish, so a long, shallow tank serves them better than a tall one of the same volume."}, {"heading": "Feeding them properly", "text": "Flakes are often eaten by mid-water fish before they reach the bottom. Offer sinking pellets, wafers or frozen foods aimed at the floor, and feed some species shortly after lights out since many bottom dwellers are most active at night. Plecos need vegetables or algae wafers, and many species also need driftwood to graze on. Watch that they actually get food; thin bellies mean they are being outcompeted."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Buying one corydoras or loach as a lone cleaner\n- Expecting them to live on scraps and algae\n- Stocking too many bottom species for the footprint\n- Rough substrate that wears down barbels\n- Adding them to a brand new tank, before there is any biofilm or algae for grazers like otocinclus to eat"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What are good bottom dwellers for a community tank?", "a": "Corydoras, kuhli loaches, bristlenose plecos and otocinclus are popular peaceful choices. Check adult size and group needs before buying."}, {"q": "Do bottom feeders clean the tank?", "a": "Not really. They eat leftover food and some algae but still produce waste. You still need water changes and gravel vacuuming."}, {"q": "How many bottom dwellers can I have?", "a": "It depends on floor area more than gallons. Plan your bottom species based on the tank's footprint and each species' adult size and group size."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$captive-bred$g$, $g$Captive-Bred$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Captive-bred describes fish that were spawned and raised in aquariums, hobbyist setups or fish farms rather than collected from wild rivers and lakes.$g$, $g$Most fish in pet stores today are captive-bred, and knowing the difference helps you predict how easily a fish will adjust to your water. Captive-bred stock is often used to tap water, eats prepared foods readily and takes pressure off wild populations, though quality varies a lot by breeder.$g$, $g$Captive-Bred vs Wild-Caught Fish: Which Should You Buy?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why many keepers prefer it", "text": "- Usually adapted to a wider range of pH and hardness than wild fish\n- Already eats flakes, pellets and frozen foods\n- Fewer wild parasites, though quarantine is still smart\n- No collection pressure on wild habitats\n- Often easier to find in bright color strains and long-finned forms"}, {"heading": "The downsides", "text": "Captive-bred does not automatically mean healthy. Some mass-produced strains suffer from inbreeding, weaker immune systems, deformities or shorter lifespans. Fancy guppies, bettas and some angelfish lines are known for this. Fish raised on farms in very different water may still need careful acclimation, and some farm fish are given hormones or dyes to boost color, a practice many hobbyists avoid supporting."}, {"heading": "Captive-bred vs tank-raised vs farmed", "text": "These labels are used loosely. Captive-bred usually means spawned in captivity. Farmed or pond-raised fish are bred in large outdoor ponds, mostly in Florida and Southeast Asia. Some sellers call a fish tank-raised when it was collected wild as a juvenile and grown out in captivity, which is not the same thing, so ask if it matters to you."}, {"heading": "Choosing good stock", "text": "Look for active fish with full color, clean fins and rounded bellies. Local breeders and hobbyist clubs often sell healthier, less inbred fish than mass farms, and can tell you the water they were raised in. Quarantine still applies: give any new fish 2 to 4 weeks in a separate tank before it joins your display."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are captive-bred fish better than wild-caught?", "a": "For most beginners, yes. They tend to be hardier in tap water and eat prepared food, though wild-caught fish can show stronger natural color."}, {"q": "How can I tell if a fish is captive-bred?", "a": "Ask the seller. Common store species like guppies, platies, mollies and most bettas are almost always captive-bred, while some tetras and many rare species are still collected wild."}, {"q": "Do captive-bred fish still need quarantine?", "a": "Yes. They can still carry ich, bacteria and internal parasites from the farm or store, so quarantine for 2 to 4 weeks is a good habit."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$caudal-fin$g$, $g$Caudal Fin$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$The caudal fin is a fish's tail fin, the large fin at the back of the body that provides most of the forward thrust when the fish swims.$g$, $g$The shape of the tail tells you a lot about how a fish lives, from fast open-water swimmers to slow hovering species. It is also the fin where fin rot, nipping damage and injuries usually show first, so knowing what a healthy tail looks like helps you spot problems early.$g$, $g$Caudal Fin (Tail Fin) in Fish: Types, Damage and Regrowth$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common tail shapes", "text": "- Forked: deep notch, built for speed, as in danios, tetras and barbs\n- Rounded: broad and fan-like for quick bursts and maneuvering, common in many cichlids and killifish\n- Lyre-shaped: long upper and lower points, seen in lyretail mollies and some killifish\n- Bred forms: veiltail, delta, halfmoon and crowntail bettas, or the long flowing tails of fancy guppies and goldfish\n\nLong, heavy tails are bred for looks and make fish slower and weaker swimmers, so those fish do best in gentle flow."}, {"heading": "Signs of trouble", "text": "Watch for ragged or receding edges, white or red margins, fraying, holes, blood streaks and clamped tails held tightly together. Ragged edges with a white or dark rim that keeps advancing point to fin rot. Clean crescent-shaped bites or missing chunks point to fin nipping. Tail biting, especially in bettas, shows as sudden loss without rot."}, {"heading": "Regrowth", "text": "Caudal fins usually grow back if the base of the fin is intact. New growth looks clear and thin at first, often as a fine white or transparent edge, which is normal and not a fungus. Expect weeks to a few months for full regrowth. Clean water with nitrate below about 20 ppm, steady temperature and good food speed it up.\n\nIf the edges keep receding, look red or grow fuzzy white tufts after a week of clean water, treat for fin rot or fungus in a hospital tank rather than waiting."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Will a fish's tail fin grow back?", "a": "Yes, usually, as long as the damage has not reached the body. Keep water very clean and regrowth starts within days."}, {"q": "Why is my fish's tail fin ragged?", "a": "The most common causes are fin rot from poor water, nipping from tankmates, snagging on rough decor or, in bettas, tail biting."}, {"q": "What is the clear edge on my fish's tail?", "a": "That is usually new fin tissue regrowing. It looks thin and clear or slightly white at first and fills in with color over time."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dither-fish$g$, $g$Dither Fish$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$A dither fish is a calm, active species kept with shy or aggressive fish because its relaxed, open swimming signals that the tank is safe.$g$, $g$Shy fish often hide constantly in a tank that feels empty or exposed, and territorial fish may focus all their aggression on one target. A small group of dither fish can bring timid fish out into view and spread aggression around, making the whole tank calmer and more natural.$g$, $g$Dither Fish: What They Are and the Best Species to Use$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How dithering works", "text": "In the wild, fish read safety from the behavior of other fish. A school swimming calmly in the open means no predator is nearby. In a tank, nervous fish like apistogrammas, dwarf cichlids, hatchetfish or wild-caught species take the same cue and venture out more. With territorial cichlids, dithers also give them something else to posture at, so a single tankmate or mate is not chased nonstop."}, {"heading": "Choosing dither fish", "text": "- Peaceful and quick, so they can avoid trouble\n- Mostly use the middle and upper water, away from the main fish's territory on the bottom\n- Not small enough to be eaten by the fish they are dithering\n- Kept in a proper group, usually six or more\n\nCommon picks include rasboras, danios, hatchetfish, pencilfish and many tetras for dwarf cichlids. Giant danios, rainbowfish or larger barbs suit bigger cichlids."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Choosing dithers that also breed on the bottom or claim caves just adds competition. Adding fin nippers like tiger barbs to a tank of angelfish or bettas causes new problems. Very small fish like neon tetras can become food for angelfish and larger cichlids. Dithers will not fix a tank that is too small or has no hiding places, so provide both."}, {"heading": "Is it always needed?", "text": "No. Many community tanks have dithering built in because they already hold active schooling fish. It matters most in species tanks with shy or aggressive centerpiece fish."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What are good dither fish for cichlids?", "a": "For dwarf cichlids, small tetras, rasboras or pencilfish work well. For larger cichlids, try giant danios, rainbowfish or robust barbs that will not fit in their mouths."}, {"q": "Do dither fish reduce aggression?", "a": "They can spread it out, since territorial fish have more targets to display at, but they do not replace enough space and hiding spots."}, {"q": "How many dither fish should I add?", "a": "A group of six or more is typical, since a lone or small group of dithers will be nervous themselves and defeat the purpose."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dorsal-fin$g$, $g$Dorsal Fin$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$The dorsal fin is the fin on top of a fish's back that keeps it upright, stops it from rolling and helps it turn sharply.$g$, $g$How a fish carries its dorsal fin is one of the quickest health checks you can do. A fish that holds it raised and open is usually comfortable, while one that keeps it clamped flat is often stressed, cold or getting sick. The dorsal fin also helps you sex many species.$g$, $g$Dorsal Fin in Fish: Clamped Fins, Damage and What It Means$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Shapes and variations", "text": "Dorsal fins range from tiny and spiked to tall and sail-like. Some fish have two dorsal fins, like rainbowfish and gobies, and many cichlids have a spiny front section and a soft back section. Tetras and many catfish also have a small fleshy adipose fin behind the dorsal, which is a separate fin. Sailfin mollies and bettas have been bred for especially large dorsals."}, {"heading": "Clamped dorsal fin", "text": "A dorsal held flat against the body for more than a short time is a stress signal. Common causes:\n\n- Ammonia, nitrite or high nitrate\n- Temperature too low or swinging\n- Bullying from tankmates\n- Early disease such as ich, velvet or internal parasites\n- New arrival still settling in\n\nTest your water first, check the heater, and watch for spots, scratching or rapid breathing. Fixing water usually brings fins back up within a day or two."}, {"heading": "Damage and nipping", "text": "The dorsal fin is a favorite target for fin nippers, especially on slow fish with tall fins like angelfish, bettas and sailfin mollies. Torn edges from nipping are clean bites, while fin rot looks ragged with discolored margins. Fins regrow in clean water once the cause is removed."}, {"heading": "Sexing by the dorsal fin", "text": "In many species males have a longer, more pointed or more colorful dorsal. Examples include corydoras, some tetras, rainbowfish and sailfin mollies, where males usually carry the bigger sail. Always compare several adults, since young fish show few differences."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Why is my fish's dorsal fin down?", "a": "A clamped dorsal usually means stress from poor water, wrong temperature, bullying or early illness. Test the water first."}, {"q": "Can a fish live without a dorsal fin?", "a": "Yes. Fish that lose part of it usually regrow it, and fish with permanent damage often adapt, though they may swim less steadily."}, {"q": "Will a torn dorsal fin heal?", "a": "Usually, yes. Keep water clean and stable and remove any fin nippers, and it typically regrows over a few weeks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fin-nipping$g$, $g$Fin Nipping$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Fin nipping is when a fish repeatedly bites at the fins of its tankmates, tearing small pieces out of tails and other fins.$g$, $g$Nipped fins are more than cosmetic. Constant harassment stresses victims and torn fins can turn into fin rot. Most fin nipping comes from predictable causes, wrong tankmates, too few of a schooling species or a cramped tank, so it is usually easy to prevent with good planning.$g$, $g$Fin Nipping Fish: Why It Happens and How to Stop It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common fin nippers", "text": "- Tiger barbs and some other barbs\n- Serpae tetras, black skirt tetras and some other tetras\n- Many puffers\n- Some danios when kept in small groups\n- Some gouramis and cichlids, mostly in cramped tanks\n\nCommon victims are slow fish with long fins: bettas, angelfish, fancy guppies, long-finned danios and fancy goldfish."}, {"heading": "Why fish nip", "text": "Many nippers are schooling fish that spend energy chasing each other. In small groups they turn that energy on other fish. Crowding, boredom, competition for food and an empty layout make it worse. For some species it is just part of feeding behavior and cannot be trained out."}, {"heading": "How to stop it", "text": "- Keep nippy schooling fish in groups of eight or more so they focus on each other\n- Do not mix known nippers with long-finned or slow fish\n- Add plants, wood and rocks to break up sight lines\n- Feed enough, in a few spots, so fish are not competing\n- Upgrade the tank if it is overcrowded\n- Rehome a single bully if one fish keeps causing trouble"}, {"heading": "Telling nipping from fin rot", "text": "Nipping leaves clean, crescent-shaped bites or split fins, and damage appears suddenly. Fin rot starts at the edges and creeps inward, often with white, red or dark margins. Watch the tank for a few minutes after the lights come on, when nippers are often most active. If both are present, separate the nipper first, then treat the injured fish for fin rot in clean water."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How can I tell which fish is fin nipping?", "a": "Watch the tank quietly, especially at feeding time and right after lights on. Nippers dart in at slow fish from behind."}, {"q": "Do nipped fins grow back?", "a": "Yes, in clean water fins usually regrow within a few weeks once the nipping stops."}, {"q": "Will tiger barbs nip fins if kept in a large group?", "a": "A group of eight or more greatly reduces it, but even then they are not a safe match for bettas, angelfish or other long-finned fish."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$gravid-spot$g$, $g$Gravid Spot$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$A gravid spot is a dark patch on the belly of female livebearers, just in front of the anal fin, that darkens and grows as she carries developing fry.$g$, $g$For anyone keeping guppies, mollies, platies or swordtails, the gravid spot is the easiest way to tell a female is pregnant and roughly how close she is to giving birth. That helps you plan cover or a separate space for the fry before they arrive and get eaten.$g$, $g$Gravid Spot on Guppies and Mollies: How to Tell if Pregnant$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it is", "text": "The gravid spot is darkened skin over the area where the female carries her young. Mature females usually show a faint spot even when not pregnant. As fry develop, the spot gets larger and darker, and the belly swells. In light-colored guppies you can sometimes see the tiny eyes of the fry through the skin near the end. On black mollies and dark platies the spot can be hard to see, so watch belly shape instead."}, {"heading": "Signs she is close to giving birth", "text": "- The belly develops a boxy or squared-off look just behind the gills\n- The spot is very dark and large\n- She hides, hangs near the heater or in plants, or stays near the surface\n- Loss of appetite and trembling or straining\n\nLivebearer pregnancy usually lasts about 21 to 30 days, depending on species and temperature."}, {"heading": "Protecting the fry", "text": "Adults eat newborn fry, including the mother. Thick plants like java moss, floating plants or hornwort give fry places to hide. Breeder boxes work but can stress the female, so move her only when she is very close and return her soon after birth. Many keepers prefer a separate, heavily planted tank."}, {"heading": "Good to know", "text": "Female livebearers store sperm and can have several batches of fry from one mating. A female bought from a mixed store tank may give birth weeks later even with no males in your tank."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I know if my guppy is pregnant?", "a": "Look for a darker, larger gravid spot near the anal fin and a swollen belly. Near the end, the belly looks boxy behind the gills."}, {"q": "How long are guppies and mollies pregnant?", "a": "Usually around 21 to 30 days. Mollies often run a little longer, and cooler water slows things down."}, {"q": "Do males have a gravid spot?", "a": "No. Males have a gonopodium, a narrow rod-like anal fin, instead. The gravid spot is a female trait."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hardy$g$, $g$Hardy$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Hardy describes a fish, plant or invertebrate that tolerates a wide range of water conditions and handles small beginner mistakes without getting sick easily.$g$, $g$Starting with hardy species gives new keepers room to learn without losing fish to every small swing in temperature or water chemistry. Hardy does not mean indestructible, though, and even the toughest fish suffer in an uncycled tank or with chronic neglect.$g$, $g$Hardy Fish for Beginners: What Hardy Really Means$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What makes a fish hardy", "text": "Hardy species accept a broad pH and hardness range, handle moderate temperature changes, eat prepared foods readily and resist common diseases. Many have been bred in captivity for generations, so they are adapted to tap water. Examples include zebra danios, white cloud mountain minnows, platies, many barbs, bristlenose plecos, some corydoras and many livebearers. Hardy plants include java fern, anubias, java moss and cryptocorynes."}, {"heading": "What it does not mean", "text": "- It does not mean a fish can live in a bowl or tiny tank\n- It does not replace cycling; ammonia and nitrite still burn gills\n- It does not mean any size or temperament fits any tank\n- It does not mean tolerating constant poor water. Hardy fish often show stress later, through disease or shortened life\n\nSome hardy fish, like common goldfish and many plecos, grow large and need big tanks even though they are tough."}, {"heading": "Hardy vs easy", "text": "A fish can be hardy and still a poor beginner choice. Tiger barbs are tough but nippy. Convict cichlids are nearly indestructible but aggressive breeders. Look at temperament, adult size and group needs as well as toughness."}, {"heading": "Using hardy fish wisely", "text": "Hobbyists once added a few hardy fish to cycle new tanks. Many now consider fishless cycling kinder, since even hardy fish are harmed by ammonia. If you do cycle with fish, stock lightly and test daily, changing water whenever ammonia or nitrite reach about 0.5 ppm."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the hardiest aquarium fish?", "a": "Zebra danios, white cloud minnows, platies and bristlenose plecos are among the hardiest common choices for a properly cycled tank."}, {"q": "Can hardy fish live in an uncycled tank?", "a": "They survive longer than sensitive fish, but ammonia and nitrite still damage their gills and can kill them. Cycle the tank first."}, {"q": "Are bettas hardy fish?", "a": "Bettas tolerate low oxygen thanks to their labyrinth organ, but they still need a heated, filtered tank of at least 5 gallons to stay healthy."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$labyrinth-organ$g$, $g$Labyrinth Organ$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$The labyrinth organ is a maze-like breathing structure above the gills that lets bettas, gouramis and related fish gulp and absorb oxygen from air at the surface.$g$, $g$Fish with a labyrinth organ regularly swim up for a breath of air, and that is normal behavior, not a sign of trouble. They also must be able to reach the surface at all times, and they need warm, humid air above the water to stay healthy.$g$, $g$Labyrinth Organ: How Bettas and Gouramis Breathe Air$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Which fish have one", "text": "The labyrinth organ belongs to a group called labyrinth fish, or anabantoids. Common aquarium examples include bettas, all gouramis, paradise fish and climbing perch. Snakeheads also breathe air but use a different structure. In the wild these fish live in warm, still, low-oxygen water such as rice paddies, swamps and slow streams, where air breathing gives them an advantage."}, {"heading": "How it works", "text": "The organ sits in a chamber above the gills and is made of folded, bony plates covered in a thin, blood-rich membrane. The fish gulps air at the surface, holds it in the chamber where oxygen passes into the blood, then releases the used air. Many labyrinth fish are obligate air breathers, so they can drown if they cannot reach the surface, even in well-oxygenated water."}, {"heading": "Care tips", "text": "- Leave open water at the surface; do not cover it completely with floating plants\n- Use a lid with a small gap, so the air layer above the water stays warm and humid. Breathing cold, dry air is thought to harm the organ\n- Keep the water level an inch or two below the lid\n- Use gentle filter flow; these fish prefer calm water\n- Do not rely on the organ as an excuse for unfiltered bowls; waste still builds up"}, {"heading": "In fry", "text": "Fry are not born with a working labyrinth organ. It develops around three to six weeks of age, which is a delicate stage when breeders keep the tank covered and the air warm."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can bettas live without a filter because of the labyrinth organ?", "a": "They can survive, but ammonia still builds up and poisons them. Bettas do best with a heated, gently filtered tank."}, {"q": "Why does my gourami go to the surface?", "a": "Gouramis breathe air with their labyrinth organ, so regular trips to the surface are normal. Constant gasping at the surface, though, can signal poor water."}, {"q": "Can labyrinth fish drown?", "a": "Yes. Most need to breathe air regularly and can drown if they cannot reach the surface, for example when trapped in a net or under plants."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$lateral-line$g$, $g$Lateral Line$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$The lateral line is a row of tiny sensory pores running along each side of a fish's body that senses vibration, pressure changes and water movement.$g$, $g$The lateral line explains a lot of fish behavior, from how schools turn together to why tapping on the glass is so stressful. It is also where you may notice pitting or erosion that signals poor water or nutrition, especially in cichlids, so it is worth knowing what it should look like.$g$, $g$Lateral Line in Fish: What It Does and Signs of Damage$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it works", "text": "Along the lateral line, fish have small canals under the scales lined with sensory cells called neuromasts. Movement in the surrounding water bends tiny hair-like structures on these cells, which sends signals to the brain. The same type of sensor is spread around the head. You can often see the lateral line as a thin row of dotted scales from the gill cover to the tail."}, {"heading": "What fish use it for", "text": "- Sensing nearby fish so a school can turn at the same moment\n- Detecting prey moving in murky water or the dark\n- Feeling predators approach from behind\n- Judging current and avoiding obstacles; blind cave tetras rely heavily on it\n\nBecause it is sensitive to vibration, tapping the glass, loud pumps on the stand and slamming doors can startle fish."}, {"heading": "Head and lateral line erosion", "text": "Head and lateral line erosion, often called HLLE or hole in the head, shows as pits or pale eroded patches around the head and along the lateral line. It is seen in freshwater fish like oscars, discus and other large cichlids, and in many marine fish. Its exact cause is still debated. Poor water quality and high nitrate, poor diet lacking vitamins, stray electrical current and long-term use of activated carbon have all been suggested, and the parasite Hexamita is often linked with hole in the head disease in cichlids."}, {"heading": "What to do", "text": "Improve water quality with larger, more frequent water changes, feed a varied diet with vegetables and quality foods, and check for internal parasites. Mild cases often heal, though deep pits may scar."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What does the lateral line do in fish?", "a": "It senses vibrations and water movement, letting fish feel nearby fish, prey, predators and obstacles without seeing them."}, {"q": "Can a fish lateral line heal?", "a": "Mild erosion often heals once water quality and diet improve, but severe pitting may leave permanent scars."}, {"q": "Does tapping on the glass hurt fish?", "a": "It does not injure them, but the vibration startles and stresses them through the lateral line, so it is best avoided."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$nocturnal$g$, $g$Nocturnal$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Nocturnal describes fish and invertebrates that are mostly active at night or in darkness and rest or hide during daylight hours.$g$, $g$Many popular aquarium fish, especially catfish, loaches, plecos and knifefish, are nocturnal, so they can seem shy or even missing while the lights are on. Knowing this keeps you from worrying needlessly and reminds you to feed them when they are actually awake.$g$, $g$Nocturnal Aquarium Fish: How to Care for Night-Active Fish$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common nocturnal fish", "text": "- Most plecos and many other suckermouth catfish\n- Kuhli loaches and many other loaches\n- Glass catfish, pictus catfish and upside-down catfish\n- Black ghost knifefish and other knifefish\n- Many shrimp, snails and crayfish become more active after dark\n\nSome species, like corydoras, are crepuscular, most active at dawn and dusk."}, {"heading": "Feeding them", "text": "Day-active tankmates eat most food before nocturnal fish come out. Drop sinking pellets, wafers or frozen food in shortly after lights go out, or 15 to 30 minutes before. Target caves and hiding spots, and check that bellies look rounded rather than sunken. Remove uneaten food the next morning if it is still there."}, {"heading": "Helping them feel secure", "text": "Give each nocturnal fish a cave, driftwood hollow or tube to rest in during the day. A tank with plenty of cover, dim areas under floating plants and fewer hyperactive tankmates helps them venture out sooner. Many become more visible over time as they settle in."}, {"heading": "Watching them at night", "text": "A dim blue or red light lets you watch without fully disrupting their night, since many fish see red light poorly. Keep it low and avoid running bright lights around the clock. Most tanks do well with 8 to 10 hours of light and a real dark period, which both day and night species need. Avoid tapping the glass or switching bright room lights on suddenly at night, since startled nocturnal fish can crash into decor."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do fish need darkness at night?", "a": "Yes. Fish need a regular dark period for rest and normal behavior. Turn tank lights off for 12 to 16 hours a day."}, {"q": "Why do I never see my pleco?", "a": "Most plecos are nocturnal and hide in caves or under wood during the day. Look for them after lights out."}, {"q": "Is a moonlight on all night OK for fish?", "a": "A very dim light for an hour or two is fine for viewing, but leaving it on all night can disturb sleep patterns. Most keepers put it on a timer."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$schooling-fish$g$, $g$Schooling Fish$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Schooling fish are species that naturally swim in tight, coordinated groups, all turning and moving together, and feel unsafe when kept alone or in pairs.$g$, $g$Keeping schooling fish in proper numbers is one of the biggest factors in whether they thrive. A good-sized group looks better, colors up more and behaves naturally, while a lonely few often hide, stay pale, get sick more easily or start nipping tankmates.$g$, $g$Schooling Fish: How Many to Keep and Best Species$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How many to keep", "text": "Six is the usual minimum for most schooling species, but eight to twelve or more is better, and bigger groups show tighter, more natural behavior. Plan your stocking around the full group, not one or two fish. If your tank cannot fit a proper group, choose a different species rather than a smaller school."}, {"heading": "Popular schooling fish", "text": "- Rummynose tetras, one of the tightest schoolers in the hobby\n- Harlequin and lambchop rasboras\n- Cardinal and neon tetras\n- Zebra and pearl danios\n- Cherry and tiger barbs\n- Otocinclus and corydoras, which group closely along the bottom\n\nNote that many of these actually shoal loosely most of the time and only school tightly when startled."}, {"heading": "Setting up for a school", "text": "A longer tank with open swimming space in the middle and plants or decor around the edges gives a school room to move and a place to retreat. Add the whole group at once when you can, or in a few batches through quarantine, so new fish are not isolated. Peaceful tankmates and calm surroundings let them relax and spread out."}, {"heading": "Signs the group is too small", "text": "Fish that hide constantly, stay pale, dart around nervously or chase and nip tankmates are often in too small a group. Adding more of the same species usually fixes it quickly, as long as the tank has room. Quarantine the new additions first so you do not bring disease into an established group."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the minimum number for schooling fish?", "a": "Six is the common minimum for small schooling species, but groups of eight to twelve or more are better."}, {"q": "Can different schooling fish school together?", "a": "Usually not. Fish prefer their own species, and mixed groups rarely school well. Keep a full group of each species."}, {"q": "Why are my schooling fish not schooling?", "a": "Most tetras and rasboras only school tightly when they feel threatened. In a calm tank they spread out and shoal loosely, which is a good sign."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$sexual-dimorphism$g$, $g$Sexual Dimorphism$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Sexual dimorphism means males and females of the same species look different, through size, color, fin shape, body shape or special features.$g$, $g$Telling males from females matters when you want a breeding pair, need to avoid a tank full of fighting males, or want to keep a ratio that protects females from harassment. Some species are easy to sex at a glance, while others are nearly impossible until they mature or spawn.$g$, $g$How to Tell Male vs Female Fish: Sexual Dimorphism Guide$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common differences", "text": "- Color: males are usually brighter, as in guppies, endlers, many killifish and dwarf gouramis\n- Fins: males often have longer, pointed dorsal and anal fins, as in bettas and many tetras\n- Body shape: females are usually rounder and fuller, especially when carrying eggs, as in corydoras and tetras\n- Size: males are larger in many cichlids, while females are larger in many livebearers\n- Special features: livebearer males have a gonopodium, bristlenose pleco males grow bushy bristles, and goldfish males show white breeding stars on their gill covers in season"}, {"heading": "Hard-to-sex species", "text": "Some species are monomorphic, meaning males and females look nearly the same. Discus, angelfish, many tetras and young fish of most species are tricky. Hobbyists often buy a group of six juveniles and let pairs form naturally. In cichlids, looking at the breeding tube that appears just before spawning, called the papilla, is the most reliable sign: it is blunt and wide on females and narrow and pointed on males."}, {"heading": "Why the ratio matters", "text": "For livebearers like guppies, mollies and platies, keep about two or three females per male so no single female gets constantly chased. Male bettas and many gouramis fight each other, so only one male per tank unless it is large and well planted. In apistogrammas and kribensis, a single pair or one male with a few females works best."}, {"heading": "Tips", "text": "Compare several fish side by side, since differences are easier to see in a group. Many traits appear only at maturity, which can take a few months to a year."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How can I tell if my fish is male or female?", "a": "Compare color, fin length and belly shape against others of the same species. Males are often brighter with longer fins, and females are rounder."}, {"q": "At what age can you sex fish?", "a": "Many livebearers can be sexed at 4 to 8 weeks, while cichlids, tetras and many others may take several months to a year."}, {"q": "Can you tell male from female angelfish?", "a": "Not reliably by looks. The surest way is the breeding tube that appears just before spawning, which is blunter on females."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$shoaling$g$, $g$Shoaling$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Shoaling is when fish gather in a loose social group of their own kind, staying near each other but swimming independently rather than moving in tight formation.$g$, $g$Many fish sold as schooling fish actually spend most of their time shoaling. Knowing the difference helps you read your tank: relaxed shoaling usually means fish feel safe, while sudden tight schooling often means they are startled or stressed. Either way, these fish still need a proper group.$g$, $g$Shoaling vs Schooling Fish: What Is the Difference?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Shoaling vs schooling", "text": "A shoal is any group of fish that stays together for social reasons. A school is a shoal that moves in tight coordination, all facing the same direction and turning together. Fish can switch between the two. Neon tetras, for example, spread out across the tank and pick at food when calm, then pull into a tight school if a net or large fish approaches."}, {"heading": "Fish that mostly shoal", "text": "- Most tetras, including neons, cardinals and black skirts\n- Most rasboras and danios\n- Many barbs\n- Corydoras, which shoal along the bottom\n- Many livebearers, which group loosely\n\nA few species, like rummynose tetras, school tightly much of the time even when relaxed."}, {"heading": "What it means for your tank", "text": "Shoaling fish still need company. Kept alone or in pairs, they become stressed, hide and lose color. Plan on six as a minimum and eight to twelve or more if space allows. Mixing several shoaling species is fine, but each needs its own group, since fish shoal most comfortably with their own species."}, {"heading": "Reading the behavior", "text": "Fish spread out, foraging and showing good color is the goal. If your shoal stays bunched tightly in one corner for long periods, look for a cause, such as an aggressive tankmate, bright open space with no cover, or a recent disturbance. Fish that stay apart from the group, hang at the surface or sit on the bottom may be sick and are worth a closer look."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the difference between shoaling and schooling?", "a": "Shoaling fish stay together loosely as a social group. Schooling fish move in tight, coordinated formation. Many fish do both depending on how safe they feel."}, {"q": "Are neon tetras schooling or shoaling fish?", "a": "Mostly shoaling. They spread out when relaxed and form a tight school when startled."}, {"q": "How many shoaling fish should I keep?", "a": "At least six of one species, and more if your tank has room. Larger groups are calmer and more natural."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$territorial$g$, $g$Territorial$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Territorial describes fish that claim a section of the tank, such as a cave, plant or corner, and chase away other fish that come too close.$g$, $g$Territorial behavior is the root of most aggression problems in community tanks. Understanding which fish defend space, and how much they need, lets you plan tank size, layout and tankmates so everyone has room, rather than ending up with one bully and a lot of torn fins.$g$, $g$Territorial Fish: How to Reduce Aggression in Your Tank$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common territorial fish", "text": "- Most cichlids, from small apistogrammas and rams to large Central American and African species\n- Male bettas and many gouramis\n- Red tail and rainbow sharks\n- Some plecos and loaches, which guard caves\n- Many species become far more territorial when breeding"}, {"heading": "Why fish defend territory", "text": "Territory gives a fish access to food, mates and safe breeding sites. Aggression usually peaks during spawning and while guarding eggs or fry. Fish are also most likely to target tankmates that look like them, since those are their rivals, so similar-looking or same-shaped species clash most."}, {"heading": "How to reduce aggression", "text": "- Give enough floor space; territorial fish care about footprint more than height\n- Break up sight lines with rocks, wood and plants so fish cannot see each other across the tank\n- Provide more caves than territorial fish so none has to fight for one\n- Avoid mixing similar-looking species or more than one male of an aggressive species in a small tank\n- Add dither fish to spread attention around\n- Rearrange decor when adding new fish so everyone starts over\n- Overstocking African mbuna cichlids is a known strategy to spread aggression, but it needs a large tank and heavy filtration"}, {"heading": "When to separate", "text": "If one fish is constantly chased, hiding, losing scales or not eating, separate it. Damaged fish rarely recover while being harassed. A tank divider, a separate tank or rehoming the aggressor are all reasonable options, and moving the bully out for a few days can reset its territory."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I stop my fish from being territorial?", "a": "You cannot stop the instinct, but more space, broken sight lines, extra caves and compatible tankmates greatly reduce fighting."}, {"q": "Why did my fish suddenly become aggressive?", "a": "Sudden aggression often means it is preparing to breed or guarding eggs. A new fish or changed layout can also trigger it."}, {"q": "Does rearranging the tank reduce aggression?", "a": "Often, yes. Moving rocks and decor erases claimed territories, so fish have to reestablish them on more equal terms."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$wild-caught$g$, $g$Wild-Caught$g$, $g$Fish Behavior & Biology$g$, $g$Wild-caught describes fish collected from rivers, lakes and streams in their natural habitat, rather than bred in captivity, and then shipped into the aquarium trade.$g$, $g$Wild-caught fish can show richer natural colors and behaviors, and some species are only available this way. They also arrive more stressed, often carry parasites and may need water closer to their native conditions, so they usually take more care than captive-bred fish.$g$, $g$Wild-Caught Fish: Pros, Cons and How to Care for Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why keepers choose them", "text": "- Natural color patterns and behaviors that captive strains may have lost\n- Fresh bloodlines for breeding programs\n- Species that are rarely or never bred in captivity, such as many wild bettas, some catfish and dwarf cichlids\n- Many cardinal tetras are still wild collected, and some sustainable collection projects support local communities and help protect river habitats"}, {"heading": "Challenges", "text": "Wild fish go through capture, holding and long shipping, so they arrive stressed and sometimes thin. They commonly carry internal parasites, worms and gill flukes. Many come from very soft, acidic blackwater and struggle in hard tap water at first. Some will not recognize flakes or pellets as food right away."}, {"heading": "Helping them settle in", "text": "- Quarantine for at least 4 weeks, and many keepers treat for internal parasites during that time\n- Match their native water as closely as you can, often softer water with a pH around 6.0 to 7.0, then shift gradually if needed\n- Keep lights dim and give plenty of cover\n- Offer live or frozen foods like bloodworms and brine shrimp, then mix in prepared foods\n- Acclimate slowly, especially if your water differs a lot from the seller's"}, {"heading": "Buying responsibly", "text": "Ask sellers where fish came from and how long they have been in store. Fish that have been in store for a week or two and are eating are usually a safer buy than ones fresh off the plane."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are wild-caught fish harder to keep?", "a": "Usually, yes. They may need softer water, carry parasites and take time to accept prepared foods, though many adjust well with good care."}, {"q": "Is buying wild-caught fish bad for the environment?", "a": "It depends on the species and collection practices. Some fisheries are well managed and support local communities, while others harm wild populations."}, {"q": "Should I deworm wild-caught fish?", "a": "Many experienced keepers routinely treat wild-caught fish for internal parasites during quarantine, since infections are common."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$acclimation$g$, $g$Acclimation$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Acclimation is the process of slowly adjusting new fish or invertebrates to your tank's temperature and water chemistry before releasing them, so the change does not shock them.$g$, $g$To acclimate a fish properly is one of the simplest ways to cut losses with new arrivals. Fish moved straight from a bag into water with a different temperature, pH or hardness can go into shock, and sensitive species and shrimp may die within hours even if they looked perfectly healthy in the store.$g$, $g$How to Acclimate Fish: Float and Drip Methods Step by Step$g$, $g$[{"heading": "The float method", "text": "This works for most hardy fish when your water is similar to the store's.\n\n- Turn off the tank light to lower stress.\n- Float the sealed bag in the tank for 15 to 20 minutes so the temperature evens out.\n- Open the bag and add about half a cup of tank water every 5 minutes for 15 to 20 minutes.\n- Net the fish into the tank and throw the bag water away instead of pouring it in."}, {"heading": "The drip method", "text": "Use this for shrimp, sensitive fish, wild-caught fish or when your water is very different from the seller's. After floating, pour the fish and their water into a clean bucket. Run airline tubing from the tank to the bucket, tie a loose knot or use a valve so it drips about 2 to 4 drops per second, and let the water volume double, then double again. That usually takes 45 to 60 minutes for fish and 1 to 2 hours for shrimp."}, {"heading": "The ammonia trap with shipped fish", "text": "Fish that spent many hours in a bag build up ammonia, but the bag water's pH drops from their breath, which keeps that ammonia in a less toxic form. Once you open the bag, the pH rises and the ammonia becomes more harmful. For fish shipped overnight, many keepers float to match temperature, then move them fairly quickly into tank water rather than doing a long acclimation in old bag water."}, {"heading": "After release", "text": "Keep the lights off for a few hours and skip feeding until the next day. Watch for hiding, clamped fins or rapid breathing. Ideally, every new fish goes into a quarantine tank for 2 to 4 weeks first, and you acclimate them into that tank the same way."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long should you acclimate fish?", "a": "About 30 to 40 minutes total for most fish using the float method, and 45 minutes to 2 hours with the drip method for sensitive fish and shrimp."}, {"q": "Should I put the store water in my tank?", "a": "No. Store water can carry ammonia, diseases and medications. Net the fish out and discard the bag water."}, {"q": "Why did my fish die after acclimation?", "a": "Common causes are a big difference in pH or hardness, temperature shock, ammonia from a long shipment, stress, or disease the fish already carried."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$aquarium-salt$g$, $g$Aquarium Salt$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Aquarium salt is plain sodium chloride, made for freshwater tanks without iodine or additives, used short-term to treat certain illnesses and reduce stress.$g$, $g$Salt is cheap and genuinely useful against some parasites, nitrite poisoning and minor wounds. It is also overused. Many fish, plants and invertebrates are harmed by it, it does not evaporate out of the tank, and keeping it in the water all the time can do more harm than good.$g$, $g$Aquarium Salt: How to Use It, Dosage and Which Fish Hate It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "When salt helps", "text": "- Nitrite poisoning: chloride competes with nitrite at the gills and protects fish while you fix the cycle\n- Ich and some external parasites, especially for salt-tolerant fish like goldfish and livebearers\n- Minor wounds and stress after shipping or injury\n- Short dips for some external parasites, done at higher strength for only a few minutes and only by experienced keepers"}, {"heading": "How much to use", "text": "Doses vary by goal, and fish vary in tolerance. A mild tonic is often around 1 tablespoon per 5 gallons. Treatment levels for parasites are commonly about 1 to 3 teaspoons per gallon, added gradually over a day or two. Always dissolve salt in tank water first, and dose by the gallons of water you actually have, not the tank's label size."}, {"heading": "Who does not tolerate it", "text": "Scaleless and sensitive fish such as corydoras, plecos and other catfish, loaches, tetras from soft water and many wild-caught fish can be stressed or harmed even at low doses. Most live plants suffer at treatment levels. Shrimp and snails are sensitive too. In a mixed tank, treat sick fish in a separate hospital tank."}, {"heading": "How to remove it", "text": "Salt does not evaporate. When you top off with fresh water the salt stays behind. Only water changes remove it, so after treatment do several partial water changes over a week or two, replacing with unsalted water. When doing water changes during treatment, add back only the salt for the volume you removed."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can I use table salt in my aquarium?", "a": "Plain, non-iodized salt with no anti-caking agents works. Avoid iodized table salt and anything with additives, and never use marine salt mix in freshwater."}, {"q": "Should I add aquarium salt to my tank all the time?", "a": "No. Most keepers use it only for specific problems and short periods. Constant salt can stress soft-water fish and harm plants and invertebrates."}, {"q": "Is aquarium salt safe for shrimp and snails?", "a": "Not at treatment doses. Shrimp and snails are sensitive, so move them out or treat fish in a separate hospital tank."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$camallanus-worms$g$, $g$Camallanus Worms$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Camallanus worms are parasitic roundworms that live in a fish's intestine and show up as tiny red threads poking out of the vent.$g$, $g$These worms feed on blood, so infected fish slowly waste away and can spread the parasite to every fish in the tank. Livebearers, especially guppies and endlers, carry them often. Once you see red threads, treat the whole tank promptly and plan on a second dose to catch worms that hatch later.$g$, $g$Camallanus Worms in Fish: Signs, Treatment and Dosing$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Red or reddish brown threads hanging from the vent, often only when the fish is resting or after lights out\n- Thin body or sunken belly even though the fish eats well\n- Swollen or red vent area\n- Lethargy, hanging near the bottom and slow deaths over weeks\n\nCheck fish with a flashlight about an hour after lights out. The worms extend out of the vent more when the fish is still, so you can spot light infections you would miss during the day."}, {"heading": "How fish catch them", "text": "Camallanus usually arrives with new fish, especially cheap livebearers from crowded farms. Some species pass directly from fish to fish when tankmates eat worm larvae released into the water, and copepods and other small crustaceans can carry them too. That means one infected fish can seed the entire tank within a few weeks."}, {"heading": "How to treat camallanus", "text": "The standard treatments are levamisole, fenbendazole and flubendazole. Levamisole works by paralyzing the worms so the fish passes them, and it is usually gentler on tanks with invertebrates. Fenbendazole and flubendazole kill snails and can harm shrimp, so move invertebrates out first or treat in a hospital tank. Praziquantel, a common dewormer, does not work against camallanus.\n\nDose the whole tank, not just the fish showing worms. Do a large water change and vacuum the gravel 24 to 48 hours after dosing to remove expelled worms and eggs. Repeat the full treatment about 2 to 3 weeks later, since the first round does not kill larvae that are still developing."}, {"heading": "Preventing a repeat", "text": "Quarantine every new fish for at least 4 weeks and watch for worms at night. Many breeders deworm all new livebearers in quarantine as a routine step. Avoid moving nets, plants or decor between tanks while you are treating, and rinse anything you share in hot water."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are camallanus worms contagious to other fish?", "a": "Yes. Larvae released from an infected fish can be eaten by tankmates, so assume every fish in the tank is exposed and treat the whole aquarium."}, {"q": "Can humans get camallanus worms from fish?", "a": "No. Camallanus is a fish parasite and does not infect people, though washing your hands after tank work is always a good habit."}, {"q": "Does levamisole hurt shrimp and snails?", "a": "Levamisole is generally considered gentler on invertebrates than fenbendazole or flubendazole, but reactions vary, so many keepers still move shrimp out or treat fish in a separate tank."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$cloudy-eye$g$, $g$Cloudy Eye$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Cloudy eye is a white or grayish haze that forms over the surface of one or both of a fish's eyes, usually from irritation, injury or infection.$g$, $g$A cloudy eye tells you something has damaged the eye's surface, and the cause is often poor water quality you can fix. Most cases clear up within a week or two once the water is clean, but left alone it can turn into a deeper infection or blindness. Which eyes are affected is a helpful clue.$g$, $g$Cloudy Eye in Fish: Causes and How to Treat It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "One eye or both", "text": "If only one eye is cloudy, an injury is the most likely cause: a scrape on decor, a net, or a bite from a tankmate. If both eyes are cloudy, think about the water first, such as high ammonia, nitrite or nitrate, a pH swing, or a general bacterial infection. Both eyes clouding along with other signs like frayed fins or lethargy points to a broader health problem."}, {"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- A milky or bluish film over the eye\n- The eye looks dull instead of clear and shiny\n- Fish bumps into things or misses food\n- Swelling behind the eye, which may mean pop-eye is developing\n- White fuzz on the eye, which suggests a fungal or bacterial infection"}, {"heading": "How to treat it", "text": "Start with the water. Test it, then do a 25 to 50 percent water change and keep up smaller changes every couple of days for a week. Clean water alone resolves many cases.\n\nIf the cloudiness does not improve after about a week, or if it worsens, move the fish to a hospital tank and use a broad-spectrum antibacterial medication. Some keepers add aquarium salt at about 1 teaspoon per gallon in the hospital tank to support healing, but skip salt with scaleless fish and never add it to a planted tank with shrimp or snails.\n\nInjury-related cases usually heal on their own if the water stays clean. Remove sharp decor and separate aggressive tankmates."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Regular water changes, a cycled filter and smooth decor prevent most cases. Older fish and fish with protruding eyes, like telescope goldfish, are especially prone, so give them a tank free of sharp edges."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can a fish recover from cloudy eye?", "a": "Yes. Most fish recover fully within one to two weeks once the water is clean, though severe or untreated infections can leave lasting damage."}, {"q": "Is cloudy eye contagious?", "a": "Cloudy eye from an injury is not. When it comes from a bacterial infection or bad water, other fish in the tank are at risk, so check the whole tank."}, {"q": "Can old age cause cloudy eyes in fish?", "a": "Yes. Older fish can develop cataracts, which look cloudy from inside the eye rather than as a surface film and do not respond to treatment."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$columnaris$g$, $g$Columnaris$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Columnaris is a fast-spreading bacterial infection caused by Flavobacterium columnare that creates white, gray or yellowish patches on the skin, fins, mouth and gills.$g$, $g$Columnaris can kill fish within a day or two, especially in warm water, so quick action matters. It is often mistaken for fungus because of its cottony look, which leads keepers to use the wrong medication. Learning its signs, including the classic saddle-shaped patch on the back, helps you treat it in time.$g$, $g$Columnaris in Fish: Symptoms, Pictures of Signs and Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- White or grayish patches, often a band across the back called saddleback\n- Fuzzy or cottony growth around the mouth, sometimes called cotton wool or mouth fungus\n- Frayed, eroded fins with a white edge\n- Pale or brown, damaged gills and rapid breathing\n- Lethargy and loss of appetite\n\nThe fast-moving form can kill in under 24 hours with few visible signs, while a slower form develops over several days."}, {"heading": "Columnaris vs fungus", "text": "True fungus looks like long, fluffy cotton strands and usually grows on an existing wound or dead tissue. Columnaris patches are flatter, more matted, and spread across healthy skin quickly. If a fuzzy patch shows up on a fish with no injury and spreads fast, assume columnaris. Antifungal medications will not stop it."}, {"heading": "How to treat columnaris", "text": "- Move sick fish to a hospital tank if you can.\n- Lower the temperature to around 74 to 76°F if the species allows; the bacteria thrive at 80°F and above.\n- Use an antibacterial that targets gram-negative bacteria, such as kanamycin or nitrofurazone, alone or combined, and follow the label.\n- Add strong aeration, since gill damage makes breathing hard.\n- Aquarium salt can help in some cases, but avoid it with scaleless fish, sensitive plants and invertebrates.\n- Remove carbon from the filter during treatment."}, {"heading": "Causes and prevention", "text": "The bacteria live in most aquariums and only cause trouble when fish are stressed by poor water, crowding, shipping or injuries. Livebearers like mollies and guppies are common victims. Keep water clean, avoid overcrowding, and quarantine new fish for at least 4 weeks so outbreaks start away from your display."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How fast does columnaris kill fish?", "a": "The acute form can kill within 24 to 48 hours, especially in warm water. Slower cases develop over several days, which gives you more time to treat."}, {"q": "Is columnaris contagious to other fish?", "a": "Yes. It spreads through the water and through contact with infected fish, so watch every fish in the tank and disinfect nets and equipment."}, {"q": "Can columnaris infect humans?", "a": "Flavobacterium columnare is not considered a human health risk, but wearing gloves and washing your hands after tank work is still sensible."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$drip-acclimation$g$, $g$Drip Acclimation$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Drip acclimation is a slow way to introduce new fish or invertebrates by dripping aquarium water into their container until they adjust to your tank's chemistry.$g$, $g$Shrimp, snails and some fish react badly to sudden changes in pH, hardness or temperature, and drip acclimation lets them adjust gradually. It takes longer than floating the bag, but it noticeably reduces deaths in sensitive species. For hardy fish going between similar water, a shorter acclimation is often enough.$g$, $g$How to Drip Acclimate Fish and Shrimp Step by Step$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What you need", "text": "- A clean bucket or container used only for the aquarium\n- A length of airline tubing\n- A plastic airline valve, or just a loose knot tied in the tubing\n- A net or cup for moving the animals at the end"}, {"heading": "How to drip acclimate", "text": "- Float the sealed bag in the tank for about 15 minutes so the temperature matches.\n- Gently pour the animals and their water into the bucket, tilting it so they stay covered. Set the bucket below the tank.\n- Start a siphon through the tubing from the tank to the bucket. Adjust the valve or knot to about 2 to 4 drips per second.\n- Let it drip until the water volume has roughly doubled, then pour off half and let it double again. This usually takes 45 to 90 minutes, and up to 2 hours or more for shrimp.\n- Net or cup the animals into the tank. Discard the bucket water; never pour shipping water into your aquarium."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Dripping too fast, which defeats the purpose\n- Letting the bucket get cold during a long acclimation; set it on a towel or keep the room warm\n- Leaving the drip unattended so the bucket overflows\n- Exposing shrimp to air when transferring them; scoop them in a cup with water\n\nFish that have been in a sealed bag for many hours can have a buildup of ammonia that becomes more toxic as fresh tank water raises the pH. For long shipments, some keepers skip the long drip and acclimate fish quickly, or add an ammonia binder to the bucket. This is debated, so match the method to how long the animals were in transit."}, {"heading": "When to use it", "text": "Drip acclimation is best for shrimp, snails, wild-caught fish, and any animal coming from water with a very different pH or hardness than yours. For common hardy fish from a local store with similar water, floating and slowly adding tank water over 20 to 30 minutes works fine."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long should I drip acclimate shrimp?", "a": "Most keepers drip shrimp for 1 to 2 hours, or until the water volume has at least tripled, because they are sensitive to changes in hardness and pH."}, {"q": "How many drips per second for drip acclimation?", "a": "About 2 to 4 drips per second is a good target for most fish and invertebrates."}, {"q": "Is drip acclimation better than floating the bag?", "a": "For sensitive animals and big differences in water chemistry, yes. For hardy fish moving between similar water, floating plus gradual water additions is usually enough."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dropsy$g$, $g$Dropsy$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Dropsy is a condition where fluid builds up inside a fish's body, causing a swollen belly and scales that stick out like a pinecone.$g$, $g$Dropsy is a symptom of serious internal trouble, usually kidney failure or a bacterial infection, rather than a single disease. By the time the scales lift, the fish is often very sick, so the outlook is poor. Acting at the first sign of swelling and fixing the water gives the fish its best chance and protects the rest of the tank.$g$, $g$Dropsy in Fish: Pinecone Scales, Causes and Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Swollen, rounded belly\n- Scales raised outward, giving a pinecone look when viewed from above\n- Bulging eyes\n- Pale gills and a red or swollen vent\n- Lethargy, hiding and refusing food\n- Stringy, pale feces"}, {"heading": "What causes it", "text": "Fluid builds up when the kidneys or other organs stop working properly. Common triggers include bacterial infections such as Aeromonas, long-term poor water quality, chronic stress, poor diet, and old age. Because it is a symptom, two fish with dropsy may have completely different underlying problems. It is not highly contagious by itself, but the bacteria and conditions behind it can affect other fish."}, {"heading": "How to treat dropsy", "text": "- Move the fish to a hospital tank right away to protect tankmates.\n- Add Epsom salt (magnesium sulfate) at about 1 tablespoon per 5 gallons to help draw out fluid.\n- Use a broad-spectrum antibacterial; medicated food with an antibiotic such as kanamycin works best if the fish still eats.\n- Keep the water very clean with small daily water changes and stable temperature.\n- Feed lightly with high-quality food.\n\nIf the scales are fully raised and the fish has stopped eating, recovery is unlikely. Humane euthanasia is a kind choice if the fish is clearly suffering."}, {"heading": "Preventing dropsy", "text": "Keep ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm and nitrate low with regular water changes. Avoid overfeeding, give a varied, quality diet, and quarantine new arrivals. Test the main tank whenever a fish develops dropsy, since the cause there may still be affecting others."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can a fish survive dropsy?", "a": "Some do if it is caught early, before the scales fully raise. Advanced cases with pineconing and no appetite rarely recover."}, {"q": "Is dropsy contagious?", "a": "Dropsy itself is not usually contagious, but the bacteria or poor water behind it can affect other fish, so isolate the sick fish and test your tank."}, {"q": "How much Epsom salt should I use for dropsy?", "a": "A common dose is about 1 tablespoon per 5 gallons in a hospital tank. Dissolve it fully in tank water before adding."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$euthanasia$g$, $g$Euthanasia$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Euthanasia is humanely ending a fish's life when it is suffering from an illness or injury it cannot recover from.$g$, $g$Sometimes the kindest choice is ending suffering quickly instead of watching a fish decline for days. Knowing a gentle method ahead of time keeps you from using cruel options like flushing in a stressful moment. Deciding when to stop treatment is hard, but it is a real part of caring for fish responsibly.$g$, $g$How to Humanely Euthanize a Fish With Clove Oil$g$, $g$[{"heading": "When to consider it", "text": "- Advanced dropsy with fully raised scales and no appetite\n- Diseases with no reliable cure, such as fish tuberculosis\n- Severe injuries, large tumors or deformities that stop the fish from eating or swimming\n- A fish that has not improved after full treatment and is clearly suffering\n\nIf you are unsure, a vet who treats fish can help you decide."}, {"heading": "The clove oil method", "text": "Clove oil is the most widely recommended home method. It works as an anesthetic, so the fish falls asleep before it dies.\n\n- Put the fish in a small container with about a quart of its own tank water.\n- Shake a few drops of clove oil (about 4 to 5) with a little warm water in a sealed jar until it turns milky.\n- Add the mixture slowly. Within about 10 minutes the fish will lose balance and settle on the bottom.\n- Once it is fully sedated, add a much stronger dose, roughly 5 times the first amount, mixed the same way.\n- Leave the fish in the solution for at least 30 minutes after its gills stop moving, then check that there is no gill movement before disposal."}, {"heading": "Methods to avoid", "text": "- Flushing: the fish may live for a while in cold, polluted water, and it can spread disease to waterways.\n- Freezing alone: ice crystals can form in tissue while the fish is conscious; most guidelines consider it inhumane.\n- Removing from water to suffocate.\n- Alcohol, boiling water or baking soda.\n\nVets can also perform euthanasia if you prefer not to do it yourself."}, {"heading": "Afterward", "text": "Bury the fish deep enough that pets will not dig it up, or seal it in a bag with household trash. Never release a sick fish into a pond or stream. If disease caused the death, test your water and watch the remaining fish closely."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the most humane way to euthanize a fish?", "a": "Clove oil is the most widely recommended home method because it sedates the fish first. A vet can also do it for you."}, {"q": "How much clove oil do I need to euthanize a fish?", "a": "Start with about 4 to 5 drops per quart of water to sedate the fish, then add roughly 5 times that amount once it is unresponsive."}, {"q": "Is flushing a fish humane?", "a": "No. Flushed fish can survive for a while in harsh conditions, and it can introduce diseases or invasive species into local waterways."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fin-rot$g$, $g$Fin Rot$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Fin rot is a bacterial infection that makes a fish's fins look ragged, discolored and gradually shorter as the tissue breaks down.$g$, $g$Fin rot almost always starts with poor water quality, stress or an injury, so it is a signal to look at the tank as well as the fish. Caught early, fins usually grow back fully. Left alone, it can eat into the body and become life-threatening, so watch long-finned fish like bettas and fancy goldfish closely.$g$, $g$Fin Rot in Fish: Causes, Pictures of Signs and Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Frayed or ragged fin edges that get shorter over time\n- White, black or red edges on the fins\n- Red streaks or blood spots in the fins\n- Holes in the fin membrane\n- In severe cases, rot reaching the base of the fin and the body\n\nCompare with fin nipping, which leaves clean bites without discolored edges and often happens when certain tankmates are nearby."}, {"heading": "What causes it", "text": "Bacteria such as Aeromonas and Pseudomonas are present in most tanks and only attack when a fish is weakened. The usual triggers are ammonia or nitrite in the water, high nitrate, infrequent water changes, overcrowding, cold water, and injuries from nippers or sharp decor. Bettas kept in small, unheated bowls are frequent victims."}, {"heading": "How to treat fin rot", "text": "- Test the water and do a 25 to 50 percent water change. Keep up partial changes every 2 to 3 days.\n- Fix the cause: add a heater if needed, remove sharp decor, separate nippers.\n- Mild cases often heal with clean water alone within one to two weeks.\n- If it keeps spreading or reaches the body, use an antibacterial medication in a hospital tank and follow the label.\n- Remove carbon from the filter while medicating.\n\nSalt can help some fish but harms scaleless fish, sensitive plants and invertebrates."}, {"heading": "What healing looks like", "text": "New fin growth is clear or white and very thin at first. Many keepers mistake it for fungus, but healthy regrowth is smooth and even, while fungus looks fluffy. Full regrowth can take weeks to a few months, and fins may come back slightly different in shape or color."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Will fin rot go away on its own?", "a": "Mild fin rot often heals once the water is clean and the cause is fixed. If it keeps spreading after a few days of good water, it needs medication."}, {"q": "How long does it take for fins to grow back after fin rot?", "a": "Fins usually start regrowing within a week or two of recovery, and full regrowth can take one to three months."}, {"q": "Is fin rot contagious?", "a": "The bacteria are already in most tanks, but the same poor conditions can let it appear in other fish, so check the water and watch the whole tank."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fungus$g$, $g$Fungus$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Aquarium fungus is a fuzzy, cotton-like growth, usually from Saprolegnia or similar water molds, that grows on injured fish, dead tissue, eggs and leftover food.$g$, $g$Fungus usually signals that a fish already has a wound or is weakened by stress, cold or poor water. Treating the fuzz without fixing the cause rarely works. It also gets confused with columnaris and epistylis, so a correct identification before you medicate saves fish and time.$g$, $g$Fish Fungus: Signs, Causes and How to Treat It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- White, gray or light brown cotton-like tufts with long wispy strands\n- Growth on wounds, torn fins, around the mouth or on the eyes\n- Patches that may turn green or brown as debris sticks to them\n- Fuzzy white fish eggs, which are usually dead or unfertilized\n\nIf the patch looks flat and matted and spreads fast on healthy skin, suspect columnaris instead, which needs antibacterial treatment."}, {"heading": "What causes it", "text": "Water molds are present in nearly every tank and feed on dead or damaged tissue. They take hold after injuries from rough handling, fights or sharp decor, and when fish are weakened by cold water, poor water quality or another illness. Uneaten food and dead fish left in the tank give them more to grow on."}, {"heading": "How to treat fungus", "text": "- Do a 25 to 50 percent water change and remove uneaten food and dead material.\n- Move the fish to a hospital tank for treatment.\n- Use an antifungal medication and follow the label. Methylene blue is a classic choice, but it stains silicone and decor, harms plants and can damage filter bacteria, so keep it out of the display tank.\n- Aquarium salt helps some fish, but avoid it with scaleless fish, sensitive plants and invertebrates.\n- Fix the cause: stable temperature, clean water, and separation from aggressive tankmates."}, {"heading": "Protecting fish eggs", "text": "Fungus spreads from dead eggs to healthy ones. Breeders remove white, fuzzy eggs with a pipette and some add a small amount of methylene blue to a separate hatching container. Good water movement over the eggs also helps."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is fish fungus contagious?", "a": "The spores are in almost every tank, so healthy fish rarely catch it. Fish with wounds or weakened immunity are at risk, so fix the conditions behind it."}, {"q": "What does fungus look like on a fish?", "a": "It looks like white or gray cotton with fine, fluffy strands, usually growing from a wound, fin edge or the mouth."}, {"q": "How long does it take to treat fish fungus?", "a": "Mild cases often improve within a few days and clear in about a week with treatment and clean water. Deep wounds take longer to heal."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hole-in-the-head$g$, $g$Hole-in-the-Head$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Hole-in-the-head is a condition that causes pits and eroding sores on a fish's head and along its lateral line, also called HLLE.$g$, $g$Hole-in-the-head is most common in large cichlids like oscars and discus and can leave permanent scars if it goes untreated. It usually develops slowly from long-term problems like high nitrate, a poor diet or parasites, which means fixing care habits matters more than any single medication. Caught early, pits often heal.$g$, $g$Hole in the Head Disease (HLLE): Causes and Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Small pits or pinholes on the head, often near the eyes and nostrils\n- Pits that enlarge and join into open sores\n- Erosion along the lateral line on the sides of the body\n- White, stringy feces or weight loss in some cases\n- Pale or faded color"}, {"heading": "What causes it", "text": "There is no single proven cause, and the hobby still debates it. The most common suspects are:\n\n- High nitrate and infrequent water changes\n- A diet low in vitamins and variety, especially vitamin C\n- Internal parasites such as Hexamita\n- Activated carbon, which some studies have linked to lateral line erosion in marine fish\n- Chronic stress from small tanks or aggressive tankmates"}, {"heading": "How to treat it", "text": "- Increase water changes to 30 to 50 percent weekly or more and keep nitrate under 20 ppm.\n- Remove activated carbon from the filter.\n- Improve the diet with a quality pellet, vegetable matter and a vitamin supplement.\n- If the fish has white feces or weight loss, treat for Hexamita with metronidazole, ideally in medicated food.\n- Treat any sores that look infected with an antibacterial.\n\nMild pits often fill in within a few weeks to months. Deep, advanced lesions may leave permanent scarring even after healing."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Give big cichlids enough room, typically 75 gallons or more for an adult oscar, with strong filtration and frequent water changes. Feed a varied, fresh diet and avoid relying on one cheap staple. Watch your fish's head closely during regular feeding so you catch pits early."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can hole-in-the-head be cured?", "a": "Yes, in most early cases. Better water, a richer diet and removing carbon often reverse it, though deep lesions can scar."}, {"q": "Is hole-in-the-head contagious?", "a": "The condition itself is not considered contagious, but the parasites or poor water behind it can affect other fish in the tank."}, {"q": "What fish get hole-in-the-head?", "a": "It is most common in large cichlids such as oscars, discus and severums, and in marine fish like tangs, where it is called HLLE."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hospital-tank$g$, $g$Hospital Tank$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$A hospital tank is a simple, separate aquarium used to isolate and treat sick or injured fish away from the main display.$g$, $g$Treating in a hospital tank protects healthy fish, keeps medications away from plants, shrimp and snails, and lets you dose accurately in a small volume. It also gives an injured or bullied fish quiet time to heal. Having one ready before you need it can make the difference in an emergency.$g$, $g$Hospital Tank Setup: What You Need to Treat Sick Fish$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to set one up", "text": "- A 5 to 20 gallon tank, sized for the fish you keep; 10 gallons suits most community fish\n- A sponge filter, ideally kept running in your main tank so it is already seeded with beneficial bacteria\n- An adjustable heater and a thermometer\n- A lid, since sick and stressed fish may jump\n- A bare bottom, or a thin layer you can easily clean\n- A hiding spot such as a piece of PVC pipe or a clay pot\n- An air stone for extra oxygen during treatment"}, {"heading": "Using it during treatment", "text": "Fill it with water from the main tank so chemistry matches, then move the fish. Measure the real water volume and dose medication based on that, not on the tank's labeled size.\n\nMany medications harm the beneficial bacteria in the filter, so test for ammonia daily. Do partial water changes as needed and redose according to the label. Remove carbon from any filter, since it pulls medication out of the water. Keep the lights dim to reduce stress."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Setting up the tank from scratch when the fish is already sick, with no seeded filter\n- Adding gravel and decor that soak up medication and hide waste\n- Overdosing because the tank was smaller than assumed\n- Using the same net in the hospital tank and the main tank\n- Returning the fish too soon, before a few symptom-free days have passed"}, {"heading": "After treatment", "text": "Clean the tank, filter and equipment, and let everything dry fully or disinfect it before storing. Many keepers keep the hospital tank running as a quarantine tank for new arrivals, since the setup is the same."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What size should a hospital tank be?", "a": "A 10 gallon tank works for most small and medium fish. Larger fish like big cichlids or goldfish need 20 gallons or more."}, {"q": "Do you need a filter in a hospital tank?", "a": "Yes. A seeded sponge filter is best because it handles waste gently and is easy to clean, but test ammonia daily since medications can weaken it."}, {"q": "Is a hospital tank the same as a quarantine tank?", "a": "They use the same setup. A quarantine tank holds new fish for observation, while a hospital tank treats sick fish, and many keepers use one tank for both."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$internal-parasite$g$, $g$Internal Parasite$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$An internal parasite is a worm or single-celled organism that lives inside a fish's gut or organs and feeds off the fish.$g$, $g$Internal parasites cause slow weight loss and weakness that is easy to miss until a fish is very sick. They are common in wild-caught and imported fish, and some spread through a whole tank. Knowing the type of parasite matters, because each group needs a different medication.$g$, $g$Internal Parasites in Fish: Signs, Types and Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Weight loss or a sunken belly despite a good appetite\n- White, stringy or clear feces\n- Red threads from the vent, a sign of camallanus worms\n- Bloating in some cases\n- Lethargy, darkened color and hiding\n- Loss of appetite in advanced cases"}, {"heading": "Common types and treatments", "text": "- Roundworms (nematodes) such as camallanus and capillaria: treated with levamisole, fenbendazole or flubendazole\n- Tapeworms and flukes: treated with praziquantel\n- Protozoa such as Hexamita: treated with metronidazole\n\nIf you are not sure which one you have, a combination of praziquantel plus a nematode treatment covers most worms. A vet or experienced keeper with a microscope can examine a fecal sample for a firm answer."}, {"heading": "How to treat", "text": "Medicated food is the most effective option because the drug goes straight to the gut. Use it while fish are still eating, feeding for the length the label suggests. For fish that stopped eating, dose the water in a hospital tank.\n\nMost dewormers need a second round 2 to 3 weeks later to kill parasites that hatch after the first dose. Vacuum the gravel and do a water change after each round. Fenbendazole and flubendazole kill snails and can harm shrimp, so treat fish separately if you keep invertebrates."}, {"heading": "Preventing them", "text": "Quarantine new fish for 4 to 6 weeks and watch their feces and body shape. Many keepers deworm wild-caught fish and livebearers routinely during quarantine. Be careful with live foods from ponds or streams, which can carry parasite larvae."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I know if my fish has internal parasites?", "a": "The classic signs are weight loss despite eating, white stringy feces and a sunken belly. Red threads from the vent mean camallanus worms."}, {"q": "Can internal parasites spread to other fish?", "a": "Many can, either through eggs in the water or when tankmates eat feces or infected tissue. Treat the whole tank if you see worms."}, {"q": "What is the best medicine for internal parasites in fish?", "a": "It depends on the parasite: praziquantel for tapeworms and flukes, levamisole or fenbendazole for roundworms, and metronidazole for protozoa like Hexamita."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$pop-eye$g$, $g$Pop-eye$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Pop-eye, or exophthalmia, is a condition where fluid builds up behind a fish's eye and pushes it outward so it bulges from the socket.$g$, $g$Pop-eye is a symptom, not a disease, and the cause decides the treatment. A single bulging eye is usually an injury, while both eyes bulging often means bad water or an internal infection. Most fish recover with clean water and time, but serious cases can damage or cost the eye.$g$, $g$Pop-Eye in Fish: Causes and How to Treat It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- One or both eyes bulging noticeably from the head\n- A cloudy or white film over the bulging eye\n- A ring of white or red tissue around the eye\n- In severe cases, the eye may burst or be lost\n\nNote that some fancy goldfish, like telescope eyes, naturally have protruding eyes. Look for a change from the fish's normal look."}, {"heading": "One eye or both", "text": "One eye bulging usually points to an injury, such as a collision with decor, rough netting or a fight. Both eyes bulging points to a water quality problem or a bacterial infection affecting the whole body. If pop-eye appears with a swollen belly and raised scales, it may be part of dropsy, which is much more serious."}, {"heading": "How to treat pop-eye", "text": "- Test the water and do a 25 to 50 percent water change, then keep water pristine with regular changes.\n- Move the fish to a hospital tank if you plan to medicate.\n- Add Epsom salt at about 1 tablespoon per 5 gallons to help reduce swelling.\n- For both-eye cases or signs of infection, use a broad-spectrum antibacterial according to the label.\n- Remove sharp decor and separate aggressive tankmates if it was an injury.\n\nSwelling usually goes down over one to several weeks. Eyes that are badly damaged may stay cloudy or be lost, but fish adapt well to living with one eye."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Keep ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm and nitrate low. Choose smooth decor, net fish gently, and avoid housing aggressive fish with peaceful ones. Quarantine new fish to keep bacterial infections out."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can pop-eye in fish heal on its own?", "a": "Injury cases often heal with clean water alone. Pop-eye in both eyes or with other symptoms usually needs treatment."}, {"q": "Is pop-eye contagious?", "a": "Pop-eye from an injury is not. If it comes from a bacterial infection or bad water, other fish can be affected, so check the whole tank."}, {"q": "How long does pop-eye take to go away?", "a": "Mild cases improve within one to two weeks. Severe swelling can take a month or more to settle."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$quarantine$g$, $g$Quarantine (QT)$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Quarantine is keeping new or sick fish in a separate tank, called a quarantine tank, for several weeks before they join your main aquarium.$g$, $g$Most diseases in home aquariums arrive with new fish, plants or invertebrates. A few weeks in a quarantine tank lets hidden problems like ich, worms or bacterial infections show up where they are easy to treat, and keeps your established fish safe. Many experienced keepers call it the best insurance in the hobby.$g$, $g$How to Quarantine New Fish: Quarantine Tank Setup and Steps$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Setting up a quarantine tank", "text": "- A 10 to 20 gallon tank, sized for the fish you buy\n- A seeded sponge filter kept running in your main tank so it is ready\n- A heater, thermometer and lid\n- A bare bottom and a few hiding places like PVC pipe or a pot\n- Separate nets, siphon and buckets that never touch the display tank\n\nThe same tank works as a hospital tank when a fish gets sick."}, {"heading": "How long to quarantine", "text": "Most keepers quarantine for 4 to 6 weeks. Two weeks catches fast problems like ich and columnaris, but internal parasites and slow infections can take longer to show. Watch fish daily for spots, clamped fins, odd feces, weight loss and scratching. If you treat a problem, restart the clock after the last symptoms clear."}, {"heading": "To medicate or just watch", "text": "Keepers disagree on preventive treatment. Some treat every new fish with a dewormer and a parasite medication during quarantine, which catches problems that do not show obvious signs. Others prefer to observe and treat only when needed, to avoid stressing fish with drugs they may not need. Either way, quarantine works best when you know your fish's source and watch closely. Deworming is common for livebearers and wild-caught fish."}, {"heading": "Quarantining plants and invertebrates", "text": "Plants can carry snails, algae, pest eggs and even fish parasites. Rinse them, remove pots and wool, and hold them in a separate container of water for a couple of weeks if you want to be sure. Shrimp and snails can also carry parasites and should be quarantined in their own tank, since many fish medications are toxic to them."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long should I quarantine new fish?", "a": "Four to six weeks is the common recommendation. At minimum, two weeks catches the most common fast-moving diseases."}, {"q": "Do I really need a quarantine tank?", "a": "It is not required, but it is the best way to keep disease out of an established tank. One sick new fish can wipe out a whole display."}, {"q": "Can I quarantine fish without a cycled filter?", "a": "Yes, but you will need daily ammonia testing and frequent water changes. Keeping a sponge filter seeded in your main tank is much easier."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$slime-coat$g$, $g$Slime Coat$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$The slime coat is the thin layer of mucus covering a fish's skin, scales and gills that protects it from infection, parasites and injury.$g$, $g$The slime coat is a fish's first line of defense, packed with compounds that block bacteria and parasites. Rough handling, poor water and harsh chemicals can strip it away, leaving the fish open to disease. Gentle care and clean water keep it strong, and changes in the coat are often an early warning sign.$g$, $g$Fish Slime Coat: What It Does and How to Protect It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it does", "text": "The mucus is made by special cells in the skin. It traps and sheds bacteria, parasites and debris, contains natural antibodies and enzymes that fight infection, reduces drag while swimming, and helps the fish control the salt and water balance in its body. Fish constantly renew it, which is why a healthy coat recovers quickly from minor damage."}, {"heading": "What damages it", "text": "- Handling fish with dry hands or rough nets\n- Ammonia, nitrite and high nitrate\n- Sudden swings in pH or temperature\n- Chlorine and chloramine from untreated tap water\n- Overuse of salt and some medications\n- Parasites and bacterial infections\n- Shipping stress and fights"}, {"heading": "Signs of slime coat trouble", "text": "A stripped coat makes skin look dull, patchy or reddened. The opposite is also a warning: excess mucus shows up as a gray, white or bluish film on the body, often from parasites such as Costia or Chilodonella, or from irritating water. Fish with either problem may scratch against decor, clamp their fins or breathe quickly. If you see a cloudy film, test the water first, then look closely for parasites."}, {"heading": "How to protect it", "text": "Keep water clean and stable with regular water changes and a quality dechlorinator. Avoid handling fish when you can; when you must, use a soft fine-mesh net or herd them into a container so they stay in water. Some water conditioners include ingredients that coat damaged skin temporarily during shipping or treatment, which can help, but they do not replace good water quality."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does it take for a fish's slime coat to grow back?", "a": "Minor damage usually repairs within a few days in clean water. Severe stripping can take a week or more."}, {"q": "Why does my fish have a white film on its body?", "a": "A cloudy film is often excess slime coat caused by irritation, such as poor water or skin parasites. Test the water and inspect the fish closely."}, {"q": "Does aquarium salt help the slime coat?", "a": "Small amounts can stimulate mucus production, which is why salt is used in some treatments. It harms scaleless fish, sensitive plants and invertebrates, so use it carefully."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$stress$g$, $g$Stress$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Stress in fish is a strain on the body caused by poor water, bad tankmates, sudden changes or handling, which weakens the immune system over time.$g$, $g$Most fish diseases begin with stress. A stressed fish releases hormones that suppress its immune system, so bacteria and parasites that are harmless to healthy fish can suddenly take hold. Learning to spot stress early and fix its cause prevents far more problems than any medication can treat.$g$, $g$Signs of Stress in Fish and How to Reduce It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs of a stressed fish", "text": "- Faded or darkened color\n- Hiding more than usual or pacing the glass\n- Clamped fins\n- Loss of appetite\n- Rapid breathing or hanging at the surface\n- Scratching against decor\n- Stress stripes or blotches in species like bettas and cichlids"}, {"heading": "Common causes", "text": "- Ammonia, nitrite or high nitrate\n- Temperature or pH swings, including from large untreated water changes\n- Aggressive or fin-nipping tankmates\n- A tank that is too small, overcrowded or bare, with nowhere to hide\n- Keeping schooling fish alone or in groups under 6\n- Shipping, netting and moving between tanks\n- Loud vibration, constant tapping, or lights left on around the clock\n\nShort-term stress from a move usually passes within days. Ongoing stress from a poor setup is the bigger danger, because it wears fish down slowly."}, {"heading": "How to reduce it", "text": "Start by testing your water and fixing anything off. Keep the temperature steady with a properly sized heater, and do regular water changes of 20 to 30 percent with dechlorinated, temperature-matched water. Research every species before you buy: right tank size, compatible tankmates, preferred water and group size. Add plants, rocks or wood so fish can break line of sight. Give a regular light schedule of about 8 to 10 hours with darkness at night."}, {"heading": "Helping new fish settle", "text": "Dim the lights for the first day, acclimate slowly, and feed lightly. Some fish hide for a few days after arrival, which is normal. Rearranging decor slightly when adding fish to a tank with territorial species can reduce aggression toward newcomers."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How can I tell if my fish is stressed?", "a": "Look for faded color, hiding, clamped fins, loss of appetite and rapid breathing. Stress stripes are a clear sign in bettas and many cichlids."}, {"q": "Can stress kill a fish?", "a": "Yes. Severe stress can kill directly, and ongoing stress weakens the immune system so diseases take hold more easily."}, {"q": "How long does it take a fish to recover from stress?", "a": "Fish usually recover from short-term stress like a move within a few days. Recovery from long-term stress depends on fixing the cause."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$swim-bladder-disorder$g$, $g$Swim Bladder Disorder$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Swim bladder disorder is a buoyancy problem where a fish floats, sinks or swims sideways or upside down because its swim bladder is not working properly.$g$, $g$Swim bladder problems are especially common in fancy goldfish and bettas, often from overfeeding or constipation. Many mild cases clear up with a short fast and a better diet. Others come from infection, injury or body shape, so knowing the likely cause helps you decide whether to wait it out or treat.$g$, $g$Swim Bladder Disease in Fish: Causes and Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Floating at the top, often tilted or upside down\n- Sinking to the bottom and struggling to rise\n- Swimming sideways, head-down or tail-down\n- A curved back or swollen belly\n- Struggling to stay level after eating"}, {"heading": "Common causes", "text": "- Overfeeding and constipation, especially with dry floating foods\n- Gulping air while eating at the surface\n- Body shape: round fancy goldfish have compressed organs\n- Cold water, which slows digestion\n- Bacterial infection or internal parasites\n- Injury from a fall or fight\n- Genetic defects in some fish"}, {"heading": "How to treat it", "text": "- Fast the fish for 2 to 3 days. This alone fixes many mild cases.\n- Raise the temperature slightly within the species' range, such as to the upper 70s for bettas or around 72°F for goldfish, to help digestion.\n- After fasting, offer a small amount of a high-fiber food. Shelled, cooked peas are a long-standing remedy; some keepers question them, but a small piece does no harm.\n- Soak dry food before feeding and switch goldfish to sinking pellets.\n- Lower the water level in a hospital tank so a struggling fish can reach the surface easily.\n- If the fish has other signs like clamped fins, red streaks or a swollen belly with raised scales, treat for infection with an antibacterial."}, {"heading": "Living with chronic cases", "text": "Some fish, especially round goldfish, have permanent buoyancy problems. They can still live well with shallow water, smooth surfaces to rest on, small soaked meals and a careful diet. Watch for sores on fish that rest at the surface or bottom constantly."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can swim bladder disease be cured?", "a": "Many cases caused by diet or constipation clear up within days with fasting and better food. Cases caused by body shape or injury may be permanent."}, {"q": "Do peas help swim bladder disorder?", "a": "A small piece of cooked, shelled pea can help constipated fish pass waste. It is a fiber remedy, not a cure for infection or injury."}, {"q": "Is swim bladder disorder contagious?", "a": "No. It is usually caused by diet, body shape or injury. If an infection is behind it, other fish could be at risk."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$algae-wafer$g$, $g$Algae Wafer$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$An algae wafer is a flat, sinking disc of plant-based fish food designed for bottom-dwelling herbivores and grazers like plecos, otocinclus and shrimp.$g$, $g$Algae wafers get vegetable-rich food to the bottom of the tank, where many grazers eat, instead of letting it be stolen at the surface. They make it easier to feed plecos and other algae eaters properly once natural algae runs out. They are a supplement, though, and work best as part of a varied diet.$g$, $g$Algae Wafers: Which Fish Eat Them and How Often to Feed$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Who eats them", "text": "- Bristlenose and other algae-eating plecos\n- Otocinclus, though many are shy about wafers at first\n- Corydoras and other catfish\n- Shrimp and snails\n- Many loaches, mollies and other grazers\n\nNot every pleco is a vegetarian. Clown, zebra and many other plecos need more protein or wood, so check what your species really eats."}, {"heading": "How to feed them", "text": "Drop wafers in after lights go out for nocturnal fish like plecos, or near their favorite hiding spot. A common starting point is one wafer per pleco, or a quarter wafer for a small group of shrimp, every other day. Break wafers into pieces for small fish and shrimp so they do not sit uneaten.\n\nWafers soften slowly and stay intact for hours, so they can be grazed over time. Remove anything left after 12 to 24 hours to keep it from fouling the water."}, {"heading": "Choosing a good wafer", "text": "Read the ingredient list. Many wafers list fish meal or wheat first, with only a small amount of algae. For true herbivores, look for spirulina, kelp or other algae near the top of the list. Protein-rich wafers suit omnivores like corydoras. Rotating two or three foods covers more nutritional needs than relying on one."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Assuming algae eaters can live on tank algae alone; most need supplemental food\n- Overfeeding, which leads to ammonia spikes and snail booms\n- Feeding during the day when faster fish eat them first\n- Using wafers as the only food instead of adding blanched vegetables and other variety"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How often should I feed algae wafers?", "a": "Every other day is a good starting point for most plecos and grazers. Adjust based on how quickly they are eaten and how much algae is in the tank."}, {"q": "Do shrimp eat algae wafers?", "a": "Yes. Shrimp love them, but feed small pieces and remove leftovers within a few hours so they do not foul the water."}, {"q": "Can algae wafers cloud the water?", "a": "Uneaten wafers break down and can cause cloudy water and ammonia spikes. Feed only what gets eaten and remove leftovers."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$blanched-vegetables$g$, $g$Blanched Vegetables$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Blanched vegetables are fresh vegetables like zucchini, cucumber or spinach briefly boiled until soft, then fed to plant-eating fish, shrimp and snails.$g$, $g$Fresh vegetables add fiber and nutrients that dry foods often lack, which helps plecos, goldfish, snails and shrimp stay healthy. Blanching softens tough vegetables so fish can graze them easily, and it helps them sink. They are cheap, easy to prepare, and a great way to add variety to a grazer's diet.$g$, $g$Blanched Vegetables for Fish: How to Prepare and Feed Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Best vegetables to try", "text": "- Zucchini and cucumber, the most popular choices\n- Spinach and romaine lettuce\n- Shelled peas, a favorite of goldfish\n- Green beans, kale and broccoli\n- Sweet potato and carrot, cooked until soft\n\nAvoid onions, garlic in large amounts, spicy peppers, citrus and anything seasoned or cooked with oil or salt. Spinach is high in oxalates, so feed it as an occasional treat rather than daily."}, {"heading": "How to blanch them", "text": "- Wash vegetables well, and peel cucumber or zucchini if they are not organic, to reduce pesticides.\n- Cut into slices about 1/4 inch thick, or into fish-sized bites.\n- Drop into boiling water for 1 to 2 minutes for soft vegetables like zucchini and spinach, or longer for carrots and sweet potato.\n- Move to cold water to stop cooking.\n- Let cool completely before feeding.\n\nPeas need the shell removed after cooking. Extra slices can be frozen and thawed for later feedings."}, {"heading": "How to feed them", "text": "Most vegetables float at first. Weigh slices down with a stainless steel fork, a clean rock, or a plastic vegetable clip. Plecos and other nocturnal grazers do best if you add vegetables in the evening.\n\nStart with a small piece and watch how fast it disappears. Remove leftovers within 12 to 24 hours, before they go mushy and pollute the water. Offering vegetables two or three times a week works well for most herbivores."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving vegetables in too long, which leads to cloudy water and ammonia\n- Feeding unwashed vegetables that may carry pesticides, which are deadly to shrimp\n- Overfeeding vegetables to carnivorous fish that cannot digest them well"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long do you blanch zucchini for fish?", "a": "About 1 to 2 minutes in boiling water, until it is soft but holds its shape. Cool it in cold water before feeding."}, {"q": "How long can I leave vegetables in my fish tank?", "a": "Remove uneaten vegetables within 12 to 24 hours to keep them from rotting and fouling the water."}, {"q": "Can I feed fish vegetables without blanching?", "a": "Some soft foods like cucumber can be fed raw, but blanching softens them, helps them sink and makes them easier for fish to graze."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$bloodworms$g$, $g$Bloodworms$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Bloodworms are the bright red larvae of non-biting midge flies, sold frozen, freeze-dried or live as a protein-rich treat for aquarium fish.$g$, $g$Almost every carnivorous and omnivorous fish goes wild for bloodworms, which makes them useful for tempting picky eaters, fattening up new arrivals and conditioning breeders. They are rich and fairly low in fiber, so they work best as a treat or part of a rotation rather than the whole diet.$g$, $g$Bloodworms for Fish: How Often to Feed and Which Type to Buy$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Frozen, freeze-dried or live", "text": "Frozen bloodworms are the best all-round choice. Freezing keeps most of the nutrition and kills many of the pathogens that live worms can carry. Thaw a cube in a cup of tank water, then pour off the melt water, which is full of dissolved juices that pollute the tank.\n\nFreeze-dried worms are convenient and shelf stable but swell in the gut, so soak them for a few minutes before feeding. Live bloodworms are the most exciting for fish but are usually harvested from the wild and carry the highest risk of bringing in disease."}, {"heading": "How much and how often", "text": "Two or three bloodworm meals a week is plenty for most community tanks. Feed only what the fish finish in about two minutes and siphon out any worms that sink into the gravel, because they rot quickly and spike ammonia. For fish like bettas, a few worms per feeding is a full meal.\n\nWatch for bloating after a bloodworm meal, especially in bettas and goldfish. A swollen belly that lasts more than a day is a cue to fast the fish and cut back."}, {"heading": "Who should eat them", "text": "- Great for: bettas, gouramis, cichlids, loaches, corydoras, killifish and most predatory species\n- Use sparingly for: herbivores like mbuna, which can bloat on rich protein\n- Good for: wild-caught or stubborn fish that refuse prepared foods"}, {"heading": "Handling tips", "text": "Some people develop skin or breathing allergies after handling bloodworms often. Use tweezers or a feeding cone, wash your hands afterward and keep frozen packs in the freezer until use so they don't thaw and refreeze."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can fish eat only bloodworms?", "a": "No. Bloodworms are not a complete diet on their own. Rotate them with a quality flake or pellet and other foods."}, {"q": "Are bloodworms actually worms?", "a": "No. They are the larvae of midge flies, and their red color comes from hemoglobin that helps them live in low-oxygen mud."}, {"q": "Do you need to thaw frozen bloodworms?", "a": "Yes, it is best to thaw them in a little tank water first and drain off the liquid. Dropping a frozen cube straight in can also chill small fish that try to eat it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$brine-shrimp$g$, $g$Brine Shrimp$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Brine shrimp are tiny saltwater crustaceans of the genus Artemia, fed to aquarium fish as hatched babies, frozen adults or live adults.$g$, $g$Newly hatched baby brine shrimp are one of the best first foods for fry because they are small, nutritious and wiggle in a way that triggers feeding. Adult brine shrimp are a light, easy treat for grown fish, though they are less nutritious than the babies unless they have been gut loaded.$g$, $g$How to Hatch Baby Brine Shrimp for Fry (and Feeding Adults)$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to hatch baby brine shrimp", "text": "- Fill a bottle or hatchery with about 1 liter of water and 1.5 to 2 tablespoons of salt\n- Add about half a teaspoon of eggs (cysts)\n- Run an airline to the bottom so the eggs keep tumbling\n- Keep it at 80 to 82°F with light on it\n- Harvest in 18 to 24 hours"}, {"heading": "Harvesting and feeding", "text": "Turn off the air and let it sit for 5 to 10 minutes. The empty shells float, unhatched eggs sink, and the orange babies gather near a light at the bottom. Siphon them out, rinse them in fresh water through a fine net, and feed. Avoid feeding shells, which can block fry guts.\n\nBaby brine shrimp start losing nutrition within about 12 hours, so hatch small batches daily. They live only a few hours in freshwater, so feed amounts the fry can clean up."}, {"heading": "Frozen and adult brine shrimp", "text": "Frozen adults are a good occasional treat for community fish and are easy to portion. On their own they are mostly water and shell, so they work best mixed into a rotation. Enriched or gut loaded versions offer more value. Thaw and rinse before feeding.\n\nLive adult brine shrimp are sold at some stores and are a great treat for predators and picky fish. Rinse them first, since they come in salt water."}, {"heading": "Decapsulated eggs", "text": "Decapsulated eggs have the hard shell removed chemically. They can be fed directly to small fry without hatching and hatch a bit more reliably, with no shells to separate."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long do baby brine shrimp live in freshwater?", "a": "Usually a few hours, often less than a day. Feed what your fry can eat quickly so dead shrimp don't foul the water."}, {"q": "Are brine shrimp good for fish?", "a": "Baby brine shrimp are excellent nutrition, especially for fry. Adult brine shrimp are a fine treat but shouldn't be the main diet."}, {"q": "Why are my brine shrimp not hatching?", "a": "The usual causes are water that is too cold, old or poorly stored eggs, too little salt or not enough aeration. Aim for 80 to 82°F with strong bubbling."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$carnivore$g$, $g$Carnivore$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$A carnivore is a fish whose natural diet is mostly other animals, such as insects, worms, crustaceans or smaller fish, rather than plants or algae.$g$, $g$Knowing a fish is carnivorous tells you to feed protein-rich foods and to think hard about tankmates. Many carnivores will eat any fish or shrimp that fits in their mouth, and feeding them a plant-heavy diet can lead to poor color, weight loss and weak immune systems.$g$, $g$Carnivorous Fish: What to Feed Them and Tankmate Tips$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What carnivores eat", "text": "In the wild, carnivores eat insects, larvae, worms, crustaceans and in some cases other fish. In the aquarium, the base of the diet should be a high-protein pellet or flake made mostly from fish, shrimp or insect meal. Add frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp, mysis, krill or chopped seafood a few times a week for variety.\n\nFeed most adult carnivores once a day, or every other day for large predators, in an amount they finish in a couple of minutes. Overfeeding meaty foods is a fast route to fatty livers and dirty water."}, {"heading": "Examples in the hobby", "text": "- Bettas and many killifish, which hunt insects at the surface\n- Most larger cichlids, such as oscars and many peacock bass\n- Pufferfish, which need hard-shelled snails to wear down their beaks\n- Arowanas, bichirs and other large predators\n- Leaf fish and other ambush hunters, which often only take live or moving food"}, {"heading": "Feeder fish: think twice", "text": "Live feeder goldfish and rosy reds are cheap but often carry parasites and disease, and goldfish contain enzymes that can cause vitamin deficiencies with long-term use. Most carnivores can be trained onto frozen and prepared foods, which are safer and more complete. If you do feed live fish, quarantine and gut load them first."}, {"heading": "Tankmates and waste", "text": "A good rule is that anything that fits in a carnivore's mouth may become food, including shrimp and small tetras. Protein-heavy diets also produce more waste, so carnivore tanks usually need stronger filtration and regular water changes."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can carnivorous fish eat flakes?", "a": "Many can, as long as the flake is a high-protein formula. Larger carnivores usually do better on pellets plus frozen meaty foods."}, {"q": "Do carnivorous fish need live food?", "a": "Most do not. Many can be weaned onto frozen and prepared foods, which carry less disease risk. A few specialized hunters will only eat live prey."}, {"q": "Is a betta a carnivore?", "a": "Yes. Bettas are insectivores and need a protein-rich diet such as betta pellets with occasional frozen bloodworms or brine shrimp."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$daphnia$g$, $g$Daphnia$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Daphnia are tiny freshwater crustaceans, often called water fleas, that are fed live, frozen or freeze-dried as a nutritious treat for small fish.$g$, $g$Daphnia are small enough for nano fish and juvenile fry, they trigger natural hunting behavior and their shells add roughage that helps keep digestion moving. Live daphnia also survive in freshwater indefinitely, so uneaten ones don't rot and can even help clear green water.$g$, $g$Daphnia for Fish: Benefits, Feeding and How to Culture Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why fish keepers use them", "text": "Daphnia are a popular remedy for constipation and mild bloating, especially in bettas and fancy goldfish. The tough outer shell (exoskeleton) acts as fiber. They are also a gentle food for fussy or newly imported fish, and they work well for small species like rasboras, chili rasboras and killifish."}, {"heading": "How to culture daphnia", "text": "- Use a container of 5 gallons or more, ideally a tub outdoors in mild weather\n- Fill with dechlorinated water and keep it between 60 and 75°F\n- Feed green water, spirulina powder or a pinch of active dry yeast every few days\n- Add little or no aeration, only very gentle bubbles if any\n- Harvest about a quarter of the population with a fine net a few times a week\n- Do partial water changes to keep the culture from crashing"}, {"heading": "Feeding tips", "text": "Rinse live daphnia in a net before adding them so you don't pour in culture water. Feed them two or three times a week as a treat. Frozen daphnia are lighter in nutrition but still useful for fiber. Outdoor cultures can pick up insect larvae, so check before feeding them to a shrimp tank or fry tank.\n\nIf a fish bloats after a rich meal, a day of fasting followed by daphnia is the usual first step. A fish that eats daphnia and still doesn't pass waste within a couple of days may have something more serious going on than simple constipation.\n\nDaphnia cultures are sensitive to soap, chlorine and pesticides, so always use dechlorinated water and clean tools."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do daphnia help with constipation in fish?", "a": "Many keepers use them for that. The shell provides roughage, so fasting a day and then feeding daphnia is a common first step for a bloated fish."}, {"q": "Can daphnia live in a fish tank?", "a": "Yes. They survive fine in freshwater until eaten, which makes them one of the cleanest live foods to use."}, {"q": "Are daphnia safe for shrimp tanks?", "a": "Yes, daphnia do not harm shrimp. They compete a little for the same tiny food, but they are a safe addition."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fasting-day$g$, $g$Fasting Day$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$A fasting day is a planned day, usually once a week, when you skip feeding your fish so their digestive systems can clear and rest.$g$, $g$Healthy adult fish handle a missed day easily, and a weekly fast helps prevent bloating and constipation while cutting the waste that feeds ammonia and algae. It is also a simple first step when a fish looks swollen or isn't passing waste. Skip fasting for fry, which need frequent meals to grow.$g$, $g$Should You Fast Your Fish? Benefits of a Weekly Fasting Day$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why it helps", "text": "Fish in nature don't eat on a fixed schedule, and many go days between big meals. In the aquarium, food is often too regular and too rich. One day off a week gives the gut time to empty, reduces leftover food decaying in the tank and gives you an easy reset if anyone has been overfed."}, {"heading": "Who benefits most", "text": "- Bettas, which are prone to constipation and swim bladder trouble\n- Fancy goldfish, whose compact bodies make bloating common\n- Large cichlids and predators fed meaty, rich foods\n- Any tank where water quality has been slipping"}, {"heading": "Who should not fast", "text": "Fry and young juveniles burn energy quickly and should eat several small meals a day. Very small fish such as nano rasboras can also go thin quickly, so keep their fasts short. Sick or underweight fish need food more than rest unless the problem is bloating.\n\nWatch your fish on the day after a fast. Healthy fish should eat eagerly. If a fish ignores food for several days in a row, look for other signs of illness and test the water."}, {"heading": "Making it routine", "text": "Pick the same day each week, such as water change day, so it becomes a habit. If you use an automatic feeder, turn it off that day. Some keepers fast two days in a row before a short trip instead of using vacation feeder blocks, which often foul the water."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long can fish go without food?", "a": "Most healthy adult fish can go 3 to 7 days without food, and some larger fish longer. Fry should not be left without food."}, {"q": "Is it OK to skip feeding fish one day a week?", "a": "Yes. A weekly fasting day is safe and usually beneficial for healthy adult fish."}, {"q": "Should I fast a bloated fish?", "a": "Fasting for 1 to 3 days is a good first step for a bloated or constipated fish, often followed by daphnia or a small piece of blanched, skinned pea."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$flake-food$g$, $g$Flake Food$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Flake food is a dried, paper-thin fish food made from blended ingredients, used as a daily staple for many small and medium community fish.$g$, $g$Flakes are convenient, cheap and accepted by most surface and mid-water fish, which makes them the first food many beginners buy. Quality varies a lot between products, and flakes lose vitamins fast once the container is opened, so choosing a good formula and keeping it fresh matters more than most people think.$g$, $g$Flake Fish Food: How Much to Feed and Flakes vs Pellets$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Choosing a good flake", "text": "Read the ingredient list. The first ingredients should be whole fish, shrimp, krill, insect meal or spirulina, not wheat flour or soy fillers. Use a vegetable or spirulina flake for herbivores and a protein-rich flake for carnivores. Color-enhancing flakes contain pigments like astaxanthin and are fine to rotate in."}, {"heading": "How much to feed", "text": "Feed a small pinch that is eaten in about two minutes, once or twice a day. Crush flakes between your fingers for small fish like tetras and rasboras. If flakes are drifting to the bottom uneaten, you are feeding too much.\n\nBottom dwellers like corydoras and loaches rarely get enough from flakes alone, since faster fish grab them at the surface. Give them a sinking pellet or wafer, ideally right after the lights go off.\n\nIf you see cloudy water or fuzzy leftovers a few hours after feeding, cut back the pinch size."}, {"heading": "Keeping flakes fresh", "text": "Vitamin C and other nutrients break down within a couple of months of opening. Buy a size you will use in one to three months, keep the lid closed and store it somewhere cool and dry, not on top of a warm light. Some keepers freeze a backup container."}, {"heading": "Flakes vs pellets", "text": "Flakes float and slowly sink, so they suit top and mid-water fish. Pellets hold together longer, foul the water less and are better for bottom feeders and larger fish. Many keepers use both, plus frozen foods a few times a week."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can fish live on flakes alone?", "a": "Many community fish can survive on a quality flake, but they do best with variety such as pellets, frozen foods and vegetables mixed in."}, {"q": "How long does fish flake food last after opening?", "a": "Aim to use it within about one to three months. It doesn't go dangerous right away, but it loses vitamins and freshness over time."}, {"q": "Do flakes make the water cloudy?", "a": "Overfeeding flakes can. Uneaten flakes break down quickly, so feed only what is eaten in a couple of minutes."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$frozen-food$g$, $g$Frozen Food$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Frozen food is fish food such as bloodworms, brine shrimp, mysis or daphnia that is packaged and frozen, usually in small cubes or flat packs.$g$, $g$Frozen foods give fish much of the nutrition and excitement of live food with lower disease risk and long storage. They are one of the easiest ways to add variety, bring a picky fish onto food or condition fish for breeding. Thawing and rinsing them first keeps the tank cleaner.$g$, $g$Frozen Fish Food: How to Thaw, Feed and Store It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common types", "text": "- Bloodworms: rich treat for most fish\n- Brine shrimp: light, easy treat, better when enriched\n- Mysis shrimp: high in protein and fatty acids, great all-rounder\n- Daphnia: small, high in fiber, good for constipation\n- Cyclops: tiny, ideal for nano fish and juveniles\n- Spirulina or vegetable blends: for herbivores and omnivores\n\nMysis and brine shrimp are a good starting pair for most community tanks. Rotate two or three types to give a wider range of nutrients."}, {"heading": "How to feed it", "text": "Pop a cube or break off a piece of a flat pack and drop it into a small cup of tank water. Once thawed, after about 5 to 10 minutes, pour it through a fine net or let it settle and drain off the liquid, which carries phosphate and dissolved waste. Feed with a pipette or spoon. Most tanks do well with frozen food two to four times a week."}, {"heading": "Storage", "text": "Keep packs in the coldest part of the freezer and avoid thaw and refreeze cycles, which ruin texture and nutrition. If cubes turn pale, crumbly or covered with freezer burn, toss them. Most packs are best used within about six months."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Feeding a whole cube to a small tank is a frequent cause of cloudy water and ammonia spikes. Small tanks often need only half a cube or less. Siphon up leftovers that fall into the gravel."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do you have to thaw frozen fish food?", "a": "It is strongly recommended. Thawing lets you drain off the liquid and keeps fish from swallowing hard, cold pieces."}, {"q": "How often should I feed frozen food?", "a": "Two to four times a week is typical, alongside a dry staple. Some keepers feed it daily to picky or carnivorous species."}, {"q": "Is frozen food better than live food?", "a": "It is safer, since freezing kills many pathogens, and easier to store. Live food is a bit more stimulating and slightly fresher in nutrition."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$gut-loading$g$, $g$Gut Loading$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Gut loading is feeding live food organisms a nutritious diet shortly before you offer them to your fish, so the prey arrives packed with extra nutrients.$g$, $g$Live foods are only as nutritious as what they have eaten. Brine shrimp, feeder insects and even feeder fish can be fairly empty, but a few hours of good food turns them into a much better meal. Keepers use it when raising fry, conditioning breeders and feeding predators that only take live prey.$g$, $g$Gut Loading Live Food for Fish: How and Why to Do It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to gut load", "text": "Feed the prey a rich food for 6 to 24 hours before you use it. For adult brine shrimp, use spirulina powder, green water, or a commercial enrichment with fatty acids and vitamins. For blackworms and other worms, a pinch of crushed flake or algae wafer works. Feeder insects take fresh greens and crushed fish pellets.\n\nVitamin and omega-3 enrichment is especially helpful for marine-style predators and for fish being conditioned to spawn. Keep the enrichment water aerated so the prey stay alive while they feed, and use only a small amount of food so the container doesn't foul."}, {"heading": "Gut loading vs enrichment", "text": "The two terms overlap. Gut loading usually means giving prey a good meal so its stomach is full. Enrichment usually means soaking very small prey, like baby brine shrimp, in a product rich in omega-3 fatty acids and vitamins for a few hours. Both aim for the same result: a more nutritious bite."}, {"heading": "Feeder fish", "text": "If you must feed live fish to a predator, quarantine them first and feed them a quality flake for several days. Many feeder fish arrive starved and sick. Gut loading improves their value, but it does not remove the disease risk, so frozen and prepared foods are still the safer choice for most predators."}, {"heading": "Tips", "text": "- Use prey within a day of gut loading, before the food passes through\n- Rinse prey before feeding so the gut-loading food doesn't cloud the tank\n- Don't overcrowd prey containers, or they die before feeding time"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does gut loading take?", "a": "Most live foods are well loaded after 6 to 24 hours of feeding."}, {"q": "What should I gut load brine shrimp with?", "a": "Spirulina powder, green water, or a fatty acid enrichment product are the most common choices."}, {"q": "Does gut loading make feeder fish safe?", "a": "No. It improves their nutrition, but feeder fish can still carry parasites and disease, so quarantine matters more."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$herbivore$g$, $g$Herbivore$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$A herbivore is a fish whose natural diet is mainly plant matter, such as algae, aquatic plants, leaves and soft vegetation, rather than animal protein.$g$, $g$Herbivorous fish have long guts built to digest plant material, and too much protein can cause bloating and serious digestive illness, especially in African mbuna. Knowing a fish is a herbivore guides what you feed it and warns you that it may also eat your live plants.$g$, $g$What to Feed Herbivorous Fish: Veggies, Algae and Safe Foods$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Examples", "text": "- Mbuna cichlids from Lake Malawi, which graze algae off rocks\n- Many plecos, such as bristlenose, which rasp algae and soft wood\n- Silver dollars, which eat most aquarium plants\n- Some tropheus and goodeids\n- Otocinclus, which graze soft algae and biofilm"}, {"heading": "What to feed", "text": "Base the diet on a spirulina or vegetable flake or pellet. Add blanched vegetables two to three times a week: zucchini, cucumber, spinach, shelled peas and romaine are common choices. Blanch them in boiling water for a minute or two to soften them, weigh them down with a fork or veggie clip and remove leftovers within 24 hours so they don't rot.\n\nSpirulina-based flakes and algae wafers are the easiest daily staple. Feed small amounts two or three times a day for grazers like mbuna, which naturally eat little and often, instead of one big meal.\n\nLeftover vegetables cloud water quickly in warm tanks, so pull them out by the next morning."}, {"heading": "Protein and bloat", "text": "Malawi bloat is a serious illness linked to rich, high-protein foods in mbuna. Avoid feeding them bloodworms or beef heart. Even herbivores accept a little animal protein, but it should stay a small part of the diet."}, {"heading": "Plants in the tank", "text": "Many herbivores will eat soft-leaved plants. Tough plants like anubias, java fern and bolbitis usually survive, while vallisneria and stem plants often disappear. Some keepers simply accept that a herbivore tank will be decorated with rock and wood."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do herbivorous fish need protein?", "a": "A small amount, yes, which most quality spirulina foods already contain. Too much protein causes problems in many herbivores."}, {"q": "What vegetables can fish eat?", "a": "Zucchini, cucumber, spinach, shelled peas, romaine lettuce and kale are commonly fed. Blanch them first and remove leftovers within a day."}, {"q": "Will herbivorous fish eat my plants?", "a": "Many will. Hardy plants like anubias and java fern tend to survive, while soft plants often get eaten."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$live-food$g$, $g$Live Food$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Live food is any living prey fed to aquarium fish, such as baby brine shrimp, daphnia, blackworms, microworms, insect larvae or feeder fish.$g$, $g$Live food triggers natural hunting behavior, tempts picky or wild-caught fish and is one of the best ways to condition fish for breeding and feed tiny fry. The trade-off is effort and some disease risk, so culturing your own or buying from clean sources makes a big difference.$g$, $g$Live Food for Aquarium Fish: Best Types and How to Use Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Popular live foods", "text": "- Baby brine shrimp: classic first food for fry\n- Microworms and vinegar eels: tiny, easy to culture, great for very small fry\n- Daphnia: small fish and fiber for digestion\n- Blackworms: rich and loved by loaches, cories and bettas\n- Mosquito and glass worm larvae: excellent for conditioning\n- Feeder fish: only for predators, and best avoided when possible"}, {"heading": "Risks to know", "text": "Live foods collected from ponds can bring in parasites, leeches, hydra or predatory insect larvae. Blackworms and tubifex from dirty sources can carry bacteria. Feeder fish are the biggest risk and often arrive sick. Cultured foods such as brine shrimp, microworms and home-raised daphnia are much safer."}, {"heading": "Using live food well", "text": "Rinse live food in a net with clean water before feeding. Feed amounts that will be eaten within a few minutes, except prey like daphnia that survive in the tank. For breeding, feeding live food daily for a week or two often brings fish into spawning condition.\n\nMany breeders pair a week of live food with a slightly cooler water change to trigger spawning in species like corydoras and killifish. Feed the culture you plan to use a day ahead so the prey is well fed and more nutritious when it reaches your fish."}, {"heading": "Culturing at home", "text": "Most live food cultures are cheap and simple. A shoebox of oatmeal starts microworms, a bottle and an airline hatches brine shrimp and a tub outdoors grows daphnia. Keep two cultures of each so one crash doesn't leave you without food."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is live food better than frozen?", "a": "Live food is more stimulating and slightly fresher, but frozen is safer and easier. Most fish do great on a mix of frozen and dry foods."}, {"q": "What is the best live food for fry?", "a": "Baby brine shrimp for most fry, and microworms or vinegar eels for very small fry that can't yet fit brine shrimp in their mouths."}, {"q": "Can live food make fish sick?", "a": "Yes. Wild-collected live food and feeder fish can carry parasites and bacteria. Cultured live foods carry much lower risk."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$omnivore$g$, $g$Omnivore$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$An omnivore is a fish that naturally eats both animal and plant foods, such as insects, small crustaceans, algae and plant matter.$g$, $g$Most popular community fish, including tetras, livebearers, barbs, danios, gouramis and corydoras, are omnivores. That makes them easy to feed, but they still do best on variety. A mix of quality dry food, frozen meaty food and some greens keeps their color strong and their immune systems healthy.$g$, $g$Omnivorous Fish Diet: What to Feed and How Often$g$, $g$[{"heading": "A simple feeding plan", "text": "- Daily: a quality omnivore flake or micro pellet as the staple\n- Two or three times a week: frozen brine shrimp, daphnia, mysis or bloodworms\n- Once or twice a week: a spirulina food or blanched vegetables\n- One day a week: no food at all\n\nFeed small amounts once or twice a day, enough to be eaten in about two minutes.\n\nVariety matters more than any single premium food. A cheap, decent flake rotated with frozen foods and greens usually beats an expensive food fed alone every day. Change foods gradually if your fish are fussy, mixing a little of the new food with the old."}, {"heading": "Leaning one way or the other", "text": "Omnivores are a spectrum. Mollies and many barbs lean toward plants and algae and benefit from regular spirulina. Gouramis and many cichlids lean toward meaty foods. Check what your species eats in the wild and shift the balance a little in that direction."}, {"heading": "Feeding mixed tanks", "text": "In a community tank, different fish eat at different levels. Use a floating food for surface feeders, a slow-sinking food for mid-water fish and a sinking wafer or pellet for bottom dwellers like corydoras. Feed bottom dwellers after lights out if faster fish steal everything."}, {"heading": "Signs the diet is off", "text": "Faded color, thin bellies, frayed fins and constant grazing on plants can all point to an unbalanced or too-small diet. Bloated bellies and stringy white waste can mean too much rich protein."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are most aquarium fish omnivores?", "a": "Yes. The majority of common community fish are omnivores, which is part of why they are easy to keep."}, {"q": "Can omnivorous fish eat only flakes?", "a": "They can survive on a quality flake, but they look and breed better with frozen foods and greens added."}, {"q": "Do omnivorous fish eat plants?", "a": "Some nibble soft plants or algae. Most leave healthy, well-established plants alone if they are well fed."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$overfeeding$g$, $g$Overfeeding$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$Overfeeding is giving fish more food than they can eat in a few minutes, so leftovers rot and extra waste pollutes the water.$g$, $g$Overfeeding is one of the most common causes of fish death for beginners. Uneaten food and extra waste break down into ammonia and nitrite, feed algae and bacterial blooms, and can overwhelm a filter, especially in a new or small tank. Fish also suffer from bloating and fatty livers when stuffed every day.$g$, $g$Overfeeding Fish: Signs, Problems and How Much to Feed$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs you are overfeeding", "text": "- Food reaching the bottom and sitting uneaten\n- Cloudy or hazy water, often a bacterial bloom\n- Rising ammonia, nitrite or nitrate readings\n- Algae growing much faster than usual\n- Snail populations exploding\n- Fish with swollen bellies or long trailing waste"}, {"heading": "How much to feed", "text": "Feed what your fish finish in about two minutes, once or twice a day. An old rule of thumb says a fish's stomach is about the size of its eye, which is a handy reminder to keep portions small. Fish always act hungry, so begging is not a sign they need more. Bottom feeders may need a sinking wafer, but only enough for them.\n\nIf several people in the house feed the fish, agree on one feeder or a written schedule. Double feeding is one of the easiest ways for a tank to slide into trouble without anyone noticing.\n\nIn a new tank that is still cycling, feed even less, every other day if needed, until ammonia and nitrite stay at zero."}, {"heading": "How to fix it", "text": "Cut back right away and skip a day of feeding. Siphon leftover food from the gravel and do a 25 to 50 percent water change if ammonia or nitrite show up. Clean the filter gently in tank water if it is clogged with uneaten food. Test daily until readings are back to zero."}, {"heading": "Vacations and feeders", "text": "Vacation feeder blocks and well-meaning pet sitters are classic causes of overfeeding. Healthy adult fish can go several days without food, so a short trip usually needs no feeding at all. For longer trips, an automatic feeder set to small portions is safer."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can overfeeding kill fish?", "a": "Yes. It usually kills through ammonia and nitrite spikes from rotting food, and sometimes through bloating and digestive problems."}, {"q": "How do I know if I'm overfeeding my fish?", "a": "Leftover food on the bottom, cloudy water, extra algae, booming snails and rising ammonia or nitrate are the clearest signs."}, {"q": "Is it better to underfeed or overfeed fish?", "a": "Slight underfeeding is much safer than overfeeding for healthy adult fish. Fry are the exception and need frequent small meals."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$pellet$g$, $g$Pellet$g$, $g$Food & Nutrition$g$, $g$A pellet is a compressed piece of dry fish food that comes in floating, slow-sinking or sinking forms and sizes from tiny micro pellets to large chunks.$g$, $g$Pellets let you match food size and position to the fish you keep, so bottom dwellers and mid-water fish get fed and not just the fastest swimmers. They hold together longer than flakes, which means less mess, and many large fish do better on pellets than any other staple food.$g$, $g$Pellets vs Flakes for Fish: Choosing the Right Pellet Size$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Floating vs sinking", "text": "Floating pellets suit surface feeders like bettas, gouramis and many cichlids, and let you watch who eats. Slow-sinking pellets reach mid-water fish like tetras and rainbowfish. Sinking pellets and wafers are for corydoras, loaches, plecos and other bottom feeders."}, {"heading": "Picking the right size", "text": "The pellet should fit easily in the fish's mouth. Micro pellets around 0.5 to 1 mm suit nano fish and small tetras, 1 to 2 mm suits most community fish, and larger cichlids and goldfish take 3 mm and up. If fish spit pellets out repeatedly, try a smaller size.\n\nWhen switching from flakes, mix a few crushed pellets in with the old food for a week. Many fish ignore new foods at first and accept them once they learn they are edible.\n\nWafers are flat sinking pellets made for plecos and bottom dwellers. Herbivore wafers lean on algae and spirulina, while carnivore wafers lean on shrimp and fish meal."}, {"heading": "Soaking and bloating", "text": "Dry pellets swell when wet. Fish that gulp many pellets at once, such as bettas and fancy goldfish, can bloat or develop buoyancy trouble. Soaking pellets in tank water for a minute or two before feeding is a simple fix many keepers use."}, {"heading": "Quality and storage", "text": "Look for a whole protein source near the top of the ingredient list, with fillers like wheat further down. Keep the container sealed and cool, and use it within a few months of opening."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are pellets better than flakes?", "a": "Not always, but pellets usually make less mess and suit larger and bottom-dwelling fish better. Many keepers feed both."}, {"q": "Should I soak fish pellets?", "a": "It is a good idea for bettas, goldfish and any fish prone to bloating. A minute or two in tank water softens them."}, {"q": "How many pellets should I feed my fish?", "a": "Feed what is eaten in about two minutes. For a betta, that is often just 2 to 4 small pellets per meal."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$beginner-fish$g$, $g$Beginner Fish$g$, $g$General & Hobby$g$, $g$A beginner fish is a hardy, peaceful and easy-to-feed species that tolerates small mistakes, making it a good choice for someone setting up a first aquarium.$g$, $g$Your first fish shape whether the hobby feels fun or frustrating. Hardy species survive the learning curve of water changes, feeding and cycling, and they help you build good habits before you try sensitive fish. Even beginner fish still need a cycled tank, a heater and the right tank size.$g$, $g$Best Beginner Fish for a First Tank (and Ones to Avoid)$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Good choices for a first tank", "text": "- Platies, mollies and swordtails: colorful livebearers that eat anything\n- Zebra and leopard danios: very tough, active, best in groups of 6 or more\n- White Cloud Mountain minnows: unheated tanks around 64 to 72°F\n- Corydoras catfish: peaceful bottom dwellers, kept in groups\n- Harlequin rasboras and many tetras: once the tank is fully cycled\n- A single betta: in 5 gallons or more with a heater"}, {"heading": "Fish that are often sold to beginners but shouldn't be", "text": "Common plecos grow to over 12 inches. Goldfish are hardy but get large and very messy, needing much more space than a small bowl. Oscars and other big cichlids outgrow most starter tanks quickly. Bala sharks and iridescent sharks are schooling fish that reach a foot or more. Always check adult size before buying.\n\nAlso be wary of fish that need very specific water. Discus, wild bettas, many Caridina shrimp and most saltwater species are best left until you have experience."}, {"heading": "Setting yourself up to succeed", "text": "Cycle the tank before adding fish, or use a fish-in cycle with a few hardy fish and daily testing. Add fish a few at a time over several weeks so the beneficial bacteria can keep up. A 20 to 30 gallon tank is actually easier than a tiny one, because water quality stays more stable."}, {"heading": "Next steps", "text": "Once you have kept a tank stable for a few months, you can try more sensitive fish like cardinal tetras, apistogrammas or shrimp."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the easiest fish to take care of?", "a": "Platies, zebra danios and White Cloud Mountain minnows are among the toughest and easiest fish for a new keeper."}, {"q": "Are goldfish good for beginners?", "a": "They are hardy, but they grow large and produce lots of waste, so they need a big tank and strong filtration. They are not a good fit for small starter tanks or bowls."}, {"q": "How many fish should I start with?", "a": "Start with just a few hardy fish and add more gradually over several weeks while you test the water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$centerpiece-fish$g$, $g$Centerpiece Fish$g$, $g$General & Hobby$g$, $g$A centerpiece fish is a standout species, usually larger, more colorful or more personable than its tankmates, chosen as the main focal point of an aquarium.$g$, $g$Picking a centerpiece first helps you plan the rest of the tank: its size sets the minimum tank volume, its temperament limits which tankmates are safe and its water needs set the parameters. A well-chosen centerpiece makes a community tank feel designed instead of random.$g$, $g$Best Centerpiece Fish for Community Tanks by Tank Size$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Popular choices by tank size", "text": "- 10 to 20 gallons: a betta, honey or dwarf gourami, or a pair of German blue rams\n- 20 to 40 gallons: pearl gourami, a pair of apistogrammas or a group of three kribensis\n- 40 to 75 gallons: angelfish, a group of rainbowfish, or a bristlenose pleco with character\n- 75 gallons and up: a group of discus, a severum, or a larger peaceful cichlid"}, {"heading": "How to choose", "text": "Check the adult size, not the size in the store. Make sure the fish fits your water: discus and rams want warm, soft water around 82 to 86°F, while many rainbowfish are fine in harder water. Consider personality too. Gouramis and cichlids interact with their keepers, which is part of the appeal."}, {"heading": "Planning tankmates", "text": "Build the community around the centerpiece. Avoid fin nippers like tiger barbs with long-finned fish such as angelfish and bettas. Remember that angelfish will eat small fish like neon tetras as they grow. Pick dither fish, such as a school of medium tetras or rasboras, that are too big to be eaten and calm enough not to stress the main fish.\n\nAdd the centerpiece last when possible, after the dither fish have settled, so it isn't the only fish in the tank while you finish stocking."}, {"heading": "One or several?", "text": "Some centerpiece species, like many gouramis, fight with their own kind in small tanks. Others, like angelfish or discus, do better in groups. Research before deciding whether to buy one or several."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is a good centerpiece fish for a 20 gallon tank?", "a": "A pearl or honey gourami, a pair of German blue rams or a betta are common choices for a 20 gallon community."}, {"q": "Can you have two centerpiece fish?", "a": "Sometimes, if they are compatible and the tank is big enough. Two male gouramis or two territorial cichlids in a small tank often fight."}, {"q": "Are angelfish good centerpiece fish?", "a": "Yes, they are a classic choice for tanks of about 30 gallons or more, but they will eat small fish like neon tetras."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$doa$g$, $g$DOA$g$, $g$General & Hobby$g$, $g$DOA stands for dead on arrival and describes fish, shrimp, snails or plants that die during shipping and arrive already dead when the box is opened.$g$, $g$Anyone who buys livestock online or by mail will run into DOA policies sooner or later. Knowing how live-arrival guarantees work, and what to do the moment a package arrives, helps you get a refund or replacement and protects the animals that did survive.$g$, $g$DOA Fish Meaning: Dead on Arrival and Live Arrival Guarantees$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What to do when a box arrives", "text": "- Open the box as soon as it arrives, in dim light\n- Photograph or video the sealed bags before opening them, showing any dead animals clearly\n- Note the time and contact the seller within their deadline, often 2 hours to the same day\n- Float and acclimate survivors right away; don't wait on paperwork"}, {"heading": "Live arrival guarantees", "text": "Most sellers offer a live arrival guarantee, but terms vary. Many require you to be home for delivery, to choose overnight or express shipping and to send clear photos of the unopened bag. Some only credit the cost of the animal, not shipping. Read the policy before you buy. Weather holds are common: sellers often won't ship when temperatures are below about 32°F or above about 90°F along the route."}, {"heading": "Why fish die in shipping", "text": "Long delays, temperature swings, ammonia buildup in the bag and low oxygen are the main causes. Fish that seem fine on arrival but die in the first few days are often from ammonia shock during acclimation, not shipping itself. Don't release bag water into your tank, and don't spend hours acclimating fish sitting in high ammonia.\n\nBuy from sellers with good reviews who list their shipping methods clearly, and avoid ordering right before a holiday or weekend when packages can sit in transit an extra day or two."}, {"heading": "Selling on a marketplace", "text": "If you ship fish yourself, fast the fish for a day or two first, use breather bags or plenty of oxygen, insulate the box and add heat or cold packs for the season."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What does DOA mean in fish?", "a": "Dead on arrival: the fish died during shipping and was dead when the package was opened."}, {"q": "What is a live arrival guarantee?", "a": "A seller promise to refund or replace animals that arrive dead, usually as long as you send photos within a set time."}, {"q": "Why did my fish die a day after shipping?", "a": "Common causes are shipping stress, ammonia shock during acclimation, temperature swings or a mismatch in water parameters."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$gallon$g$, $g$Gallon$g$, $g$General & Hobby$g$, $g$A gallon is a unit of volume, and in the US hobby tanks are sized by gallons, with one US gallon equal to about 3.8 liters.$g$, $g$Tank volume drives almost every decision in fishkeeping: how many and which fish you can keep, how big a heater and filter you need and how much medication or water conditioner to add. Larger volumes also stay more stable, which is why bigger tanks are often easier for beginners.$g$, $g$How to Calculate Aquarium Gallons (and Why It Matters)$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Calculating tank volume", "text": "Measure the inside length, width and height in inches, multiply them together and divide by 231. For example, a tank 30 by 12 by 16 inches holds 5,760 cubic inches, or about 25 gallons.\n\nThe real water volume is lower than the rated size. Substrate, rock, wood and the gap below the rim usually take up 10 to 20 percent. Use the actual water volume when dosing medications, and the rated size when choosing equipment."}, {"heading": "Common tank sizes", "text": "- 5 to 10 gallons: nano tanks for a betta, shrimp or a few tiny fish\n- 20 gallons: long or high, a popular starter size\n- 29 and 40 gallons: room for a real community\n- 55 and 75 gallons: 4 foot tanks for larger schools and cichlids"}, {"heading": "Rules of thumb", "text": "The old one inch of fish per gallon rule is a rough guess at best. It ignores body mass, activity and waste, and fails for big fish. Think instead about the adult size of the fish, swimming space and filtration. For heaters, about 3 to 5 watts per gallon is a common starting point. Filters are often sized to turn over the tank volume 4 to 6 times an hour."}, {"heading": "Metric and imperial gallons", "text": "A UK (imperial) gallon is about 4.5 liters, larger than a US gallon. If a guide or medication label is from outside the US, double-check which one it uses."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I calculate how many gallons my tank is?", "a": "Multiply length, width and height in inches, then divide by 231 to get US gallons."}, {"q": "How much does a gallon of water weigh?", "a": "About 8.3 pounds, so a full 55 gallon tank with gravel and decor weighs over 600 pounds."}, {"q": "Is the one inch per gallon rule accurate?", "a": "Not really. It is a loose guideline that works poorly for large, thick-bodied or messy fish."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$lfs$g$, $g$LFS$g$, $g$General & Hobby$g$, $g$LFS is hobby shorthand for local fish store, usually meaning an independent aquarium shop rather than a big chain pet store.$g$, $g$A good local fish store can be one of your best resources: you can see fish before you buy, get advice for your own water and often find species and plants that chains don't carry. A bad one can sell sick fish and poor advice, so it pays to learn how to tell the difference.$g$, $g$How to Pick a Good Local Fish Store (LFS) and Spot Red Flags$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs of a good store", "text": "- Clean tanks with clear water and few dead fish\n- Staff who ask about your tank size and water before selling fish\n- Willingness to hold fish for a few days or test your water\n- Fish that have been in the store a while, not just arrived\n- Clear labels with scientific names and adult sizes"}, {"heading": "Red flags", "text": "Tanks with many dead or sick fish, white spots or clamped fins, and shared filtration systems where one disease spreads to every tank. Staff who will sell you ten fish for a tank you set up that day, or a large pleco for a 10 gallon, are another warning sign.\n\nAlso be cautious of stores that dye or tattoo fish, sell bettas in tiny cups for weeks, or push products you don't need. Good stores make money on loyalty, not on pressure sales."}, {"heading": "Buying smart", "text": "Watch the fish you want for a few minutes. Look for clear eyes, full bodies, open fins and active swimming. Avoid buying from a tank that has any sick or dead fish in it, even if your fish looks fine. Ask when the fish came in; waiting a week after arrival often improves survival. Quarantine new fish at home whenever you can."}, {"heading": "Building a relationship", "text": "Regular customers often get first pick of new shipments, special orders and honest advice. Many shops also buy or trade fish, shrimp and plants you breed or grow."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What does LFS stand for in fishkeeping?", "a": "Local fish store, usually an independent aquarium shop."}, {"q": "Is it better to buy fish from an LFS or a chain store?", "a": "A good LFS usually has healthier stock and better advice, but quality varies at both. Judge the specific store by its tanks."}, {"q": "Should I quarantine fish from a local fish store?", "a": "Yes. Quarantine any new fish for 2 to 4 weeks if you can, no matter where you bought it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$mts$g$, $g$MTS$g$, $g$General & Hobby$g$, $g$MTS stands for Multiple Tank Syndrome, the hobby's joking name for the common urge to keep setting up more and more aquariums.$g$, $g$Almost every longtime fishkeeper has a case of MTS. It is a fun part of the hobby, but each new tank adds maintenance time, power costs and weight on floors. Planning for it, rather than letting it sneak up, keeps the hobby enjoyable instead of overwhelming.$g$, $g$Multiple Tank Syndrome (MTS): What It Means and How to Manage It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it usually starts", "text": "It often begins with a practical second tank: a quarantine tank, a hospital tank or a grow-out tank for surprise fry. Then comes a shrimp tank, a planted nano, a breeding project and a tank for that one species you couldn't resist. Before long, a shelf in the garage is full."}, {"heading": "Keeping it manageable", "text": "- Put tanks near a water source, or set up a water change system with hoses or a pump\n- Group tanks on one sturdy rack to share space and electricity\n- Keep a simple maintenance log for each tank\n- Use separate nets and siphons, or disinfect between tanks, to avoid spreading disease\n- Only add a tank when you have time for its weekly water changes\n\nA small used rack, an extra sponge filter running in your main tank and spare heaters make it easy to set up a new tank quickly, since a seeded sponge brings beneficial bacteria with it."}, {"heading": "Floors and power", "text": "Water weighs about 8.3 pounds per gallon, so several large tanks in one room add up fast. Place big tanks near load-bearing walls and check your floor if you are unsure. Heaters and lights on many tanks also show up on the power bill, so a well-insulated room helps."}, {"heading": "The upside", "text": "Multiple tanks let you keep species that can't live together, breed fish, and always have a spare tank ready in an emergency. Many keepers end up selling or trading their extra fish, plants and shrimp, which can help cover costs."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What does MTS mean in the aquarium hobby?", "a": "Multiple Tank Syndrome, a joking term for the habit of setting up more and more tanks."}, {"q": "Is a second tank worth it?", "a": "Yes, especially as a quarantine or hospital tank. It is one of the best investments a fishkeeper can make."}, {"q": "How many fish tanks is too many?", "a": "When maintenance becomes a chore you skip, you have too many. The right number depends on your time, space and budget."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$sexing$g$, $g$Sexing$g$, $g$General & Hobby$g$, $g$Sexing is figuring out whether a fish or invertebrate is male or female by looking at body shape, color, fins, size or behavior.$g$, $g$Knowing the sex of your fish matters for breeding, for preventing unwanted fry and for avoiding aggression. Many species fight when kept with too many males, and livebearers need more females than males to spread out the chasing. Some fish are easy to sex while others only reveal it when spawning.$g$, $g$How to Tell If a Fish Is Male or Female (Fish Sexing Guide)$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common clues", "text": "- Livebearers: males have a gonopodium, a rod-like anal fin used for mating, while females have a fan-shaped anal fin\n- Bettas and gouramis: males are usually more colorful with longer fins; female bettas often show a white egg spot\n- Corydoras: females are rounder and larger when viewed from above\n- Tetras and rasboras: females are usually plumper; males slimmer and brighter\n- Cichlids: males often have longer, pointed dorsal and anal fins and brighter color"}, {"heading": "Shrimp and snails", "text": "Female cherry shrimp are larger, more colorful and show a yellow or green saddle of developing eggs behind the head. Mystery snails can be sexed by looking for the male sheath inside the shell, while many pest snails like ramshorns and bladder snails are hermaphrodites."}, {"heading": "When sexing is hard", "text": "Juveniles of most species look alike until they mature. Some fish, like many plecos and angelfish, are hard to sex reliably outside of breeding. In those cases, buying a group of 6 juveniles and letting pairs form naturally is a common approach. Venting, checking the genital papilla, is used by breeders but takes practice."}, {"heading": "Getting ratios right", "text": "For livebearers, keep at least 2 to 3 females per male. Many tetras and rasboras school well in mixed groups.\n\nFor cichlids, gouramis and bettas, too many males in a small tank often leads to fighting. Keep one male or one bonded pair unless the tank is large with plenty of territory. Shrimp colonies work well with roughly equal numbers, since each female carries eggs about once a month."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How can you tell if a guppy is male or female?", "a": "Male guppies are smaller, brightly colored and have a pointed gonopodium. Females are larger, plainer and have a fan-shaped anal fin."}, {"q": "At what age can you sex fish?", "a": "It depends on the species, but many become clear once they reach sexual maturity, from a few weeks for livebearers to several months for cichlids."}, {"q": "Can fish change sex?", "a": "A few species can, mostly marine fish like clownfish and wrasses. Most freshwater aquarium fish do not."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$amano-shrimp$g$, $g$Amano Shrimp$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Amano shrimp (Caridina multidentata) are hardy, translucent freshwater shrimp that grow to about 2 inches and are prized for eating algae and leftover food.$g$, $g$Amanos are among the best algae-eating invertebrates for planted tanks, tackling hair algae, soft film and leftovers that fish ignore. They were popularized by aquascaper Takashi Amano. Their larvae need brackish water to develop, so they almost never breed in a home tank and won't overrun it.$g$, $g$Amano Shrimp Care: Algae Eating, Tank Size and Tankmates$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Care basics", "text": "- Tank: 10 gallons or more; a group of 3 to 6 in a 20 gallon works well\n- Temperature: 70 to 80°F\n- pH: about 6.5 to 7.8, with moderate hardness\n- Lifespan: often 2 to 3 years or more\n- Diet: algae, biofilm and leftovers, plus algae wafers or blanched vegetables if algae runs out"}, {"heading": "How well they clean algae", "text": "Amanos are great on hair algae, thread algae, soft green film and decaying plant matter. They are less effective on hard green spot algae and black beard algae, though some will pick at softened black beard algae. A rough guide is one Amano per 2 to 5 gallons for heavy algae duty. They are not a substitute for fixing light, nutrients and maintenance."}, {"heading": "Tankmates", "text": "Peaceful community fish like tetras, rasboras and corydoras are good matches. Avoid bettas that hunt shrimp, cichlids, large barbs and anything big enough to eat a 2 inch shrimp. Amanos can be pushy at feeding time and may steal food from smaller shrimp, but they don't harm them."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Amanos are sensitive to copper and many medications, so remove them before treating fish. They are skilled escape artists, so keep a lid on the tank. A failed molt, called the white ring of death, can point to unstable parameters or poor mineral content, and big water changes with very different water can trigger it."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do Amano shrimp breed in freshwater?", "a": "They lay eggs in freshwater, but the larvae need brackish water to survive, so they almost never breed in a normal tank."}, {"q": "How many Amano shrimp should I get?", "a": "A common guide is one per 2 to 5 gallons, depending on how much algae you need them to handle."}, {"q": "Will Amano shrimp eat my plants?", "a": "No. They eat algae and decaying plant matter, not healthy plants, though they may pick at a dying leaf."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$assassin-snail$g$, $g$Assassin Snail$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Assassin snails (Clea helena) are small, striped yellow and brown freshwater snails that hunt and eat other snails, making them a natural pest snail control.$g$, $g$When bladder, ramshorn or Malaysian trumpet snails explode in a tank, assassin snails offer a slow but chemical-free fix that won't harm fish, shrimp or plants. They breed slowly and only lay single eggs, so they don't become a new pest themselves.$g$, $g$Assassin Snails for Pest Control: Do They Work and How Many?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How they control pests", "text": "Assassin snails track prey by scent, then use a long proboscis to eat the snail inside its shell. They work best on small and medium snails. One assassin may eat a snail every few days, so expect a visible drop over several weeks to a few months, not overnight. A rough starting point is 1 assassin per 5 to 10 gallons for a heavy outbreak, fewer for a light one."}, {"heading": "Care basics", "text": "- Temperature: 72 to 82°F\n- pH: about 7.0 to 8.0, with moderate to hard water to keep shells strong\n- Substrate: sand or fine gravel, since they like to burrow\n- Diet: snails first, then leftover food, sinking pellets and frozen bloodworms\n- Size: about 0.75 to 1 inch"}, {"heading": "Will they harm anything else?", "text": "They are safe with fish and adult shrimp. They may occasionally scavenge shrimp eggs or very weak shrimplets, but most shrimp keepers find them low risk. They will eat snails you want to keep, including small nerites and baby mystery snails, so think before adding them to a tank with prized snails."}, {"heading": "Breeding", "text": "They need both a male and a female and lay single eggs in small capsules on hard surfaces. Babies stay buried for months. Numbers stay modest, especially once pest snails run out.\n\nOnce pest snails are gone, feed them a sinking pellet or frozen food a few times a week. Remove extra assassins or give them away if you don't want them hunting your nerites."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do assassin snails eat shrimp?", "a": "They don't hunt healthy adult shrimp. At most they may scavenge dead shrimp or occasionally eggs."}, {"q": "How long do assassin snails take to clear pest snails?", "a": "Usually several weeks to a few months, depending on how many assassins you add and how many pest snails there are."}, {"q": "Will assassin snails take over my tank?", "a": "Unlikely. They breed slowly, lay one egg at a time and their numbers level off when food runs low."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$berried$g$, $g$Berried$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Berried describes a female shrimp, crayfish or other crustacean that is carrying a cluster of fertilized eggs under her tail on the swimmerets.$g$, $g$A berried shrimp is a sign your colony is healthy and comfortable. Knowing what to expect during the roughly month-long carrying period, and what can make her drop the eggs, helps more of the babies survive to adulthood. It is also a cue to keep things calm in the tank.$g$, $g$Berried Shrimp: How Long Until Eggs Hatch and How to Help$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it looks like", "text": "The eggs sit under the tail and look like a small bunch of berries. They are often yellow, green or the color of the female's saddle, and turn darker as they develop. Near hatching, you can sometimes see tiny eyes inside each egg. The female constantly fans her swimmerets to keep the eggs clean and oxygenated.\n\nFemales usually become berried right after molting, when they release scent that sends males swimming frantically around the tank. This burst of activity, sometimes called the mating dance, is normal and harmless."}, {"heading": "How long it takes", "text": "For cherry shrimp and other Neocaridina, the eggs usually hatch in about 21 to 30 days, faster in warmer water around 76 to 78°F and slower in cooler water. Caridina such as crystal shrimp take a similar time. Babies hatch as tiny copies of the adults."}, {"heading": "Helping the eggs hatch", "text": "- Keep water stable; avoid big water changes or large temperature swings\n- Don't move or net the female unless you must\n- Avoid adding new fish or medications during this time\n- Provide plenty of moss and plants for cover once babies hatch\n- Use a sponge or a pre-filter on the filter intake so babies aren't sucked in"}, {"heading": "Dropped eggs", "text": "Young first-time mothers sometimes drop their eggs. Stress from parameter changes, a molt or being chased by fish can also cause it. Usually the next clutch goes better once the tank settles."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long are shrimp berried for?", "a": "Usually around 3 to 4 weeks for cherry shrimp, depending on temperature."}, {"q": "Can a berried shrimp molt?", "a": "She normally waits to molt until after the eggs hatch. A molt while berried usually causes the eggs to be lost."}, {"q": "Will fish eat baby shrimp?", "a": "Yes. Most fish will eat baby shrimp, so dense moss and plants or a shrimp-only tank greatly improve survival."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$biofilm$g$, $g$Biofilm$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Biofilm is a thin, slimy layer of bacteria, algae, fungi and other microorganisms that grows on glass, plants, wood, rocks and substrate in an aquarium.$g$, $g$Biofilm is a natural food source that baby shrimp, snails, otocinclus and many fry depend on. It is a sign of a maturing tank rather than dirt. It can sometimes appear as a surface film or a cloudy coating on new wood, which is usually harmless.$g$, $g$Aquarium Biofilm: Is It Good or Bad and How to Manage It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why it matters", "text": "In a shrimp tank, biofilm is the main food for newborn shrimplets, which are too small to eat most prepared foods. Otocinclus, nerite snails and many plecos graze on it constantly. A well-established tank with lots of surface area from wood, rock, moss and leaves grows more of it, which is one reason shrimp do better in mature tanks.\n\nBiofilm also helps break down waste and gives new tanks a head start on surfaces where beneficial bacteria can settle. Keepers who set up a shrimp tank often let it run with lights on for a few extra weeks, just to grow a good layer before adding shrimp."}, {"heading": "Surface film", "text": "An oily or cloudy film on the water surface is a different kind of biofilm built from proteins and oils. It can block gas exchange and dull light. Fix it by aiming a filter outlet so the surface ripples, adding a surface skimmer or laying a paper towel on the surface to lift it off. Overfeeding makes it worse."}, {"heading": "White fuzz on new wood", "text": "New driftwood often grows a white, cotton-like fungal or bacterial film in the first few weeks. It is harmless, often eaten by shrimp and snails, and usually fades on its own. You can rinse or brush it off if it bothers you."}, {"heading": "Encouraging biofilm", "text": "- Add botanicals like catappa leaves and alder cones\n- Use driftwood and porous rock for more surface area\n- Dust in powdered biofilm or spirulina foods made for shrimp\n- Don't scrub every surface during maintenance; clean only the front glass"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is biofilm harmful to fish?", "a": "Normally no. Surface biofilm can reduce oxygen exchange if it gets thick, so break it up with surface movement."}, {"q": "What eats biofilm in an aquarium?", "a": "Shrimp, snails, otocinclus, many plecos and fry all graze on biofilm."}, {"q": "How do I get rid of white slime on driftwood?", "a": "Leave it alone or brush it off. It usually disappears within a few weeks, and shrimp and snails often eat it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$caridina-shrimp$g$, $g$Caridina Shrimp$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Caridina shrimp are a genus of freshwater dwarf shrimp, including crystal red, crystal black, bee, taiwan bee and tiger shrimp, that usually need soft, acidic water.$g$, $g$Caridina are some of the most striking shrimp in the hobby, with bold patterns and high-grade lines that are bred like show animals. They are less forgiving than Neocaridina like cherry shrimp, so they suit keepers who are comfortable testing water and keeping parameters steady.$g$, $g$Caridina Shrimp Care: Crystal and Bee Shrimp Water Parameters$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Water parameters", "text": "- pH: about 5.8 to 6.8 for crystal and bee shrimp\n- GH: about 4 to 6\n- KH: 0 to 1\n- TDS: about 100 to 160 ppm\n- Temperature: 68 to 74°F, rarely above 76°F\n\nTiger shrimp tolerate a bit more range, but stable soft water still suits them best."}, {"heading": "Setting up a Caridina tank", "text": "Most keepers use an active buffering soil substrate that holds pH low, plus reverse osmosis (RO) or RO/DI water remineralized with a GH-only product made for Caridina. Tap water is usually too hard. A sponge filter keeps babies safe. Let the tank cycle fully, often 4 to 6 weeks with buffering soil, which can leach ammonia at first."}, {"heading": "Feeding and maintenance", "text": "Feed lightly, a few times a week, and remove leftovers within a few hours. Good biofilm and leaf litter matter. Do small, steady water changes of about 10 percent with water matched closely for TDS and temperature, since sudden swings can cause failed molts. Active soils lose buffering over 1 to 2 years and need replacing.\n\nWatch for early warning signs: shrimp swimming frantically, hiding all day or dying around molts. Test TDS, GH and temperature first, since small shifts cause most Caridina problems. Many breeders keep a log of every water change and parameter reading."}, {"heading": "Mixing with other shrimp", "text": "Different Caridina types like crystal and taiwan bee can interbreed, which some breeders do on purpose. Caridina and Neocaridina won't crossbreed, but they need very different water, so they don't share a tank well."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can Caridina and Neocaridina live together?", "a": "They won't interbreed, but they prefer different water, so one group usually struggles. Keeping them apart is best."}, {"q": "Do Caridina shrimp need RO water?", "a": "Most crystal and bee shrimp need soft water, and RO water remineralized for Caridina is the most reliable way to get it in most areas."}, {"q": "Are Caridina shrimp good for beginners?", "a": "Usually not. Start with cherry shrimp or other Neocaridina, then move to Caridina once you are comfortable with stable water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$cherry-shrimp$g$, $g$Cherry Shrimp$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Cherry shrimp (Neocaridina davidi) are small, hardy freshwater dwarf shrimp, bred in many colors, that eat algae and biofilm and breed easily in home aquariums.$g$, $g$Cherry shrimp are the best starting point for shrimp keeping. They handle a wide range of tap water, help clean up algae and leftovers and breed readily without special care. They are also tiny and gentle, so tank choice and tankmates decide whether a colony thrives or disappears.$g$, $g$Cherry Shrimp Care: Tank Setup, Breeding and Best Tankmates$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Care basics", "text": "- Tank: 5 gallons or more, cycled and stable for at least a few weeks\n- Temperature: 65 to 80°F, around 72 to 76°F is ideal\n- pH: about 6.5 to 8.0\n- GH: about 6 to 8; KH: about 2 to 8\n- Size: about 1 to 1.5 inches\n- Lifespan: around 1 to 2 years"}, {"heading": "Feeding", "text": "They graze biofilm and soft algae all day. Add a small amount of shrimp food, blanched spinach or crushed algae wafer a few times a week, and remove leftovers within a few hours. Overfeeding is a common reason colonies crash."}, {"heading": "Breeding", "text": "A healthy colony breeds on its own. Females carry eggs for about 3 to 4 weeks, and babies hatch fully formed. Moss, plants and a sponge filter or pre-filter keep the young safe. Selectively bred colors tend to revert toward wild brown over generations, so many keepers remove dull shrimp to keep colors strong."}, {"heading": "Tankmates and dangers", "text": "Small, peaceful fish like otocinclus, chili rasboras and pygmy corydoras work best. Most other fish, including bettas and many tetras, will eat babies and sometimes adults. Copper in medications, plant fertilizers or snail treatments is deadly to shrimp, so check every product. Acclimate new shrimp slowly, by drip if possible, over 1 to 2 hours.\n\nColors range from red to yellow, orange, blue, green and black. Mixing color lines produces wild-type brown babies over time, so keep one color per tank if you want them to stay bright."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How many cherry shrimp should I start with?", "a": "Start with about 10 for a good mix of males and females, which helps a colony get going quickly."}, {"q": "Can cherry shrimp live with bettas?", "a": "Sometimes, in a heavily planted tank, but many bettas hunt shrimp. Expect some losses, especially of babies."}, {"q": "Why are my cherry shrimp dying?", "a": "The usual causes are an uncycled or unstable tank, copper or other chemicals, overfeeding, or big swings during water changes."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$copper-sensitivity$g$, $g$Copper Sensitivity$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Copper sensitivity is the way shrimp, snails and other invertebrates are poisoned by copper, even at trace levels that fish tolerate without trouble.$g$, $g$Copper hides in many fish medications, some plant fertilizers, old plumbing and certain decorations, so it is one of the easiest ways to wipe out a shrimp colony by accident. Knowing where it comes from lets you keep invertebrates and still treat sick fish safely.$g$, $g$Copper and Shrimp: Why It Kills Inverts and How to Avoid It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why copper is so dangerous", "text": "Shrimp and snails use copper-based blood (hemocyanin) and absorb dissolved metals through their gills and skin. Levels as low as 0.03 to 0.1 ppm can kill sensitive dwarf shrimp, while many fish medications run at 0.15 to 0.25 ppm. Losses can be sudden, or they can show up slowly as failed molts, lethargy and snails that stay sealed in their shells.\n\nFish are not fully immune either. Copper is a deliberate poison used at a dose fish barely tolerate, which is why it is measured and used in bare quarantine tanks."}, {"heading": "Where copper sneaks in", "text": "- Fish medications for ich, velvet and other parasites (check the label for copper sulfate or chelated copper)\n- Some liquid plant fertilizers, though most list copper in tiny amounts considered shrimp safe\n- Water that sits in copper pipes, especially the first draw in the morning\n- Painted or metal decorations and coins dropped into a tank\n- Substrate, rock or equipment that previously held a copper treatment"}, {"heading": "How to protect invertebrates", "text": "Read every ingredient list before dosing an invert tank. Treat sick fish in a separate hospital tank whenever you can. Run the tap for 30 seconds to a minute before filling buckets if your home has copper plumbing.\n\nIf copper has already been used, remove it with fresh activated carbon or a metal-absorbing resin and do several large water changes. Silicone, gravel and decor can slowly release copper back into the water, so many keepers never move gear from a copper-treated tank into a shrimp tank."}, {"heading": "Signs of copper poisoning", "text": "Shrimp may swim erratically, flip on their backs, lose color or die in large numbers within a day. Snails often retract and stop moving. A home copper test kit can confirm the problem, and the fix is the same: stop the source, run carbon and change water."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is copper safe for shrimp in any amount?", "a": "Trace amounts in shrimp-safe fertilizers and tap water are usually tolerated, but any copper-based medication should be treated as lethal to shrimp and snails."}, {"q": "How do I remove copper from an aquarium?", "a": "Do large water changes and run fresh activated carbon or a copper-absorbing resin for at least a week. Retest with a copper kit before adding invertebrates."}, {"q": "Does copper hurt aquarium plants?", "a": "Plants need a little copper as a micronutrient, but medication-level doses can damage or melt sensitive species, so remove plants you care about before treating."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$invertebrate$g$, $g$Invertebrate$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$An invertebrate is any animal without a backbone; in freshwater aquariums that mainly means shrimp, snails, crayfish, crabs and small clams.$g$, $g$Inverts graze algae, clean up leftovers and add color and activity, but they play by different rules than fish. They need stable water, minerals for their shells and exoskeletons, and protection from copper and many fish medications. Treat them as their own group when planning a tank.$g$, $g$Freshwater Aquarium Invertebrates: Types and Basic Care$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common freshwater inverts", "text": "- Dwarf shrimp: cherry, amano, crystal and ghost shrimp\n- Snails: nerite, mystery, ramshorn, Malaysian trumpet and rabbit snails\n- Crayfish and dwarf crayfish, which are messy and often eat plants and slow fish\n- Freshwater crabs, many of which need land areas and escape-proof lids\n- Clams and mussels, which filter feed and are hard to keep alive long term"}, {"heading": "Water and diet needs", "text": "Most inverts want stable temperature and parameters more than any exact number. Shrimp and snails need some hardness to build shells and molt, so a GH of 6 to 8 degrees and KH of 2 to 6 suits many common species, while crystal shrimp prefer softer water. Ammonia and nitrite must be at zero, and nitrate ideally under 20 ppm.\n\nThey graze biofilm and algae, but most also need supplemental foods like blanched vegetables or sinking invert foods with calcium."}, {"heading": "Tankmates and safety", "text": "Many fish see small shrimp as food, so pair them with tiny, peaceful species or give them dense plants and moss. Never use copper-based medications around inverts, and check fertilizers and treatments for warnings. Acclimate slowly, often by drip over an hour, since sudden changes can cause failed molts."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Adding shrimp to an uncycled tank, keeping them with larger fish, and making big sudden water changes with very different water are the main causes of losses. Moderate, regular changes with matched temperature work best."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What invertebrates can live with fish?", "a": "Nerite and mystery snails and amano shrimp do well with most peaceful community fish. Cherry shrimp do best with small, peaceful tankmates."}, {"q": "Do aquarium invertebrates need a heater?", "a": "Many shrimp and snails are fine at room temperature from about 68 to 78 F, but a heater keeps things stable, which they value more than any exact number."}, {"q": "Why do my shrimp and snails keep dying?", "a": "The usual causes are an uncycled tank, copper exposure, soft water lacking minerals, or sudden swings during water changes."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$molting$g$, $g$Molting$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Molting is the process where shrimp, crayfish and crabs shed their hard outer shell so they can grow, then harden a new, larger one.$g$, $g$Each molt is a risky moment: the animal is soft and defenseless for hours, and a failed molt is a common cause of shrimp deaths. Stable water with the right mineral content and good hiding spots make molts go smoothly.$g$, $g$Shrimp Molting: Normal Signs, Failed Molts and How to Help$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What a normal molt looks like", "text": "Young shrimp molt every week or two, adults roughly every 3 to 6 weeks. The shell splits between the head and tail and the shrimp backs out, leaving a whole clear ghost of itself behind. The molt is not a dead shrimp; a dead one is usually pink, white or opaque and does not move.\n\nLeave the old shell in the tank. Shrimp eat it to recycle calcium and minerals."}, {"heading": "Failed molts", "text": "A shrimp stuck partway out with a white ring around its middle, often called the white ring of death, is having trouble. Common causes:\n\n- GH too low, so there are not enough minerals to build a new shell\n- GH too high or sudden swings, which can make the shell too hard to split\n- Big water changes with very different water\n- Poor diet without calcium and iodine\n- Copper or other toxins in the water"}, {"heading": "How to support healthy molting", "text": "Keep GH around 6 to 8 degrees for most neocaridina and amano shrimp, or 4 to 6 for many caridina. Test tap water and match it closely during changes. Do smaller water changes of 10 to 20 percent weekly rather than big infrequent ones. Feed a varied diet with mineral-rich foods, and provide moss, plants and caves so freshly molted shrimp can hide."}, {"heading": "Molting and breeding", "text": "Female shrimp molt just before mating and release scent that sends males racing around the tank. Berried females carry eggs until they hatch, and a new molt soon after hatching is normal."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is my shrimp dead or did it molt?", "a": "A molt is a clear, empty shell. A dead shrimp has flesh inside, often turns pink or white, and does not move."}, {"q": "Should I remove shrimp molts from the tank?", "a": "No. Shrimp eat old shells to reclaim minerals. Only remove them if they sit untouched for several days."}, {"q": "Why are my shrimp dying while molting?", "a": "Failed molts usually come from mineral problems, either too low or swinging GH, or from large sudden water changes."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$mystery-snail$g$, $g$Mystery Snail$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$The mystery snail is a large, peaceful apple snail sold in colors like gold, blue, ivory and black, popular as a scavenger in community tanks.$g$, $g$Mystery snails clean up leftover food and soft algae, rarely bother healthy plants and are fun to watch. They do need hard water for their shells, a tight lid since they climb, and a plan for egg clutches they lay above the waterline.$g$, $g$Mystery Snail Care: Tank Size, Food, Eggs and Lifespan$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Basic care", "text": "Mystery snails grow to about 2 inches. One or two do well in a 10 gallon or larger tank. Keep the water at about 68 to 82 F, pH 7 to 8, and GH of 8 or more so the shell stays strong. They breathe air through a siphon tube, so leave an inch or two of space between the water and the lid, and keep the lid tight because they explore."}, {"heading": "Feeding", "text": "They eat leftover flakes and pellets, but in a clean tank they need their own food. Offer sinking pellets, algae wafers and blanched vegetables like zucchini, spinach or cucumber a few times a week. Calcium sources such as cuttlebone or calcium-rich foods help shell growth. A starving mystery snail may start nibbling soft plants."}, {"heading": "Eggs and breeding", "text": "Mystery snails have separate sexes. Females lay a pink, firm clutch of eggs above the waterline, often on the lid. Each one can hold 100 or more eggs that hatch in about 2 to 4 weeks. To prevent babies, remove the clutch and freeze it before throwing it away. To hatch them, leave the clutch in a humid spot above the water."}, {"heading": "Health signs and mistakes", "text": "- A snail floating for a day can be normal; check with a sniff test, since a dead snail smells strongly\n- Pitted or cracked shells point to soft water or low calcium\n- Avoid fin-nipping fish and puffers that pick at their tentacles\n- Never dose copper medications with snails in the tank\n\nThey live about 1 to 2 years."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do mystery snails eat plants?", "a": "Rarely when well fed. They prefer decaying leaves and algae but may eat soft plants if hungry."}, {"q": "How do I know if my mystery snail is dead?", "a": "A dead snail hangs limply out of the shell or the shell is empty, and it smells very strongly of rot."}, {"q": "How long do mystery snails live?", "a": "Usually 1 to 2 years, with cooler water often meaning a slower pace and longer life."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$nerite-snail$g$, $g$Nerite Snail$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$The nerite snail is a small algae-eating snail that lays eggs which cannot hatch in freshwater, so it will not overrun an aquarium.$g$, $g$Nerites are among the best algae grazers in the hobby, cleaning glass, rocks and leaves without eating healthy plants. Their only real drawbacks are hard white egg dots on decor and their habit of climbing out of tanks without lids.$g$, $g$Nerite Snail Care: Algae Eating, Eggs and Tank Setup$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why keepers love them", "text": "Nerites eat green spot algae, film algae, diatoms and soft green algae that many other cleaners ignore. They don't breed in freshwater because their larvae need brackish water, so a few snails stay a few snails. Popular types include zebra, tiger, olive, horned and racing nerites, most reaching 0.5 to 1 inch."}, {"heading": "Care requirements", "text": "- Temperature 72 to 80 F\n- pH 7 to 8 with moderate hardness; soft acidic water erodes their shells\n- One snail per 5 gallons is a good starting point\n- A lid, since nerites often crawl above the waterline and out\n- Supplemental algae wafers or blanched vegetables once algae runs low\n\nAdd them to a mature tank with some algae, not a brand new one."}, {"heading": "About the eggs", "text": "Females lay small white sesame-seed eggs on hardscape, glass and wood. The eggs don't hatch in freshwater but are hard to scrape off. A razor blade on glass works, and rocks can be scrubbed. Keeping only one or two snails or choosing a species that lays fewer eggs reduces the problem."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Nerites often flip over and can't right themselves, so turn them back over if you see one stuck. A snail that doesn't move for days may still be alive; check whether the trapdoor (operculum) is closed tightly before assuming it is dead. Copper medications will kill them. Most live 1 to 2 years."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do nerite snails reproduce in freshwater?", "a": "They lay eggs, but the eggs won't hatch without brackish water, so the population won't grow."}, {"q": "How many nerite snails should I get?", "a": "About one per 5 gallons, adjusted to how much algae is available."}, {"q": "Why is my nerite snail not moving?", "a": "Nerites often rest for a day or two. If it smells or the body hangs out of the shell, it has died."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$pest-snail$g$, $g$Pest Snail$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$A pest snail is any small snail, like bladder, ramshorn or Malaysian trumpet snails, that arrives uninvited and multiplies quickly in an aquarium.$g$, $g$Most pest snails are harmless and even useful cleaners, but they breed to match the food supply. A sudden snail boom is really a feeding problem, so the lasting fix is feeding less, not just removing snails. They also make good food for snail-eating fish.$g$, $g$Pest Snails in Aquarium: How They Get In and How to Control Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How they get in", "text": "Pest snails nearly always arrive as tiny clear egg clutches or babies on live plants. They can also come in with decor, gravel or water from another tank. A single snail can start a colony because bladder snails and many ramshorns don't need a partner. Tissue-culture plants are the safest way to avoid them."}, {"heading": "Harmless or harmful", "text": "They eat leftover food, algae, biofilm and dead plant matter, and they rarely damage healthy plants. The downsides are looks, clogged filter intakes and the extra waste of a large population. A tank swarming with snails also tells you there is too much uneaten food rotting somewhere."}, {"heading": "Control methods", "text": "- Feed only what fish finish in about 2 minutes\n- Vacuum substrate and remove dead leaves\n- Place a blanched lettuce or zucchini slice overnight and lift it out full of snails\n- Crush egg clutches on glass when you spot them\n- Add assassin snails, or snail-eating fish like loaches or dwarf puffers if your tank suits them\n\nAvoid chemical snail killers. They often contain copper, harm shrimp and other inverts, and a mass die-off can cause an ammonia spike."}, {"heading": "Prevention", "text": "Quarantine or dip new plants before adding them. A short dip in a diluted bleach or alum solution, followed by a thorough rinse and dechlorination, kills most eggs, though some delicate plants don't tolerate it. Inspect each stem and leaf before planting."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I get rid of pest snails naturally?", "a": "Reduce feeding, trap them with blanched vegetables and add assassin snails. Their numbers drop once food is limited."}, {"q": "Are pest snails bad for my aquarium?", "a": "Usually not. They clean up waste, but a population explosion signals overfeeding."}, {"q": "Will assassin snails kill all pest snails?", "a": "They keep numbers down well over a few months but rarely wipe out every snail. They also can't breed fast enough to become a problem themselves."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$algae-scraper$g$, $g$Algae Scraper$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$An algae scraper is a hand tool, such as a blade, pad or magnetic cleaner, used to remove algae from aquarium glass and acrylic.$g$, $g$Clean glass makes the tank enjoyable to look at, and scraping during water changes keeps algae from building into a stubborn crust. Picking the right type for your tank material matters, because the wrong scraper or a grain of sand can scratch it permanently.$g$, $g$Best Way to Clean Aquarium Glass: Choosing an Algae Scraper$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Types of scrapers", "text": "- Magnetic cleaners: one half inside, one outside, so you clean without wetting your hands; best for light film\n- Blade scrapers: a metal razor or plastic blade on a handle; best for hard green spot algae on glass\n- Pads and sponges: soft pads on a stick or by hand for everyday film\n- Plastic card scrapers: safe for acrylic tanks and tight corners"}, {"heading": "Glass vs acrylic", "text": "Glass tolerates metal razor blades, held at a shallow angle and pushed in one direction. Acrylic scratches easily, so use only plastic blades and pads labeled acrylic safe. Never use a kitchen scouring pad; many contain cleaners or abrasives that are unsafe for fish and can scratch either material."}, {"heading": "How to scrape without scratching", "text": "Start at the top and work down so loosened algae settles where you will siphon it. Stay about half an inch above the substrate, since a single grain of sand trapped under a magnet or blade can leave a long scratch. Rinse the pad often. Avoid the silicone seams with razor blades, because cutting the silicone can weaken a seal. Scrape before a water change so the siphon can pick up the debris."}, {"heading": "When scraping is not enough", "text": "If glass needs scraping more than once or twice a week, look at the cause: too many hours of light, direct sunlight, excess nutrients or overfeeding. Nerite snails and otocinclus help with soft algae between cleanings but won't fix an imbalance."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do you get hard algae off aquarium glass?", "a": "Green spot algae usually needs a razor blade scraper on glass or a plastic blade on acrylic. Pads rarely remove it."}, {"q": "Can you use a razor blade on an aquarium?", "a": "Yes on glass, held at a shallow angle and kept away from silicone seams. Never on acrylic."}, {"q": "How often should I clean algae off my aquarium glass?", "a": "Most tanks need a quick wipe once a week. Needing it more often points to too much light or nutrients."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$bioload$g$, $g$Bioload$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$Bioload is the total amount of waste produced by everything living in an aquarium, from fish and invertebrates to the food you add.$g$, $g$Bioload decides how much ammonia your filter and bacteria have to handle each day. When it outgrows the tank's capacity, ammonia and nitrate climb, fish get stressed and disease follows, so it is the key idea behind stocking, feeding and maintenance.$g$, $g$Aquarium Bioload Explained: How Many Fish Your Tank Can Handle$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What adds to bioload", "text": "Fish waste, uneaten food, decaying plants and dead animals all break down into ammonia. Big-bodied and messy fish like goldfish, plecos, oscars and cichlids produce far more waste than the same length of slim tetras. Feeding is the part you control most directly, since every bit of food that goes in eventually becomes waste."}, {"heading": "What handles bioload", "text": "- Beneficial bacteria in the filter and substrate convert ammonia to nitrite and then nitrate\n- Live plants absorb ammonia and nitrate as nutrients\n- Water changes physically remove nitrate and dissolved waste\n- Water volume dilutes waste, so bigger tanks are more forgiving\n\nA tank is in balance when ammonia and nitrite stay at 0 ppm and nitrate rises slowly enough that your normal water changes keep it under about 20 to 40 ppm."}, {"heading": "Signs the bioload is too high", "text": "Ammonia or nitrite readings above zero in a cycled tank, nitrate that climbs past 40 ppm within a week, cloudy water, algae blooms and fish gasping at the surface are common signals. Low oxygen often comes with high bioload because waste breakdown uses oxygen too."}, {"heading": "How to manage it", "text": "Add new fish slowly, a few at a time, so bacteria can catch up. Feed sparingly. Upgrade filtration or increase water changes for heavy-waste species. Research adult size and waste output before buying, not just the size at the store. When unsure, stock lighter than you think you can."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I know if my tank's bioload is too high?", "a": "Test for ammonia, nitrite and nitrate. Any ammonia or nitrite, or nitrate rising fast between changes, means the tank is over its capacity."}, {"q": "Do snails and shrimp add to bioload?", "a": "Yes, but very little. Snails in large numbers can add up, and large snails like mystery snails produce noticeable waste."}, {"q": "Does a bigger filter let me keep more fish?", "a": "Partly. More filtration handles more ammonia, but space, oxygen, behavior and nitrate buildup still limit how many fish a tank can hold."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$evaporation$g$, $g$Evaporation$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$Evaporation is the loss of aquarium water to the air as vapor, leaving dissolved minerals and salts behind so they slowly become more concentrated.$g$, $g$A tank can lose a gallon or more a week to evaporation, and if you only ever top off, hardness and dissolved solids creep up over time. Understanding it explains why topping off and water changes are two separate jobs.$g$, $g$Aquarium Water Evaporation: Why It Happens and How to Reduce It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How fast tanks evaporate", "text": "Open-top tanks, warm water, strong surface agitation, dry indoor air and fans or air conditioning all speed evaporation. A typical covered 29 gallon might lose a quarter to half a gallon a week, while an open-top tank at 80 F in a dry home can lose several gallons. Only pure water evaporates; minerals, salts and pollutants stay behind."}, {"heading": "Why it matters", "text": "As the water level drops, everything left behind becomes more concentrated. GH, KH and total dissolved solids rise, which is a real concern for soft-water fish and sensitive shrimp. Low water can also make hang-on-back filters gurgle or run dry, and expose heaters, which can crack if they run out of water."}, {"heading": "How to reduce evaporation", "text": "- Use a lid or glass cover; this is the single biggest fix\n- Lower the room temperature or the tank temperature if fish allow\n- Keep fans and vents from blowing across the surface\n- Avoid extra surface splashing beyond what fish need for oxygen\n\nDon't eliminate surface movement entirely, since gas exchange keeps oxygen levels healthy."}, {"heading": "Handling evaporation the right way", "text": "Top off with dechlorinated fresh water, or with pure RO water in a soft-water or shrimp tank, to restore the level without adding more minerals. Keep up regular partial water changes as well, since topping off alone never removes nitrate or excess minerals. In brackish tanks, never add salt when topping off, because the salt did not evaporate."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is it normal for my fish tank water to evaporate?", "a": "Yes. All tanks lose water, especially open-top or warm tanks. Losing a few percent per week is normal."}, {"q": "Should I top off evaporated water or do a water change?", "a": "Both. Top off to keep the level up between changes, and still do regular partial water changes to remove waste."}, {"q": "Why does my aquarium have white crust at the waterline?", "a": "That is mineral residue left by evaporation. Lower the water slightly and wipe it off with a cloth dampened with plain white vinegar, keeping vinegar out of the tank, then wipe with clean water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$mature-tank$g$, $g$Mature Tank$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$A mature tank is an established aquarium, usually at least six months old, with a large, stable bacteria colony and settled water chemistry.$g$, $g$Mature tanks handle feeding, new fish and small mistakes far better than new ones, which is why many sensitive species are recommended for them only. Their filter media is also the fastest way to cycle a new tank. Patience is the main ingredient.$g$, $g$Mature Aquarium vs New Tank: What Makes a Tank Established$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What maturity looks like", "text": "Cycling takes about 4 to 8 weeks, but a tank is fully mature only after several months. By then bacteria colonize every surface, biofilm coats glass and decor, plants are rooted and the tank handles daily waste with ammonia and nitrite always at 0 ppm. Numbers stay steady between water changes and pH no longer drifts much."}, {"heading": "Why it matters", "text": "- Resists ammonia spikes when you add fish or overfeed slightly\n- Biofilm and microfauna provide constant grazing for shrimp, otocinclus and fry\n- Less algae trouble once plants and nutrients settle\n- Better survival for sensitive species like crystal shrimp, discus and many wild-caught fish\n\nThat is why stores and breeders often say an animal needs a mature tank."}, {"heading": "Seeding new tanks from a mature one", "text": "Moving a used filter sponge, some media or a handful of substrate from a healthy mature tank into a new one carries a big dose of beneficial bacteria. A new tank can be cycled in days rather than weeks this way. Only seed from tanks without disease outbreaks, and still test water closely for the first few weeks."}, {"heading": "Keeping a tank mature", "text": "Don't replace all filter media at once or rinse it in chlorinated tap water. Avoid deep cleaning everything on the same day. Keep up steady water changes so old-tank syndrome, where nitrate builds and pH slowly falls, does not creep in over the years. Replace only part of the media at a time when it truly wears out."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does it take for a tank to mature?", "a": "Cycling takes about 4 to 8 weeks, but most keepers consider a tank mature after 6 months or more of stable operation."}, {"q": "Can I speed up tank maturity?", "a": "Seeding with media from an established tank helps the bacteria side quickly, though biofilm and plant balance still take time."}, {"q": "Why do shrimp do better in mature tanks?", "a": "Mature tanks have stable chemistry and lots of biofilm, which young shrimp graze on constantly."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$mulm$g$, $g$Mulm$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$Mulm is the soft brown sludge of fish waste, decayed plant matter and bacteria that settles in substrate, filters and quiet corners of a tank.$g$, $g$A little mulm is normal and even useful: it feeds plant roots and houses beneficial bacteria. Too much traps waste, raises nitrate and can create low-oxygen pockets, so the goal is to manage it rather than scrub it away entirely.$g$, $g$What Is Mulm in an Aquarium? When to Leave It and When to Clean$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Where it collects", "text": "Mulm builds up under decor, in plant thickets, in the corners behind filters, deep in gravel and inside filter sponges and canisters. It looks like fine brown dust that puffs up when disturbed. Flow patterns decide where it lands; dead spots with little water movement collect the most."}, {"heading": "Good mulm vs too much", "text": "In planted tanks, a thin layer around roots acts as a natural fertilizer and is often left alone. Shrimp and snails pick through it for food. Problems start when it gets thick:\n\n- Nitrate and phosphate rise, feeding algae\n- Clogged filters lose flow and oxygen\n- Deep layers in gravel can go anaerobic and smell like rotten eggs when stirred\n- A big cloud released all at once can cloud water and stress fish"}, {"heading": "How to manage it", "text": "Vacuum one section of open substrate at each water change rather than the whole bed. Hover the siphon just above sand and push it into gravel. Rinse filter sponges in removed tank water when flow drops, never under tap water. Boost flow in dead spots with better filter placement. In heavily planted areas, gently siphon only the surface layer."}, {"heading": "Using mulm", "text": "Many keepers squeeze filter sponge water onto new tanks or use it to seed new plants, since it is full of bacteria and nutrients. Only reuse it from healthy tanks. Never move mulm from a tank with sick fish."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is mulm bad for an aquarium?", "a": "Not in small amounts. It feeds plants and bacteria, but large buildups raise nitrate and reduce oxygen."}, {"q": "Should I vacuum mulm in a planted tank?", "a": "Lightly. Siphon open areas and the surface, but leave root zones mostly undisturbed."}, {"q": "How do I stop mulm buildup?", "a": "Feed less, improve flow in dead spots and vacuum a different section of substrate at each water change."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$overstocking$g$, $g$Overstocking$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$Overstocking is keeping more fish or invertebrates than an aquarium's size, filtration and maintenance routine can support in good health.$g$, $g$Overstocked tanks run on the edge: waste builds quickly, oxygen runs low, fish fight over space and disease spreads easily. It is one of the most common reasons beginners face constant algae, cloudy water and unexplained losses. Fixing it early saves fish and frustration.$g$, $g$Overstocked Fish Tank: Signs, Risks and How to Fix It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs a tank is overstocked", "text": "- Ammonia or nitrite readings in a tank that has been cycled\n- Nitrate climbing past 40 ppm within a week\n- Fish gasping at the surface, especially in the morning\n- Constant aggression, torn fins or fish hiding all the time\n- Frequent disease outbreaks and cloudy water\n- Algae that won't stay under control"}, {"heading": "Why it happens", "text": "Most overstocking comes from buying fish at juvenile size without checking adult size, adding schooling fish in big groups to small tanks, or slowly accumulating extra fish one at a time. Some species, like common plecos, goldfish and oscars, grow far larger and messier than people expect. Space and territory also count: a tank can have clean water and still be overcrowded for territorial fish."}, {"heading": "How to fix it", "text": "Rehome some fish through a local fish store, club or hobbyist. Until then, increase water changes, for example 30 to 50 percent twice a week, and test often. Add filtration rated well above your tank size, increase surface agitation for oxygen, and feed lightly. Upgrading to a larger tank is the most lasting fix if you want to keep everyone."}, {"heading": "Planning to avoid it", "text": "Check each species' adult size, minimum tank size, swimming level and temperament before buying. Use the inch-per-gallon rule only as a loose sanity check for small, slim fish; it fails badly for large or bulky species. Leave room for growth."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How many fish is too many for my tank?", "a": "It depends on adult size, waste output and behavior, not just tank volume. Research each species and watch your water tests."}, {"q": "Can a bigger filter fix overstocking?", "a": "It helps with ammonia but not with space, aggression, oxygen or nitrate buildup. Fewer fish or a bigger tank is the real fix."}, {"q": "What happens if you overstock a fish tank?", "a": "Water quality falls, fish get stressed and sick, and losses become more likely over time."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$stocking$g$, $g$Stocking$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$Stocking is the choice of which species, and how many of each, to keep in an aquarium based on its size, filtration and the fish's needs.$g$, $g$A good stocking plan makes everything else easier: clean water, peaceful tankmates and fewer losses. Most beginner problems trace back to stocking, whether it is too many fish, the wrong mix or fish that outgrow the tank. Plan before you shop.$g$, $g$How to Stock a Fish Tank: Choosing How Many and Which Fish$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What to consider", "text": "- Adult size, not store size\n- Minimum tank length for active swimmers\n- Water needs: temperature, pH and hardness that all species share\n- Temperament: peaceful, semi-aggressive, fin-nippers or territorial\n- Swimming level: top, middle or bottom, to use the whole tank\n- Group size: schooling fish usually need 6 or more\n- Waste output: goldfish and large cichlids need far more filtration"}, {"heading": "Why simple rules fall short", "text": "The old one inch of fish per gallon rule is a rough guide at best. It treats a 6 inch oscar like six neon tetras, which is badly wrong. Body mass, activity level and territory matter more than length. Use it only as a sanity check for small, slim community fish."}, {"heading": "Stocking in order", "text": "Add fish in small groups over weeks, giving bacteria time to catch up. Start with hardy species, often a school of peaceful midwater fish, then add bottom dwellers, and add centerpiece or more territorial fish last so they don't claim the whole tank early. Test ammonia and nitrite a few days after each addition."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mixing fish that need very different temperatures, buying single schooling fish, choosing fish based only on color, and putting fin-nippers like tiger barbs with long-finned fish like bettas are the classic errors. Ask about each fish's adult needs before buying. Then buy from a store or breeder that quarantines its stock, and quarantine new fish yourself for 2 to 4 weeks when you can."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How many fish can I put in a 20 gallon tank?", "a": "Often 10 to 15 small fish like tetras or rasboras, plus a few bottom dwellers, depending on species and filtration."}, {"q": "How many fish should I add at once?", "a": "Add 3 to 6 small fish at a time in a cycled tank, then wait a week or two before adding more."}, {"q": "Is the inch per gallon rule accurate?", "a": "Only for small, slim fish. It underestimates the needs of large, bulky or messy species."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$top-off$g$, $g$Top-Off$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$A top-off is adding fresh water to an aquarium to replace water lost to evaporation, bringing it back to its normal level.$g$, $g$Topping off keeps filters, heaters and parameters working as intended between water changes. Because only pure water evaporates, the right top-off water keeps hardness and salinity steady, while the wrong habit can slowly drift your water away from what your fish need.$g$, $g$How to Top Off an Aquarium: Tap Water, RO or Auto Top-Off$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Choosing the right water", "text": "- Dechlorinated tap water works for most community tanks\n- In soft-water or shrimp tanks, pure RO or distilled water avoids slowly building hardness, since minerals did not evaporate\n- Never add salt in a brackish or salted tank during a top-off; salt stays behind, so adding more raises salinity\n- Match the temperature roughly when adding more than a few percent of the tank volume"}, {"heading": "How to do it", "text": "Mark the normal water line with a small piece of tape. Pour new water slowly into a cup or onto a decoration so you don't disturb substrate. Treat tap water with dechlorinator before or as you add it. Top off every few days in fast-evaporating tanks so the level never drops much."}, {"heading": "Auto top-off systems", "text": "An auto top-off uses a float or optical sensor and a small pump to refill evaporated water from a reservoir. It keeps levels and parameters very stable, especially on open-top tanks and sumps. Use one with a backup sensor or limited reservoir so a stuck switch can't flood the tank. Check it regularly."}, {"heading": "Top-off is not a water change", "text": "Topping off only replaces water; it removes nothing. Nitrate, dissolved waste and minerals keep building unless you also do partial water changes, typically 20 to 30 percent weekly. Skipping changes and only topping off is a common path to old-tank syndrome. Test nitrate before a change to see how fast it builds."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can I top off my aquarium with tap water?", "a": "Yes, if you add dechlorinator. In soft-water or shrimp tanks, RO or distilled water is often better."}, {"q": "How often should I top off my fish tank?", "a": "Whenever the level drops noticeably, usually every few days to once a week depending on evaporation."}, {"q": "Do I need to add salt when topping off?", "a": "No. Salt does not evaporate, so topping off with salt water would raise salinity."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$understocking$g$, $g$Understocking$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$Understocking is keeping noticeably fewer fish than a tank could support, leaving extra room in water quality, space and filtration capacity.$g$, $g$A lightly stocked tank is more forgiving of missed water changes, overfeeding and equipment hiccups. Many experienced keepers deliberately understock, because fish show better color and behavior when they are not competing for space. It trades a fuller look for a calmer, healthier tank that is easier to care for.$g$, $g$Understocking a Fish Tank: Benefits and When It Goes Too Far$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Benefits", "text": "- Ammonia and nitrate stay lower and change slowly, giving you a margin for mistakes\n- Less aggression, since fish have room for territories\n- More oxygen per fish and less disease pressure\n- Easier maintenance and fewer algae problems\n- Room to add fish later without upgrading equipment"}, {"heading": "When understocking backfires", "text": "Schooling fish need a group, usually 6 or more, to feel secure; two or three tetras alone in a big tank often hide and fade. Some cichlids are actually less aggressive in slightly crowded setups where no single fish can dominate, so understocking is not always calmer for every species. A very bare tank can also feel exposed to shy fish without plants or cover."}, {"heading": "Planning a lightly stocked tank", "text": "Pick one or two good-sized schools of small fish, a group of bottom dwellers, and maybe a single centerpiece. Fill the space with plants and hardscape instead of more fish. Plants also use waste as fertilizer, making a lightly stocked planted tank one of the most stable setups you can run. Add fish slowly and watch how they use the space before deciding to add more. Space them out over weeks."}, {"heading": "Understocking and cycling", "text": "A very lightly stocked tank grows only a small bacteria colony. If you later add many fish at once, it can still have an ammonia spike, so build up gradually. Test ammonia a few days after any large addition."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is it better to understock a fish tank?", "a": "For most community tanks, yes. Water quality is more stable and fish are less stressed."}, {"q": "Can a tank be too understocked?", "a": "Yes, if schooling fish are kept in tiny groups or shy fish lack cover. Group size matters more than total numbers."}, {"q": "Does understocking reduce algae?", "a": "Usually, since less waste means fewer nutrients, though lighting still plays the biggest role."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$water-change$g$, $g$Water Change$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$A water change, usually a partial water change, is removing part of the aquarium's water and replacing it with clean, dechlorinated water.$g$, $g$It is the most important routine task in fishkeeping. Changing a portion of the water removes nitrate and dissolved waste and restores minerals, while leaving enough old water that conditions stay stable for fish. No filter or additive replaces it.$g$, $g$How to Do a Partial Water Change: How Much and How Often$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How much and how often", "text": "Most tanks do well with a partial water change of 20 to 30 percent once a week. Heavily stocked tanks or messy fish may need 40 to 50 percent weekly, or two changes a week. Lightly stocked planted tanks can sometimes stretch to every two weeks. Let your nitrate readings guide you: aim to keep nitrate under about 20 to 40 ppm."}, {"heading": "Step by step", "text": "- Unplug heaters and filters if the water level will drop below them\n- Siphon water out with a gravel vacuum, cleaning a section of substrate as you go\n- Wipe glass and rinse filter sponges in the removed tank water if needed\n- Prepare new water at about the same temperature, within 2 F\n- Add dechlorinator for the full tank volume if you use chloramine-treated tap water and fill straight from the tap\n- Refill slowly, then plug equipment back in"}, {"heading": "Why partial and not all", "text": "Changing all the water at once swings temperature, pH and hardness, which shocks fish and can trigger failed molts in shrimp. A partial change keeps those swings small. Fish in tanks that have gone months without changes are adjusted to poor water, so restore them with several smaller changes, about 10 to 20 percent every day or two, rather than one huge one."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Forgetting dechlorinator, refilling with water much colder or warmer than the tank, rinsing filter media under tap water, and replacing filter cartridges at the same time as a big change all cause problems. Keep a dedicated bucket that never touches soap."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How often should I change my aquarium water?", "a": "Weekly is the standard, usually 20 to 30 percent, adjusted based on nitrate readings and stocking."}, {"q": "Can I do a 100 percent water change?", "a": "Avoid it with fish in the tank. Sudden changes in temperature and chemistry can shock or kill them; only do it in emergencies like a poisoning."}, {"q": "Do I need to remove fish during a water change?", "a": "No. Leave fish in place and work slowly; moving them causes more stress than the change."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$ammonia-spike$g$, $g$Ammonia Spike$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$An ammonia spike is a sudden rise in ammonia in aquarium water, usually during cycling or after something overwhelms or damages the beneficial bacteria.$g$, $g$Ammonia burns fish gills and skin and can kill within days, even at levels well under 1 ppm in alkaline water. Catching a spike early with a test kit and acting fast with water changes is often the difference between minor stress and losing fish.$g$, $g$Ammonia Spike in Fish Tank: Signs, Causes and Quick Fixes$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common causes", "text": "- New tank that is not fully cycled\n- Adding too many fish at once\n- Overfeeding or uneaten food rotting\n- A dead fish, snail or large amount of decaying plant matter\n- Cleaning or replacing all filter media at once, or rinsing it in chlorinated tap water\n- Power outage that stops the filter for hours\n- Medications that harm beneficial bacteria"}, {"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "Fish gasp at the surface, breathe rapidly, sit near the bottom with clamped fins or show red or inflamed gills. Water may turn cloudy or smell off. A test reading anywhere above 0 ppm in an established tank is a warning. Ammonia is more toxic at higher pH and temperature, so 0.5 ppm at pH 8 is much more dangerous than the same reading at pH 6.5."}, {"heading": "What to do right away", "text": "Do a 30 to 50 percent water change with temperature-matched, dechlorinated water, and repeat daily until ammonia reads 0.25 ppm or lower. Use a water conditioner that binds ammonia to make it less toxic for about 24 hours. Stop feeding for a day or two. Find and remove the source, whether a dead animal or rotting food. Add aeration, since stressed fish need oxygen."}, {"heading": "Preventing future spikes", "text": "Cycle tanks fully before adding fish, add new fish gradually, feed lightly and clean filter media in old tank water only. Test weekly, and always test after adding fish or after any disruption."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does an ammonia spike last?", "a": "In a cycled tank with the cause removed, a few days. In a new tank still cycling, it can last weeks, so daily water changes may be needed."}, {"q": "What level of ammonia is dangerous for fish?", "a": "Anything above 0 ppm causes stress over time. Levels of 0.5 ppm and up are an emergency, especially at higher pH."}, {"q": "Do water changes stop an ammonia spike?", "a": "They dilute it immediately. A 50 percent change cuts ammonia roughly in half, but you still need to fix the cause."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$biological-filtration$g$, $g$Biological Filtration$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Biological filtration is the work done by beneficial bacteria living on filter media and tank surfaces, turning toxic ammonia and nitrite into far less harmful nitrate.$g$, $g$It is the most important kind of filtration because ammonia and nitrite are invisible and can kill fish at low levels. A tank with healthy biological filtration can handle daily waste safely, while one without it stays dangerous no matter how clear the water looks.$g$, $g$Biological Filtration in Aquariums: How It Works$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Where the bacteria live", "text": "The bacteria that do this work cling to surfaces, not the water. Most of them live in the filter, where oxygen-rich water flows past constantly, but gravel, rocks, wood and plant leaves hold colonies too.\n\nThat is why bio media like ceramic rings, sponge and porous stone is built with so much surface area. More surface means more room for bacteria and a bigger safety margin when you add fish or feed a little heavy. Sponge filters and canister filters packed with ceramic media are both strong choices for bacteria."}, {"heading": "What keeps it healthy", "text": "- Steady flow: bacteria need oxygen, so a filter that stops for more than a few hours starts losing them.\n- A food source: they eat ammonia, so an empty tank slowly loses its colony.\n- Gentle cleaning: rinse sponges and media in removed tank water, not tap water, because chlorine and chloramine kill bacteria.\n- Stable conditions: a pH that crashes below about 6.0 slows the bacteria down a lot."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "The biggest one is cleaning too well. Scrubbing or replacing all the media at once throws away the colony and triggers ammonia spikes.\n\nOther problems include running the filter on a timer, leaving the filter off after a power outage without checking it, and dosing some antibiotics in the main tank, which can harm the colony. If you must replace media, swap no more than about a third at a time and wait a few weeks before the next swap."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does it take to establish biological filtration?", "a": "In a new tank it usually takes 4 to 8 weeks. Seeding with media from a healthy tank can cut that to days."}, {"q": "Do I need special bio media?", "a": "Not strictly. A plain sponge holds plenty of bacteria for most tanks, though ceramic or porous media adds extra capacity in a small space."}, {"q": "Does a water change remove the good bacteria?", "a": "No. Very few of the bacteria float in the water, so normal water changes leave the colony in place as long as you dechlorinate the new water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$chemical-filtration$g$, $g$Chemical Filtration$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Chemical filtration uses media such as activated carbon or resins to pull dissolved substances like tannins, odors, leftover medication and some pollutants out of aquarium water.$g$, $g$It is the optional third type of filtration, after mechanical and biological. Used well, it polishes water, clears tea-colored tint and removes medication after treatment, but it does not replace water changes and can work against you if you forget it is there.$g$, $g$Chemical Filtration: Activated Carbon and When to Use It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common types", "text": "- Activated carbon: removes tannins, odors and many medications. The most common choice.\n- Ion exchange resins: pull out organics or nitrate, depending on the resin. Some can be recharged.\n- Phosphate removers: help in tanks with stubborn phosphate and algae.\n- Zeolite: absorbs ammonia for a short time, useful in emergencies but it fills up fast."}, {"heading": "When it helps", "text": "Run carbon after a course of medication to strip what is left, after you add new driftwood if you dislike the brown tint, or if the tank smells off even with good maintenance.\n\nMany healthy community tanks run without any chemical media at all. Regular water changes of 25 to 50 percent a week do most of the same job by diluting what builds up."}, {"heading": "How to use it properly", "text": "Place chemical media after the mechanical stage so debris does not clog it. Rinse carbon before use to remove dust.\n\nCarbon stops working once its pores fill, often within 2 to 4 weeks in a busy tank. After that it just becomes extra surface for bacteria, so replace it if you are relying on it for a purpose."}, {"heading": "Mistakes to avoid", "text": "Take carbon out before medicating, or it will absorb the treatment and leave your fish unprotected. In planted tanks, carbon can also remove some liquid fertilizer, so many plant keepers skip it. Never use zeolite in tanks that get aquarium salt, since salt makes it release the ammonia it was holding."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do I need activated carbon in my aquarium filter?", "a": "No. Most freshwater tanks do fine without it as long as you do regular water changes. It is a useful tool, not a requirement."}, {"q": "How often should I change filter carbon?", "a": "Every 2 to 4 weeks if you are using it to remove tannins, odors or medication. Old carbon is harmless but no longer filters chemically."}, {"q": "Does carbon remove ammonia?", "a": "Not in a useful amount. Ammonia is handled by biological filtration, and zeolite is the chemical media that targets it short term."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$cycling$g$, $g$Cycling$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Cycling, also called cycling a tank, is growing enough beneficial bacteria in a new aquarium to convert fish waste safely before fish are added.$g$, $g$It is the single biggest step between setting up a tank and keeping fish alive in it. Skipping it causes new tank syndrome, where ammonia and nitrite build up and poison fish in the first few weeks, and it is the top reason beginner tanks lose fish early.$g$, $g$How to Cycle a Fish Tank: Steps, Timeline and Signs$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What happens during a cycle", "text": "First, an ammonia source starts the process, either bottled ammonia, fish food or the fish themselves. Bacteria that eat ammonia grow and turn it into nitrite, so ammonia falls and nitrite climbs. A second group of bacteria then turns nitrite into nitrate.\n\nThe tank is cycled when it handles a full day's ammonia with 0 ppm ammonia and 0 ppm nitrite showing on a test, and nitrate rising."}, {"heading": "How to cycle a tank", "text": "- Set up the tank with filter and heater running and dechlorinated water.\n- Choose a method: fishless cycling with an ammonia source is safest, fish-in cycling is possible with daily care.\n- Test ammonia, nitrite and nitrate every couple of days with a liquid test kit.\n- Optionally add bottled bacteria or seeded media to speed things up.\n- When readings hit 0, 0 and some nitrate, do a large water change and add fish slowly."}, {"heading": "How long it takes", "text": "Expect 4 to 8 weeks for a fishless cycle from scratch. A warm tank around 78 to 82°F with pH above 7 cycles faster. Seeding from an established tank can cut it to a week or two."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Do not deep clean the filter mid-cycle, and do not stop adding ammonia once the tank is cycled, or the bacteria starve. Very high nitrite can stall the second stage, so a water change helps if nitrite stays off the chart for a week or more. Adding a full stock of fish at once can overwhelm even a cycled tank."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I know when my tank is cycled?", "a": "When it can turn a dose of ammonia into 0 ppm ammonia and 0 ppm nitrite within 24 hours, with nitrate showing on the test."}, {"q": "Can I cycle a tank in 24 hours?", "a": "Not truly from scratch. Bottled bacteria and seeded media can make it very fast, but you should still confirm with tests before adding fish."}, {"q": "Do I need to cycle a tank with live plants?", "a": "Yes, though heavy planting helps by absorbing some ammonia. You still need a bacterial colony, so test before stocking."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$denitrification$g$, $g$Denitrification$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Denitrification is the process where bacteria in low-oxygen areas convert nitrate into nitrogen gas, which bubbles out of the water and leaves the tank.$g$, $g$It is the last step that can truly remove nitrogen from an aquarium instead of just changing its form. In most home freshwater tanks it happens only in small amounts, so water changes and plants still do most of the work of keeping nitrate in check.$g$, $g$Denitrification in Aquariums: Can It Lower Nitrate?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it works", "text": "The bacteria that do this live where oxygen is very low or absent. With no oxygen to breathe, they use the oxygen in nitrate instead and release nitrogen gas. They also need a carbon food source, which comes from fish waste and organic matter or, in dedicated reactors, from added carbon.\n\nThese zones form deep inside porous rock, thick dense media and sand beds more than a few inches deep."}, {"heading": "Where you see it in home tanks", "text": "- Deep sand beds, mostly in saltwater systems.\n- Very porous bio media with slow flow through its core.\n- Dedicated denitrifying reactors and sulfur reactors, which are more common in reef keeping.\n- Thick layers of undisturbed substrate in planted tanks."}, {"heading": "Limits and risks", "text": "In a typical freshwater tank the effect is small and hard to measure. If you see nitrate climbing, the real fixes are feeding less, adding plants, and doing water changes of 25 to 50 percent a week.\n\nDeep anaerobic substrate is debated in freshwater. If it is disturbed, pockets can release hydrogen sulfide, which smells like rotten eggs and is toxic to fish. Burrowing snails like Malaysian trumpet snails help by stirring sand gently."}, {"heading": "When to bother with it", "text": "Large, heavily stocked tanks such as cichlid or goldfish setups sometimes use denitrifying media or reactors to cut water change load. For most community tanks, treat any denitrification as a small bonus, not a plan. Plants remain the more reliable natural way to pull nitrate out between water changes."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does denitrification happen in freshwater aquariums?", "a": "Yes, in small amounts inside porous media, rock and deep substrate. It rarely removes enough nitrate to replace water changes."}, {"q": "What is the difference between nitrification and denitrification?", "a": "Nitrification needs oxygen and turns ammonia into nitrite and then nitrate. Denitrification happens without oxygen and turns nitrate into nitrogen gas."}, {"q": "Is a deep sand bed safe in a freshwater tank?", "a": "It can be, but it is debated. Keep sand to a few inches, or use burrowing snails, to avoid toxic gas pockets."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$filter-media$g$, $g$Filter Media$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Filter media are the materials inside an aquarium filter, such as sponge, floss, ceramic rings and carbon, that trap debris, house bacteria or remove dissolved substances.$g$, $g$The media, not the filter box, does the real work of keeping water clean and safe. Choosing the right mix and cleaning it the right way decides whether your water stays clear and whether your bacteria colony survives each maintenance day.$g$, $g$Types of Filter Media and the Right Order to Use Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Types of media", "text": "- Coarse sponge: mechanical filtration that catches large debris and holds lots of bacteria.\n- Fine floss or pads: polish the water by trapping tiny particles. Clogs fast.\n- Ceramic rings, porous stone, bio balls: biological media with large surface area for bacteria.\n- Activated carbon and resins: chemical media for tannins, odors and medication."}, {"heading": "The right order", "text": "Water should flow from coarse to fine to biological, then chemical. Coarse sponge first keeps fine floss from clogging in a day, and keeping debris off the bio media protects the bacteria living there.\n\nIf your filter came with one all-in-one cartridge, you can often replace it with a sponge and a bag of ceramic media for better results and less waste. In small hang-on-back filters, a coarse sponge plus a pouch of ceramic rings is a simple, effective setup."}, {"heading": "Cleaning and replacing", "text": "Rinse sponges and bio media in a bucket of removed tank water every 2 to 6 weeks, just enough to clear clogs. Tap water's chlorine kills the bacteria.\n\nSponges and ceramic media last for years. Floss is cheap and can be replaced when it is clogged. Never replace everything at once; change no more than a third of the media at a time."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Throwing out the cartridge every month as packaging suggests is the classic one, since it throws away bacteria too. Packing media so tightly that water bypasses it is another. Leaving carbon in while medicating is a third. Finally, using only fine floss in a heavily stocked tank leads to clogging and poor flow within days."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How often should I replace filter media?", "a": "Sponges and ceramic media rarely need replacing, only rinsing. Replace floss when it falls apart or stays clogged, and carbon every 2 to 4 weeks if you use it."}, {"q": "What order should filter media go in?", "a": "Coarse mechanical first, then fine mechanical, then biological, then chemical if you use it."}, {"q": "Can I rinse filter media in tap water?", "a": "Avoid it. Use water removed from the tank so chlorine or chloramine does not kill the beneficial bacteria."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fish-in-cycling$g$, $g$Fish-in Cycling$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Fish-in cycling is establishing a new tank's beneficial bacteria while fish are already living in it, using their waste as the ammonia source.$g$, $g$It often happens by accident when someone buys fish and a tank on the same day. It can be done safely, but it means testing and changing water often for several weeks, because the fish are exposed to ammonia and nitrite the whole time the bacteria are growing.$g$, $g$Fish-in Cycling: How to Keep Fish Safe in a New Tank$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to do it safely", "text": "- Keep the stock light: a few hardy fish, not a full tank.\n- Feed small amounts once a day or every other day.\n- Test ammonia and nitrite daily with a liquid kit.\n- Change 25 to 50 percent of the water whenever ammonia plus nitrite reaches about 0.5 ppm.\n- Use a dechlorinator that also binds ammonia and nitrite temporarily.\n- Keep the temperature steady in the 76 to 80°F range."}, {"heading": "Speeding it up", "text": "Bottled bacteria products can shorten the process. A handful of filter media or a squeezed sponge from a healthy tank works even better. Live plants help too, since they take up ammonia directly.\n\nExpect the cycle to take 4 to 8 weeks. It may feel slower than fishless cycling because frequent water changes keep ammonia low, which is the point. Avoid adding shrimp or snails until the cycle is finished, since invertebrates are very sensitive to ammonia and nitrite."}, {"heading": "Signs your fish are struggling", "text": "Gasping at the surface, red or inflamed gills, lethargy, clamped fins and refusing food all suggest ammonia or nitrite stress. Test right away and do a water change if readings are up."}, {"heading": "When it is done", "text": "The tank is cycled when ammonia and nitrite stay at 0 ppm for about a week without water changes and nitrate is rising. Add new fish a few at a time over the next weeks so the bacteria can keep up. Keep testing weekly after that, since a newly cycled tank can still slip if you overfeed or add too many fish at once."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How many water changes during a fish-in cycle?", "a": "As many as it takes to keep ammonia plus nitrite under about 0.5 ppm. That can mean daily changes early on."}, {"q": "Can I add more fish during a fish-in cycle?", "a": "No. Wait until ammonia and nitrite stay at zero, then add fish gradually."}, {"q": "What fish are best for a fish-in cycle?", "a": "Hardy species that tolerate some stress, like zebra danios, white cloud minnows or platies. Avoid sensitive fish and shrimp."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fishless-cycling$g$, $g$Fishless Cycling$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Fishless cycling is preparing a new aquarium's biological filter by dosing an ammonia source, such as pure ammonia or fish food, before any animals are added.$g$, $g$It lets you grow a strong bacteria colony without exposing any fish to toxic ammonia and nitrite spikes. It is the most humane and controllable way to start a tank, and it lets you stock more fish at once when the cycle finishes.$g$, $g$Fishless Cycling: Step-by-Step Guide With Ammonia$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What you need", "text": "- A running tank with filter and heater, filled with dechlorinated water.\n- An ammonia source: pure ammonia with no soaps or scents, ammonium chloride, or a pinch of fish food left to rot.\n- A liquid test kit for ammonia, nitrite and nitrate.\n- Optional: bottled bacteria or media from a healthy tank."}, {"heading": "Step by step", "text": "Dose ammonia to about 2 ppm and test every day or two. When ammonia starts dropping, nitrite will appear. Redose ammonia to 2 ppm whenever it falls to near zero.\n\nOnce nitrate shows up and nitrite begins falling, keep going. The tank is ready when a 2 ppm dose turns into 0 ppm ammonia and 0 ppm nitrite within 24 hours. Do a large water change to lower nitrate, then add fish. Write down each test result so you can see the pattern and know when nitrite has peaked."}, {"heading": "Tips to go faster", "text": "Raise the temperature to around 80 to 86°F, keep the pH above 7, and run good surface agitation for oxygen. Seeded media can cut weeks off the process. Without help, expect 4 to 8 weeks."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Overdosing ammonia far above 4 ppm can stall the cycle. If nitrite stays sky high for more than a week, do a water change to bring it down. Keep dosing ammonia right up until fish go in, or the colony will start to shrink. Also check that your ammonia source contains nothing but ammonia and water; if it foams when shaken, it has surfactants and should not be used."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How much ammonia for fishless cycling?", "a": "Dose to about 2 ppm and redose when it drops near zero. Higher doses are not faster and can stall the bacteria."}, {"q": "How long does fishless cycling take?", "a": "Usually 4 to 8 weeks from scratch, and as little as one to two weeks with seeded media."}, {"q": "Can I use fish food instead of ammonia?", "a": "Yes. Rotting food releases ammonia, but it is harder to measure and messier. Pure ammonia gives more predictable results."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$mini-cycle$g$, $g$Mini-Cycle$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$A mini-cycle is a short return of ammonia or nitrite in an established tank after its bacteria colony is damaged or suddenly asked to handle more waste.$g$, $g$It can catch keepers off guard because the tank was fine yesterday. Spotting it early and doing water changes keeps fish safe while the bacteria recover, which usually takes days to a couple of weeks. Knowing the usual triggers lets you avoid most of them.$g$, $g$Aquarium Mini-Cycle: Causes, Signs and How to Fix It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common causes", "text": "- Cleaning the filter in tap water or replacing all the media at once.\n- Adding many fish in one go.\n- A filter that sat off for hours during a power outage.\n- Some medications, especially certain antibiotics, dosed in the main tank.\n- A big jump in feeding, or a dead fish or snail rotting unnoticed."}, {"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "Cloudy white water, fish gasping at the surface, red gills, clamped fins or sudden lethargy. A test kit is the only sure sign: any ammonia or nitrite reading above 0 ppm in an established tank means the bacteria are behind. Cloudy water alone can also be a harmless bacterial bloom, which is why testing matters more than looks."}, {"heading": "How to fix it", "text": "Change 25 to 50 percent of the water and repeat daily or every other day while ammonia plus nitrite is above about 0.5 ppm. Use a dechlorinator that binds ammonia.\n\nFeed lightly, every other day if needed, and do not clean the filter again until readings are back at zero. Adding bottled bacteria or seeded media can speed recovery. Skip adding new fish until the tank tests clean for about a week."}, {"heading": "How to prevent it", "text": "Rinse media in removed tank water, replace no more than a third at a time, stock new fish gradually, and test for a few days after any big change. If the power goes out for hours, rinse the filter sponge in tank water before restarting it, since stagnant media can release a burst of waste."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does a mini-cycle last?", "a": "Usually a few days to two weeks, depending on how much of the colony was lost."}, {"q": "Should I do water changes during a mini-cycle?", "a": "Yes. Water changes keep ammonia and nitrite low while the bacteria catch up, and they do not slow recovery."}, {"q": "Can cleaning the gravel cause a mini-cycle?", "a": "Rarely on its own, since most bacteria are in the filter. Cleaning the gravel and filter on the same day raises the risk."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$nitrogen-cycle$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$The nitrogen cycle is the natural process in which beneficial bacteria turn toxic ammonia from fish waste into nitrite and then into much less harmful nitrate.$g$, $g$It is the most important idea in fishkeeping. Almost every early fish death, cloudy tank or mystery illness traces back to the cycle being missing, broken or overloaded, and understanding it explains why filters, water changes and slow stocking matter.$g$, $g$The Aquarium Nitrogen Cycle Explained for Beginners$g$, $g$[{"heading": "The three stages", "text": "- Ammonia: released by fish gills, waste, uneaten food and decay. Toxic even at 0.25 ppm over time.\n- Nitrite: produced when the first group of bacteria eats ammonia. Also toxic, it blocks fish blood from carrying oxygen.\n- Nitrate: produced when a second group eats nitrite. Much safer, but it builds up and should be kept under about 20 to 40 ppm."}, {"heading": "Where it happens", "text": "The bacteria live on surfaces, mostly inside the filter, but also on gravel, decor and plants. They need oxygen and steady flow, which is why the filter should run all the time.\n\nIn a new tank these bacteria do not exist yet. Growing them is called cycling and takes about 4 to 8 weeks. Fishless cycling with a bottled ammonia source is the safest way to grow them before fish arrive."}, {"heading": "How nitrate leaves the tank", "text": "The cycle stops at nitrate in most aquariums. You remove it with regular water changes of 25 to 50 percent a week. Live plants and algae also use some, and a small amount is turned into nitrogen gas in low-oxygen spots."}, {"heading": "Keeping the cycle healthy", "text": "Feed only what fish eat in a minute or two, add fish gradually, and clean filter media in removed tank water. Test ammonia, nitrite and nitrate regularly. In an established tank, ammonia and nitrite should always read 0 ppm. A liquid test kit is more accurate than paper strips for these readings. If ammonia or nitrite ever shows up, do a water change and look for the cause, such as overfeeding, a dead fish or a recently cleaned filter."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What are safe levels of ammonia, nitrite and nitrate?", "a": "Ammonia and nitrite should be 0 ppm. Nitrate is best kept under about 20 to 40 ppm for most fish."}, {"q": "How long does the nitrogen cycle take in a new tank?", "a": "Usually 4 to 8 weeks to establish, faster if you seed with media from a mature tank."}, {"q": "Do water changes reset the nitrogen cycle?", "a": "No. The bacteria live on surfaces, not in the water, so dechlorinated water changes do not harm them."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$seeding$g$, $g$Seeding$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Seeding is jump-starting a new aquarium's beneficial bacteria by adding filter media, sponge, gravel or decor taken from an established, healthy tank.$g$, $g$Because the bacteria that run the nitrogen cycle live on surfaces, moving those surfaces moves the colony. Seeding can cut a 4 to 8 week cycle down to days, which makes it the fastest safe way to set up a new or quarantine tank.$g$, $g$How to Seed a New Aquarium Filter From an Old Tank$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What to transfer", "text": "- Filter media: the best choice. A sponge, a handful of ceramic rings or a piece of floss carries the most bacteria.\n- Sponge squeezings: wring a mature sponge into the new filter or tank.\n- Gravel or substrate: a cup or two in a mesh bag in the new filter helps, though it carries fewer bacteria.\n- Decor and wood: some help, but less than filter material.\n\nOld tank water does very little, since few bacteria float freely."}, {"heading": "How to do it", "text": "Move the media in a container of tank water so it stays wet, and get it into the new filter within an hour or so. Put it where water flows through it. Then feed the new tank an ammonia source, either a few fish or a fishless dose.\n\nTest ammonia and nitrite daily for the first week or two. If they stay at 0 ppm, add fish gradually."}, {"heading": "Limits", "text": "Seeded bacteria are sized for the old tank's load. Taking a small amount gives a head start, not a full cycle. Remove no more than about a third of the old filter's media so the donor tank stays stable."}, {"heading": "Risks", "text": "Pests and diseases can travel with media, including parasites, snails and algae. Only seed from a tank with healthy fish that have had no illness for several weeks, and avoid seeding from a tank currently being medicated."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does a seeded tank take to cycle?", "a": "Often a few days to two weeks, depending on how much media you moved and how many fish you add."}, {"q": "Can I seed a tank with gravel from another tank?", "a": "Yes, but it works less well than filter media. Put the gravel where water flows over it, ideally in the filter."}, {"q": "Does old tank water help cycle a new tank?", "a": "Very little. Nearly all the bacteria live on surfaces, so move media instead of water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$surface-agitation$g$, $g$Surface Agitation$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$Surface agitation is movement or rippling at the top of the aquarium water that speeds gas exchange, letting oxygen in and carbon dioxide out.$g$, $g$Oxygen enters water at the surface, so a still surface means less oxygen for fish and filter bacteria. It matters most in warm, crowded or heavily planted tanks, during medication, and at night when plants stop producing oxygen. Getting it right is simple and costs almost nothing.$g$, $g$Surface Agitation: Does Your Aquarium Need More Oxygen?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to create it", "text": "- Point a filter outflow slightly upward so it ripples the surface.\n- Let a hang-on-back filter spill from just above the water line.\n- Add an air stone or sponge filter; the bursting bubbles do most of the work.\n- Use a small powerhead aimed near the top for large tanks."}, {"heading": "Signs you need more", "text": "Fish gasping or hanging at the surface, especially early in the morning, rapid gill movement, or an oily film on the surface all point to low oxygen. Warm water holds less oxygen, so above about 82°F agitation becomes more important, including when you raise heat to treat ich. Medications and some water treatments can also lower oxygen, so add an air stone during any treatment."}, {"heading": "Balancing it in planted tanks", "text": "Strong agitation drives out carbon dioxide, which plants use. Tanks with CO2 injection usually aim for a gentle ripple across the surface, enough to break any film without a rolling boil. Many keepers run an air stone only at night when CO2 is off.\n\nLow-tech planted tanks without added CO2 are less sensitive, and normal agitation is fine."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Assuming bubbles in the tank equal oxygen is the big one; what matters is surface movement, not bubble count. Letting floating plants cover the whole surface also cuts gas exchange. Too much flow can stress slow fish like bettas, so aim agitation without blasting the whole tank. If the power goes out, gently stirring the surface by hand every so often helps keep fish going until it returns."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does an air stone add oxygen to an aquarium?", "a": "Yes, mainly by moving the surface as the bubbles rise and burst, which speeds gas exchange."}, {"q": "Can there be too much surface agitation?", "a": "For fish, rarely, though strong flow can tire weak swimmers. In CO2-injected planted tanks, too much wastes CO2."}, {"q": "What causes an oily film on the aquarium surface?", "a": "Usually proteins and fats from food. Better surface agitation breaks it up and keeps it from forming."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$algae$g$, $g$Algae$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Algae are simple photosynthetic organisms that grow on glass, decor, plants and in the water itself, natural in small amounts but unsightly when they bloom.$g$, $g$Every tank has some algae, and a little is a sign of a living system. An outbreak almost always points to an imbalance of light, nutrients and plant growth, so fixing the cause works far better than scrubbing or adding chemicals.$g$, $g$Aquarium Algae Types, Causes and How to Get Rid of It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common types", "text": "- Green spot: hard green dots on glass and slow leaves. Usually long light periods or low phosphate.\n- Green hair and thread: long strands. Excess light with unbalanced nutrients.\n- Black beard: dark tufts on wood and leaf edges. Linked to unstable CO2 and poor flow.\n- Green water: free-floating algae that turns the tank pea green.\n- Brown diatoms: dusty brown film, common in new tanks.\n- Blue-green slime: actually cyanobacteria, not true algae."}, {"heading": "What causes outbreaks", "text": "Too much light is the most common cause, either a light left on more than 8 hours a day or direct sunlight. Excess nutrients from overfeeding and skipped water changes come next. In planted tanks, giving strong light without enough fertilizer or CO2 lets algae outcompete plants."}, {"heading": "How to control it", "text": "Set the light on a timer for 6 to 8 hours. Feed less and change 25 to 50 percent of the water weekly. Add more fast-growing plants or floating plants to compete for nutrients.\n\nRemove what you can by hand, then let algae eaters help: nerite snails, otocinclus, amano shrimp and bristlenose plecos each target different types. Chemical algaecides are a last resort, and many harm plants and invertebrates."}, {"heading": "Mistakes to avoid", "text": "Changing many things at once makes it hard to tell what worked. Adding algae eaters without fixing the cause rarely solves anything. Give changes 2 to 3 weeks to show results."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is algae bad for fish?", "a": "Usually not. Most algae is harmless and some fish eat it, though heavy growth can mean poor water quality underneath."}, {"q": "How long should aquarium lights be on to prevent algae?", "a": "About 6 to 8 hours a day for most tanks, set on a timer for consistency."}, {"q": "Why is my fish tank turning green?", "a": "That is green water, a bloom of free-floating algae, usually from excess light and nutrients. A blackout of a few days or a UV sterilizer usually clears it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$aquascaping$g$, $g$Aquascaping$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Aquascaping is the art of arranging plants, rock, wood and substrate in an aquarium to create a natural-looking or designed underwater landscape.$g$, $g$A good scape does more than look nice; it gives fish cover, territory and swimming room, and it gives plants space to grow. Even a simple tank benefits from a few design basics, and many keepers find it the most creative part of the hobby.$g$, $g$Aquascaping for Beginners: Styles, Layouts and Tips$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Popular styles", "text": "- Nature style: imitates landscapes like hills, forests or riverbanks, with wood, stone and fine-leaved plants.\n- Iwagumi: a minimalist rock layout with a few stones and one or two carpeting plants.\n- Dutch: dense, colorful rows of stem plants with little visible hardscape.\n- Jungle: wild, overgrown, low-maintenance planting.\n- Biotope: recreates a real habitat with plants, fish and decor from one region."}, {"heading": "Design basics", "text": "Place the main focal point off-center, about a third of the way across the tank, instead of dead center. Slope the substrate higher toward the back to add depth. Use small plants in front, medium in the middle and tall ones behind.\n\nRepeat a few plant species instead of using one of everything; it looks more natural. Leave open swimming space for your fish."}, {"heading": "How to build one", "text": "Start with hardscape. Arrange rock and wood in the dry tank, step back and adjust until it looks right. Then add substrate, plant with the tank partly filled, and fill slowly over a plate or bag to avoid stirring things up.\n\nPlant heavily from the start; dense planting helps hold back algae while the tank settles."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Using too many different rock or wood types, crowding the front glass, and buying plants without checking their light needs. Rock that raises pH, like some limestone, can be a problem for soft water fish. Finally, skipping a plan for maintenance matters: fast stem plants need weekly trims, while Anubias and moss need very little."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the easiest aquascape style for beginners?", "a": "A jungle or simple nature style with hardy plants like Anubias, Java fern and Cryptocoryne is the most forgiving."}, {"q": "Do I need CO2 for aquascaping?", "a": "No. Many beautiful scapes use low-light plants and no CO2. Carpets and red stem plants are where CO2 helps most."}, {"q": "How long does it take for an aquascape to grow in?", "a": "Usually 2 to 3 months for plants to fill in, longer for slow growers like Anubias and moss."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$carpeting-plant$g$, $g$Carpeting Plant$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$A carpeting plant is a low-growing aquarium plant that spreads by runners across the substrate, covering the foreground like a lawn.$g$, $g$A full green carpet is one of the most striking looks in the hobby, and it also traps debris and gives small fish and shrimp cover. Most carpets need bright light, fine substrate and often CO2, though a few easier options work in low-tech tanks.$g$, $g$Best Carpeting Plants for Aquariums, With and Without CO2$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common choices", "text": "- Dwarf hairgrass: grass-like blades, moderate to high light.\n- Monte Carlo: small round leaves, one of the easier true carpets.\n- Dwarf baby tears: very fine and short, demanding, usually needs CO2.\n- Dwarf sagittaria: taller grass that carpets in low to moderate light without CO2.\n- Marsilea and some mosses: slow but tolerant of low light."}, {"heading": "How to plant a carpet", "text": "Use fine substrate such as sand or a nutrient-rich aqua soil, since tiny roots struggle in coarse gravel. Split pots into small plugs and plant them about 1 inch apart with tweezers, pushing them deep enough that they stay put.\n\nThe dry start method, growing the carpet in a damp, covered tank for 4 to 8 weeks before flooding, avoids floating plants and early algae. Planting more densely at the start fills the carpet faster and leaves less bare substrate for algae."}, {"heading": "What they need to thrive", "text": "Bright light reaching the bottom of the tank is the main requirement. CO2 injection makes most carpets grow fast and dense; without it, choose low-tech species. Regular fertilizer, including root nutrients, keeps them green."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Plugs floating up is the most common early issue; burrowing fish and big snails make it worse. Carpets that grow upward instead of outward usually lack light. Thick carpets can trap debris and rot underneath, so trim them low and vacuum gently above them. Patience helps most: trim gently, keep the light steady, and avoid moving plugs once they root."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the easiest carpeting plant without CO2?", "a": "Dwarf sagittaria is one of the easiest. Monte Carlo and some mosses can also carpet slowly in a low-tech tank with good light."}, {"q": "How long does it take for a carpet to fill in?", "a": "Usually 4 to 8 weeks with CO2 and good light, and several months without it."}, {"q": "Can carpeting plants grow in gravel?", "a": "Some can, but fine sand or aqua soil works much better because the roots are tiny."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$co2-injection$g$, $g$CO2 Injection$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$CO2 injection is adding carbon dioxide gas to a planted aquarium, usually from a pressurized cylinder, to supply plants with carbon and greatly boost their growth.$g$, $g$Carbon is often the nutrient that limits plant growth, so injection unlocks fast growth, dense carpets and strong red colors. It is the defining feature of high-tech tanks, but too much CO2 suffocates fish, so it needs careful setup and monitoring.$g$, $g$CO2 Injection for Planted Tanks: Setup and Safe Levels$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How a system works", "text": "- A pressurized cylinder stores the gas.\n- A regulator lowers the pressure, and a needle valve sets the bubble rate.\n- A solenoid valve on a timer turns gas on and off.\n- A bubble counter shows the flow rate.\n- A diffuser or reactor dissolves the gas into the water.\n- A drop checker shows the level by color.\n\nDIY yeast or citric acid kits exist for small tanks, but they are harder to control."}, {"heading": "Safe levels and dialing it in", "text": "Most planted tanks aim for about 20 to 30 ppm CO2. A drop checker filled with 4 dKH reference solution turns lime green at roughly that level, blue when too low and yellow when too high.\n\nStart with a low bubble rate and raise it slowly over a week or two while watching fish. A pH drop of about 1.0 from fully degassed water to the peak of the CO2 period is a common target."}, {"heading": "Timing", "text": "Turn CO2 on 1 to 2 hours before the lights so levels are up when plants start working, and off about an hour before lights out. Plants do not use CO2 in the dark, and running it overnight is the most common cause of fish deaths."}, {"heading": "Warning signs and mistakes", "text": "Fish gasping at the surface, breathing hard or lying still mean too much CO2. Turn it off, increase surface agitation and do a water change. Never adjust the needle valve and walk away; check fish for the next few hours. Shrimp are especially sensitive."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do I need CO2 in a planted tank?", "a": "No. Many plants grow well without it. CO2 matters most for carpets, red stem plants and fast, dense growth under strong light."}, {"q": "How many bubbles per second of CO2?", "a": "It depends on tank size and diffuser. Start around 1 bubble per second for a 20 to 30 gallon tank and adjust using a drop checker and your fish's behavior."}, {"q": "Should CO2 run at night?", "a": "No. Plants do not use it in the dark, so turn it off about an hour before lights out to protect your fish."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$cyanobacteria$g$, $g$Cyanobacteria$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Cyanobacteria, often called blue-green algae or BGA, is a photosynthetic bacteria that forms slimy blue-green, dark green or reddish sheets over substrate, plants and decor.$g$, $g$Despite the nickname, it is a bacteria, not true algae, which is why many algae fixes do not touch it. It smothers plants, has a strong musty smell, and spreads fast in still water, so it is worth tackling as soon as you spot it.$g$, $g$Blue-Green Algae (Cyanobacteria) in Aquariums: How to Fix It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to identify it", "text": "- Slimy sheets that peel off in one piece, unlike stringy or fuzzy algae.\n- Blue-green, dark green, or sometimes red to purple color.\n- A strong earthy, musty smell when disturbed.\n- Often starts on the substrate against the front glass, where light hits and flow is weak.\n- Bubbles trapped under the film in the afternoon."}, {"heading": "What causes it", "text": "Low flow and dead spots are the most common factor. Excess organic waste from overfeeding and infrequent cleaning help it along. In planted tanks, very low nitrate with plenty of phosphate can favor it, which is why it sometimes shows up in tanks that seem clean. Direct sunlight on the tank also feeds it."}, {"heading": "How to get rid of it", "text": "- Siphon out as much as you can and do a 30 to 50 percent water change.\n- Improve circulation, especially along the bottom.\n- Do a blackout: cover the tank fully for 3 to 4 days with no light, keeping filter and aeration running, then do another water change.\n- In planted tanks, check that nitrate is not near zero.\n\nSome keepers use the antibiotic erythromycin as a last resort. It works well on cyanobacteria but should be used carefully and only as directed, and it is not allowed for sale in every area. Hydrogen peroxide spot treatment can harm shrimp at high doses."}, {"heading": "Preventing a comeback", "text": "Keep up weekly water changes, feed less, vacuum the substrate surface, and keep flow strong enough that nothing is stagnant. Return light to 6 to 8 hours after a blackout."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is cyanobacteria harmful to fish?", "a": "It is mostly a nuisance, but heavy growth can lower oxygen at night and some strains release toxins. Clear it promptly."}, {"q": "Do snails or shrimp eat blue-green algae?", "a": "Very few animals eat it reliably. Removing the cause and doing a blackout works better than relying on cleaners."}, {"q": "Will a blackout kill my plants?", "a": "Most healthy plants handle 3 to 4 days of darkness without trouble. Delicate or weak plants may lose a few leaves."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$diatoms$g$, $g$Diatoms$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Diatoms are tiny single-celled algae with glass-like silica shells that coat glass, substrate and decor in a dusty brown film, especially in new tanks.$g$, $g$Often called brown algae, diatoms are a normal phase in almost every new aquarium and are harmless to fish. They usually fade on their own within a few weeks to a couple of months as the tank matures and other organisms start competing for nutrients.$g$, $g$Brown Algae (Diatoms) in a New Tank: Causes and Removal$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to identify them", "text": "Diatoms form a soft brown or golden film that wipes off easily with a finger or sponge. They appear on glass, sand, rocks and slow-growing leaves. Unlike black beard or green spot algae, they do not cling hard or form tufts."}, {"heading": "Why they appear", "text": "New tanks have spare nutrients and silicates, which diatoms use to build their shells. Silicate comes from tap water, some sand and new substrate. Low light can also favor diatoms over green algae. As plants grow and the biological balance settles, diatoms lose their edge."}, {"heading": "How to get rid of them", "text": "- Wipe glass with an algae pad and siphon brown patches off the substrate during water changes.\n- Keep up weekly water changes of 25 to 50 percent.\n- Add nerite snails, which eat diatoms eagerly and do not breed in freshwater.\n- Otocinclus catfish love diatoms but should only go into a stable, cycled tank, and need extra food once the diatoms are gone.\n- Use RO water or silicate-removing media only if diatoms keep coming back in an older tank.\n\nScrubbing the glass every few days keeps the tank looking clean while you wait for diatoms to fade on their own."}, {"heading": "When to be concerned", "text": "In a tank older than about 3 months, persistent diatoms may point to high silicate in the source water or very low light. Test or treat the source water rather than adding more algae eaters."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are diatoms harmful to fish?", "a": "No. They are harmless and many fish and snails eat them. They are only a cosmetic problem."}, {"q": "How long do diatoms last in a new tank?", "a": "Usually a few weeks to two months. They fade as the tank matures and plants grow in."}, {"q": "What eats brown diatom algae?", "a": "Nerite snails, otocinclus catfish, many shrimp and bristlenose plecos all graze on diatoms."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dragon-stone$g$, $g$Dragon Stone$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Dragon stone, also called Ohko stone, is a lightweight, cratered clay-based rock in tan to reddish brown, prized in aquascaping for its rugged, textured look.$g$, $g$It brings dramatic, rocky texture to a scape without weighing the tank down, and it is generally considered inert, so it will not raise pH or hardness. Its holes also make perfect spots to tuck mosses and small epiphytes. It is also safe for shrimp and snails.$g$, $g$Dragon Stone in Aquariums: Is It Safe and How to Prep It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What makes it popular", "text": "The deep holes and crevices give a weathered, mountain-like look that fits Iwagumi and nature style layouts. Because it is lighter than most stone, you can build tall structures with less risk to the glass.\n\nPieces are often sold in mixed sizes, and choosing one or two large anchor rocks with smaller supporting pieces gives the most natural result."}, {"heading": "How to prepare it", "text": "- Soak and scrub each piece with a stiff brush to remove packed clay and dirt in the holes.\n- Rinse until the water runs mostly clear.\n- Some keepers soak for a day or two to loosen leftover clay.\n- Do a vinegar test if unsure: a few drops that fizz mean the rock contains carbonate and may raise pH.\n\nLet the rocks dry fully after scrubbing so you can spot any remaining loose clay before they go in the tank."}, {"heading": "Using it in a scape", "text": "Place rocks on a thin bed of substrate or directly on the glass bottom for stability, then build substrate around them. Angle the texture in the same direction across all pieces so it looks like one rock formation. Fill holes with moss, Bucephalandra or small Anubias for a softer look."}, {"heading": "Downsides", "text": "Its clay can crumble or cloud water early on, and the deep holes trap fish waste, so siphon around it during water changes. Sharp edges may scratch delicate fish like bettas. Because pieces are fragile, avoid stacking heavy rock on top of thin sections."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does dragon stone raise pH?", "a": "Generally no. It is considered inert, though a vinegar fizz test on a piece is a quick way to be sure."}, {"q": "Do you need to clean dragon stone before use?", "a": "Yes. Scrub out the clay in the holes and rinse well, or it can cloud the water."}, {"q": "Is dragon stone safe for shrimp?", "a": "Yes. It does not leach anything harmful and its holes give shrimp lots of grazing surface."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$driftwood$g$, $g$Driftwood$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Driftwood is cured, waterlogged wood used in aquariums for decoration, structure and shelter, and as a surface for plants and biofilm to grow on.$g$, $g$Beyond its looks, driftwood gives fish cover and territory, provides grazing for shrimp and plecos, and slowly releases tannins that slightly lower pH and tint the water amber. Some plecos even need wood in their diet. Most pieces need some preparation before they will sink.$g$, $g$How to Prepare Aquarium Driftwood So It Sinks$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common types", "text": "- Malaysian driftwood: dense, dark, sinks quickly, releases lots of tannins.\n- Spider wood: thin, twisting branches that need soaking to sink.\n- Mopani: two-toned and heavy, strong tannins.\n- Manzanita: branchy, light colored, popular for tree-style scapes.\n- Cholla: hollow cactus skeleton, great for shrimp."}, {"heading": "How to prepare it", "text": "Scrub off loose bark and dirt with a brush and plain water. Boil small pieces for 1 to 2 hours to sterilize, help them sink and release some tannins. Soak larger pieces in a tub for 1 to 3 weeks, changing the water as it turns brown.\n\nIf a piece still floats, weigh it down with a rock or attach it to a slate base until it waterlogs."}, {"heading": "White fuzz and tannins", "text": "A white or clear fuzzy coating often appears in the first few weeks. It is harmless bacteria and fungus feeding on sugars in the wood. Snails, shrimp and plecos eat it, and it fades on its own.\n\nTannins are harmless and many soft water fish like them. Carbon and water changes clear them if you prefer clear water. A large new piece can drop pH noticeably in soft water, so test for a few weeks after adding it."}, {"heading": "Wood to avoid", "text": "Avoid soft, rotting wood and resinous woods like pine and cedar. Collected wood is risky: it can carry pesticides or pests, and freshly cut wood rots. Only use dead, hard, well-dried wood, and clean it thoroughly."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does driftwood take to sink?", "a": "Anywhere from a few days to a few weeks. Boiling and soaking speed it up, or you can weigh it down."}, {"q": "Is the white fuzz on driftwood harmful?", "a": "No. It is a harmless biofilm that fades in a few weeks, and shrimp and snails eat it."}, {"q": "Does driftwood lower pH?", "a": "Slightly, through tannins. The effect is strongest in soft water and small in hard water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dry-start-method$g$, $g$Dry Start Method$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$The dry start method is growing aquarium plants, usually carpets, in damp substrate inside a sealed, unfilled tank for several weeks before flooding it.$g$, $g$Most carpeting plants grow faster out of water, where they get carbon straight from the air. This lets them root firmly and spread before algae can take hold, and it stops tiny plugs from floating away, making carpets possible even without CO2.$g$, $g$Dry Start Method: How to Grow an Aquarium Carpet$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to set it up", "text": "- Fill the tank with a fine substrate such as aqua soil, sloped as you want it.\n- Wet the substrate until it is damp but not swimming; a little water should pool if you press it.\n- Plant small plugs about 1 inch apart.\n- Mist the plants and cover the top tightly with plastic wrap or a glass lid.\n- Run the light for 8 to 10 hours a day."}, {"heading": "Daily care", "text": "Mist once or twice a day to keep humidity high. Lift the cover for a few minutes every day or two to let in fresh air, which cuts mold. Keep the room warm, ideally 70 to 80°F.\n\nFertilizer is usually not needed with aqua soil. In inert sand, a light misting of diluted liquid fertilizer once a week helps."}, {"heading": "How long and how to flood", "text": "Most dry starts take 4 to 8 weeks, until the carpet covers most of the ground and holds firm when tugged. Fill the tank very slowly over a plate or plastic bag so the substrate stays put. Expect some leaves to melt as plants switch to underwater growth, and do frequent water changes in the first few weeks since aqua soil may release ammonia."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Mold appears as white fluff; remove it and ventilate more. Yellowing or drying means not enough humidity. Good plants for a dry start include Monte Carlo, dwarf baby tears, dwarf hairgrass and many mosses. Epiphytes like Anubias also grow well this way."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long should a dry start last?", "a": "Usually 4 to 8 weeks, until the carpet is well rooted and covers most of the substrate."}, {"q": "Do you need CO2 after a dry start?", "a": "Not always. Some carpets stay healthy without it, but most grow denser with CO2 once flooded."}, {"q": "How do I stop mold during a dry start?", "a": "Air the tank every day or two, remove decaying leaves, and avoid soaking the substrate too much."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dutch-style$g$, $g$Dutch Style$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Dutch style is an aquascaping style built from dense, carefully trimmed groups of many colorful plant species arranged in terraced rows, with little or no visible hardscape.$g$, $g$It is the oldest formal aquascaping style, developed in the Netherlands in the mid-1900s, and it showcases plant-growing skill more than rock or wood design. It is stunning when done well but needs strong light, steady fertilizing and frequent trimming.$g$, $g$Dutch Aquascape Style: Rules, Plants and How to Start$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Key features", "text": "- Plants cover nearly all the substrate, often 80 percent or more.\n- Groups of one species each, with clear contrast in color, leaf shape and texture.\n- Plants step from short in front to tall in back, forming terraces.\n- Narrow lines of tall plants, called streets, run from front to back to create depth.\n- Wood and rock are minimal or hidden.\n\nA common guideline is about one species per 4 inches of front glass width."}, {"heading": "Good plants for the style", "text": "Stem plants do most of the work: Rotala, Ludwigia, Limnophila and Alternanthera for color and fine texture. Low plants like Staurogyne and Cryptocoryne fill the front, and a few bold plants like a sword or Aponogeton serve as focal points. Red plants need strong light and good iron. Plan the layout on paper first so neighboring groups contrast instead of blending together."}, {"heading": "Equipment and care", "text": "Most Dutch tanks run strong lighting, CO2 injection and a full fertilizer routine. Expect to trim stem plants weekly, replanting the healthy tops and discarding the lower stems to keep groups tidy and bushy. Water changes of 30 to 50 percent a week keep nutrients balanced."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Planting groups too small or too close in color makes the scape look muddy. Letting stem plants grow to the surface shades everything below. Skipping trims for a few weeks is the quickest way to lose the shape."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do you need CO2 for a Dutch aquascape?", "a": "It is not strictly required, but most successful Dutch tanks use CO2 because the style relies on fast, colorful stem plants."}, {"q": "What is the difference between Dutch style and nature style?", "a": "Dutch style focuses on rows of contrasting plants with little hardscape, while nature style uses rock and wood to imitate a natural landscape."}, {"q": "How often do you trim a Dutch aquascape?", "a": "Usually weekly for stem plants, with lighter touch-ups on slower plants every few weeks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$epiphyte$g$, $g$Epiphyte$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$An epiphyte is a plant that grows attached to rock, wood or other surfaces instead of rooting in the substrate, taking its nutrients from the water.$g$, $g$Epiphytes are some of the easiest aquarium plants. Most handle low light, need no CO2, and can decorate hardscape in tanks with bare bottoms or inert sand. They also suit fish that dig, since there are no roots in the substrate to uproot.$g$, $g$Epiphyte Aquarium Plants: How to Attach and Grow Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common epiphytes", "text": "- Anubias: tough dark leaves, very low light needs.\n- Java fern: long leaves, often grows little plantlets on its leaves.\n- Bucephalandra: small, colorful leaves, slow growing.\n- Mosses like Java moss and Christmas moss: cover wood and rock.\n- Bolbitis: a fern that likes good flow."}, {"heading": "How to attach them", "text": "Tie the rhizome or moss to wood or rock with cotton thread, which dissolves in a few weeks, or fishing line. Gel superglue also works well and is safe once cured; a small dab on the hardscape holds the plant in place underwater.\n\nYou can also wedge plants into gaps between rocks or wood. Within a few weeks roots grip the surface on their own."}, {"heading": "Care tips", "text": "Keep the rhizome above the substrate at all times; burying it causes rot. Feed with a light liquid fertilizer since these plants feed from the water. Moderate flow helps bring nutrients and keeps debris off their leaves.\n\nPropagate by cutting the rhizome into pieces with several leaves each, or by collecting the baby plants Java fern grows on its leaves. Many epiphytes also grow well out of water in a humid tank, which is handy for a dry start or a paludarium."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Slow leaves can collect algae, so keep light moderate and the light period around 6 to 8 hours. Java fern can develop black or brown patches from low nutrients. Moss can trap debris and needs a gentle rinse during maintenance."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can epiphytes be planted in substrate?", "a": "Their roots can touch the substrate, but the rhizome must stay above it or it will rot."}, {"q": "Is superglue safe for attaching aquarium plants?", "a": "Yes. Plain cyanoacrylate gel glue is widely used and is safe once it cures, which happens almost instantly in water."}, {"q": "Do epiphytes need fertilizer?", "a": "They grow without much, but a light liquid fertilizer helps since they feed from the water column."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$floating-plant$g$, $g$Floating Plant$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$A floating plant is an aquatic plant that grows on the water's surface, with its leaves in the air and its roots hanging down into the tank.$g$, $g$Floating plants pull nitrogen and phosphate straight from the water, which makes them one of the easiest ways to fight algae and keep nitrate down. They also dim harsh light, calm skittish fish and give fry, bettas and gouramis a place to hide and build nests.$g$, $g$Floating Aquarium Plants: Best Types, Care and Control$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Popular choices", "text": "- Duckweed: tiny, very fast, and almost impossible to remove once established\n- Salvinia: small fuzzy leaves, easy to thin by hand\n- Amazon frogbit: round leaves with long, feathery roots\n- Red root floater: turns red under strong light and low nitrate\n- Water lettuce: large rosettes, best for open-top or big tanks\n- Water sprite: can float or be planted"}, {"heading": "Care and light", "text": "Most floaters need little more than light and a still surface. Keep the water level a few inches below a lid or light so leaves don't scorch or stay soggy from condensation. Strong filter outflow pushes them underwater and rots the leaves, so aim the return along the glass or use a floating ring made from airline tubing to corral them. They feed from the water column, so in very clean, lightly stocked tanks a small dose of liquid fertilizer keeps them green."}, {"heading": "Keeping them under control", "text": "Floaters can double in a week or two. Scoop out a third to a half whenever they cover more than about half the surface, because a full mat shades the plants below and can reduce gas exchange at the surface. Every handful you remove takes the nutrients it absorbed out of the tank with it, which is part of why they work so well against algae."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Adding duckweed by accident is the classic one: it hitches a ride on store plants and spreads to every tank you own. Rinse new plants and pick off stray floaters. Also avoid floaters with goldfish or large cichlids and some plecos unless you want them eaten, and don't let a thick mat starve a carpet plant of light."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do floating plants need fertilizer?", "a": "Usually not in a stocked tank, since fish waste feeds them. If leaves turn pale or yellow in a lightly stocked tank, a small dose of an all-in-one liquid fertilizer helps."}, {"q": "Do floating plants reduce oxygen in the tank?", "a": "They add oxygen during the day, but a solid mat covering the whole surface can cut gas exchange. Keep some open water and they are a net positive."}, {"q": "How do I get rid of duckweed?", "a": "Net it out daily, including bits stuck to glass and equipment, and keep going for several weeks. Missing even a few leaves lets it come back."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$green-spot-algae$g$, $g$Green Spot Algae$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Green spot algae is a hard, slow-growing algae that forms small round green dots on aquarium glass, rocks and the leaves of slow plants.$g$, $g$It is usually a sign that light is outpacing nutrients, especially phosphate, rather than a sign of dirty water. It won't hurt fish, but it clouds the front glass and can cover Anubias and other slow growers, so fixing the cause saves a lot of scraping.$g$, $g$Green Spot Algae: What Causes It and How to Get Rid of It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to recognize it", "text": "Green spot algae looks like tiny, flat, bright green circles about the size of a pinhead that gradually merge into patches. It feels hard and crusty and won't wipe off with a soft sponge. It favors the front glass, where light is strongest, and old leaves on slow plants such as Anubias, Java fern and Bucephalandra."}, {"heading": "What causes it", "text": "- Low phosphate, often below about 0.5 ppm, in tanks with good light\n- A long photoperiod or very strong light for the plants you keep\n- Unstable or low CO2 in a high-tech tank\n- Slow plants with old leaves that stay in place for months\n\nThe phosphate link is widely reported but not universal, so treat it as a strong lead rather than a guarantee."}, {"heading": "How to remove it", "text": "Scrape glass with a razor blade or plastic card scraper; use plastic on acrylic so you don't scratch it. On leaves, it is usually easier to trim the worst ones. Nerite snails eat it slowly and are one of the few grazers that make a dent. Most algae-eating fish and shrimp ignore it."}, {"heading": "Stopping it coming back", "text": "Test phosphate and, if it is very low, dose a bit more with your fertilizer routine or a phosphate supplement to reach roughly 1 to 2 ppm in a planted tank. Cut lighting to 6 to 8 hours or lower its intensity, and keep CO2 steady if you inject it. Light regrowth on the glass every week or two is normal in a bright tank and just part of maintenance."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is green spot algae harmful to fish?", "a": "No. It is harmless to fish and invertebrates. The problem is looks and shading on slow plant leaves."}, {"q": "Does too much light cause green spot algae?", "a": "Strong light combined with low phosphate is the most common trigger. Shortening the photoperiod and raising phosphate usually slows it down."}, {"q": "What eats green spot algae?", "a": "Nerite snails are the most reliable grazers. Otocinclus and shrimp barely touch it, so scraping remains the main fix."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hardscape$g$, $g$Hardscape$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Hardscape is the non-living framework of an aquascape, mainly the rocks, driftwood and sometimes sloped substrate, arranged before any plants go in.$g$, $g$The hardscape sets the shape, depth and focal point of the whole layout, and it's the one part that's hard to change later. It also affects water chemistry, swimming space and where plants can grow, so choosing safe materials and placing them carefully pays off for years. Some keepers build an entire hardscape scape, a layout of mostly rock and wood with few or no plants.$g$, $g$Aquarium Hardscape: How to Choose and Arrange Rock and Wood$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Choosing materials", "text": "Popular rocks include dragon stone, lava rock, slate and Seiryu stone; popular woods include spider wood, Malaysian driftwood and manzanita. Test any rock with a few drops of vinegar: fizzing means it contains carbonate and will raise pH and hardness, which matters for soft-water fish and shrimp. Soak or boil wood to remove tannins and help it sink. Avoid painted or treated materials and anything with metal veins."}, {"heading": "Arranging it", "text": "- Pick one clear focal point, often placed about one-third in from a side rather than dead center\n- Use one main rock type and one wood type for a natural look\n- Vary sizes and slope substrate from about 1 to 2 inches in front to 4 or more in back\n- Leave open swimming space and room to reach in for maintenance\n- Set heavy rock directly on the glass or an egg crate base before adding substrate"}, {"heading": "Hardscape-only scapes", "text": "A hardscape scape relies on texture, color and composition with few or no plants, sometimes just mosses or a single species. It is low maintenance and avoids the lighting and fertilizer needs of a planted tank. The trade-off is less natural nutrient uptake, so algae on rock is more likely; keep lighting modest and stocking light, or add a few hardy epiphytes."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Stacking rocks without stability is the big one: a collapse can crack the glass, so glue or pin stacks and test them by pushing gently. Other mistakes include using too many rock types, crowding the glass edges, and forgetting that wood floats for weeks unless weighted or presoaked."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do I know if a rock is aquarium safe?", "a": "Most inert rocks are safe. Do a vinegar test for carbonate, avoid rocks with rust or metallic streaks, and scrub before use."}, {"q": "Do you put hardscape before or after substrate?", "a": "Usually heavy rocks go on the bare glass first, then substrate goes around them so fish can't dig under them. Lighter wood can go on top of the substrate."}, {"q": "What is a hardscape-only aquarium?", "a": "It's a layout built mostly or entirely from rock and wood with little or no live plant life. It looks clean and needs less upkeep but has fewer plants to compete with algae."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$high-tech-tank$g$, $g$High-tech Tank$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$A high-tech tank is a planted aquarium that uses injected CO2, strong lighting and regular fertilizer dosing to grow plants quickly and densely.$g$, $g$This approach lets you grow demanding carpets, red stem plants and the lush layouts seen in aquascaping contests. The catch is that strong light without matching CO2 and nutrients leads to algae fast, so a high-tech tank rewards consistency and punishes neglect.$g$, $g$High Tech vs Low Tech Planted Tank: What a High Tech Setup Needs$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What you need", "text": "- Pressurized CO2 with a regulator, solenoid, bubble counter and diffuser or reactor\n- A drop checker to confirm CO2 levels, usually aiming for lime green, roughly 30 ppm\n- A strong planted-tank light on a timer\n- A nutrient-rich active substrate or root tabs\n- A dosing routine for macro and micro nutrients\n- Good flow so CO2 and nutrients reach every plant"}, {"heading": "Running it day to day", "text": "Turn CO2 on 1 to 2 hours before the lights and off about an hour before they go off. Keep a 6 to 8 hour photoperiod, and dose fertilizer several times a week or daily. Change 30 to 50 percent of the water weekly to reset nutrient levels. Expect to trim stems every week or two, since fast growth is the point."}, {"heading": "Fish and CO2 safety", "text": "Too much CO2 suffocates fish. Signs include fish gasping at the surface or hanging near the outflow in the morning. Raise CO2 slowly over several days, watch your fish after each change, and keep good surface movement so oxygen stays high. A solenoid on a timer prevents CO2 running all night when plants aren't using it."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "The biggest mistake is turning the light up before CO2 and fertilizer are dialed in. Others include fluctuating CO2, too little flow, and letting dead leaves rot in place. When algae shows up, fix the balance first, lowering light if needed, rather than reaching for chemicals."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do I need CO2 for a planted tank?", "a": "No. Many plants grow well in low-tech tanks. CO2 matters when you want fast growth, carpets and demanding species under strong light."}, {"q": "How much CO2 is safe for fish?", "a": "Around 30 ppm is the common target for a planted tank, and most community fish tolerate it with good surface agitation. Watch for gasping and back off if you see it."}, {"q": "Why does my high-tech tank have so much algae?", "a": "Usually CO2 is too low or unstable for the light intensity, or flow is poor. Lower the light, stabilize CO2 and check that nutrients aren't running out."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$iwagumi$g$, $g$Iwagumi$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Iwagumi is a minimalist Japanese aquascaping style built around a few carefully placed stones and usually a single low carpet of plants.$g$, $g$Its open, mountain-like look is beautiful but unforgiving, since there are few plants to hide algae or mistakes. Learning the stone rules and the care a carpet needs helps you build a layout that looks calm and natural rather than empty.$g$, $g$Iwagumi Aquascape: Stone Layout Rules and How to Build One$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Stone placement", "text": "Iwagumi uses an odd number of stones, typically three, five or seven, of a single rock type such as Seiryu or dragon stone. The traditional names are Oyaishi, the largest main stone; Fukuishi, the secondary stone; Soeishi, accent stones beside the main one; and Suteishi, small sacrificial stones. Place the main stone off center, roughly one-third across, and angle all stones as if shaped by the same wind and water."}, {"heading": "Plants and fish", "text": "Classic Iwagumi uses one or two carpet plants such as dwarf hairgrass, Monte Carlo or Hemianthus callitrichoides. Fish are usually a single schooling species, like rasboras or tetras, so the movement stays simple. Many keepers add Amano shrimp or Otocinclus for algae control."}, {"heading": "Setup and care", "text": "- Use a nutrient-rich substrate sloping from about 1 inch in front to 3 or 4 inches behind the stones\n- Pressurized CO2 and strong light are usually needed for dense carpets\n- Start with a dry start method or dense planting to cover ground fast\n- Do large water changes, 50 percent or more, several times a week in the first month\n- Trim carpets evenly so lower layers don't die off and lift"}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Mixing rock types, using an even number of stones, and making all stones the same height all break the natural look. Algae is the main battle, since a young carpet uses little of the available light. Start with a shorter photoperiod around 6 hours and increase slowly as the carpet fills in."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How many rocks should an Iwagumi have?", "a": "An odd number, usually three to seven main stones, with one clearly dominant. Small filler stones are fine as long as the overall arrangement keeps a clear hierarchy."}, {"q": "Can you do an Iwagumi without CO2?", "a": "It is possible with slow, low-light carpets or mosses, but results are slower and less dense. Most classic Iwagumi layouts use injected CO2."}, {"q": "What rock is best for Iwagumi?", "a": "Seiryu stone and dragon stone are the most popular. Seiryu raises hardness slightly, so choose an inert rock if you keep soft-water species."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$liquid-fertilizer$g$, $g$Liquid Fertilizer$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Liquid fertilizer is a plant food dosed directly into aquarium water, supplying nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium and trace elements that plants absorb through their leaves.$g$, $g$Plants that feed from the water column, like stem plants, mosses and floaters, often run short of nutrients even in a stocked tank. Steady liquid dosing keeps them growing and coloring up, and healthy plants are your best defense against algae.$g$, $g$Liquid Fertilizer for Aquarium Plants: How and When to Dose$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What's in it", "text": "- Macronutrients: nitrogen (as nitrate), phosphorus (as phosphate) and potassium\n- Micronutrients: iron, manganese, magnesium, boron and other trace elements\n- All-in-one products contain both, while some keepers dose them separately or mix their own dry salts\n\nPotassium deficiency shows as pinholes in older leaves; iron deficiency shows as pale or yellow new growth."}, {"heading": "How to dose", "text": "Start with half the label dose in a low-tech tank and adjust from there. High-tech tanks with CO2 usually need the full dose, sometimes daily. Dose after your weekly water change and then on a set schedule, since a steady supply works better than occasional big doses. Many keepers aim for roughly 10 to 20 ppm nitrate and 1 to 2 ppm phosphate in planted tanks."}, {"heading": "Safety with fish and shrimp", "text": "Fertilizers made for aquariums are safe at the recommended dose. Overdosing can harm shrimp and snails, and some products contain copper as a trace element. It is usually a tiny amount, but shrimp keepers should dose carefully and avoid products with added copper. Never use houseplant or garden fertilizer, which can contain ammonia-based nitrogen at levels that harm fish."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Dosing heavily under weak light, which feeds algae instead of plants, is the most common one. Others are using only liquid fertilizer for heavy root feeders like swords and crypts, which also need root tabs, and changing several things at once so you can't tell what helped."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How often should I add liquid fertilizer to my aquarium?", "a": "Low-tech tanks often do well with one or two doses a week. High-tech tanks with CO2 may need dosing three times a week or daily."}, {"q": "Is liquid fertilizer safe for shrimp?", "a": "Most aquarium fertilizers are shrimp safe at normal doses. Start low, avoid overdosing, and skip products that list copper as a main ingredient."}, {"q": "Does liquid fertilizer cause algae?", "a": "Only when nutrients outpace what plants can use, usually from too much fertilizer under weak light. Balanced dosing with healthy plants actually helps prevent algae."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$low-tech-tank$g$, $g$Low-tech Tank$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$A low-tech tank is a planted aquarium run without injected CO2, using modest lighting, hardy plants and little or no fertilizer.$g$, $g$It is the easiest way to enjoy live plants: lower cost, less equipment and slower growth that forgives missed water changes. Plant choice and lighting balance matter more here, since strong light without CO2 tends to cause algae. Many keepers find a well-planted low-tech tank stays stable for years.$g$, $g$Low Tech Planted Tank: Setup, Best Plants and Care Tips$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Good plants for low tech", "text": "- Java fern, Anubias and Bucephalandra tied to wood or rock\n- Cryptocoryne and Amazon swords in the substrate, with root tabs\n- Java moss and other mosses\n- Vallisneria and dwarf sagittaria for backgrounds\n- Floating plants like salvinia and frogbit\n- Easy stems like hornwort, water wisteria and Ludwigia repens"}, {"heading": "Setting one up", "text": "Use plain gravel or sand with root tabs, or an aquarium soil for a nutrient boost. Choose low to medium light and run it for 6 to 8 hours on a timer. Plant heavily from the start, since a full tank uses nutrients that would otherwise feed algae. Gentle surface movement keeps CO2 from the air available to plants."}, {"heading": "Maintenance", "text": "Change 20 to 30 percent of the water every week or two, trim dead leaves, and dose a light all-in-one fertilizer once a week if plants look pale. Many keepers add liquid carbon as a mild boost, but it can melt Vallisneria and some mosses. Growth is slow, so expect months rather than weeks for a tank to fill in. Pull out any dying leaves before they rot, and thin floating plants so they don't shade everything below."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "The biggest mistake is a light that's too strong, which grows algae faster than the plants can compete. Others include choosing high-light carpet plants that won't survive without CO2, and burying rhizomes of Anubias and Java fern. Start simple and add plants as you learn what thrives."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do low tech planted tanks need fertilizer?", "a": "Often only a little. Fish waste covers much of what slow plants need, but root tabs for root feeders and a weekly dose of liquid fertilizer help most tanks."}, {"q": "Can you grow a carpet in a low tech tank?", "a": "It's harder. Dwarf sagittaria, some crypts and mosses can carpet slowly, but most popular carpet plants need CO2 and strong light."}, {"q": "How many hours of light for a low tech planted tank?", "a": "Six to eight hours a day is a good range. If algae appears, shorten it before changing anything else."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$melt$g$, $g$Melt$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Melt is when a newly planted aquarium plant suddenly loses leaves, which turn soft, translucent and rot away as the plant adjusts to new conditions.$g$, $g$Melt scares a lot of new plant keepers into throwing out plants that would have recovered. Knowing why it happens and how to handle it saves money and helps you tell normal adjustment from a real problem. Melt usually stops within a couple of weeks.$g$, $g$Aquarium Plant Melt: Why New Plants Melt and How to Save Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why plants melt", "text": "Most aquarium plants are grown emersed at farms, with leaves above water. Once submerged, the plant drops those air-grown leaves and grows new ones shaped for underwater life. Sudden changes in light, temperature, CO2 or water chemistry can also trigger melt in plants that were already submersed. Cryptocoryne are famous for it, but many stem and carpet plants melt too."}, {"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Leaves turning see-through, mushy or brown, often within a week of planting\n- Whole leaves breaking off at the base\n- Healthy-looking roots or rhizome even as leaves die\n- New, often different-shaped leaves appearing within a few weeks"}, {"heading": "How to handle it", "text": "Remove melting leaves promptly so they don't rot and add ammonia to the tank. Leave the roots or rhizome in place, since a crypt that looks completely dead often regrows from the roots in 2 to 6 weeks. Keep conditions stable rather than moving the plant or changing lights. Root tabs near crypts and swords help them rebuild. Avoid dosing extra fertilizer to save a melting plant, since rotting leaves plus extra nutrients is a classic recipe for an algae bloom."}, {"heading": "Reducing melt", "text": "Tissue culture plants, sold in small cups, are often grown submersed or adapt faster and tend to melt less. Plant into a cycled tank with stable parameters, keep CO2 and lighting consistent, and avoid moving plants around once placed. A few weeks of patience is usually all it takes."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Will my plant grow back after melting?", "a": "Usually, yes. As long as the roots, rhizome or crown are firm and healthy, new leaves typically appear within a few weeks."}, {"q": "Why do Cryptocoryne melt so easily?", "a": "Crypts react strongly to any change in light, water or temperature. They often lose every leaf, then regrow if left alone in the substrate."}, {"q": "How long does plant melt last?", "a": "Most melt happens in the first one to two weeks, and recovery with new growth usually takes two to six weeks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$nature-aquarium$g$, $g$Nature Aquarium$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Nature Aquarium is an aquascaping style created by Takashi Amano that recreates natural landscapes, like forests, hills and riverbanks, using plants, wood and stone.$g$, $g$It is the style behind most modern planted tank photos and contests. Understanding its principles helps you design a tank that looks grown rather than placed, even if you never follow the style strictly. Its ideas about balance, depth and restraint apply to almost any planted layout.$g$, $g$Nature Aquarium Style: Takashi Amano's Principles Explained$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Core principles", "text": "Nature Aquarium layouts aim to show a landscape in miniature. They use asymmetry, a clear focal point placed off center, and a sense of depth from foreground to background. Plants are chosen by leaf size and texture so smaller leaves near the back make the tank feel larger. The goal is balance between living things, including fish, plants and bacteria, not just a pretty picture."}, {"heading": "Typical elements", "text": "- Driftwood and rock arranged as one natural structure\n- Mosses and epiphytes softening hard edges\n- Carpet plants in the foreground, stem plants in the background\n- A single school of small fish, such as cardinal tetras or rummy-nose tetras\n- Open space or a sandy path to suggest distance"}, {"heading": "How it differs from other styles", "text": "Iwagumi, often grouped under Nature Aquarium, focuses mainly on stones and minimal plants. Dutch style uses rows of colorful stem plants with little or no hardscape, like a garden. Nature Aquarium sits between them, blending hardscape and dense planting to mimic wild scenes."}, {"heading": "Keeping one going", "text": "Most Nature Aquarium tanks use injected CO2, strong light and regular fertilizer to keep dense growth healthy. Frequent trimming is needed to maintain shape, and big water changes in the first month help prevent algae. A low-tech version is possible with slow plants like mosses, ferns and crypts, but takes longer to mature. Expect a new layout to take two to three months to grow in, and photograph it regularly so you can see what to trim."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Who invented the Nature Aquarium style?", "a": "Japanese aquascaper and photographer Takashi Amano developed and popularized it starting in the 1990s."}, {"q": "What fish suit a Nature Aquarium?", "a": "Small schooling fish like tetras and rasboras are classic choices, often with Amano shrimp and Otocinclus for algae control."}, {"q": "Is Nature Aquarium the same as Iwagumi?", "a": "Not exactly. Iwagumi is a stone-focused style often considered part of the Nature Aquarium family, while Nature Aquarium also covers wood and dense planted layouts."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$pearling$g$, $g$Pearling$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Pearling is when tiny oxygen bubbles form on and rise from aquarium plant leaves because the water is saturated with oxygen from photosynthesis.$g$, $g$It is a satisfying visual sign that plants are photosynthesizing hard, usually under strong light with plenty of CO2. It isn't required for healthy plants, though, so a tank that never pearls can still be doing perfectly well. Treat it as a bonus, not a goal.$g$, $g$Plant Pearling in Aquarium: What It Means and How to Get It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why it happens", "text": "Plants release oxygen as they photosynthesize. Normally that oxygen dissolves into the water. When the water becomes saturated, extra oxygen forms visible bubbles on leaf surfaces and drifts upward. That is why pearling usually shows up several hours into the light period, once oxygen has built up."}, {"heading": "What it does and doesn't mean", "text": "Pearling tells you plants are photosynthesizing strongly and oxygen levels are high, which fish appreciate. But it depends on how saturated the water is, not just on plant health. Heavy surface agitation pushes oxygen out of the water and can stop pearling even in a thriving tank. Low-tech tanks with healthy plants may rarely pearl at all. If you see it every day and then it stops, check your CO2 and light, since a sudden change can be an early sign something drifted."}, {"heading": "Fresh water trick", "text": "Many keepers notice pearling right after a water change. Tap water often holds dissolved gas that comes out as bubbles as it warms, so this is not always real pearling. If bubbles appear everywhere, including glass and decor, it's likely just gas from the new water. True pearling forms mainly on leaves and builds gradually during the day."}, {"heading": "How to encourage it", "text": "- Stable, adequate CO2, usually a lime green drop checker\n- Strong enough light for your plants, on a consistent 6 to 8 hour photoperiod\n- Balanced fertilizer so nothing is limiting growth\n- Moderate surface movement rather than heavy splashing\n\nChase plant health, not bubbles; pearling follows once growth is strong."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is pearling good for my aquarium?", "a": "Yes, it shows strong photosynthesis and high oxygen. But a tank that doesn't pearl can still be healthy."}, {"q": "Why are my plants pearling after a water change?", "a": "Often it's dissolved gas from fresh tap water coming out of solution, not true pearling. Real pearling builds during the light period."}, {"q": "Do you need CO2 for plants to pearl?", "a": "Not strictly, but pearling is much more common with injected CO2 and strong light. Low-tech tanks pearl less often."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$photoperiod$g$, $g$Photoperiod$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Photoperiod is the number of hours each day that your aquarium light is switched on.$g$, $g$It is one of the simplest settings to control and one of the biggest factors in algae. Too long and algae takes over; too short and plants struggle. A steady schedule on a timer also gives fish a reliable day and night rhythm.$g$, $g$Aquarium Photoperiod: How Many Hours of Light Do You Need?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Recommended hours", "text": "- Fish-only tanks: about 6 to 10 hours, mostly for viewing\n- Low-tech planted tanks: about 6 to 8 hours\n- High-tech planted tanks: about 6 to 8 hours at high intensity\n- New planted tanks: start around 6 hours and add 30 minutes every week or two if algae stays away\n\nIntensity matters as much as duration: a bright light for 6 hours can deliver more energy than a dim one for 10."}, {"heading": "Use a timer", "text": "A simple outlet timer or a light with a built-in schedule keeps the photoperiod identical every day, which plants and fish handle better than random hours. Many modern lights include sunrise and sunset ramps, which reduce startling fish when lights snap on."}, {"heading": "Split photoperiods", "text": "Some keepers run a siesta, such as 4 hours on, 3 to 4 hours off, then 4 hours on. The idea is that algae is set back during the break while plants recover. Results are mixed, and it's debated in the hobby, but it can help if you want the tank lit in both morning and evening."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Leaving lights on 12 or more hours to enjoy the tank is the most common cause of algae in beginner tanks. Others include sunlight from a nearby window adding uncontrolled hours, and changing the schedule often. If algae appears, cutting the photoperiod by 1 to 2 hours is a smart first step."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How many hours should aquarium lights be on?", "a": "Most tanks do well with 6 to 8 hours a day. Fish-only tanks can go a bit longer if algae isn't a problem."}, {"q": "Is it bad to leave aquarium lights on all night?", "a": "Yes. Fish need darkness to rest, and long photoperiods fuel algae. Use a timer to give a consistent day and night."}, {"q": "Do aquarium plants need 12 hours of light?", "a": "No. Planted tanks grow well on 6 to 8 hours of good light, and longer periods usually help algae more than plants."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$rhizome-plant$g$, $g$Rhizome Plant$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$A rhizome plant is an aquarium plant, such as Anubias, Java fern or Bucephalandra, that grows from a thick horizontal stem called a rhizome.$g$, $g$Rhizome plants are some of the toughest, most beginner-friendly plants in the hobby, thriving in low light without CO2. They need different planting from typical plants, though, since burying the rhizome is the most common way people kill them. Because they attach to decor, they also work in tanks with bare bottoms or sand.$g$, $g$Rhizome Aquarium Plants: How to Plant Anubias and Java Fern$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common rhizome plants", "text": "- Anubias, including nana, nana petite and barteri\n- Java fern and its varieties\n- Bucephalandra, often called buce\n- Bolbitis, the African water fern\n\nThey are slow growing, have tough leaves most fish won't eat, and handle a wide range of water conditions."}, {"heading": "How to plant them", "text": "Keep the rhizome above the substrate. Tie it to driftwood or rock with cotton thread or fishing line, glue it with gel superglue, or wedge it into a crevice. You can push the roots into gravel as long as the rhizome sits on top. Within a few weeks the roots grip the surface on their own. Remove tight bands or lead weights from store plants, since they can crush the rhizome over time."}, {"heading": "Care and propagation", "text": "Low to moderate light and a weekly dose of liquid fertilizer are usually enough. To propagate, cut the rhizome with a sharp blade into pieces that each have several leaves, then attach them separately. Java fern also produces plantlets on its leaves. They tolerate temperatures from about 68 to 82°F."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "- Rotting rhizome: soft, mushy and smelly, usually from burying it; cut away the rot and reattach the healthy part\n- Algae on leaves: slow leaves collect green spot and other algae, so place them in shadier spots\n- Holes or yellowing: often potassium or general nutrient shortage\n- Very slow growth: normal, so don't overdose fertilizer to speed it up"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can you bury the rhizome of Anubias?", "a": "No. A buried rhizome tends to rot. Keep it above the substrate and let the roots anchor into gravel or wood."}, {"q": "How do I attach Anubias to driftwood?", "a": "Tie it on with cotton thread or fishing line, or use a gel superglue on the rhizome. The roots will grip within a few weeks."}, {"q": "Do rhizome plants need CO2?", "a": "No. They grow well in low-tech tanks with low to moderate light and modest fertilizer."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$root-tabs$g$, $g$Root Tabs$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Root tabs are small fertilizer capsules or tablets pushed into the substrate to feed aquarium plants that draw nutrients through their roots.$g$, $g$Heavy root feeders like Amazon swords, Cryptocoryne and Vallisneria often struggle in plain gravel or sand, which has almost no nutrients. Root tabs put food right where the roots are, which helps these plants grow full and green without dosing more into the water.$g$, $g$Root Tabs for Aquarium Plants: How to Use Them and How Often$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Plants that need them", "text": "- Amazon swords and other Echinodorus\n- Cryptocoryne\n- Vallisneria and sagittaria\n- Aponogeton and other bulb plants\n- Carpets and stem plants in inert substrate also benefit\n\nFloating plants, mosses and rhizome plants attached to decor feed from the water and don't need them."}, {"heading": "How to use them", "text": "Push each tab deep into the substrate, close to the plant's roots, and cover the hole so it doesn't release into the water. A common spacing is one tab every 4 to 6 inches across planted areas, or one or two near each large sword. Long tweezers make it easy. Replace them about every 2 to 3 months, since most release nutrients slowly over that time. Hold the tab with tweezers until it is fully buried."}, {"heading": "Signs plants need them", "text": "Yellowing older leaves, slow growth and small new leaves on root feeders are classic signs. Swords and crypts that melt and recover poorly in sand or gravel often turn around within a few weeks of adding tabs. If yellowing shows on new leaves instead, the shortage is more likely iron, which liquid fertilizer fixes faster."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Placing tabs too shallow can cause an ammonia or nutrient spike in the water and feed algae. Using too many at once in a small tank can do the same. Nutrient-rich aquarium soils often don't need tabs for the first 6 to 12 months. Most tabs are safe with shrimp, but avoid products containing copper."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How often should I put root tabs in my aquarium?", "a": "Usually every 2 to 3 months, depending on the product and how heavily planted the tank is."}, {"q": "Are root tabs safe for fish and shrimp?", "a": "Yes, when buried properly and used as directed. Avoid any with copper if you keep shrimp."}, {"q": "Do I need root tabs with aquarium soil?", "a": "Not at first. Most soils supply enough nutrients for 6 to 12 months, after which root tabs keep heavy feeders healthy."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$seiryu-stone$g$, $g$Seiryu Stone$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Seiryu stone is a blue-gray limestone with white and lighter veins, prized for its jagged, mountain-like look in aquascapes, especially Iwagumi layouts.$g$, $g$It is one of the most striking hardscape rocks, but it contains calcium carbonate and slowly raises pH and hardness. That suits many livebearers and Malawi cichlids but can be a problem for soft-water fish and shrimp like Caridina crystal reds.$g$, $g$Seiryu Stone in Aquarium: Does It Raise pH? How to Use It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Effect on water", "text": "Seiryu stone is a form of limestone, so it slowly dissolves and adds carbonate and calcium. In practice it often raises GH and KH by a degree or two and pushes pH up a few tenths, depending on how much rock you use, your water, and whether you use an acidic soil. Test your water after setup and again after a few weeks to see the real effect."}, {"heading": "Who it suits", "text": "- Good: livebearers, Neocaridina shrimp, goldfish, rift lake cichlids and most community fish in hard or neutral water\n- Use with care: soft-water fish like many tetras, wild bettas, discus and Apistogramma\n- Avoid or pair with acidic soil and reverse osmosis water: Caridina shrimp, which need soft, stable water"}, {"heading": "Preparing and placing it", "text": "Scrub the rock with a stiff brush and water to remove dirt; skip soaps. Place heavy pieces directly on the glass or a base layer before adding substrate. Bury the base of each rock partly so it looks rooted in the landscape, and angle the stripes the same way on every stone for a natural, layered look."}, {"heading": "Alternatives", "text": "If you want a similar look without the hardness increase, inert gray rocks such as slate or gray granite-type stones are options; always run the vinegar test before buying. Some keepers seal Seiryu with a clear epoxy coating, but this changes the surface and is not commonly recommended."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does Seiryu stone raise pH?", "a": "Yes, usually slightly. It contains calcium carbonate, so it gradually raises pH, KH and GH."}, {"q": "Is Seiryu stone safe for shrimp?", "a": "It's fine for Neocaridina like cherry shrimp, which like harder water. Caridina shrimp need soft water, so use it carefully or choose an inert rock."}, {"q": "How do I test if a rock will change my water?", "a": "Put a few drops of vinegar on it. Fizzing shows carbonate, which means it will raise hardness and pH."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$spider-wood$g$, $g$Spider Wood$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Spider wood is a light, branching aquarium driftwood made from shrub roots, with thin twisting limbs that look like tree roots or spider legs.$g$, $g$Its fine, airy branches make it one of the easiest ways to build tree-like and jungle-style layouts. It does need prep, though: it floats, leaches tannins and often grows a white fuzz at first, all of which surprise new keepers.$g$, $g$Spider Wood for Aquariums: How to Prep, Sink and Use It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Preparing it", "text": "Rinse and scrub off loose dirt, then soak the wood in a bucket for several days to a few weeks, changing the water as it turns brown. Boiling smaller pieces for an hour or two speeds up sinking and removes more tannins. Spider wood is light and may float for a while; weigh it down with rocks, or attach it to a slate base with screws or glue."}, {"heading": "White fungus and tannins", "text": "A white, cottony or slimy film often appears on new wood within the first weeks. It is a harmless bacterial and fungal biofilm that snails, shrimp and Otocinclus happily eat, and it fades on its own. Tannins tint the water tea-colored and lower pH slightly. Activated carbon and water changes clear it if you prefer clear water, or enjoy it for a blackwater look."}, {"heading": "Using it in a scape", "text": "- Combine several pieces to create a larger tree or root structure\n- Anchor branch bases in rock or substrate so they look rooted\n- Tie mosses, Anubias or Bucephalandra onto branches\n- Trim branches that block access for maintenance\n- Leave swimming room under the canopy"}, {"heading": "Common issues", "text": "Spider wood is softer than many hardwoods and can break down over a few years, especially thin branches. Remove pieces that turn mushy. The thin tips can be sharp, so take care with long-finned fish like bettas. Inspect wood every few months and check under the bark for soft spots."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is the white fuzz on spider wood dangerous?", "a": "No. It's a harmless biofilm that usually disappears in a few weeks, and shrimp and snails eat it."}, {"q": "How long does spider wood take to sink?", "a": "Anywhere from a few days to a few weeks of soaking. Boiling or weighing it down speeds things up."}, {"q": "Does spider wood lower pH?", "a": "Slightly. Its tannins can reduce pH a little, especially in soft water with low KH."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$stem-plant$g$, $g$Stem Plant$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$A stem plant is an aquarium plant that grows leaves along an upright central stem and is propagated by cutting and replanting the tops.$g$, $g$Stem plants grow fast, fill backgrounds and midgrounds quickly and pull lots of nutrients from the water, which helps keep algae down. They range from easy beginner plants to demanding red species that need CO2 and strong light. Regular trimming is what keeps them dense and attractive.$g$, $g$Aquarium Stem Plants: How to Plant, Trim and Propagate Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Popular stem plants", "text": "- Easy: water wisteria, Ludwigia repens, Hygrophila polysperma, Limnophila sessiliflora, Bacopa caroliniana\n- Moderate: Rotala rotundifolia, Hygrophila pinnatifida, Pogostemon stellatus 'Octopus'\n- Demanding: Rotala macrandra, Ludwigia senegalensis, Alternanthera reineckii"}, {"heading": "How to plant them", "text": "Remove any lead weights or rock wool from the stems. Strip the lowest one or two leaf nodes and push each stem 1 to 2 inches into the substrate with tweezers, one or two stems per hole. Spacing stems an inch or so apart gives each one light and makes a fuller group. Planting in groups of a single species looks more natural than scattering singles. Expect a little melt in the first two weeks as farm-grown stems adjust."}, {"heading": "Trimming and propagation", "text": "When stems reach the surface, cut the top section and replant it, which becomes a new plant. The cut stem left behind will sprout side shoots, making the group bushier. Trimming the same group in steps of different heights creates a rounded bush shape. Remove leggy, bare bottoms by replanting the tops and pulling the old stems."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "- Leggy stems with bare bases: not enough light reaching the bottom\n- Pale new leaves: iron or other nutrient shortage\n- Stunted or twisted tips: often CO2 or calcium issues\n- Melting after planting: normal for emersed-grown stems; trim back and wait If a whole group struggles, try a brighter spot in the tank before adding more fertilizer."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How do you propagate stem plants?", "a": "Cut the top few inches and plant them in the substrate. Both the cutting and the remaining stem will continue growing."}, {"q": "Why are my stem plants losing their bottom leaves?", "a": "Usually the lower leaves aren't getting enough light, often from crowding or tall growth shading them. Trim and replant the tops."}, {"q": "Do stem plants need CO2?", "a": "Many easy stems grow fine without it. Red and compact species generally need CO2 and stronger light to look their best."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$walstad-method$g$, $g$Walstad Method$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$The Walstad method is a low-tech planted tank approach that uses ordinary organic potting soil under gravel, dense planting and little or no filtration.$g$, $g$Named for ecologist Diana Walstad, it lets plants and soil microbes handle much of the waste, which cuts down on equipment and water changes. It can be very stable once established, but it needs careful setup and patience in the first months.$g$, $g$Walstad Method Tank: How to Set Up a Natural Planted Tank$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it works", "text": "Soil releases nutrients and CO2 as it slowly breaks down, feeding plants without injected CO2 or much fertilizer. Plants absorb ammonia directly and compete with algae for nutrients. Floating plants handle surface nutrients and shade the tank. The result is a self-balancing system with modest fish stocking."}, {"heading": "Setting one up", "text": "- Use about 1 inch of organic potting soil without added fertilizers or wetting agents; sift out wood chips\n- Cap it with 1 to 1.5 inches of fine gravel or sand\n- Plant heavily on day one with a mix of stems, floaters and rooted plants\n- Fill slowly over a plate to avoid stirring up soil\n- Use moderate light and add fish gradually after several weeks Choose a spot without direct sunlight, which can tip the balance toward algae."}, {"heading": "Maintenance", "text": "Many Walstad tanks get water changes only every few months, mainly topping off evaporation and removing excess plant growth. Some keepers use a small filter or powerhead for circulation, which is fine. Stock lightly, since the system relies on plants to process waste."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Using soil with added fertilizer can cause ammonia spikes that kill fish in the first weeks. Planting too lightly invites algae. Deep vacuuming or uprooting plants releases soil into the water. Results vary by soil, water and plants, so it isn't fully hands-off, and the method's minimal water change approach is debated for heavily stocked tanks."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What soil is used for the Walstad method?", "a": "Plain organic potting soil without added fertilizers, moisture crystals or wetting agents. Sift out large bark and wood pieces."}, {"q": "Do Walstad tanks need a filter?", "a": "Not strictly, but many keepers use a small filter or powerhead for circulation. Plants do most of the biological work."}, {"q": "How long does a Walstad tank take to establish?", "a": "Usually 1 to 3 months. Expect an initial ammonia release and some cloudiness, so add fish slowly."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$blackwater$g$, $g$Blackwater$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Blackwater is soft, acidic water stained tea-brown by tannins from leaves and wood, recreating rivers and streams that flow through tropical forest floors.$g$, $g$Many popular fish, including tetras, Apistogramma, bettas and rasboras, evolved in blackwater habitats. Recreating it can improve their color, comfort and breeding, but the soft, low-pH conditions are less stable and need careful monitoring. It is a natural, attractive look as long as you test water regularly.$g$, $g$Blackwater Aquarium Setup: Tannins, pH and Best Fish$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What makes water blackwater", "text": "In the wild, blackwater rivers like the Rio Negro have very low hardness, pH often between 4 and 6, and lots of dissolved tannins and humic substances from decaying leaves. In aquariums, keepers aim for pH around 5.5 to 6.8, low KH and GH, and visibly tinted water."}, {"heading": "How to create it", "text": "- Add driftwood, Indian almond leaves, alder cones or other aquarium-safe botanicals\n- Start with soft water, often reverse osmosis or rainwater mixed with tap\n- Use peat in the filter to soften and acidify water\n- Let leaf litter build up on the bottom as cover and grazing for fish and shrimp\n- Remove activated carbon, which strips tannins"}, {"heading": "Fish that benefit", "text": "Cardinal tetras, rummy-nose tetras, chocolate gouramis, wild bettas, Apistogramma, discus and many rasboras do well in blackwater. Many captive-bred fish adapt to tap water but still show better color and spawning behavior in soft, tannin-rich water. Leaf litter also grows biofilm and microorganisms that fry and shrimp graze on, which helps raise young fish."}, {"heading": "Things to watch", "text": "Low KH makes pH swing more easily, and a pH crash below about 5 can stall biological filtration and stress fish. Test pH and KH weekly and change water with properly prepared water. Leaves break down and add organic load, so replace them as they fall apart. Blackwater isn't suitable for livebearers, African cichlids or Neocaridina shrimp, which prefer harder water."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is blackwater good for all fish?", "a": "No. It suits soft-water species from South America and Southeast Asia. Livebearers, goldfish and rift lake cichlids prefer harder water."}, {"q": "Do tannins harm fish?", "a": "No, tannins are generally harmless and often beneficial for soft-water species. They only lower pH a bit and tint the water."}, {"q": "How do I make my aquarium water brown naturally?", "a": "Add driftwood, Indian almond leaves or alder cones. The tannins will gradually tint the water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$buffer$g$, $g$Buffer$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$A buffer is a substance in water, mainly carbonate and bicarbonate, that resists changes in pH by neutralizing added acids or bases.$g$, $g$Buffering capacity, measured as KH, is what keeps aquarium pH steady day after day. Low buffering lets pH swing or crash, which stresses fish and can stall the beneficial bacteria in your filter, so understanding buffers helps prevent hard-to-diagnose problems.$g$, $g$Aquarium pH Buffer: What KH Does and How to Raise It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How buffering works", "text": "Fish waste, decaying food and the nitrogen cycle all produce acids. Carbonate and bicarbonate absorb those acids, keeping pH stable until the buffer gets used up. Once KH runs low, pH can drop quickly, sometimes overnight. That's why stable KH matters more for most fish than hitting an exact pH number."}, {"heading": "Recommended KH levels", "text": "- Most community tanks: about 3 to 8 dKH (roughly 50 to 140 ppm)\n- Soft-water species and Caridina shrimp: around 0 to 2 dKH, with extra care\n- Livebearers and goldfish: about 8 to 12 dKH\n- African rift lake cichlids: 10 dKH or higher\n\nTest KH every week or two, especially in older tanks with long gaps between water changes."}, {"heading": "Ways to add buffering", "text": "Regular water changes with tap water usually restore KH. Crushed coral or aragonite in the filter or substrate dissolves slowly to raise KH and pH. Baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) raises KH quickly; a common guide is about 1 teaspoon per 50 gallons to raise roughly 1 dKH, added slowly over a day or two. Commercial buffer products work too, but follow the label and avoid big jumps."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Chasing pH with chemicals that lower or raise it without addressing KH leads to swings that are worse than a stable but imperfect number. Don't combine many different pH products. Raise KH gradually, by no more than 1 to 2 dKH a day, and remember that CO2 injection lowers pH temporarily without changing KH."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is the difference between KH and pH?", "a": "pH measures how acidic or basic water is, while KH measures how much buffer the water has to resist pH changes."}, {"q": "How do I raise KH in my aquarium?", "a": "Add crushed coral or aragonite, dose small amounts of baking soda, or use a commercial buffer. Increase slowly and retest."}, {"q": "Why does my aquarium pH keep dropping?", "a": "Usually KH is getting used up between water changes. More frequent water changes or a source of carbonate stops the drop."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$chloramine$g$, $g$Chloramine$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Chloramine is a long-lasting disinfectant made by combining chlorine and ammonia, used by many city water systems in place of plain chlorine.$g$, $g$Chloramine kills fish and beneficial bacteria just like chlorine, but it doesn't evaporate when water sits out, so the old trick of aging tap water doesn't work. Many US utilities use it, which makes the right water conditioner essential. A good conditioner handles it easily.$g$, $g$Chloramine in Aquarium Water: How to Remove It Safely$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why it matters", "text": "Chloramine damages fish gills and skin, causing burns, gasping and sudden deaths, even at low concentrations. It also kills the nitrifying bacteria in your filter, which can restart the nitrogen cycle. Unlike chlorine, it stays in water for days or weeks, so it reaches your tank unless you neutralize it. A sudden fish die-off right after a water change is a classic sign that chloramine or chlorine got through."}, {"heading": "How to remove it", "text": "- Use a water conditioner labeled to remove chloramine and treat the full volume of new water\n- Look for a conditioner that also detoxifies the ammonia left over after the chlorine is neutralized\n- Add conditioner to the bucket before fish contact the water, or to the tank before refilling with a hose\n- Carbon block filters designed for chloramine can help but aren't a substitute in most aquarium setups"}, {"heading": "The leftover ammonia", "text": "When a conditioner breaks the chlorine-ammonia bond, the ammonia stays behind, usually around 0.5 to 1 ppm in treated tap water. Conditioners that bind ammonia make it temporarily less toxic, and a cycled filter then processes it. Test kits may still read ammonia after treatment, since many can't tell bound from free ammonia."}, {"heading": "How to know if your water has it", "text": "Check your water utility's annual water quality report or call them. Many large cities in the US use chloramine, and some switch seasonally. If you're not sure, treat as though chloramine is present, since a chloramine-rated conditioner also removes chlorine."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does chloramine evaporate if water sits out?", "a": "No. Chloramine is very stable and won't leave water by standing or aerating. Use a conditioner rated for chloramine."}, {"q": "How do I know if my tap water has chloramine?", "a": "Check your utility's water quality report or ask them directly. Many US cities use it."}, {"q": "Is chloramine harmful to shrimp and snails?", "a": "Yes. It's toxic to fish, invertebrates and the beneficial bacteria in your filter. Always treat new water before adding it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$chlorine$g$, $g$Chlorine$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Chlorine is a disinfectant added to most city tap water to kill germs, and it is toxic to fish, invertebrates and beneficial filter bacteria.$g$, $g$Untreated tap water is one of the most common causes of sudden fish deaths and failed cycles in new tanks. Neutralizing chlorine every time you add water is a simple habit that protects both your fish and the bacteria that keep your water safe.$g$, $g$Chlorine in Aquarium Water: How to Remove It for Fish$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why chlorine is dangerous", "text": "Chlorine burns delicate gill tissue and skin, causing fish to gasp, dart around or die within hours. Even small amounts, around 0.2 to 0.5 ppm, can harm sensitive species. It also kills nitrifying bacteria in the filter, which can lead to ammonia and nitrite spikes after a water change."}, {"heading": "How to remove it", "text": "- Use a water conditioner or dechlorinator and dose for the full volume of new water, not just the tank\n- Add it to the bucket before pouring, or to the tank before refilling with a hose\n- Aging water: plain chlorine evaporates if water sits uncovered for 24 to 48 hours with aeration, but this doesn't remove chloramine\n- Most conditioners also remove heavy metals, a useful bonus"}, {"heading": "Chlorine vs chloramine", "text": "Many utilities use chloramine instead, or switch between the two. Chloramine doesn't evaporate and releases ammonia when treated. A conditioner that handles chloramine works for both, so it's the safe default if you're unsure what your city uses."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Forgetting to dose when topping off evaporation or using a hose direct from the tap is a frequent slip. Others include dosing for only the replaced water when filling from a hose directly into the tank, and rinsing filter media in tap water, which kills bacteria. Rinse media in old tank water instead. Well water usually has no chlorine but should still be tested for other issues."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does it take for chlorine to evaporate from tap water?", "a": "Usually 24 to 48 hours with the water uncovered and aerated. It won't work if your water contains chloramine."}, {"q": "Can I put tap water straight into my fish tank?", "a": "Not without a water conditioner if it contains chlorine or chloramine. Treat it first, then add it at a similar temperature."}, {"q": "Is chlorine harmful to shrimp?", "a": "Yes. Shrimp and snails are just as sensitive as fish, and often more so, so always treat new water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$co2$g$, $g$CO2 (Carbon Dioxide)$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$CO2, or carbon dioxide, is the gas aquarium plants absorb from the water to build tissue through photosynthesis, and it is often the limiting nutrient in planted tanks.$g$, $g$Every tank has some CO2 from fish breathing and decay, which is enough for slow, easy plants. Injecting extra CO2 can transform a planted tank's growth and color, but it has to be balanced with light and fertilizer, and overdosing can suffocate fish within hours.$g$, $g$CO2 in a Planted Aquarium: Do You Need It and How to Dose It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Do you need added CO2?", "text": "Low-tech tanks with low to moderate light and plants like anubias, java fern, cryptocorynes and mosses do fine on the CO2 that fish and bacteria produce. Added CO2 makes the biggest difference in tanks with strong lighting and demanding stem plants or carpeting plants, where CO2 is usually what runs out first.\n\nIf you raise the light without raising CO2, plants can't keep up, and algae takes the leftover light and nutrients. That mismatch is the most common reason high-light tanks turn into algae farms."}, {"heading": "How CO2 is added", "text": "- Pressurized systems: a cylinder, regulator, solenoid, bubble counter and diffuser or reactor. This is the most stable and controllable option, and the usual choice for tanks over 20 gallons.\n- DIY yeast or citric acid reactors: cheap and fine for small tanks, but output varies and can surge.\n- Liquid carbon products: a glutaraldehyde-based supplement that gives a modest boost. Some plants, like vallisneria and some mosses, melt from it, and it should be dosed carefully around shrimp."}, {"heading": "Dialing it in safely", "text": "Most planted tanks aim for roughly 20 to 30 ppm. A drop checker filled with 4 dKH reference solution turns green in that range, blue when CO2 is low and yellow when it is too high. Because drop checkers lag by about two hours, watch your fish as you raise the rate.\n\nRun CO2 on a timer so it starts one to two hours before the lights and shuts off about an hour before they go off. Keep gentle surface ripple so oxygen can still get in. If fish gasp at the surface, turn CO2 off, add an airstone and do a water change."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Raising the bubble rate quickly instead of in small steps over several days.\n- Running CO2 all night, when plants aren't using it.\n- Measuring CO2 with the pH and KH chart when the water has tannins or buffers, which gives misleading numbers.\n- Ignoring a nearly empty cylinder, which can dump gas suddenly on some regulators."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is CO2 bad for fish?", "a": "At the 20 to 30 ppm most planted tanks target, healthy fish are fine. Problems start when levels climb much higher, which causes gasping, lethargy and can kill fish quickly."}, {"q": "Should I turn off CO2 at night?", "a": "Yes. Plants don't photosynthesize in the dark, so CO2 at night only builds up and lowers oxygen. A solenoid on a timer handles this automatically."}, {"q": "Can I have a planted tank without CO2?", "a": "Absolutely. Low-tech tanks with modest light and hardy plants grow slower but stay healthy and need far less fine-tuning."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dechlorinator$g$, $g$Dechlorinator$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$A dechlorinator, also called water conditioner, is a liquid or powder that instantly neutralizes the chlorine and chloramine that cities add to tap water to keep it safe.$g$, $g$Chlorine and chloramine burn fish gills and kill the beneficial bacteria in your filter, so untreated tap water can undo a cycled tank in one water change. Dosing a conditioner every time you add tap water is one of the simplest, most important habits in fishkeeping.$g$, $g$Water Conditioner for Fish Tanks: How Much to Use and When$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Chlorine vs chloramine", "text": "Many cities have switched from chlorine to chloramine, which is chlorine bound to ammonia. Chlorine alone will gas off if water sits out for a day or two, but chloramine will not, so the old trick of letting water stand no longer works for most tap water.\n\nWhen a conditioner breaks chloramine apart, it releases a small amount of ammonia. A cycled filter handles that easily, and many conditioners also bind ammonia temporarily so it's less harmful. Check your water utility's annual report to see which disinfectant it uses."}, {"heading": "How to dose it", "text": "- Dose for the volume of new water you're adding, not the whole tank, unless the label says otherwise.\n- Add it to the bucket before pouring, or to the tank just before refilling with a hose.\n- Slight overdosing is generally safe with common conditioners, and many keepers add a little extra when chloramine levels are high.\n- Use it for every top-off and water change that uses tap water, including water for rinsing filter media."}, {"heading": "What else conditioners do", "text": "Some formulas also detoxify heavy metals like copper and zinc from old plumbing, add a slime coat aid, or temporarily bind ammonia, nitrite and nitrate. The binding is temporary, roughly a day, and it does not remove those compounds or replace water changes. Test kits for ammonia may still show a reading after dosing, since salicylate tests detect bound ammonia too."}, {"heading": "When you don't need it", "text": "RO water, distilled water and rainwater collected cleanly contain no chlorine, so they don't need conditioner. Well water usually isn't chlorinated either, but it's worth testing for metals, nitrate and hardness before using it."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long after adding water conditioner can I add fish?", "a": "Most conditioners work within a minute or two, so the water is safe almost immediately. Match the temperature before adding fish."}, {"q": "Can you put too much water conditioner in a fish tank?", "a": "Small overdoses are usually harmless, but large overdoses can lower oxygen. Stick close to the label dose, up to about double for heavy chloramine."}, {"q": "Does letting tap water sit remove chloramine?", "a": "No. Chlorine evaporates over a day or two, but chloramine is stable and must be neutralized with a conditioner."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dissolved-oxygen$g$, $g$Dissolved Oxygen$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Dissolved oxygen is the amount of oxygen gas held in aquarium water, which fish, invertebrates and filter bacteria all need to breathe and stay alive.$g$, $g$Oxygen problems often look like disease: fish gasping, hanging at the surface or dying overnight. Warm water, heavy stocking, medications and CO2 injection all lower oxygen, so knowing what drains it and how to restore it can save a whole tank.$g$, $g$Low Oxygen in a Fish Tank: Signs, Causes and Quick Fixes$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs of low oxygen", "text": "- Fish gasping or crowding at the surface, especially near the filter outflow\n- Rapid gill movement and lethargy\n- Shrimp and snails climbing to the waterline\n- Losses that happen overnight or early in the morning, when plants have been using oxygen all night"}, {"heading": "What lowers oxygen", "text": "Temperature matters most: water at 86 F holds noticeably less oxygen than water at 72 F, while fish need more of it as they warm up. Overstocking, uneaten food and decay, heavy medication, and injected CO2 also cut oxygen.\n\nPlants add oxygen during the day but use it at night, so a densely planted tank with little surface movement can run low before dawn. A film of oil or protein on the surface also blocks gas exchange."}, {"heading": "How to raise it", "text": "Oxygen enters water at the surface, so surface movement is what counts. Point the filter outflow or a powerhead so the surface ripples, add an airstone, or lower the water level slightly so a hang-on-back filter splashes. Bubbles help mostly by moving water, not by the bubbles themselves.\n\nIn an emergency, do a 25 to 50 percent water change with temperature-matched, dechlorinated water and add aeration immediately. Healthy tanks generally sit around 6 to 8 mg/L; below about 5 mg/L, many fish start to struggle."}, {"heading": "Prevention", "text": "Stock sensibly, avoid overfeeding, keep filters clean and flowing, and add an airstone during heat waves or when treating with medication. Battery air pumps are worth keeping for power outages."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do I need an air pump if I have a filter?", "a": "Not always. If your filter ripples the surface well, oxygen is usually fine. An air pump is cheap insurance for warm, heavily stocked or medicated tanks."}, {"q": "Why are my fish gasping at the top of the tank?", "a": "The most common cause is low oxygen, but ammonia or nitrite poisoning causes the same behavior. Add aeration, then test your water."}, {"q": "Do plants add oxygen to an aquarium?", "a": "Yes, during the day when lit. At night they consume oxygen, so surface movement is still important in planted tanks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$gh$g$, $g$GH (General Hardness)$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$GH, or general hardness, measures how much calcium and magnesium is dissolved in your aquarium water, and is reported in degrees (dGH) or parts per million.$g$, $g$GH affects which fish, shrimp, snails and plants will thrive, how well fish handle osmotic stress, and whether invertebrates can build healthy shells. Knowing your tap water's GH tells you which species will be easy to keep and which will need adjusted water.$g$, $g$GH in Aquariums: What General Hardness Means and Ideal Levels$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Reading the numbers", "text": "One dGH equals about 17.9 ppm. Rough ranges:\n\n- 0 to 4 dGH: very soft\n- 4 to 8 dGH: soft\n- 8 to 12 dGH: moderately hard\n- 12 to 18 dGH: hard\n- Over 18 dGH: very hard\n\nGH is not the same as KH. KH measures carbonates that buffer pH, while GH measures calcium and magnesium. They often rise together in tap water but can be very different, especially in remineralized RO water."}, {"heading": "What different species need", "text": "Soft-water fish like many tetras, rasboras, discus and wild bettas do best around 2 to 8 dGH, especially for breeding. Livebearers, African rift lake cichlids and many rainbowfish prefer 10 to 20 dGH or more. Neocaridina shrimp like roughly 6 to 10 dGH, Caridina shrimp prefer about 4 to 6, and snails need moderate GH to grow strong shells.\n\nMany captive-bred fish adapt to a wider range than their wild relatives, so stability usually matters more than hitting an exact number."}, {"heading": "How to raise or lower GH", "text": "- To raise: use a GH remineralizer, crushed coral or aragonite, or wonder shell style mineral blocks in small amounts.\n- To lower: mix tap water with RO, distilled or rainwater. Peat and driftwood lower KH and pH a little but have little effect on GH.\n- Change GH slowly, no more than a couple of degrees per day, especially with shrimp."}, {"heading": "Testing tips", "text": "Liquid drop kits for GH count drops until the color changes, with each drop equal to one degree. Test your tap water as well as your tank, since evaporation and top-offs with tap water slowly raise GH over time."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is a good GH level for a freshwater aquarium?", "a": "Most community fish do well between 4 and 12 dGH. Check the specific needs of your species, since soft-water and hard-water fish differ a lot."}, {"q": "What is the difference between GH and KH?", "a": "GH measures calcium and magnesium, while KH measures carbonates and bicarbonates that stabilize pH. They are separate tests."}, {"q": "How do I lower GH in my fish tank?", "a": "Dilute with RO, distilled or rainwater during water changes. Water softener pillows and household softeners swap minerals for sodium and aren't a good long-term fix."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$nitrate$g$, $g$Nitrate$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Nitrate (NO3) is the end product of the nitrogen cycle, made when filter bacteria convert nitrite, and it slowly builds up in aquariums until removed.$g$, $g$Nitrate is far less toxic than ammonia or nitrite, but high levels stress fish, stunt growth, weaken immunity and feed algae. Tracking nitrate is the best way to know whether your water change schedule actually keeps up with your stocking and feeding.$g$, $g$Nitrate in a Fish Tank: Safe Levels and How to Lower It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Safe levels", "text": "Most keepers aim to keep nitrate under 20 to 40 ppm, and under 20 ppm for sensitive fish, shrimp and fry. Levels above 80 ppm are a clear sign of overdue maintenance and can cause lethargy, faded color and long-term health problems.\n\nTest your tap water too. Some tap water contains 10 to 40 ppm of nitrate already, which changes how low you can realistically go."}, {"heading": "How to lower nitrate", "text": "- Regular water changes, usually 25 to 50 percent weekly, are the main tool.\n- Vacuum the gravel and clean filters to remove decaying waste.\n- Feed less, and only what fish eat in a minute or two.\n- Add plants, especially fast growers and floating plants, which use nitrate as fertilizer.\n- If tap water is high in nitrate, mix in RO water."}, {"heading": "Planted tanks", "text": "In planted tanks, nitrate is a nutrient, not just a waste product. Many planted tank keepers target 10 to 20 ppm and dose nitrogen fertilizer if it drops to zero, since nitrogen-starved plants yellow from the older leaves first. Zero nitrate in a heavily planted tank isn't a goal by itself."}, {"heading": "Testing tips", "text": "Liquid nitrate kits are much more reliable than strips, but one common kit needs its second bottle shaken hard for 30 seconds or more, or it reads falsely low. Read the color in good light against a white background. A sudden jump usually means something died or the tank was overfed."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What level of nitrate is toxic to fish?", "a": "Long-term exposure above about 40 to 80 ppm harms most fish, and sudden very high levels over 100 ppm can be dangerous. Sensitive species need it lower."}, {"q": "How do I get nitrate to zero?", "a": "In most tanks you don't need to. Keeping it under 20 ppm with water changes and plants is the realistic, healthy target."}, {"q": "Does a water conditioner remove nitrate?", "a": "No. Some conditioners bind nitrate temporarily, but only water changes, plants or special media actually remove it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$nitrite$g$, $g$Nitrite$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Nitrite (NO2) is a toxic compound made when beneficial bacteria break down ammonia, and it is the middle stage of the aquarium nitrogen cycle.$g$, $g$Nitrite stops a fish's blood from carrying oxygen, so even 1 ppm can kill sensitive fish. Any nitrite reading in an established tank means something has gone wrong with the filter bacteria, and quick action matters. Regular testing during the first months of a new tank is the best way to catch it early.$g$, $g$Nitrite in a Fish Tank: Symptoms of Poisoning and How to Fix It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Where it comes from", "text": "Fish waste and decaying food produce ammonia. One group of bacteria converts ammonia into nitrite, and another converts nitrite into much safer nitrate. In a new tank, the nitrite-eating bacteria grow more slowly, so nitrite often spikes for a couple of weeks during cycling.\n\nIn an established tank, nitrite usually shows up after a filter was replaced or scrubbed in tap water, medication killed bacteria, the tank was suddenly overstocked, or something died and rotted."}, {"heading": "Signs of nitrite poisoning", "text": "- Fish gasping at the surface even with good aeration\n- Tan or brown gills, often called brown blood disease\n- Lethargy, clamped fins and loss of appetite\n- Sudden deaths, especially among smaller or sensitive fish"}, {"heading": "What to do", "text": "Safe nitrite is 0 ppm. If you see any reading:\n\n- Do a 30 to 50 percent water change with dechlorinated, temperature-matched water, and repeat daily until it reads zero.\n- Cut feeding way back or skip a day or two.\n- Add aeration.\n- Aquarium salt at about 1 teaspoon per 5 gallons helps block nitrite uptake through the gills. Skip this with salt-sensitive fish, scaleless catfish, shrimp, snails and many plants.\n- Don't clean or replace the filter media while the bacteria recover."}, {"heading": "Prevention", "text": "Cycle tanks before adding fish, add new fish a few at a time, rinse filter media only in old tank water, and test weekly for the first few months.\n\nIf you move fish to a new tank, bring along a mature sponge or filter media so the bacteria come with them."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does a nitrite spike last?", "a": "During a fishless cycle it often lasts one to three weeks. In an established tank with a minor disruption, it usually clears within a few days with water changes."}, {"q": "What is a safe nitrite level for fish?", "a": "Zero. Anything above about 0.25 ppm stresses fish, and readings of 1 ppm or more are an emergency."}, {"q": "Why do I have nitrite but no ammonia?", "a": "Your ammonia-eating bacteria are working, but the nitrite-eating bacteria haven't caught up yet. It's common during cycling and resolves with time and water changes."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$old-tank-syndrome$g$, $g$Old Tank Syndrome$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Old tank syndrome is the slow buildup of nitrate and acids, and drop in pH and KH, in an aquarium that has gone months or years without proper water changes.$g$, $g$Fish in a neglected tank adapt gradually and may look fine, but new fish die quickly and a sudden big water change can shock the residents. Recognizing old tank syndrome helps you fix it safely instead of causing the very losses you're trying to prevent.$g$, $g$Old Tank Syndrome: Signs, Causes and How to Fix It Safely$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How it develops", "text": "Every day, fish waste becomes nitrate and the nitrogen cycle produces acids. KH buffers those acids, but if water changes are skipped, the buffer slowly runs out and pH falls, sometimes into the 5s. Evaporation and top-offs concentrate dissolved solids, and debris builds up in the gravel and filter.\n\nBecause it happens over months, the existing fish adjust. The water still looks clear, so owners assume all is well."}, {"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- New fish die within days while old fish seem fine\n- Nitrate readings of 80 ppm or more\n- pH well below your tap water, and KH near zero\n- Thick mulm in the substrate, slow plant growth and stubborn algae\n- Fish that are sluggish, faded or breed rarely"}, {"heading": "How to fix it safely", "text": "The goal is to bring the tank back toward your tap water slowly. Start with 10 to 15 percent water changes every two or three days, vacuuming a small section of gravel each time. Test pH before and after; avoid moving it more than about 0.3 to 0.5 in a day.\n\nOver two to four weeks, increase to 25 percent changes as nitrate drops and pH rises. Clean the filter in old tank water, not all at once. Only add new fish after parameters match your tap water and stay stable."}, {"heading": "Prevention", "text": "Weekly water changes of 20 to 30 percent prevent old tank syndrome almost entirely. Testing nitrate, pH and KH once a month will catch slow drift long before it becomes a problem."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Why do new fish die in my old tank?", "a": "Your established fish have adapted to low pH and high nitrate. New fish are coming from clean water, and the sudden difference shocks them."}, {"q": "Can a big water change kill fish?", "a": "In a neglected tank, yes. A large change can swing pH and other values too fast, so use several small changes instead."}, {"q": "Is old tank syndrome real?", "a": "The term is informal, but the underlying problem, gradual pH decline and waste buildup from skipped maintenance, is well documented and common."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$ph-crash$g$, $g$pH Crash$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$A pH crash is a sudden, steep drop in aquarium pH, usually happening after the water's KH buffer is used up and acids are no longer neutralized.$g$, $g$A crash can drop pH by a full point or more in a day, which stresses or kills fish and can stall the filter bacteria, leading to ammonia problems too. Because it's preventable with routine maintenance and simple testing, it's worth understanding before it happens.$g$, $g$pH Crash in Aquariums: Causes, Warning Signs and How to Fix It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why it happens", "text": "The nitrogen cycle and decaying organics constantly produce acid. Carbonates, measured as KH, neutralize that acid and hold pH steady. When KH is low to begin with, or water changes are skipped, the buffer runs out and pH falls fast.\n\nTanks with soft tap water, lots of driftwood or peat, heavy stocking, or CO2 injection with very low KH are most at risk. Once pH drops below about 6, nitrifying bacteria slow down sharply, which can cause ammonia to build up later."}, {"heading": "Warning signs", "text": "- KH test reading 1 to 2 dKH or lower\n- pH noticeably lower than a week ago\n- Fish suddenly lethargic, hiding, gasping or with clamped fins\n- Cloudy water or rising ammonia after a stretch of low pH"}, {"heading": "How to fix it", "text": "Do a 20 to 30 percent water change with dechlorinated tap water, assuming your tap water has reasonable KH. Repeat over a few days rather than all at once so pH rises gradually. Raise KH long term with a small bag of crushed coral or aragonite in the filter, or a measured dose of baking soda: about 1 teaspoon per 50 gallons raises KH roughly 1 to 2 dKH. Add baking soda slowly and retest before adding more.\n\nAvoid pH-down or pH-up chemicals without fixing KH; they cause swings, not stability."}, {"heading": "Prevention", "text": "Keep KH at 3 dKH or higher for most community tanks, test KH every week or two, keep up with water changes, and don't overstock."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What causes a sudden drop in pH in an aquarium?", "a": "Usually the KH buffer runs out, letting natural acids from waste and decay drop pH quickly. Skipped water changes and soft water make it more likely."}, {"q": "How fast can pH crash?", "a": "Once KH is gone, pH can fall a full point or more within a day or two."}, {"q": "Can baking soda fix a pH crash?", "a": "Yes, baking soda raises KH and pH, but add it gradually and retest. Water changes are the safest first step."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$phosphate$g$, $g$Phosphate$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Phosphate (PO4) is a nutrient that enters aquariums through fish food, waste and some tap water, and it feeds both plants and algae.$g$, $g$In a planted tank, phosphate is an essential fertilizer that plants need alongside nitrate and potassium. In a fish-only tank, built-up phosphate often goes hand in hand with algae, so knowing where it comes from helps you control algae without starving plants.$g$, $g$Phosphate in Freshwater Aquariums: Algae Myths and Safe Levels$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Where it comes from", "text": "- Fish food, especially flakes and frozen foods, is the biggest source\n- Fish waste and decaying plants or uneaten food\n- Tap water, since many utilities add phosphate to protect pipes\n- Some fertilizers and pH-adjusting chemicals\n\nTest your tap water. If it reads 1 to 2 ppm, every water change adds phosphate along with the fresh water."}, {"heading": "Phosphate and algae", "text": "For years phosphate was blamed for most algae, and in fish-only tanks with no plants, high phosphate often does fuel growth. In planted tanks the picture is different. Many planted keepers dose 1 to 2 ppm deliberately and see less algae, because healthy plants outcompete it. Algae usually comes from an imbalance of light, CO2 and nutrients rather than one nutrient alone, and this is still debated in the hobby."}, {"heading": "Target levels", "text": "Fish-only tanks: under about 0.5 to 1 ppm is a reasonable goal. Planted tanks: roughly 0.5 to 2 ppm, raised if plants show phosphate deficiency, such as stunted growth and dark, old leaves with spots. Phosphate isn't toxic to fish at aquarium levels."}, {"heading": "How to lower it", "text": "Feed less and rinse frozen food, vacuum the gravel, do regular water changes and add plants. In fish-only tanks, phosphate-removing media in the filter works well. Use RO water for changes if tap water is high.\n\nPhosphate removers work best as a support tool, not a fix for overfeeding. If algae persists even with low phosphate, look at lighting duration and intensity next, since too much light is the more common trigger."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does phosphate cause algae in a freshwater tank?", "a": "It can contribute, especially without plants. In planted tanks, algae is more often caused by too much light or not enough CO2."}, {"q": "Is phosphate harmful to fish?", "a": "Not at normal aquarium levels. The concern is algae, not direct toxicity to fish."}, {"q": "What should phosphate be in a planted tank?", "a": "Most planted tank keepers aim for about 0.5 to 2 ppm, often dosed as part of a fertilizer routine."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$remineralization$g$, $g$Remineralization$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Remineralization is the process of adding measured minerals back into RO, distilled or rainwater so it reaches the exact GH, KH and TDS a species needs.$g$, $g$Pure water has almost no minerals, so fish and especially shrimp can't live in it safely. Remineralizing lets you build water to precise targets, which is the main way keepers succeed with sensitive soft-water shrimp and fish when their tap water doesn't cooperate.$g$, $g$How to Remineralize RO Water for Shrimp and Aquarium Fish$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why pure water needs minerals", "text": "RO and distilled water have GH and KH near zero. Fish and invertebrates need calcium and magnesium for normal body function, and shrimp need them to molt properly. With no KH, pH can also swing wildly. Using pure RO water straight in a tank is a common and avoidable mistake."}, {"heading": "How to do it", "text": "- Collect RO or distilled water in a clean container.\n- Add a remineralizer product formulated for your target: GH-only for Caridina shrimp and soft-water fish, or GH plus KH for Neocaridina and general community tanks.\n- Stir until fully dissolved, then test GH, KH and TDS.\n- Adjust gradually, and write down how much you used per gallon so you can repeat it exactly.\n- Match temperature, then use it for water changes and top-offs."}, {"heading": "Common targets", "text": "- Caridina shrimp, such as crystal reds: GH 4 to 6, KH 0 to 1, TDS about 100 to 160\n- Neocaridina shrimp, such as cherry shrimp: GH 6 to 10, KH 2 to 6, TDS about 150 to 250\n- Soft-water fish like discus or wild bettas: GH 2 to 6, KH 1 to 4\n\nThese are typical hobby ranges; breeders vary."}, {"heading": "Top-offs and mistakes", "text": "Evaporation removes water, not minerals. Top off with plain RO water to replace evaporation, and use remineralized water for water changes, otherwise TDS creeps up. Don't use RO water straight for changes, and avoid making large jumps in GH or TDS, which can cause failed molts in shrimp."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can I put RO water straight into my fish tank?", "a": "Not on its own. RO water lacks minerals and buffer, so it should be remineralized or mixed with tap water first."}, {"q": "What is the best remineralizer for shrimp?", "a": "Choose one made for your shrimp type: GH-only for Caridina and GH plus KH for Neocaridina. Use a TDS meter and GH and KH tests to dial it in."}, {"q": "Do I remineralize top-off water?", "a": "No. Top off evaporation with plain RO, since the minerals stay behind in the tank."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$ro-water$g$, $g$RO Water (Reverse Osmosis)$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$RO water is water pushed through a reverse osmosis membrane that strips out nearly all minerals, chlorine, metals, nitrate and other dissolved contaminants.$g$, $g$RO gives you a blank slate when tap water is too hard, high in nitrate or phosphate, or inconsistent. Keepers of shrimp, discus, wild-type soft-water fish and reef tanks rely on it, but it must always be remineralized or blended before it goes in the tank.$g$, $g$RO Water for Aquariums: Pros, Cons and How to Use It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "When RO is worth it", "text": "- Your tap water is very hard and you keep soft-water species\n- Tap water has high nitrate, phosphate, silicate or metals that cause problems\n- You keep Caridina shrimp, discus or other sensitive fish\n- You want consistent water that doesn't change with the seasons\n\nMost community fish do fine in dechlorinated tap water, so RO isn't necessary for every tank."}, {"heading": "How a unit works", "text": "A home RO unit pushes tap water through sediment and carbon prefilters, then the membrane. RO/DI units add a deionization stage for near-zero TDS. Expect waste water: many units send 2 to 4 gallons down the drain for every gallon made, though that water can go to plants or the garden. Replace prefilters every 6 to 12 months and check output with a TDS meter; rising TDS means the membrane is wearing out."}, {"heading": "Using RO safely", "text": "Never use straight RO for water changes. Either remineralize it to target GH and KH or blend it with tap water. For example, a 50/50 mix of RO and tap water roughly halves your tap's hardness. RO doesn't need dechlorinator, but blends with tap water do. Use plain RO only for topping off evaporation."}, {"heading": "Where to get it", "text": "You can buy a home unit, or buy RO water from fish stores and grocery store refill machines. Store-bought water is fine, but check its TDS, since poorly maintained machines can drift."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is RO water good for fish?", "a": "Yes, once remineralized or blended. Pure RO lacks the minerals fish need and can let pH swing."}, {"q": "Is distilled water the same as RO water?", "a": "They are close. Both are nearly mineral-free and are used the same way in aquariums, as long as you remineralize."}, {"q": "Do I need dechlorinator with RO water?", "a": "No, the carbon and membrane remove chlorine and chloramine. If you blend in tap water, dose conditioner for the tap portion."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$salinity$g$, $g$Salinity$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Salinity is the concentration of dissolved salts in water, measured as parts per thousand or specific gravity, and it defines freshwater, brackish and saltwater tanks.$g$, $g$Fish are built for a specific salinity range, and getting it wrong or letting it swing causes stress and death. Freshwater keepers mostly meet salinity when setting up brackish tanks or using aquarium salt as a treatment, so understanding the numbers helps avoid harm.$g$, $g$Aquarium Salinity Explained: Freshwater, Brackish and Marine$g$, $g$[{"heading": "The main ranges", "text": "- Freshwater: under about 0.5 ppt, specific gravity about 1.000\n- Low brackish: about 2 to 10 ppt, specific gravity about 1.002 to 1.007\n- High brackish: about 10 to 25 ppt, up to roughly 1.018\n- Marine and reef: about 35 ppt, specific gravity about 1.024 to 1.026\n\nBrackish fish include bumblebee gobies, figure eight puffers, archerfish and some mollies, though many of these are sold in freshwater stores. Research each species carefully."}, {"heading": "How to measure it", "text": "Use a refractometer calibrated with a 35 ppt calibration fluid; it's the most accurate common tool. Swing-arm hydrometers are cheaper but less precise and need bubble-free readings. Temperature affects readings, so measure at tank temperature or use a temperature-compensated tool.\n\nCheck salinity weekly at first, and always after topping off or doing a water change, so you learn how quickly your tank drifts."}, {"heading": "Changing salinity safely", "text": "Always mix marine salt or brackish water in a separate container, never directly in the tank. Change salinity slowly, no more than about 0.001 to 0.002 specific gravity per day. Remember that evaporation raises salinity: top off with fresh, dechlorinated water, not saltwater. Use marine salt mix, not table salt or aquarium salt, for brackish and marine setups."}, {"heading": "Salt in freshwater tanks", "text": "Aquarium salt is sometimes used short-term to treat ich or nitrite poisoning, but it doesn't belong permanently in most freshwater tanks. Corydoras, loaches, many tetras, shrimp, snails and lots of plants are sensitive to it."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What salinity is brackish water?", "a": "Brackish water usually ranges from about 2 to 25 ppt, or roughly 1.002 to 1.018 specific gravity, depending on the species."}, {"q": "Does evaporation increase salinity?", "a": "Yes. Salt stays when water evaporates, so top off with fresh water to keep salinity stable."}, {"q": "Can I use table salt in a brackish tank?", "a": "No. Use marine salt mix, which includes the minerals brackish and marine animals need."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$specific-gravity$g$, $g$Specific Gravity$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Specific gravity is the density of aquarium water compared to pure water, and it's the most common way hobbyists measure salinity in brackish and saltwater tanks.$g$, $g$Salinity can't be seen, and small differences matter, especially for corals and invertebrates. Reading specific gravity correctly lets you mix new saltwater accurately, catch evaporation creep and keep brackish species at the right level as they mature. Freshwater keepers mainly need it when setting up brackish tanks.$g$, $g$Specific Gravity in Saltwater Tanks: Ideal Range and Testing$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Target ranges", "text": "- Freshwater: about 1.000\n- Brackish: about 1.002 to 1.018, depending on species\n- Fish-only saltwater: about 1.020 to 1.025\n- Reef tanks: about 1.025 to 1.026, which equals roughly 35 ppt\n\nWhat matters most is keeping it stable. Corals and invertebrates react badly to swings of even a few thousandths."}, {"heading": "How to measure it", "text": "Refractometers are the most popular tool. Calibrate with 35 ppt calibration fluid rather than RO water, since that checks accuracy right where you need it. Place a few drops on the prism, close the cover and read the line.\n\nSwing-arm hydrometers float an arm in a water sample. They're cheap but can stick, collect salt crust or read wrong with bubbles. Glass floating hydrometers are another option. Digital meters exist but need regular calibration too.\n\nWhatever tool you use, check it against calibration fluid every month or two, and rinse it with fresh water after each reading so salt crust doesn't skew the next one."}, {"heading": "Keeping it stable", "text": "Evaporation removes only water, so salinity rises between top-offs. Top off with fresh RO or dechlorinated water daily or with an automatic top-off unit. Mix new saltwater a day ahead with a heater and pump, then check specific gravity and temperature before a water change."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Calibrating with tap or RO water and assuming the high end is accurate\n- Reading cold, freshly mixed water without temperature compensation\n- Topping off with saltwater instead of fresh water\n- Changing salinity quickly when adjusting brackish fish"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What should specific gravity be in a reef tank?", "a": "Most reef keepers keep it around 1.025 to 1.026, roughly 35 ppt."}, {"q": "Is a refractometer better than a hydrometer?", "a": "Usually, yes. A calibrated refractometer is more accurate and consistent than a swing-arm hydrometer."}, {"q": "Why does my specific gravity keep rising?", "a": "Evaporation. Top off with fresh water, not saltwater, to keep it steady."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$tannins$g$, $g$Tannins$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Tannins are natural plant compounds released by driftwood, leaves, cones and peat that tint aquarium water tea-colored and slightly soften and acidify it.$g$, $g$Tannins are harmless to fish and often beneficial, which is why blackwater keepers add them on purpose. Knowing what causes brown water helps you decide whether to embrace it for fish like bettas and tetras, or clear it for a crystal-clear look.$g$, $g$Tannins in a Fish Tank: Is Brown Water Safe and How to Remove It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Where they come from", "text": "- New driftwood, especially mopani, malaysian and spiderwood\n- Indian almond leaves, oak and guava leaves\n- Alder cones, seed pods and other botanicals\n- Peat moss and some aquasoils\n\nNew wood can leach heavily for weeks or even months, then slowly tapers off.\n\nTannin release is strongest in the first weeks after you add new material and in soft water, where the color shows faster."}, {"heading": "Benefits and effects", "text": "Tannins and the humic substances that come with them lower pH slightly, mostly in soft water with little KH. Many keepers believe they have mild antibacterial and antifungal effects, though the evidence is mostly anecdotal. Fish from blackwater habitats, such as bettas, apistogrammas, many tetras, rasboras and gouramis, often show better color and feel more secure in stained water.\n\nThe color itself does not harm fish or plants, though very dark water reduces the light reaching plants."}, {"heading": "How to reduce tannins", "text": "- Boil or soak new driftwood before use, changing the water every few days\n- Run activated carbon or purigen-style resin media in the filter\n- Keep up with regular water changes\n- Remove leaves and botanicals once they break down\n\nIf you want blackwater, simply keep adding leaves or botanicals and skip the carbon."}, {"heading": "When to be careful", "text": "In tanks with very low KH, heavy tannin use can drop pH more than expected, so test pH and KH when you add lots of botanicals. Livebearers and African cichlids prefer harder, alkaline water and may not do as well in strong blackwater."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are tannins bad for fish?", "a": "No. Tannins are safe and often beneficial, especially for soft-water species."}, {"q": "How long does driftwood release tannins?", "a": "Most wood leaches heavily for a few weeks to a few months, then slows. Soaking or boiling speeds this up."}, {"q": "How do I get rid of brown water in my aquarium?", "a": "Use activated carbon or a tannin-removing resin in the filter and do regular water changes. Pre-soaking new wood helps prevent it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$tds$g$, $g$TDS$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$TDS, or total dissolved solids, is a single number in ppm showing how much dissolved material, mostly minerals and salts, is present in your water.$g$, $g$A TDS meter gives a fast check on overall water consistency, which makes it invaluable for shrimp keepers and anyone remineralizing RO water. It can't identify what's dissolved, so it works best alongside GH, KH and nitrate tests, not as a replacement for them.$g$, $g$TDS in Aquariums: What It Means and Ideal Levels for Shrimp$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How TDS is measured", "text": "Hobby TDS pens measure electrical conductivity and convert it to an estimated ppm. Dip the tip in the water, wait for the number to settle and read it. Most pens auto-correct for temperature. Calibrate occasionally with a calibration solution, and rinse the tip after use."}, {"heading": "Useful ranges", "text": "- RO water: 0 to 10 ppm\n- Typical tap water: 50 to 400 ppm or more\n- Caridina shrimp: about 100 to 160 ppm\n- Neocaridina shrimp: about 150 to 250 ppm\n- Soft-water fish like discus: often 50 to 150 ppm\n\nMost community fish are not fussy about TDS as long as it's stable."}, {"heading": "What TDS can and can't tell you", "text": "A rising TDS between water changes shows that waste, fertilizer and minerals are accumulating, and that top-offs may be concentrating them. A sudden jump after a water change tells you the new water doesn't match.\n\nBut 200 ppm could be calcium and magnesium, or it could be sodium or nitrate. TDS doesn't reveal which, so always test GH, KH and nitrate to know what's really going on.\n\nAs a rule of thumb, if TDS rises by 50 ppm or more above what you get right after a water change, it's time to change more water or change it more often."}, {"heading": "Practical uses", "text": "- Checking that an RO unit's membrane is still working\n- Mixing remineralized water to the same TDS every time\n- Matching new water to tank water before a change, especially for shrimp\n- Spotting slow buildup in an under-maintained tank"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is a good TDS for a freshwater aquarium?", "a": "There's no single number. Many community tanks sit between 150 and 400 ppm; what matters most is stability and matching your species."}, {"q": "What TDS is best for shrimp?", "a": "Caridina shrimp usually do best around 100 to 160 ppm and Neocaridina around 150 to 250 ppm."}, {"q": "How do I lower TDS in my aquarium?", "a": "Do water changes with RO or lower-TDS water, and top off evaporation with RO rather than tap water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$test-kit$g$, $g$Test Kit$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$An aquarium test kit is a set of liquid reagents or dip strips used to measure water parameters such as ammonia, nitrite, nitrate, pH, GH and KH.$g$, $g$Most fish problems start with water that looks clear but isn't safe. Regular testing shows when a tank is cycled, warns of ammonia or nitrite spikes, and helps you figure out why fish are sick, so a reliable kit is one of the most important things you can own.$g$, $g$Best Way to Test Aquarium Water: Liquid Test Kits vs Strips$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Liquid kits vs strips", "text": "Liquid reagent kits use drops added to a water sample in a test tube, then compared to a color chart. They're more accurate, cost less per test and are the standard for cycling and troubleshooting.\n\nTest strips are fast and convenient but less precise, are easily ruined by humidity and can read high or low. They're fine for quick checks, but confirm any worrying reading with a liquid test."}, {"heading": "What to test and when", "text": "- New tank or cycling: ammonia, nitrite, nitrate and pH every day or two\n- Established tank: nitrate and pH weekly or every other week, ammonia and nitrite if anything seems off\n- Shrimp or soft-water tanks: add GH, KH and TDS\n- Always test before treating sick fish\n\nTest your tap water once too, so you know your baseline."}, {"heading": "Getting accurate results", "text": "- Follow the wait times exactly; reading early or late changes the color\n- Shake bottles hard, especially nitrate reagents, which can read falsely low otherwise\n- Rinse test tubes with tank water before and clean water after\n- Read colors against white in natural or bright light\n- Check expiration dates; old reagents give wrong results"}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "Testing right after dosing conditioner can show bound ammonia that isn't actually harmful. Most kits measure total ammonia, not just the toxic free form, so pH and temperature matter when judging danger. Writing results in a log is the best way to spot trends."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are aquarium test strips accurate?", "a": "They give a rough idea but are less accurate than liquid kits. Use them for quick checks, not for important decisions."}, {"q": "How often should I test my aquarium water?", "a": "Daily to every other day while cycling, then weekly for nitrate and pH once the tank is established."}, {"q": "Which water parameters should I test?", "a": "At minimum ammonia, nitrite, nitrate and pH. Add GH, KH and TDS for shrimp, soft-water fish and planted tanks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$water-parameters$g$, $g$Water Parameters$g$, $g$Water Chemistry$g$, $g$Water parameters are the measurable qualities of aquarium water, including temperature, pH, GH, KH, ammonia, nitrite, nitrate and TDS, that together determine whether fish can thrive.$g$, $g$Every fish species evolved in water with particular conditions, and keeping parameters suitable and stable is what keeps fish healthy, colorful and breeding. Most fish problems trace back to a parameter that drifted or spiked, so learning these basics prevents most losses.$g$, $g$Aquarium Water Parameters: Ideal Levels and What They Mean$g$, $g$[{"heading": "The core parameters", "text": "- Ammonia and nitrite: should always read 0 ppm in a cycled tank\n- Nitrate: under 20 to 40 ppm, lower for sensitive species\n- pH: most community fish handle 6.5 to 7.8\n- Temperature: most tropical fish 75 to 80 F\n- GH: about 4 to 12 dGH for most community fish\n- KH: 3 dKH or more helps keep pH steady\n\nThese are general guides. Check the needs of each species you keep."}, {"heading": "Stability beats perfection", "text": "Fish adapt to a wide range, especially captive-bred ones, but they handle sudden changes poorly. A steady pH of 7.6 is better than chasing 6.8 with chemicals and causing daily swings. Make changes slowly, keep water changes regular and consistent, and match temperature and conditioner every time.\n\nWhen you do need to change a parameter, move it a little at a time over days or weeks, and retest after each step."}, {"heading": "Choosing compatible fish", "text": "Start by testing your tap water. If it's hard and alkaline, livebearers, rainbowfish and many cichlids will be easy. If it's soft and acidic, tetras, rasboras and many catfish suit it. Choosing fish that fit your water is far easier than reshaping your water to fit the fish."}, {"heading": "Keeping a log", "text": "Write down test results with dates, along with water changes, feeding and new additions. A log shows trends like slowly rising nitrate or falling KH before fish suffer, and it makes it much easier to get useful help when something does go wrong."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What are the ideal water parameters for a freshwater aquarium?", "a": "For most community tanks: 0 ammonia, 0 nitrite, nitrate under 20 to 40 ppm, pH 6.5 to 7.8 and temperature 75 to 80 F."}, {"q": "Should I adjust my pH to match my fish?", "a": "Usually not. Most fish adapt to a stable pH, and chemical adjustments often cause dangerous swings."}, {"q": "How do I keep water parameters stable?", "a": "Do regular water changes, avoid overfeeding and overstocking, and keep KH high enough to buffer pH."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$biotope$g$, $g$Biotope$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A biotope aquarium recreates one specific natural habitat, using only fish, plants, decor and water conditions found together in that place in the wild.$g$, $g$Biotopes are a rewarding way to keep fish in conditions that match their evolution, which often brings out natural color and behavior. Planning one also teaches you a lot about where your fish come from and which species truly belong together.$g$, $g$Biotope Aquarium Ideas: How to Set Up a Natural Habitat Tank$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Popular biotope ideas", "text": "- Amazon blackwater stream: cardinal tetras, pencilfish, dwarf cichlids, leaf litter and driftwood\n- Southeast Asian peat swamp: chocolate gouramis, licorice gouramis and rasboras in soft, tannin-stained water\n- Lake Malawi rocky shore: mbuna among piled rocks with hard, alkaline water\n- Central American stream: livebearers and small cichlids over sand and stones\n- West African stream: kribensis and African tetras with roots and leaves"}, {"heading": "How to plan one", "text": "Pick a specific region, such as a single river or lake, rather than a whole continent. Research its water: temperature, pH, hardness and how clear or stained it is. Then choose fish and plants recorded there, along with matching substrate, wood, rocks and leaves.\n\nMany wild habitats have few or no plants, so a strict biotope may rely on hardscape and botanicals instead of greenery."}, {"heading": "Strict vs biotope-inspired", "text": "Competitive biotope builders follow habitats closely, down to sand color and leaf species. Most hobbyists build a biotope-inspired tank, keeping fish from the same region and similar water with a natural look, while allowing a few substitutes. Both approaches are valid, and inspired tanks are much easier to stock."}, {"heading": "Tips and mistakes", "text": "- Check that fish truly share a habitat, not just a continent\n- Plan water chemistry first; it's hard to fix later\n- Avoid mixing species from very different parameters for looks\n- Add leaf litter gradually and monitor pH in soft water\n\nTake photos of the real habitat if you can find them, and use them as a guide for how much wood, rock and leaf litter to add."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What is an example of a biotope aquarium?", "a": "An Amazon blackwater tank with cardinal tetras, pencilfish, driftwood and leaf litter in soft, tea-colored water is a classic example."}, {"q": "Do biotope tanks need plants?", "a": "Not always. Many natural habitats have few aquatic plants, so some biotopes use wood, rocks and leaves instead."}, {"q": "Is a biotope tank good for beginners?", "a": "A biotope-inspired tank can be, as long as you choose hardy species from one region and match their water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$cold-water-tank$g$, $g$Cold Water Tank$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A cold water tank is an unheated freshwater aquarium kept at room temperature, usually around 60 to 72 F, for fish that prefer cooler water.$g$, $g$Not every fish needs a heater, and keeping cool-water species in tropical temperatures shortens their lives. A cold water tank can be simple to run, but it still needs a filter, cycling and good maintenance, and some cool-water fish need far more space than people expect.$g$, $g$Cold Water Fish Tank: Best Fish and How to Set One Up$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Fish that suit cool water", "text": "- Fancy goldfish: about 65 to 72 F, but they grow large and need roughly 30 gallons or more for a pair\n- Common and comet goldfish: better suited to ponds than most tanks\n- White cloud mountain minnows: 60 to 72 F, great for 10 to 20 gallon tanks\n- Hillstream loaches: cool, fast, well-oxygenated water\n- Some danios, rice fish and dojo loaches tolerate cooler rooms\n\nPeppered corydoras and cherry shrimp also handle the low 70s."}, {"heading": "Setting one up", "text": "Cycle the tank before adding fish, just like a tropical tank. Use a filter rated for more than the tank's volume, since goldfish produce lots of waste. Cooler water holds more oxygen, but surface movement still matters. Choose a spot away from windows and heating vents.\n\nGoldfish dig and uproot plants, so hardy choices like anubias, java fern and hornwort hold up best. Plan on weekly water changes of 25 to 50 percent for goldfish tanks, since their waste output is high even at cool temperatures."}, {"heading": "Do you really not need a heater?", "text": "If your room stays steady, no. But if the house drops below about 60 F in winter or swings a lot between day and night, a small heater set to the low end of your fish's range keeps things stable. Big temperature swings stress fish more than a slightly low steady temperature."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Keeping goldfish in bowls or small tanks\n- Mixing goldfish with tropical fish that need 78 F\n- Skipping filtration because the tank is unheated\n- Assuming cool water means less maintenance"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What fish can live without a heater?", "a": "White cloud minnows, goldfish, hillstream loaches and some danios do well at room temperature if it stays stable."}, {"q": "What temperature is a cold water aquarium?", "a": "Usually about 60 to 72 F, depending on the species and the room."}, {"q": "Do goldfish need a heater?", "a": "Usually not. They prefer cooler water, though a heater can prevent big temperature swings in cold rooms."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$community-tank$g$, $g$Community Tank$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A community tank is an aquarium housing several peaceful species that share similar water needs and live together without fighting or eating each other.$g$, $g$Community tanks are the most popular setup because they offer variety and activity, but they only work when species are matched carefully. Mixing incompatible fish leads to stress, nipped fins and losses, so planning the stocking is as important as setting up the equipment.$g$, $g$How to Set Up a Community Tank: Best Fish and Stocking Tips$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Choosing compatible fish", "text": "Match three things: temperament, size and water needs. Avoid fish big enough to swallow tankmates, and avoid pairing fin nippers like tiger barbs with long-finned fish like bettas or angelfish. Then check that temperature, pH and hardness ranges overlap.\n\nThink in layers: a schooling group in the middle, bottom dwellers like corydoras, and maybe one centerpiece fish."}, {"heading": "Beginner-friendly combinations", "text": "- 20 gallons: a school of 8 to 10 small tetras or rasboras, 6 corydoras and a few nerite snails\n- 29 gallons: harlequin rasboras, platies, a group of kuhli loaches and a honey gourami\n- 40 gallons or more: a larger tetra school, a pair of dwarf gouramis or a pearl gourami, and a group of corydoras\n\nSchooling fish should be kept in groups of 6 or more."}, {"heading": "Adding fish the right way", "text": "Cycle the tank first. Add fish in small groups over weeks, starting with the hardiest, so the bacteria keep up. Quarantine new fish for 2 to 4 weeks if you can, since one sick newcomer can infect the whole tank.\n\nWatch new arrivals closely for the first few days for chasing, hiding or torn fins, and be ready to rehome a fish that doesn't fit in. Plenty of plants and decor help break lines of sight and reduce squabbles."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Buying fish on impulse without checking adult size\n- Keeping schooling fish in groups of two or three\n- Overstocking, which raises waste and aggression\n- Adding a single aggressive fish to a peaceful group"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What fish are good for a community tank?", "a": "Tetras, rasboras, corydoras, platies, mollies, honey gouramis and otocinclus are popular peaceful choices."}, {"q": "How many fish can I put in a community tank?", "a": "It depends on adult size, activity and filtration. Stock lightly at first and grow slowly while testing water."}, {"q": "Can a betta live in a community tank?", "a": "Sometimes, with calm, non-nipping tankmates in a planted tank of 10 gallons or more. Some bettas are too aggressive, so have a backup plan."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$freshwater$g$, $g$Freshwater$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A freshwater aquarium uses water with little or no salt, like rivers, lakes and streams, and is by far the most common and beginner-friendly type of fish tank.$g$, $g$Freshwater tanks offer thousands of fish, plants and invertebrates at lower cost and with more forgiving care than saltwater. Most people start here, and many stay for life, moving from simple community tanks to planted aquascapes, shrimp colonies and cichlid setups.$g$, $g$Freshwater Aquarium for Beginners: What You Need to Get Started$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why start with freshwater", "text": "Freshwater fish are generally hardier, cheaper and easier to find than marine fish. Equipment is simpler, with no need for salt mixing, protein skimmers or salinity checks. Mistakes are more forgiving, and dechlorinated tap water works for most species."}, {"heading": "Basic equipment", "text": "- A tank of 20 gallons or more if possible, since bigger tanks are more stable\n- A filter rated for your tank size\n- A heater for tropical fish, set around 75 to 80 F\n- A thermometer, light and lid\n- Substrate, decor or plants\n- Water conditioner and a liquid test kit\n- A gravel vacuum and bucket used only for the aquarium"}, {"heading": "Types of freshwater setups", "text": "Community tanks with peaceful mixed species are the most popular. Other options include planted tanks, species-only tanks, cold water goldfish tanks, shrimp tanks, African cichlid tanks with hard alkaline water, and blackwater tanks with soft, tea-colored water. Each has different water and stocking needs."}, {"heading": "First steps", "text": "Set up the tank, add conditioned water and run the filter. Cycle it for about 4 to 8 weeks so beneficial bacteria can handle waste, testing ammonia and nitrite along the way. Then add a few hardy fish, feed lightly and do weekly water changes of 20 to 30 percent.\n\nResist the urge to fill the tank all at once. Add a few fish every couple of weeks, test the water after each addition, and only add more once ammonia and nitrite stay at zero."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is a freshwater or saltwater aquarium easier?", "a": "Freshwater is generally easier and cheaper, with hardier fish and simpler equipment."}, {"q": "What size freshwater tank is best for beginners?", "a": "A 20 to 40 gallon tank is a great start. It's more stable than a small tank but still easy to maintain."}, {"q": "Can I use tap water in a freshwater aquarium?", "a": "Yes, for most fish, as long as you treat it with a dechlorinator and match the temperature."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$nano-tank$g$, $g$Nano Tank$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A nano tank is a small aquarium, usually 10 gallons or less, often used for shrimp, snails, a betta or a few tiny fish.$g$, $g$Nano tanks fit on desks and shelves and can be beautiful, but less water means temperature, ammonia and other parameters change much faster. They reward careful stocking and steady maintenance, and they're not automatically easier than larger tanks, despite how they're often marketed.$g$, $g$Nano Tank Stocking Ideas: Best Fish for Small Aquariums$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What can live in a nano", "text": "- A single betta in 5 gallons or more\n- Neocaridina shrimp colonies, starting in about 5 gallons\n- Small schooling fish like chili rasboras, ember tetras or celestial pearl danios in 10 gallons\n- Nerite or ramshorn snails\n- Pygmy corydoras in 10 gallons or more\n\nAvoid goldfish, bettas in tiny bowls, fast swimmers like zebra danios, and fish that grow over about 2 inches."}, {"heading": "Keeping it stable", "text": "Small volumes heat and cool quickly, so use an adjustable heater sized for the tank and keep it away from windows. Ammonia from one dead snail or overfeeding can spike in hours. Feed sparingly, remove leftovers, and do small weekly water changes of 15 to 25 percent. Top off evaporation often, since it concentrates minerals faster in small tanks.\n\nA small battery-powered air pump and a spare heater are cheap insurance, since a nano can overheat or chill within hours if equipment fails. Test ammonia and nitrite whenever something seems off rather than waiting for a weekly check."}, {"heading": "Equipment tips", "text": "Choose a gentle filter like a sponge filter or small hang-on-back with adjustable flow, especially for shrimp and bettas. Cover the intake with a sponge so shrimp aren't sucked in. Live plants help a lot, using waste and adding stability."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Overstocking because fish are small in the store\n- Skipping the cycle\n- Big water changes with water that doesn't match temperature\n- Using a heater that's too strong for the volume"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What size is a nano tank?", "a": "Most people mean 10 gallons or less, though some use the term for anything under about 20 gallons."}, {"q": "Are nano tanks good for beginners?", "a": "They can be, but parameters change fast. A 10 to 20 gallon tank is more forgiving for a first tank."}, {"q": "How many fish can I put in a 5 gallon tank?", "a": "Usually one betta, or a shrimp colony with a few snails. Most schooling fish need 10 gallons or more."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$planted-tank$g$, $g$Planted Tank$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A planted tank is an aquarium built around live aquatic plants, where the plants are a main feature rather than decoration alongside the fish.$g$, $g$Healthy plants use fish waste as fertilizer, compete with algae, add oxygen during the day and give fish shelter. Planted tanks range from easy low-light setups to demanding high-tech aquascapes, so choosing the right level for your time and budget makes all the difference.$g$, $g$Planted Tank for Beginners: Easy Plants, Lighting and Setup$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Low-tech vs high-tech", "text": "Low-tech tanks use modest lighting, no added CO2, and hardy plants. They grow slowly, need little trimming and are very forgiving.\n\nHigh-tech tanks use strong lighting, pressurized CO2 and regular fertilizer dosing. They grow fast and allow demanding carpets and red stem plants, but light, CO2 and nutrients must stay balanced or algae takes over."}, {"heading": "Easy plants to start with", "text": "- Java fern and anubias: attach to wood or rock, don't bury the rhizome\n- Cryptocorynes: rooted plants that like root tabs\n- Java moss and other mosses\n- Amazon sword and vallisneria\n- Hornwort and floating plants like frogbit, which soak up nitrate fast"}, {"heading": "Substrate, light and fertilizer", "text": "Root feeders grow best in nutrient-rich aquasoil or plain gravel with root tabs. Rhizome plants and floaters feed from the water and need liquid fertilizer instead. Run lights about 6 to 8 hours a day on a timer; longer periods mostly feed algae. Dose a comprehensive liquid fertilizer if plants show yellowing or holes. Many fertilizers are shrimp-safe, but check copper content first.\n\nStart with a lighter stocking of fish while plants settle in, and add fast-growing stem or floating plants early. They take up nutrients quickly and help keep algae down while slower plants establish."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "- Melting: new plants, especially crypts, often shed leaves while adjusting; trim and wait\n- Algae: usually too much light for the CO2 and nutrients available\n- Yellow leaves: often nitrogen, potassium or iron deficiency\n- Rotting rhizomes from burying java fern or anubias"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do planted tanks need CO2?", "a": "No. Low-tech planted tanks with moderate light and hardy plants do well without added CO2."}, {"q": "How long should I leave the light on in a planted tank?", "a": "Around 6 to 8 hours a day is a good starting point. Longer periods usually cause algae."}, {"q": "Are live plants good for fish?", "a": "Yes. They improve water quality, reduce algae and give fish shelter and a more natural environment."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$reef-tank$g$, $g$Reef Tank$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A reef tank is a saltwater aquarium designed to keep living corals, along with fish and invertebrates, under strong lighting and very stable water chemistry.$g$, $g$Reef tanks are some of the most beautiful aquariums in the hobby, but corals need precise, stable conditions. Freshwater keepers considering the jump should know the extra equipment, testing and cost involved, and that patience matters even more than in freshwater.$g$, $g$Reef Tank for Beginners: Equipment, Corals and Parameters$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Key parameters", "text": "- Salinity: about 1.025 to 1.026 specific gravity, around 35 ppt\n- Temperature: about 76 to 79 F\n- Alkalinity: about 7 to 11 dKH\n- Calcium: about 400 to 450 ppm\n- Magnesium: about 1,250 to 1,400 ppm\n- Nitrate and phosphate: low but not zero for most corals\n\nStability matters more than chasing exact numbers. Corals react badly to quick swings, especially in alkalinity."}, {"heading": "Essential equipment", "text": "Most reefs use live rock, a reef-capable light, powerheads for strong varied flow, a heater, a protein skimmer on larger tanks, an RO/DI unit for clean water, a refractometer and reliable test kits. Many keepers add a sump and an automatic top-off to keep salinity stable."}, {"heading": "Choosing corals", "text": "Soft corals like leathers, zoanthids, mushrooms and many polyps are the easiest starting point. LPS corals like hammers and torches need more stable chemistry. SPS corals like acropora are the most demanding and need very stable alkalinity and strong light. Start with soft corals and build experience.\n\nBuy frags, small cuttings of coral, rather than large colonies at first. They're cheaper, adapt better and let you learn how your tank's light and flow affect growth."}, {"heading": "Tips for success", "text": "Let the tank mature for a few months before adding demanding corals. Add fish slowly, quarantine them, and research reef-safety, since some fish and invertebrates eat corals. Never use copper medications in a reef tank, as it is deadly to corals and invertebrates."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are reef tanks hard to maintain?", "a": "They take more equipment and testing than freshwater, but soft coral reefs are manageable for patient beginners."}, {"q": "What is the easiest coral for beginners?", "a": "Mushrooms, zoanthids, leathers and green star polyps are commonly recommended starter corals."}, {"q": "How much does a reef tank cost to set up?", "a": "It varies widely, but lighting, flow, RO/DI and live rock make reefs cost several times more than a similar-sized freshwater tank."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$saltwater$g$, $g$Saltwater$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A saltwater, or marine, aquarium uses mixed sea salt to recreate ocean conditions for marine fish, invertebrates and sometimes corals.$g$, $g$Saltwater tanks open the door to clownfish, gobies, tangs, shrimp and corals that freshwater can't offer. They need more equipment, testing and money than freshwater, so it helps to understand the commitment before switching or adding one alongside your freshwater tanks.$g$, $g$Saltwater vs Freshwater Aquarium: Differences for Beginners$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Saltwater vs freshwater", "text": "Saltwater tanks need marine salt mix, a refractometer to check salinity, and usually RO/DI water. Marine fish are often more expensive and some are wild-caught, which can make them harder to acclimate. Water changes require mixing salt water ahead of time. On the upside, the colors and variety are unmatched."}, {"heading": "Fish-only vs reef", "text": "- FOWLR, fish-only with live rock: easier and cheaper, with fish and rock but no corals, so medications and lighting are simpler\n- Reef tank: adds corals, which require strong lighting, flow and steady calcium and alkalinity\n\nMany beginners start fish-only and add easy corals later.\n\nA fish-only tank also lets you use some medications that would harm corals, which makes treating disease simpler, though copper still must never touch invertebrates."}, {"heading": "Basic setup", "text": "Plan for at least 30 to 40 gallons for stability. Add live rock and sand, mix salt to about 1.024 to 1.026 specific gravity, heat to about 76 to 79 F and add powerheads for flow. Cycle the tank for several weeks, then add hardy fish like clownfish or gobies slowly. Top off evaporation with fresh RO water."}, {"heading": "Beginner mistakes", "text": "- Starting too small, where salinity swings quickly\n- Topping off with saltwater instead of fresh water\n- Rushing stocking before the tank matures\n- Mixing fish without checking aggression or reef-safety\n- Using copper medications in tanks with invertebrates or corals"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is saltwater harder than freshwater?", "a": "Yes, somewhat. It needs more equipment and testing, but many hobbyists keep successful fish-only tanks as beginners."}, {"q": "Can freshwater fish live in saltwater?", "a": "No. Freshwater fish can't survive marine salinity, and marine fish can't survive in freshwater."}, {"q": "What is the easiest saltwater fish?", "a": "Captive-bred clownfish are among the hardiest and most popular starter marine fish."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$species-only-tank$g$, $g$Species-Only Tank$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$A species-only tank is an aquarium dedicated to a single fish or invertebrate species, with no other tankmates, to suit that animal's needs.$g$, $g$Some animals don't do well in community tanks: they're aggressive, delicate, need special water, or get eaten. A species-only setup lets you tailor everything to one species, which often brings out natural behavior and makes breeding far easier. It also takes the guesswork out of compatibility.$g$, $g$Species-Only Tank: Best Fish and Why Keep Them Alone$g$, $g$[{"heading": "When a species-only tank makes sense", "text": "- Aggressive or territorial fish, like many cichlids or a male betta\n- Tiny or slow fish easily outcompeted, like pygmy corydoras or seahorses\n- Shrimp colonies, where fish would eat babies\n- Species with special needs, like puffers, which nip and need snails\n- Breeding projects, where fry need safety from adults of other species"}, {"heading": "Popular species-only setups", "text": "- A single betta in 5 to 10 gallons, heavily planted\n- Cherry or crystal shrimp colonies in 10 gallons or more\n- Pea puffers in groups with lots of plants and snails as food\n- Shell-dwelling cichlids from Lake Tanganyika in a sand-and-shell setup\n- A group of one rasbora or tetra species in a nano"}, {"heading": "How to set one up", "text": "Research your species' wild habitat and match temperature, pH, hardness and decor. Social species still need groups: schooling fish should be kept in 6 or more, and some cichlids need a specific ratio of males to females. Give territorial fish plenty of hiding places and broken lines of sight.\n\nBecause there are no tankmates to compete for food, feed a varied diet in amounts the group finishes within a couple of minutes, and adjust as the colony grows."}, {"heading": "Benefits and trade-offs", "text": "Species-only tanks show behavior you rarely see in mixed tanks, such as breeding displays and natural schooling. The trade-off is less variety, though many keepers find a large group of one species more striking than a mix."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What fish should be kept in a species-only tank?", "a": "Bettas, pea puffers, aggressive cichlids and shrimp colonies are common examples."}, {"q": "Can shrimp live with fish?", "a": "Some peaceful nano fish can, but most fish eat baby shrimp. A species-only tank is best for building a colony."}, {"q": "Do species-only tanks need fewer fish?", "a": "Not necessarily. Many species-only tanks hold a large group of one schooling species."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$tank-footprint$g$, $g$Tank Footprint$g$, $g$Water Types & Setup$g$, $g$Tank footprint is the length and width of an aquarium's base, which determines its floor area and how much space fish have to swim and claim territory.$g$, $g$Two tanks can hold the same gallons but suit very different fish. Long, wide tanks give active swimmers and bottom dwellers more room and more surface for oxygen exchange, so footprint often matters more than total volume when choosing a tank and planning stocking.$g$, $g$Tank Footprint vs Gallons: Choosing the Right Aquarium Size$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why footprint matters", "text": "Most fish swim horizontally, not up and down. Danios, rainbowfish and many barbs need length to swim. Corydoras, loaches and many cichlids live on the bottom and need floor space for foraging and territories. A larger footprint also means more water surface, which improves oxygen exchange.\n\nFootprint also affects stability: a wider, longer base spreads the same stocking across more surface and gives you more room for plants, rocks and caves that break lines of sight."}, {"heading": "Common footprints", "text": "- 10 gallon: 20 by 10 inches\n- 20 gallon long: 30 by 12 inches\n- 29 gallon: 30 by 12 inches, just taller than a 20 long\n- 40 gallon breeder: 36 by 18 inches\n- 55 gallon: 48 by 13 inches\n- 75 gallon: 48 by 18 inches\n\nNotice that a 20 long and a 29 share a footprint; the 29 adds height, not floor space. A 40 breeder actually has slightly more floor area than a 55, which is longer but narrower."}, {"heading": "Choosing the right shape", "text": "- Long, low tanks: active schoolers, bottom dwellers and territorial fish\n- Deep, wide tanks: aquascaping depth and cichlid territories\n- Tall tanks: angelfish and discus, which have tall bodies, though they still need decent length\n\nCheck your stand and floor too. Water weighs about 8.3 pounds per gallon, so a filled 75 gallon tank can top 800 pounds."}, {"heading": "Stocking with footprint in mind", "text": "When reading stocking advice, look for minimum tank length, such as 30 or 48 inches, not just gallons. Avoid tall, narrow column tanks for active fish."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is a 20 long better than a 20 high?", "a": "For most fish, yes. The 20 long gives more swimming length and floor space than the taller 20 high."}, {"q": "Does tank footprint matter more than gallons?", "a": "Often. Many fish need swimming length and floor area, so footprint can matter more than total volume."}, {"q": "What tank has the biggest footprint for its size?", "a": "Breeder tanks, like the 40 breeder at 36 by 18 inches, offer a large footprint for their volume."}]$g$::jsonb)
) as v(slug, term, category, definition, body, seo_title, sections, faq)
where g.slug = v.slug;

insert into public.glossary_terms (slug, term, category, definition, body, seo_title, sections, faq)
select v.slug, v.term, v.category, v.definition, v.body, v.seo_title, v.sections, v.faq
from (values
  ($g$breeder-box$g$, $g$Breeder Box$g$, $g$Breeding$g$, $g$A small enclosure that hangs inside or on the aquarium to keep pregnant fish, newborn fry, or a sick fish separated from the main tank.$g$, $g$A breeder box lets fry grow without being eaten while still sharing the main tank's heater, filter and water quality. It is cheap and handy, but a cramped box can stress adult fish, so it works best as a short-term tool rather than a home.$g$, $g$Breeder Box for Fish: How to Use One Without Stressing Fry$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Types of breeder boxes", "text": "- Floating plastic boxes: clip inside the tank, often with a slotted floor so livebearer fry drop away from the mother.\n- Net breeders: a fine mesh bag on a frame. Water flows through freely, which keeps it cleaner than solid plastic.\n- Hang-on-back boxes: sit outside the tank and get a steady flow of tank water from an air pump or small pump. These hold more water and are the best choice for raising fry."}, {"heading": "How to use one", "text": "Set the box up in an area with gentle flow, near the heater but not against it. For livebearers, move the female only when she is very close to giving birth, with a boxy, squared-off belly and a dark gravid spot. Return her to the main tank right after she drops her fry.\n\nFeed fry small amounts several times a day and siphon leftover food from the bottom of the box daily, since the small volume fouls quickly. Move fry out once they are too large to fit in adult mouths, usually after 4 to 6 weeks."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Moving a pregnant female in too early. Days of confinement can stress her enough to cause a premature birth or a lost brood.\n- Using a solid box with no water flow, which lets ammonia build up.\n- Overcrowding fry as they grow.\n- Leaving the box where tankmates nip through the mesh.\n\nIf you plan to breed regularly, a separate 5 to 10 gallon fry tank with a sponge filter is easier on the fish than any box."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do breeder boxes stress fish?", "a": "They can, especially for pregnant females kept in them for days. Use them for short stays and give fry a roomier setup as they grow."}, {"q": "How long should fry stay in a breeder box?", "a": "Usually 3 to 6 weeks, until they are too big to be eaten by the adults in the main tank."}, {"q": "Can I put a pregnant guppy in a breeder box?", "a": "Yes, but wait until she is very close to giving birth, then return her to the main tank soon after the fry arrive."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$anchor-worms$g$, $g$Anchor Worms$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Anchor worms are parasitic crustaceans, not true worms, that bury their heads in a fish's skin and leave thin, thread-like bodies trailing from the wound.$g$, $g$Anchor worms are most common on goldfish, koi and pond fish, but they can arrive on any new fish or in pond water. Each attachment site opens the door to bacterial infection, and a single female releases hundreds of larvae, so a few visible worms can quickly turn into a tank-wide problem without proper treatment.$g$, $g$Anchor Worms on Fish: Signs, Removal and Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- White, green or reddish threads about a quarter to half an inch long sticking out of the skin, often near fins\n- A red, swollen sore at the base of each thread\n- Twin egg sacs at the end of mature females, giving a forked look\n- Fish rubbing against decor, clamped fins, lethargy or loss of appetite"}, {"heading": "How fish get them", "text": "Anchor worms, from the genus Lernaea, are copepods. They come in on new fish, with water from bags, live foods from ponds or plants from outdoor setups. Only adult females attach permanently. The young larvae swim free in the water for days to weeks before finding a host, which is why the whole tank needs treating, not just the visible parasites. Warm water above about 77°F speeds up their life cycle."}, {"heading": "Treatment", "text": "- Remove visible worms with tweezers, gripping close to the skin and pulling slowly so the head comes out. Swab the wound with an antiseptic. Get help the first time if you can, since it is stressful for the fish.\n- Treat the whole tank with an antiparasitic that targets crustaceans, such as one containing diflubenzuron, repeating as the label directs to catch new larvae.\n- These medications kill shrimp, crayfish and other crustaceans, so move invertebrates out first. Snails are generally safer but check the label.\n- Watch the wounds for infection, and use an antibacterial treatment if they turn white or fuzzy."}, {"heading": "Prevention", "text": "Quarantine all new fish for at least 3 to 4 weeks and inspect them closely. Avoid adding pond water or wild-collected live food to indoor tanks."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are anchor worms visible to the naked eye?", "a": "Yes. Adult females show as thin threads up to about half an inch long sticking out of the fish's skin."}, {"q": "Can anchor worms kill fish?", "a": "Heavy infestations can, mainly through open wounds that become infected and through stress. Small fish are most at risk."}, {"q": "Can I just pull anchor worms out?", "a": "Removing them with tweezers helps, but larvae in the water will reinfect fish, so you still need to treat the entire tank."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$clamped-fins$g$, $g$Clamped Fins$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Clamped fins describes a fish holding its fins folded tightly against its body instead of spreading them, a common early sign of stress or illness.$g$, $g$Clamped fins are not a disease on their own but a warning light that something is wrong with the water, the tankmates or the fish's health. Catching it early gives you time to fix the cause before a real infection takes hold. A quick water test is almost always the right first step.$g$, $g$Clamped Fins in Fish: What Causes It and How to Fix It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it looks like", "text": "A healthy fish spreads its dorsal and tail fins as it swims. A fish with clamped fins keeps the dorsal fin flat and the tail folded like a closed fan. It often hovers in one place, rocks side to side or hides. One clamped fish may have a personal problem, but several clamped fish usually point to the water."}, {"heading": "Common causes", "text": "- Ammonia or nitrite above 0 ppm, or nitrate creeping above 40 ppm\n- A temperature drop or swing, such as a failed heater\n- pH crashes or a sudden change in hardness\n- Bullying or fin nipping from tankmates\n- Parasites such as ich, velvet or skin flukes\n- Bacterial infections like fin rot or columnaris\n- New arrivals still adjusting after shipping"}, {"heading": "What to do first", "text": "Test ammonia, nitrite, nitrate, pH and temperature. If anything is off, do a 25 to 50 percent water change with dechlorinated water matched to the tank temperature, and repeat daily until readings are safe. Check that the heater and filter are running.\n\nThen watch closely for a day or two. Look for white spots, a gold dusty sheen, frayed fins, rapid gill movement or scratching against decor. If you see a clear sign of disease, treat for that specific problem. Avoid dosing general medications on a guess, since that adds stress without fixing the cause."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Keep up regular weekly water changes, use a reliable heater sized for your tank, and research tankmates so peaceful fish are not housed with nippers. Quarantining new fish keeps parasites from reaching your display."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Why are my fish's fins clamped?", "a": "Most often it is poor water quality or a temperature problem, followed by bullying, parasites and bacterial infections. Test your water before anything else."}, {"q": "Is clamped fins a disease?", "a": "No, it is a symptom. It tells you the fish is stressed or sick, and you need to find the underlying cause."}, {"q": "How long does it take for clamped fins to go away?", "a": "If the cause was water quality, fins often open again within a day or two of fixing it. If they stay clamped, look harder for parasites or infection."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$epistylis$g$, $g$Epistylis$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Epistylis is a stalked, single-celled parasite that attaches to fish skin and fins, forming white fuzzy tufts that often lead to red sores.$g$, $g$Epistylis is easy to mistake for fungus or columnaris, and the wrong treatment lets it spread. It thrives in water with lots of dissolved waste, so an outbreak is also a sign the tank needs cleaner water. The open sores it leaves often become infected with bacteria, which is why it is sometimes called red sore disease.$g$, $g$Epistylis in Fish: How to Spot It and Treat It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Small white or gray fuzzy tufts on the body, fins or spines\n- Tufts that look like tiny cauliflower or cotton under a magnifier\n- Red, ulcer-like sores where colonies grew\n- Scratching against decor, clamped fins and lethargy\n\nIt is most common on goldfish, koi, catfish and other fish kept in warm water rich in organic waste."}, {"heading": "Epistylis vs fungus and columnaris", "text": "Epistylis colonies are tufts of tiny stalked cells and usually sit in distinct spots, often on spines or scales. True fungus grows in long, wispy strands on wounds. Columnaris spreads across the skin as flat, matted patches and moves fast. A microscope gives the only sure answer, but tufts with red sores beneath them on otherwise healthy fish point toward epistylis. It is not an alga or fungus, so antifungal products do not help."}, {"heading": "How to treat it", "text": "- Do large water changes, 30 to 50 percent, and clean the filter and gravel to cut organic waste.\n- Use an antiparasitic treatment labeled for external protozoa; formalin-based treatments are common.\n- Aquarium salt baths help some fish, but avoid salt with scaleless fish, sensitive plants and invertebrates.\n- Treat secondary red sores with an antibacterial if they worsen.\n- Strong aeration helps, since many treatments lower oxygen.\n\nFormalin and similar medications are hard on shrimp, snails and some plants, so treat in a hospital tank when possible."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Avoid overfeeding and overstocking, vacuum the substrate, and keep up weekly water changes. Ponds and goldfish tanks with heavy waste loads are most at risk, especially when water warms in summer."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is epistylis a fungus?", "a": "No. It is a stalked protozoan parasite that only looks fuzzy like fungus, which is why antifungal treatments do not work on it."}, {"q": "What causes epistylis in fish?", "a": "It takes hold in water with high organic waste, often from overfeeding, overstocking or infrequent water changes, especially in warm water."}, {"q": "Is epistylis contagious?", "a": "Yes. It spreads to other fish in the same water, so treat the whole tank and fix the water quality that let it grow."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fish-lice$g$, $g$Fish Lice (Argulus)$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Fish lice, or Argulus, are small, flat, disc-shaped crustacean parasites that clamp onto a fish's skin and feed on its blood and tissue.$g$, $g$Fish lice are big enough to see, yet they are often missed because they are nearly transparent and blend in. Their bites cause stress, red sores and secondary infections, and heavy infestations can kill small fish. They are most common on goldfish and koi, especially in ponds, and they can spread quickly once eggs hatch.$g$, $g$Fish Lice (Argulus): How to Identify and Get Rid of Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Flat, round, greenish or clear bumps about 1/8 to 1/4 inch across that can move around the body\n- Two dark eye spots visible on the parasite up close\n- Red, inflamed spots where they have fed\n- Flashing, rubbing against surfaces and jumping\n- Clamped fins, rapid breathing and lethargy"}, {"heading": "How they get in", "text": "Argulus arrives on new fish, especially goldfish and koi from outdoor ponds, and sometimes on plants or in water from wild sources. Adults can leave a fish and swim freely for a few days. Females lay rows of eggs on hard surfaces like glass, rocks and decor, and those eggs hatch over several weeks, which is why infestations come back if you only remove the visible adults."}, {"heading": "How to get rid of fish lice", "text": "- Remove visible lice with blunt tweezers. Hold the fish gently in a wet net and pull straight off; dab the spot with a disinfectant safe for fish wounds.\n- Treat the whole tank or pond with an antiparasitic product labeled for crustacean parasites like anchor worms and fish lice.\n- Repeat according to the label, usually over 2 to 3 weeks, to catch newly hatched lice.\n- Scrub glass and decor to remove egg clusters.\n- Watch for bacterial infections in bite wounds and treat if they appear.\n\nMedications that kill fish lice also kill other crustaceans, so they are deadly to shrimp, crayfish and some other invertebrates. Move those animals out before treating."}, {"heading": "Preventing them", "text": "Quarantine new pond and coldwater fish for at least 4 weeks and inspect them closely in good light. Avoid adding wild-caught plants or water to your tank. Rinse new plants and inspect them for egg clusters."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can you see fish lice with the naked eye?", "a": "Yes. Adults are about 1/8 to 1/4 inch across, but they are flat and nearly see-through, so look closely in good light."}, {"q": "Can fish lice live without a host?", "a": "Adults can swim freely for a few days, and their eggs survive on surfaces for weeks, so treatment must cover hatching periods."}, {"q": "Are fish lice dangerous to humans?", "a": "No. Argulus only feeds on fish and amphibians and does not infest people."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$fish-tuberculosis$g$, $g$Fish Tuberculosis$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Fish tuberculosis is a slow, chronic bacterial disease caused by Mycobacterium species that leads to wasting, lesions and eventual death in aquarium fish.$g$, $g$Fish TB has no reliable cure and can spread through a tank over months, so knowing the signs helps you protect the rest of your fish. It also matters to you personally: the same bacteria can cause a skin infection in people, called fish handler's disease. Simple hygiene like gloves and hand washing keeps the risk low.$g$, $g$Fish Tuberculosis (Fish TB): Symptoms, Risks and What to Do$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Gradual weight loss and a hollow belly even though the fish eats\n- Fading color and loss of appetite late in the disease\n- Open sores or ulcers that do not heal\n- Bent spine, pop-eye, fin loss or frayed fins\n- Lethargy and hanging at the surface or bottom\n\nThese signs overlap with many other illnesses, so fish TB is often suspected rather than proven. A lab examination of tissue is the only way to confirm it."}, {"heading": "How it spreads", "text": "Mycobacteria live in many aquariums at low levels and infect fish that are stressed or weakened. Fish catch it mainly by eating infected tissue, such as picking at a dead tankmate, and through open wounds. Crowding, poor water quality and leaving dead fish in the tank all raise the risk."}, {"heading": "What to do", "text": "- Remove sick and dead fish right away.\n- Euthanize fish with advanced signs humanely; treatment rarely works and some antibiotics that help are hard to use in aquariums.\n- Keep water clean and stress low for the remaining fish.\n- If you need to break down the tank, disinfect equipment with 70 percent alcohol, leaving it in contact for at least 10 minutes. Mycobacteria resist many common disinfectants, including weak bleach solutions.\n- Do not share nets or equipment between tanks during an outbreak."}, {"heading": "Protecting yourself", "text": "Wear long gloves when working in a tank with suspected TB, especially if you have cuts or scrapes on your hands. Wash your hands and arms afterward. A sore on your hand that does not heal after tank work is worth showing to a doctor, and mention your aquarium. People with a weakened immune system should take extra care."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can fish tuberculosis be cured?", "a": "There is no reliable cure in home aquariums. Most keepers remove and humanely euthanize affected fish and focus on protecting the rest of the tank."}, {"q": "Can humans get tuberculosis from fish?", "a": "People can get a skin infection from the same bacteria through cuts, usually on the hands. It is not the same as human lung tuberculosis and is treated by a doctor."}, {"q": "How do I disinfect a tank after fish TB?", "a": "Clean surfaces thoroughly, then apply 70 percent alcohol with at least 10 minutes of contact time. Replace porous items like sponges and substrate."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$gasping-at-surface$g$, $g$Gasping at the Surface$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Gasping at the surface is when fish hang at the top of the tank gulping air, usually because the water lacks oxygen or something is hurting their gills.$g$, $g$Surface gasping is one of the clearest emergency signs in fishkeeping, and fish can die within hours if the cause is low oxygen or ammonia. The good news is that a quick water change and more surface movement often fix it on the spot. Know which fish breathe air normally so you do not panic over healthy behavior.$g$, $g$Fish Gasping at the Surface: Causes and Quick Fixes$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Common causes", "text": "- Low oxygen from warm water, overcrowding or a filter that has stopped\n- Ammonia or nitrite in the water, which damages gills and blood\n- Too little surface movement, especially in heavily planted tanks overnight\n- Too much injected CO2 in planted tanks\n- Gill parasites or infections\n- Medications that reduce oxygen or irritate gills"}, {"heading": "What to do right away", "text": "- Check that the filter and any air pump are running.\n- Do a 30 to 50 percent water change with dechlorinated water matched to the tank temperature.\n- Add aeration: point the filter outflow at the surface, add an air stone, or lower the water line so the outflow splashes.\n- Turn off CO2 injection if you use it.\n- Test ammonia, nitrite and temperature.\n\nIf fish perk up within minutes of extra aeration, low oxygen was the cause. If they keep gasping in well-aerated water with good test results, look for gill parasites or disease."}, {"heading": "Normal surface breathers", "text": "Some fish breathe air as part of normal behavior. Bettas, gouramis and paradise fish have a labyrinth organ and regularly gulp at the surface. Corydoras and many loaches dart up for a quick breath and dive back down. Watch for a change in behavior: fish that normally stay low hanging at the top together is the warning sign."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Keep the surface moving, avoid overstocking, and clean filters regularly so flow stays strong. In summer, watch temperatures; warm water above about 82°F holds much less oxygen. Plant tanks with CO2 should use a timer that shuts it off before lights out."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Why are my fish gasping at the top of the tank?", "a": "The most common causes are low oxygen and ammonia or nitrite in the water. Add aeration and do a water change right away, then test."}, {"q": "How do I quickly add oxygen to my fish tank?", "a": "Do a partial water change, add an air stone, and point the filter outflow at the surface so it breaks the water."}, {"q": "Is it normal for bettas to go to the surface?", "a": "Yes. Bettas breathe air with a labyrinth organ, so short trips to the surface are normal. Constant gasping with clamped fins is not."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hexamita$g$, $g$Hexamita$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Hexamita is a tiny flagellate protozoan that lives in fish intestines and can multiply into a harmful internal infection when fish are stressed.$g$, $g$Hexamita is a common cause of wasting and white stringy poop in cichlids, especially discus, angelfish and oscars. It is often linked to hole-in-the-head disease. Many fish carry small numbers without problems, so outbreaks usually point to stress, poor water or a weak diet that also need fixing.$g$, $g$Hexamita in Fish: Symptoms and Metronidazole Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- White, stringy or clear, jelly-like feces\n- Loss of appetite, or taking food and spitting it out\n- Weight loss and a sunken belly\n- Darkening color and hiding\n- Small pits or erosions on the head in some cichlids\n\nThese signs overlap with other internal parasites, and only a microscope check of the feces confirms Hexamita."}, {"heading": "Causes and risk factors", "text": "Hexamita and its close relatives live in the gut of many fish at low levels. They multiply when fish are stressed by poor water, high nitrate, crowding, shipping or a poor diet. Discus, angelfish, oscars and other cichlids, as well as gouramis, are most often affected. The link between Hexamita and hole-in-the-head is widely reported but still debated, since that condition has several possible causes."}, {"heading": "How to treat Hexamita", "text": "Metronidazole is the standard treatment. Medicated food works best because the drug reaches the gut directly, so use it while the fish is still eating. Soak food in metronidazole or use a prepared medicated food, and feed it for 5 to 10 days.\n\nIf the fish has stopped eating, dose metronidazole in the water of a hospital tank according to the label, often repeated every other day with water changes between doses. Raising the temperature slightly within the species' range can help. Metronidazole is generally safe for filters and plants, but keep doses accurate and remove carbon while treating."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Keep nitrate low with frequent water changes, especially for discus. Feed a varied diet with quality pellets and some vegetable matter. Quarantine new cichlids for 4 weeks and watch their feces closely."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What does Hexamita poop look like?", "a": "Infected fish often pass white, stringy or clear, jelly-like feces instead of normal dark, solid waste."}, {"q": "How long does it take metronidazole to work on Hexamita?", "a": "Many fish start eating again within a few days, but continue the full course of 5 to 10 days to clear the infection."}, {"q": "Does Hexamita cause hole-in-the-head?", "a": "It is often found in fish with hole-in-the-head, but the connection is debated. Water quality, diet and carbon use are also suspected causes."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$lymphocystis$g$, $g$Lymphocystis$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Lymphocystis is a viral disease that causes white or pinkish, cauliflower-like growths on a fish's fins, skin or mouth.$g$, $g$Lymphocystis looks alarming but is rarely fatal, and most fish clear it on their own over weeks to months. There is no medication that cures it, so the job is keeping the fish healthy while its immune system works. It is often triggered by stress, so it is also a nudge to check the tank.$g$, $g$Lymphocystis in Fish: Cauliflower Growths Explained$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Firm white, gray or pink lumps, often grainy like cauliflower\n- Growths usually on fin edges, but also on the body, gills or lips\n- Lumps that grow slowly over days to weeks\n- The fish otherwise acts normally in most cases\n\nIt is more common in marine fish, but freshwater fish such as glassfish, gouramis, cichlids and some perch relatives get it too."}, {"heading": "How it spreads", "text": "The virus, an iridovirus, infects skin cells and makes them grow to many times their normal size. It spreads through contact and through water carrying shed virus from broken growths. Stress from poor water, shipping or injury makes outbreaks more likely, and many fish carry it without showing signs."}, {"heading": "How to manage it", "text": "There is no cure, and antibiotics and antiparasitic medications will not help. Focus on support:\n\n- Keep water very clean with regular water changes.\n- Feed a varied, high-quality diet to support the immune system.\n- Reduce stress from crowding or aggressive tankmates.\n- Isolate the fish if growths are breaking open, to limit spread.\n- Watch for secondary bacterial infections and treat those if they appear.\n\nMost growths shrink and fall off within one to several months. Do not cut or scrape them off, since this causes wounds and spreads the virus. Severe cases on the mouth or gills that stop eating or breathing are rare but serious."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Quarantine new fish and inspect fins closely before buying. Avoid sudden changes and overcrowding. Fish that recover often build immunity, though growths can return during later stress."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is lymphocystis fatal to fish?", "a": "Rarely. Most fish recover on their own, though growths on the mouth or gills can cause problems if they interfere with eating or breathing."}, {"q": "Is lymphocystis contagious?", "a": "Yes, mildly. It can spread through contact and water, mostly to fish that are already stressed, so isolating the affected fish is a good idea."}, {"q": "How long does lymphocystis last?", "a": "Growths usually last a few weeks to a few months before shrinking and falling off without treatment."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$malawi-bloat$g$, $g$Malawi Bloat$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Malawi bloat is a serious digestive illness of African cichlids, especially plant-eating mbuna from Lake Malawi, that causes a swollen belly and rapid decline.$g$, $g$Malawi bloat can kill a fish within a few days of the first signs, so early treatment is essential. It is closely tied to diet, and herbivorous cichlids fed rich, high-protein foods are most at risk. Learning the early signs, like a fish suddenly refusing food, gives you a real chance to save it.$g$, $g$Malawi Bloat in African Cichlids: Signs and Treatment$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Signs to watch for", "text": "- Loss of appetite, often the first sign; the fish may take food and spit it out\n- Hiding and staying away from the group\n- Swollen belly, which shows up later\n- Rapid breathing\n- White, stringy feces\n- Red or swollen vent\n\nBy the time the belly is visibly bloated, the fish may have only a day or two left without treatment."}, {"heading": "What causes it", "text": "The exact cause is debated. It is thought to involve internal bacteria or protozoa that take hold when the gut is irritated. The biggest risk factors are feeding high-protein foods such as bloodworms, beef heart or meaty pellets to herbivores like mbuna and tropheus, along with overfeeding, stress from aggression or crowding, and poor water quality."}, {"heading": "How to treat Malawi bloat", "text": "- Move the fish to a hospital tank right away.\n- Treat with metronidazole as soon as you notice signs. Medicated food works best if the fish still eats; otherwise dose the water following the label.\n- Some keepers combine metronidazole with an antibacterial for advanced cases.\n- Epsom salt at about 1 tablespoon per 5 gallons can help relieve swelling.\n- Keep the water clean and well aerated.\n\nWatch the rest of the group closely. If one fish gets it, the diet or conditions may put others at risk, and some keepers dose metronidazole food to the whole tank."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Feed mbuna and other herbivores a spirulina or vegetable-based staple, and keep meaty treats rare or skip them entirely. Feed small amounts once or twice daily, and consider one fast day a week. Keep nitrate low with weekly water changes of 30 to 50 percent, and avoid crowding beyond what your tank and filtration can handle."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can African cichlids recover from Malawi bloat?", "a": "Yes, if it is treated early, ideally when the fish first stops eating. Advanced cases with a swollen belly have a much lower survival rate."}, {"q": "Is Malawi bloat contagious?", "a": "It is debated, but fish in the same tank often share the same diet and conditions, so others may be at risk. Watch the group and fix the cause."}, {"q": "What should I feed mbuna to prevent bloat?", "a": "A spirulina or vegetable-based pellet or flake as the staple, with high-protein foods kept to a minimum or avoided."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$shimmying$g$, $g$Shimmying$g$, $g$Fish Health & Disease$g$, $g$Shimmying is when a fish wiggles its body side to side in place without moving forward, a stress sign seen most often in livebearers like mollies and guppies.$g$, $g$Shimmying almost always means something in the water is wrong for the fish, often temperature, hardness or water quality. It is common in mollies kept in soft or cold water. The fix is usually simple once you find the cause, but fish left shimmying for days can weaken and get sick.$g$, $g$Why Is My Molly Shimmying? Causes and Fixes$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it looks like", "text": "The fish stays in one spot and wriggles its body in a snake-like motion, as if swimming hard but going nowhere. Fins are often clamped. It may happen near the bottom or in midwater, and the fish may stop eating. Mollies, guppies, platies and swordtails are the usual victims, though other fish can do it too."}, {"heading": "Common causes", "text": "- Water too cold; most livebearers prefer 75 to 80°F\n- Water too soft or low in minerals; livebearers like hard, alkaline water with a pH around 7.5 to 8.2\n- Ammonia, nitrite or high nitrate\n- Sudden changes in temperature or pH after a water change\n- Stress from shipping, crowding or bullying\n- Sometimes a parasite or infection"}, {"heading": "How to fix it", "text": "- Test ammonia, nitrite, nitrate, pH, and general and carbonate hardness.\n- Check the heater and thermometer; raise the temperature slowly into the upper 70s if it is low.\n- Do a 25 to 50 percent water change if waste levels are high.\n- If your water is soft, raise hardness gradually with crushed coral in the filter or a mineral supplement made for livebearers.\n- Some keepers add a small amount of aquarium salt for mollies, but skip it if you have plants or invertebrates that do not tolerate it.\n\nMost fish stop shimmying within a few days once conditions are right. If it continues with good water, look for signs of parasites or infection."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Match livebearers to your water before you buy. If your tap water is very soft, they may need regular mineral support, or softer water species may suit you better. Keep temperatures stable and do regular water changes."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Why is my molly shimmying?", "a": "Usually the water is too cold, too soft or carries ammonia or nitrite. Mollies need warm, hard water to stay healthy."}, {"q": "Is shimmying a disease?", "a": "No. It is a stress symptom, most often caused by water conditions. Fixing the water usually stops it."}, {"q": "Does aquarium salt help shimmying?", "a": "A little salt can help mollies, but fixing temperature and hardness matters more. Avoid salt with plants, shrimp and snails that are sensitive to it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$bladder-snail$g$, $g$Bladder Snail$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$A bladder snail is a small, fast-breeding freshwater snail with a thin, glossy, left-opening shell that often arrives in aquariums as a hitchhiker on plants.$g$, $g$Bladder snails are usually considered pests because their numbers can explode, but a few of them are harmless and even helpful, eating leftover food, algae film and dead plant matter. A sudden boom is mostly a warning that the tank is getting too much food.$g$, $g$Bladder Snails in Aquarium: Pest or Helpful and How to Control$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to identify them", "text": "Bladder snails (Physella species) have a smooth, thin, teardrop-shaped shell usually under half an inch, often tan or brown with speckles. The opening faces left when the point is up. They move quickly, can hang from the water surface on a thread of mucus and lay clear, jelly-like egg clumps on glass and plants. They are hermaphrodites, so a single snail can start a colony."}, {"heading": "How they get in", "text": "They and their eggs ride in on live plants, moss, decor and even in bags with new fish. Rinsing doesn't remove eggs reliably. Dipping plants in a mild alum or diluted bleach solution, or buying tissue-culture plants, greatly lowers the chances."}, {"heading": "Harmful or harmless?", "text": "They don't eat healthy plants and don't harm fish or shrimp. They clean up leftovers and dead leaves. The problem is appearance and numbers. A huge population adds waste and can clog intakes, and mass die-offs can spike ammonia."}, {"heading": "How to control them", "text": "- Feed less, which is the single most effective step\n- Remove snails and egg clumps by hand during maintenance\n- Leave a blanched lettuce or zucchini slice in overnight and remove it with the snails on it\n- Add assassin snails, which hunt them slowly and safely\n- Some fish like loaches and puffers eat snails, but check compatibility first\n\nAvoid snail-killing chemicals. Most contain copper, which kills shrimp and other invertebrates, and dying snails can pollute the tank."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are bladder snails bad for my aquarium?", "a": "Not directly. They are harmless to fish and plants, but they can multiply quickly if there is extra food."}, {"q": "How do bladder snails reproduce so fast?", "a": "They are hermaphrodites, can self-fertilize and lay egg clumps every few days, so one snail can become hundreds."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a bladder snail and a pond snail?", "a": "Bladder snails have a left-opening, more rounded shell and are smaller. Pond snails (Lymnaea) have a right-opening, more pointed shell."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$detritus-worms$g$, $g$Detritus Worms$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Detritus worms are tiny white or tan segmented worms that live in substrate and filters, feeding on leftover food, waste and decaying matter.$g$, $g$They are harmless to fish, shrimp and plants and actually help break down waste. A sudden swarm of them wriggling on the glass or in open water, though, is a clear sign of overfeeding or dirty substrate that needs attention.$g$, $g$Detritus Worms in Aquariums: Harmful or Not, and How to Remove$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What they look like", "text": "Detritus worms are annelids, relatives of earthworms, usually 0.25 to 1 inch long, thin and white to light brown. They wiggle in an S shape and mostly stay hidden in gravel, sand and filter sponges. People often confuse them with planaria, but detritus worms have no flat arrow-shaped head and no eyespots."}, {"heading": "Harmful or harmless", "text": "They don't bite, don't carry fish diseases and don't eat plants. Most tanks with food going into them have some, and many fish happily snack on them.\n\nThe problem is what a population boom says about the tank. Lots of worms means lots of uneaten food and rotting waste, which also means higher ammonia and nitrate. Worms often leave the substrate and swim in open water when oxygen in the gravel drops, so a sudden swarm can also point to poor oxygen or a water-quality problem."}, {"heading": "How to reduce them", "text": "- Feed less, only what fish finish in about 1 to 2 minutes\n- Vacuum the substrate during water changes, focusing on dead spots\n- Rinse filter sponges in old tank water when they clog\n- Remove dead plant leaves and any dead animals quickly\n- Keep fish that graze the bottom, like corydoras or many tetras, which pick them off\n\nOnce food supply drops, numbers fall on their own within a few weeks."}, {"heading": "Should you use chemicals", "text": "Dewormers like fenbendazole will kill detritus worms, but they also kill snails and can harm shrimp. Since the worms are harmless, treating the cause is almost always the better choice."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are detritus worms harmful to fish?", "a": "No. They are harmless scavengers, and many fish eat them. A large population just signals excess food or waste."}, {"q": "Why are there worms swimming in my fish tank?", "a": "Detritus worms come out of the substrate when there is too much food or too little oxygen in the gravel. Do a water change, vacuum, and cut back on feeding."}, {"q": "Are detritus worms the same as planaria?", "a": "No. Planaria are flatworms with a triangular head and eyespots that glide smoothly, while detritus worms are round, segmented and wiggle."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$dragonfly-nymph$g$, $g$Dragonfly Nymph$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$A dragonfly nymph is the aquatic larval stage of a dragonfly, a fierce predator that can hitch a ride into aquariums on plants or live food.$g$, $g$Nymphs grab and eat shrimp, fry and even small adult fish, and they can hide for months before you notice losses. Spotting one early and pulling it out by hand is the only real fix, since no tank-safe treatment targets them.$g$, $g$Dragonfly Nymph in Aquarium: How to Spot and Remove One$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How they get in", "text": "Dragonfly and damselfly eggs or tiny nymphs come in on aquatic plants, especially pond-grown or outdoor-farmed plants, and sometimes in live foods or collected wild water. Tanks kept outdoors or near open windows in summer can also get eggs laid directly in them."}, {"heading": "How to recognize one", "text": "Dragonfly nymphs are brown, green or gray, chunky, with six legs and large eyes, usually 0.5 to 2 inches long. Damselfly nymphs are slimmer with three leaf-like gills at the tail. Both move slowly along plants and decor, then strike with a hinged lower jaw that shoots out to grab prey.\n\nTypical signs are shrimp or fry vanishing over weeks, small fish with bite wounds, and an odd bug lurking in moss or plant thickets."}, {"heading": "Removing them", "text": "- Catch it with a net or tweezers; it won't fight back much\n- Search plant thickets, moss and hardscape at night with a flashlight when they are active\n- Check the filter intake sponge and any prefilter\n- Look for shed exoskeletons, which mean more may be present\n\nChemical treatments that would kill a nymph would also kill shrimp and snails, so manual removal is the standard approach."}, {"heading": "Keeping them out", "text": "Quarantine new plants for a couple of weeks in a separate container and inspect them closely. Many keepers dip plants in a mild bleach or alum solution, or buy lab-grown tissue-culture plants, which arrive free of pests."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Will a dragonfly nymph eat my fish?", "a": "Yes. Larger nymphs can catch fish up to about their own body length, and they readily eat shrimp and fry."}, {"q": "How long can a dragonfly nymph live in an aquarium?", "a": "Depending on species, a nymph can live underwater for months to more than a year before it tries to climb out and become an adult."}, {"q": "How did a dragonfly nymph get in my tank?", "a": "Usually as an egg or tiny nymph on new plants or in live food. Quarantining and inspecting plants prevents most cases."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hydra$g$, $g$Hydra$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Hydra are tiny freshwater animals related to jellyfish, with a thin stalk and stinging tentacles, that often appear on aquarium glass and decor.$g$, $g$Hydra are harmless to adult fish but can sting and eat shrimplets and newly hatched fry, so they matter most in breeding tanks. A sudden bloom almost always means too much food in the water, which is also the key to getting rid of them.$g$, $g$Hydra in Fish Tank: Are They Harmful and How to Remove Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What hydra look like", "text": "Hydra are 0.1 to 0.5 inch long, white, tan or green, with a body stuck to a surface and 4 to 12 thread-like tentacles waving at the top. They contract into a small blob when disturbed. They reproduce by budding, so one can turn into dozens in a couple of weeks when food is plentiful."}, {"heading": "How they get in and why they bloom", "text": "They come in on plants, decor, live food and even water from a store. A few hydra can stay unnoticed for months. They multiply fast when there is lots of tiny food in the water column, such as baby brine shrimp, powdered fry food or crushed flakes left floating."}, {"heading": "Removal options", "text": "- Cut back feeding and siphon leftovers so their food source disappears\n- Scrape and siphon visible hydra off the glass\n- Add fish that eat them, such as gouramis or some mollies, in tanks without fry\n- Fenbendazole is the most common treatment; it kills hydra and planaria but also kills snails, so remove snails first\n- Some products made for this purpose are shrimp safe at the right dose, but always read labels and follow them exactly\n\nAfter a treatment, vacuum dead hydra and do a 25 to 50 percent water change, since the die-off adds waste."}, {"heading": "Do you need to act", "text": "In a tank of adult fish, a handful of hydra is mostly cosmetic. In a shrimp or fry tank, deal with them early before they spread."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are hydra harmful to fish?", "a": "Not to adult fish. They can kill and eat fry and baby shrimp, and large hydra can sting very small adult shrimp."}, {"q": "Does fenbendazole kill hydra?", "a": "Yes, it works well on hydra and planaria, but it also kills snails and should be used with care in shrimp tanks."}, {"q": "What causes hydra in an aquarium?", "a": "They arrive on plants or live food and bloom when there is excess fine food in the water."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$malaysian-trumpet-snail$g$, $g$Malaysian Trumpet Snail$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$The Malaysian trumpet snail is a small livebearing snail with a cone-shaped shell that burrows through sand and gravel and multiplies quickly.$g$, $g$MTS stir the substrate, which helps prevent compacted dead spots and eats leftover food. Because they breed without eggs you can see, populations can explode in tanks with too much food, and many keepers count them as pests. Feeding habits decide which side of that line they land on.$g$, $g$Malaysian Trumpet Snails: Pest or Helpful Substrate Cleaner?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why people keep them", "text": "Malaysian trumpet snails (often shortened to MTS) spend the day buried, turning over sand and gravel as they feed. That gentle digging lets water reach the deeper layers and keeps the bottom from turning black and stagnant. At night they come up the glass to graze. They eat leftovers, biofilm and decaying plant matter, and they rarely damage healthy plants."}, {"heading": "Care basics", "text": "They are hardy and adapt to a temperature range of about 70 to 82 F and a pH of 7 to 8. They need some hardness for shells; thin or pitted shells point to soft, acidic water. Adults reach about 1 inch. They give birth to live young, so a single snail can start a colony."}, {"heading": "Controlling the population", "text": "Numbers follow food. A tank full of trumpet snails is a sign of overfeeding.\n\n- Cut feeding and siphon leftovers\n- Drop a blanched vegetable slice in at night and remove it in the morning with the snails on it\n- Add a snail-eating predator like assassin snails, or loaches in tanks large enough for them\n- Avoid chemical snail killers, which can cause a die-off that spikes ammonia and also harm shrimp\n\nTheir hard shell and operculum make them tougher for fish to eat than ramshorns or bladder snails."}, {"heading": "Is it a pest", "text": "It depends on your goals. In planted tanks with sand they are often welcome; in a show tank with bare glass, hundreds of snails on the glass at night can be unwanted."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are Malaysian trumpet snails bad for your tank?", "a": "Not usually. They aerate substrate and eat waste, but a large population means you are feeding too much."}, {"q": "How do Malaysian trumpet snails reproduce?", "a": "They give birth to live young and some populations reproduce without a mate, so one snail can start a colony."}, {"q": "Do Malaysian trumpet snails eat plants?", "a": "They mostly eat decaying leaves and algae, rarely healthy plants, unless they are underfed."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$planaria$g$, $g$Planaria$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Planaria are small flatworms with a triangular head and two eyespots that glide along aquarium glass and can prey on shrimp and eggs.$g$, $g$In fish-only tanks they are mostly a nuisance, but in shrimp tanks planaria eat shrimplets, eggs and even weak adults. Like most tank pests they thrive on extra food, so cutting feeding is part of every successful removal plan. Fenbendazole and traps are the usual tools.$g$, $g$Planaria in Aquarium: How to Identify and Get Rid of Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to identify them", "text": "Planaria are flat, white, brown or black worms about 0.1 to 0.6 inch long, with an arrow or spade-shaped head and two eyespots that look cross-eyed. They glide smoothly instead of wiggling. They come out on the glass at night or after feeding. If the worm is round and wriggles, it is more likely a harmless detritus worm."}, {"heading": "Why they are a problem", "text": "Planaria hunt small, slow or molting animals. They eat shrimp eggs, baby shrimp and snail eggs, and in large numbers they can stress and kill adult shrimp. They multiply quickly by splitting in two, and cutting one up only creates more."}, {"heading": "How to get rid of them", "text": "- Cut back feeding and siphon leftover food\n- Use a planaria trap baited with a bit of meat overnight and empty it in the morning\n- Fenbendazole is a common treatment and kills planaria and hydra, but it also kills snails, so remove snails first\n- Some products made for planaria are shrimp safe when dosed exactly as labeled\n- Many fish, like small gouramis and some tetras, eat planaria in tanks without shrimp\n\nAfter treatment, siphon dead worms and change 25 to 50 percent of the water, since a die-off fouls the tank."}, {"heading": "Prevention", "text": "Planaria hitch in on plants and with live food. Inspect and quarantine new plants, don't overfeed, and keep the substrate clean. A trap left in overnight every so often shows whether any remain."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are planaria harmful to fish?", "a": "Not to healthy adult fish, but they can eat fish eggs, fry, baby shrimp and weak adult shrimp."}, {"q": "How did planaria get in my tank?", "a": "They arrive on plants, decor or with live food, then multiply when there is extra food."}, {"q": "Does fenbendazole kill planaria without killing shrimp?", "a": "Many shrimp keepers use it successfully at standard doses, but it kills snails and results vary, so follow dosing carefully."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$ramshorn-snail$g$, $g$Ramshorn Snail$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$The ramshorn snail is a small freshwater snail with a flat, coiled shell that eats algae and leftovers and breeds readily in aquariums.$g$, $g$Ramshorns come in red, pink, blue and brown and are a favorite cleanup crew in shrimp tanks. They lay eggs that hatch in freshwater, so the population grows with available food and they are often labeled pest snails. Feeding lightly keeps them helpful.$g$, $g$Ramshorn Snail Care: Breeding, Diet and Population Control$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Care basics", "text": "Ramshorns reach about 0.5 to 1 inch and do fine in tanks as small as 5 gallons. They tolerate 65 to 80 F and prefer pH above 7 with some hardness; thin, pitted shells point to soft acidic water. They eat algae, biofilm, leftovers and decaying plant leaves, and rarely bother healthy plants unless starving. Their blood carries hemoglobin, which is why red and pink varieties look red."}, {"heading": "Breeding", "text": "Each snail has both male and female parts, and any two can mate. They lay clear jelly-like clutches of 10 to 30 eggs on glass, plants and decor. Babies hatch in about 2 to 3 weeks. Population size follows food supply closely, so a well-fed tank will soon have many."}, {"heading": "Keeping numbers in check", "text": "- Feed fish only what they finish in a couple of minutes\n- Scrape egg clutches off the glass\n- Trap snails overnight with a blanched vegetable slice\n- Add assassin snails, or snail-eating fish where suitable\n- Sell or trade extras with other hobbyists, since colorful ramshorns are popular\n\nSkip chemical snail killers, which harm shrimp and can cause ammonia spikes from mass die-offs."}, {"heading": "Common confusion", "text": "The common aquarium ramshorn is a small planorbid snail. The giant ramshorn sold in some stores is a different animal, a kind of apple snail that can pass 1.5 inches and will eat plants. Ask what species you are buying if plants matter to you."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Do ramshorn snails eat live plants?", "a": "Usually not. They prefer algae and decaying leaves, but hungry snails may chew soft plants."}, {"q": "How fast do ramshorn snails reproduce?", "a": "Quickly. With plenty of food, a few snails can become hundreds within a couple of months."}, {"q": "Are ramshorn snails good for shrimp tanks?", "a": "Yes. They are shrimp safe and help clean leftover food and algae."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$scuds$g$, $g$Scuds$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Scuds are small, shrimp-like crustaceans called amphipods, usually under half an inch, that scavenge in plants and substrate and often arrive as hitchhikers.$g$, $g$Scuds are a mixed bag. Fish love eating them and they clean up waste, but large populations can nibble plants and some keepers report them harassing or eating baby shrimp. Whether they are welcome depends on your tank. Most fish eat them on sight.$g$, $g$Scuds in Aquarium: Harmful or Helpful and How to Remove Them$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to recognize scuds", "text": "Scuds are amphipods, gray to tan or greenish, about 0.1 to 0.5 inch long, with curved bodies flattened side to side. They swim on their sides in quick darting bursts and hide in moss, plant roots and leaf litter. Unlike dwarf shrimp, they don't have a smooth upright posture or long antennae sweeping forward."}, {"heading": "How they get in", "text": "They hitch rides on plants, moss and wood from other tanks or ponds, and sometimes arrive in live food cultures. Since they breed fast, a couple can become hundreds in a few months in a tank without fish."}, {"heading": "Harmful or helpful", "text": "- Helpful: they eat detritus and leftovers, and they make excellent live food for fish\n- Neutral to bad: large colonies may graze soft plants, moss and new growth\n- Debated: some shrimp keepers report scuds eating shrimplets or weak shrimp, while others keep both together without trouble\n\nIn a fish tank, scuds rarely last long. In shrimp-only or plant-only tanks they can explode."}, {"heading": "Controlling them", "text": "The easiest control is a few small fish that eat them, but that won't work in a shrimp breeding tank where fish would also eat shrimplets. Otherwise, reduce feeding and trap them with food at night. Chemical treatments that kill scuds also kill shrimp, so avoid them in mixed tanks. Many hobbyists move valuable shrimp to a new tank instead. Check new moss carefully before adding it to a shrimp tank."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are scuds bad for shrimp tanks?", "a": "Opinions differ. They compete for food and may eat baby shrimp, so many breeders prefer to keep them out."}, {"q": "How do I get rid of scuds in my aquarium?", "a": "Add small fish that eat them if the tank allows, cut feeding, and trap them at night. Chemicals that kill scuds kill shrimp too."}, {"q": "Can fish eat scuds?", "a": "Yes. Most fish eat scuds eagerly, and some keepers culture them as live food."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$seed-shrimp$g$, $g$Seed Shrimp (Ostracods)$g$, $g$Invertebrates$g$, $g$Seed shrimp, or ostracods, are tiny crustaceans enclosed in a two-part shell that look like moving seeds zipping around aquarium glass and substrate.$g$, $g$Seed shrimp are harmless scavengers that eat biofilm, algae and leftover food, and fish snack on them. A population boom points to overfeeding or dirty substrate, so control comes down to cleaner habits rather than chemicals. Most tanks host a few without anyone noticing.$g$, $g$Seed Shrimp (Ostracods) in Aquarium: Harmless or a Problem?$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What they are", "text": "Ostracods are small crustaceans, usually 0.02 to 0.1 inch long, with a hinged shell that looks like a tiny bean or seed. They are white, tan, green or brown and scoot around in short jerky bursts. Some species stay on the bottom while others swim in open water. They arrive on plants, in live food and with water or decor from other tanks."}, {"heading": "Are they harmful", "text": "For nearly all tanks, no. They eat biofilm, algae, decaying matter and leftovers, which is the same job as other cleanup crew. A few species may nibble at fish eggs or very soft plant tissue, and large numbers compete with shrimp for food. Very rarely, a keeper reports ostracods swarming a stressed or dying snail, but they don't attack healthy animals."}, {"heading": "Keeping numbers down", "text": "- Feed less, and remove leftover food after a few minutes\n- Vacuum substrate and clean filter sponges regularly\n- Add small fish that eat them, such as many tetras, rasboras or killifish\n- In shrimp tanks, accept a low population; they are part of a normal micro-ecosystem\n\nChemical treatments that kill seed shrimp would also kill dwarf shrimp, so they are not recommended."}, {"heading": "Seed shrimp vs other critters", "text": "Copepods are teardrop shaped with antennae and a jerky swimming style, but no shell. Daphnia are larger and hop through open water. Ostracods are the only common tank critter that looks like a tiny walking clam."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Are seed shrimp bad for an aquarium?", "a": "No. They are harmless scavengers, but a big population means too much food in the tank."}, {"q": "Will fish eat seed shrimp?", "a": "Many will, especially small tetras, rasboras and killifish, though the hard shell makes them less appealing to some fish."}, {"q": "Do seed shrimp hurt cherry shrimp?", "a": "Not directly. They compete for food, but they don't attack healthy shrimp."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$surface-film$g$, $g$Surface Film$g$, $g$Maintenance$g$, $g$Surface film is a thin, oily or cloudy layer that forms on top of aquarium water, made of proteins, oils and bacteria.$g$, $g$Besides looking dull, a film on the surface blocks gas exchange, which can lower oxygen and let carbon dioxide build up. It also cuts light to plants. The fix is usually simple: more surface movement and less excess food or fat.$g$, $g$Oily Film on Aquarium Water Surface: Causes and How to Remove It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What causes it", "text": "Surface film, sometimes called biofilm or protein film, comes from fats and proteins released by food, especially oily foods like some flakes and frozen foods, plus waste from fish and plant matter. Bacteria feed on these and form a skin. It builds fastest in tanks with still surfaces, heavy feeding and lots of plants releasing organic compounds. Hand lotion and oils from your skin can add to it too."}, {"heading": "Why it matters", "text": "- Reduces oxygen exchange, which can show as fish breathing hard near the surface\n- Lets carbon dioxide build up overnight\n- Blocks light reaching plants\n- Looks shiny, rainbow colored or dusty, and can trap debris"}, {"heading": "How to remove it", "text": "Lay a clean paper towel flat on the surface and lift it away; repeat a few times. You can also skim the top layer with a cup during a water change, letting water pour into it from the surface. For a lasting fix, add surface agitation by raising the filter outlet just below the surface, adding an air stone or using a surface skimmer attachment on the filter intake."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Feed smaller amounts and remove leftovers. Rinse your hands without soap and dry them before reaching in, or wear clean gloves. Keep up with weekly water changes. In planted tanks with CO2, aim for a gentle ripple across the surface rather than a perfectly still top, which balances gas exchange with plant needs."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is a film on top of my fish tank harmful?", "a": "Not directly toxic, but it reduces oxygen exchange and light, so it is worth removing."}, {"q": "How do I get rid of oily film in my aquarium?", "a": "Blot it with a paper towel, then add surface movement with your filter outlet, an air stone or a surface skimmer."}, {"q": "Why does my aquarium have a white film on top?", "a": "Usually proteins and bacteria from food and waste on a calm surface. More agitation and less feeding fix it."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$bacterial-bloom$g$, $g$Bacterial Bloom (Cloudy Water)$g$, $g$Nitrogen Cycle & Filtration$g$, $g$A bacterial bloom is a sudden explosion of free-floating bacteria that turns aquarium water milky white or gray, most often in new tanks.$g$, $g$Bacterial blooms are common and usually harmless on their own, but they signal that the tank's balance is off, often extra nutrients or an immature bacteria colony. Knowing the cause helps you decide whether to wait it out or act.$g$, $g$Cloudy Fish Tank Water: Bacterial Bloom Causes and Fixes$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why it happens", "text": "Free-floating heterotrophic bacteria multiply quickly when there is excess dissolved organic matter in the water. Common triggers:\n\n- New tank setup before the bacteria colony settles\n- Overfeeding or rotting food\n- Disturbed substrate or a deep gravel cleaning\n- A large filter clean or media replacement\n- Dead animals or decaying plants\n- Medications that kill off some of the filter bacteria"}, {"heading": "Is it dangerous", "text": "The bacteria themselves are not harmful to fish. The risk is in what comes with a bloom: the bacteria use a lot of oxygen, and the underlying cause, such as an ammonia spike or rotting food, can hurt fish. Test ammonia and nitrite and add aeration. If both read 0 ppm and fish behave normally, the bloom is mostly a cosmetic issue."}, {"heading": "How to clear it", "text": "In most cases, the best approach is patience. A bloom usually clears in 3 days to 2 weeks as the tank finds balance. Help it along by feeding lightly every other day, removing any decaying matter and doing a modest 20 to 25 percent water change. Avoid large daily water changes and repeated filter cleaning, which feed the cycle of imbalance. A UV sterilizer clears blooms fast but doesn't fix the cause."}, {"heading": "Telling it apart from other cloudiness", "text": "Milky white or gray haze is usually bacterial. Green water is a free-floating algae bloom. Fine particles that settle right after a disturbance are substrate dust, which clears with mechanical filtration. Yellow or tea-colored water comes from tannins in wood or leaves and is harmless."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "How long does a bacterial bloom last?", "a": "Usually 3 days to 2 weeks. Feeding lightly and leaving the filter alone helps it clear faster."}, {"q": "Is cloudy water bad for fish?", "a": "The bacteria are not harmful, but the bloom lowers oxygen and often comes with an ammonia problem, so test your water."}, {"q": "Should I do a water change for a bacterial bloom?", "a": "A small one of 20 to 25 percent helps. Large repeated changes often prolong the bloom."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$amazon-sword$g$, $g$Amazon Sword$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Amazon sword is a hardy, fast-growing rosette plant from the genus Echinodorus, with long sword-shaped leaves, often used as a centerpiece in larger freshwater tanks.$g$, $g$It is one of the most popular beginner plants because it tolerates low to moderate light and grows big and lush. It is a heavy root feeder that can reach 16 to 20 inches tall, so it suits tanks of 20 gallons or more.$g$, $g$Amazon Sword Plant Care: Light, Root Tabs and Melting$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Care basics", "text": "- Light: low to moderate; it grows faster and more compact under brighter light.\n- Temperature: 72 to 82°F.\n- pH: about 6.5 to 7.5, though it adapts widely.\n- Substrate: sand or gravel with root tabs, or a nutrient-rich planted substrate.\n- CO2: not required."}, {"heading": "Planting and feeding", "text": "Plant the roots in the substrate but keep the crown, where leaves meet roots, just above it. Push a root tab into the gravel near the base every 2 to 3 months. Its roots spread wide, so give it space away from other root feeders.\n\nTrim old outer leaves at the base when they yellow or collect algae."}, {"heading": "Propagation", "text": "A healthy sword sends up long stalks that grow small plantlets along them. Once a plantlet has a few leaves and roots of its own, cut it off and plant it. You can also pin the stalk down so the plantlets root while still attached."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Melting after purchase is normal. Most store plants are grown out of water, and the old leaves die back as new underwater leaves grow. Leave the crown alone and wait a few weeks.\n\nYellow leaves with green veins usually mean low iron, and yellow or holey older leaves often mean low potassium or general hunger. Root tabs and a comprehensive liquid fertilizer fix most of this. Goldfish and large cichlids may uproot or eat it."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does Amazon sword need root tabs?", "a": "It does best with them. It feeds mainly through its roots, so plain gravel needs root tabs every few months."}, {"q": "Why is my Amazon sword melting?", "a": "Usually because it was grown out of water and is switching to underwater leaves. Keep the crown healthy and new growth will follow."}, {"q": "How big does an Amazon sword get?", "a": "Often 16 to 20 inches tall and wide in good conditions, so it is best in tanks 20 gallons or larger."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$anubias$g$, $g$Anubias$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Anubias is a genus of slow-growing, very hardy African plants with thick dark leaves that grow from a rhizome attached to rock or wood rather than buried in substrate.$g$, $g$It is close to unkillable in the right setup: it thrives in low light, needs no CO2, and most fish, including many plant-eaters, leave its tough leaves alone. The one rule that matters is never burying the rhizome, which rots when covered.$g$, $g$Anubias Care: How to Attach It and Avoid Rhizome Rot$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Care basics", "text": "- Light: low to moderate. Strong light leads to algae on the slow leaves.\n- Temperature: 72 to 82°F.\n- pH: about 6.0 to 7.5.\n- Feeding: takes nutrients from the water, so a light liquid fertilizer helps.\n- Growth: slow, often one new leaf every few weeks.\n\nPopular kinds include Anubias barteri, the larger coffeefolia, and the tiny nana petite."}, {"heading": "How to plant it", "text": "Tie the rhizome to wood or rock with cotton thread or fishing line, or use a dab of gel superglue. Within a few weeks roots grip the surface and the tie can be removed.\n\nYou can also wedge it between rocks, or set the roots in sand with the rhizome sitting on top."}, {"heading": "Propagation", "text": "Cut the rhizome with a clean blade into pieces that each have at least 3 or 4 leaves. Each piece grows into a new plant. Side shoots that form naturally can be split off the same way."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Rhizome rot shows as a soft, mushy brown section with leaves falling off. Cut away the rotten part and keep the rhizome uncovered.\n\nGreen spot algae often settles on old leaves because they last so long. Shade the plant, shorten the light period, or wipe leaves gently. Nerite snails clean it well. Yellowing leaves usually point to low nitrogen or iron. Plant-eating fish like silver dollars usually leave it alone, which makes it a good choice for tough tanks."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can you bury Anubias roots?", "a": "The roots can go into the substrate, but the rhizome must stay above it or it will rot."}, {"q": "Does Anubias need fertilizer?", "a": "Not much, but it grows better with a light liquid fertilizer since it feeds from the water."}, {"q": "Why does my Anubias have algae on the leaves?", "a": "Its slow leaves give algae time to settle. Lower or shade the light and reduce the light period."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$cladophora$g$, $g$Cladophora$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Cladophora is a branching, wiry green filament algae that forms tough mats in aquariums; the familiar marimo moss ball is a close relative once grouped with it.$g$, $g$As a pest it is one of the hardest algae to get rid of because few animals eat it and it clings to plants and hardscape. As a moss ball, a slow-growing relative is a charming, easy plant. Knowing which one you have decides whether to remove it or keep it.$g$, $g$Cladophora Algae vs Marimo Moss Ball: Identify and Remove$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to identify it", "text": "Pest cladophora grows as stiff, branched dark green threads that feel rough, almost like wet steel wool, unlike slimy hair algae. It often tangles into moss and around stem plants and rarely floats free.\n\nMarimo moss balls are round, dense, velvety green spheres that grow only a few millimeters a year. They are now classified as Aegagropila linnaei but are still widely sold under the old Cladophora name."}, {"heading": "How it gets in", "text": "Pest cladophora almost always hitchhikes on new plants or moss. Inspect and rinse new plants, and quarantine them for a week or two in a separate container if possible."}, {"heading": "How to control it", "text": "- Pull out as much as possible by hand, winding it around a toothbrush.\n- Remove badly infested moss or plant portions entirely.\n- Spot treat remaining patches with liquid carbon or hydrogen peroxide using a syringe with the filter off for a few minutes. Overdosing can harm shrimp, snails and delicate plants like Vallisneria.\n- Keep nutrients balanced and lights at 6 to 8 hours to slow regrowth.\n\nFew animals eat it, though some keepers report amano shrimp and certain fish picking at it."}, {"heading": "Caring for moss balls", "text": "Keep them in cool water under 78°F with low light, rotate them now and then, and squeeze them out in tank water every few weeks. A brown or white patch means too much light or heat. Buy from trusted sources; in 2021 some shipments carried invasive zebra mussels."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is a marimo moss ball cladophora?", "a": "Marimo are a close relative once classified as Cladophora and now named Aegagropila linnaei. Many sellers still use the old name."}, {"q": "How do you get rid of cladophora algae?", "a": "Remove it by hand, cut out infested plant parts, and spot treat what is left with liquid carbon or hydrogen peroxide."}, {"q": "Does anything eat cladophora?", "a": "Very little does reliably. Amano shrimp may pick at it, but manual removal is the main fix."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$cryptocoryne$g$, $g$Cryptocoryne$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Cryptocoryne, often called crypts, is a genus of hardy, slow to moderate growing rosette plants from Asia that root in the substrate and thrive in low light.$g$, $g$Crypts are a backbone of low-tech planted tanks because they handle dim light and need no CO2. They come in many colors and sizes, from short foreground species to tall background ones, but they are famous for melting when moved or disturbed.$g$, $g$Cryptocoryne Care and How to Stop Crypt Melt$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Care basics", "text": "- Light: low to moderate.\n- Temperature: 72 to 82°F.\n- pH: about 6.0 to 7.5; most adapt widely.\n- Substrate: they feed through roots, so use root tabs or nutrient-rich soil.\n- CO2: not needed.\n\nEasy, popular species include Cryptocoryne wendtii in green, brown and red forms, the small parva, and the tough lutea."}, {"heading": "Crypt melt", "text": "Melting is when a crypt's leaves turn see-through and dissolve within days of planting or after a big change in water, light or CO2. It looks alarming but is usually not fatal. Leave the roots and crown in place, remove the rotting leaves, and new leaves adapted to your tank usually appear within 2 to 4 weeks.\n\nAvoid moving crypts once settled, since uprooting can trigger another melt."}, {"heading": "Planting and propagation", "text": "Plant the roots and keep the crown just at the substrate surface. Crypts spread by underground runners that pop up as new plants a few inches away. Once a baby has several leaves, separate it carefully with tweezers or scissors and replant, or let it form a clump."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "Slow growth and pale leaves usually mean hunger, so add root tabs every 2 to 3 months. Holes or yellowing on older leaves can mean low potassium. Too much light without matching nutrients can lead to algae on the slow leaves. Bright light combined with CO2 can make some red forms color up more, but most crypts do fine without either."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Why is my Cryptocoryne melting?", "a": "It is reacting to a change, such as being moved, new water conditions or switching from out-of-water growth. Keep the roots in place and new leaves will usually grow back."}, {"q": "Do Cryptocoryne need root tabs?", "a": "They grow much better with them, since they feed mainly through their roots."}, {"q": "How fast do crypts grow?", "a": "Slowly to moderately. Expect a new leaf every week or two once established, faster with good nutrients."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$duckweed$g$, $g$Duckweed$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Duckweed is a tiny floating plant, usually Lemna minor, with one to three small round leaves and dangling roots, known for spreading extremely fast across the surface.$g$, $g$Some keepers grow it on purpose as a cheap nutrient sponge and fish food, while others consider it a pest that is nearly impossible to remove. It soaks up nitrate and shades out algae, but it can also block light and cover the surface in days.$g$, $g$Duckweed in Aquariums: Benefits and How to Get Rid of It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Benefits", "text": "- Absorbs ammonia and nitrate quickly, helping water quality.\n- Shades the tank, which helps hold back algae and suits fish that like dim light.\n- Gives fry, bettas and gouramis cover near the surface.\n- Goldfish, some cichlids and silver dollars eat it as a green snack."}, {"heading": "How it gets in", "text": "Duckweed almost always hitchhikes on new plants, in bags of fish or on nets used in an infested tank. A single leaf can turn into a full surface layer within a few weeks in a well-fed, well-lit tank. Rinsing new plants and inspecting them closely, or dipping them in a separate container for a few days, catches most stray leaves."}, {"heading": "How to control or remove it", "text": "Scoop it out with a fine net several times a week; a cup or open-ended container can corral it first. Removing it completely takes persistence, since any leaf left behind regrows. Turning off the filter while netting helps it clump.\n\nA floating ring or tube can confine it to one area. Lower surface flow and higher light make it grow faster, so stronger surface agitation helps slow it. Avoid herbicides, which harm other plants and can crash oxygen."}, {"heading": "Problems to watch for", "text": "A thick mat blocks light to plants below and slows gas exchange, which can lower oxygen. It can also clog filter intakes and skimmers. Check new plants and rinse them before adding them to keep it out of tanks where you do not want it."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is duckweed good for an aquarium?", "a": "It can be. It lowers nitrate and shades out algae, but it spreads fast and needs regular thinning."}, {"q": "How do I get rid of duckweed permanently?", "a": "Net it out often and thoroughly until no leaves remain. Even one leaf can restart it, so check for weeks afterward."}, {"q": "Do fish eat duckweed?", "a": "Yes. Goldfish, silver dollars, many cichlids and some gouramis eat it readily."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$green-water$g$, $g$Green Water$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Green water is an algae bloom of free-floating, single-celled algae that turns aquarium water a cloudy pea-soup green.$g$, $g$It is not usually dangerous to fish, but it blocks light, hides your fish and can swing oxygen levels at night in a heavy bloom. Normal water changes and filter media don't remove it well, so knowing what actually works saves weeks of frustration.$g$, $g$Green Water in Aquarium: Causes and How to Clear It Fast$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Why it happens", "text": "Green water is caused by microscopic suspended algae, often Chlorella or Euglena types, multiplying in the water itself. Blooms usually follow a combination of excess light and excess nutrients: a tank near a sunny window, lights left on 10 or more hours, overfeeding, or an ammonia spike from a new tank or a big disturbance like a deep substrate clean."}, {"heading": "How to clear it", "text": "- UV sterilizer: the most reliable fix, often clearing water in 3 to 7 days\n- Blackout: turn off the lights and cover the tank completely for 3 to 4 days, then do a large water change\n- Diatom or very fine micron filtration catches the cells\n- Cut feeding and photoperiod while you treat\n\nWater changes alone rarely work because the algae regrows from what remains within a day or two."}, {"heading": "Care during a blackout", "text": "Fish and most plants handle a few dark days well. Add an air stone, since algae and plants consume oxygen in the dark and a dense bloom dying off can drop oxygen quickly. Feed lightly, keep the lid on, and don't peek, since even a few hours of light can restart the bloom. Expect some leaf loss on demanding plants."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Keep the tank out of direct sunlight, run lights 6 to 8 hours on a timer, and feed only what fish finish in a minute or two. Fast-growing stem and floating plants starve suspended algae of nutrients. Avoid algaecides: they kill the bloom all at once, which can crash oxygen and harm shrimp and snails."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Is green water bad for fish?", "a": "Mild green water is generally harmless and some breeders even use it for fry. A thick bloom can lower oxygen at night, so add aeration and clear it."}, {"q": "Why won't my green water go away after water changes?", "a": "Free-floating algae double quickly, so a partial change only dilutes them briefly. A UV sterilizer or a full blackout targets the cells themselves."}, {"q": "How long does it take a UV sterilizer to clear green water?", "a": "Usually 3 to 7 days with the right size unit and slow enough flow through it. Clean the quartz sleeve if it stops working."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hair-algae$g$, $g$Hair Algae$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Hair algae is a catch-all name for long, soft, green filamentous algae that grows in strands on plants, wood, rocks and decor.$g$, $g$It grows fast, tangles around stem plants and carpets, and can smother them if ignored. Because it thrives on unbalanced light, nutrients and CO2, beating it means fixing those conditions, not just pulling it out. Most outbreaks clear within a few weeks once the balance is right.$g$, $g$Hair Algae in Aquarium: Causes, Removal and Prevention$g$, $g$[{"heading": "What it looks like", "text": "Hair algae forms green threads that can be short and fuzzy or several inches long, often waving in the current. Common types include Cladophora, Spirogyra, Rhizoclonium and Oedogonium, which hobbyists often lump together. Spirogyra feels slimy and very fine; Cladophora is coarser, branched and tougher to kill."}, {"heading": "Common causes", "text": "- Strong light, especially new brighter fixtures or long photoperiods\n- Excess nutrients from overfeeding or a big fertilizer increase\n- CO2 that fluctuates or runs low in a high-tech tank\n- A tank with too few fast-growing plants\n- Hitchhikers on new plants"}, {"heading": "How to remove it", "text": "Twirl it off with a toothbrush or a rough wooden skewer and siphon out loose bits during a water change. Trim badly covered leaves. Spot-treating with liquid carbon or hydrogen peroxide through a syringe, with the filter off for a few minutes, kills stubborn tufts; go easy, since both can melt sensitive plants like Vallisneria and peroxide can harm shrimp at higher doses. Amano shrimp, Siamese algae eaters and some livebearers eat soft hair algae well."}, {"heading": "Keeping it away", "text": "Drop the photoperiod to 6 to 7 hours or lower intensity, feed less, and add fast growers or floating plants to compete for nutrients. In CO2 tanks, aim for stable levels with a drop checker reaching lime green before lights on. Change 30 to 50 percent of the water weekly while you rebalance, and give it two to four weeks: algae declines slowly once conditions turn against it."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What causes hair algae in a planted tank?", "a": "Usually too much light compared with CO2 and nutrients, so the plants can't use all the light and algae takes advantage. Unstable CO2 is a very common trigger."}, {"q": "Do Amano shrimp eat hair algae?", "a": "Yes, Amano shrimp are among the best hair algae eaters, especially on soft, short strands. A group of several per 10 gallons works better than one or two."}, {"q": "Is hair algae harmful to fish?", "a": "It isn't toxic, but thick mats can trap tiny fry and shrimplets and choke plants. Remove it before it spreads."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$hornwort$g$, $g$Hornwort$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Hornwort is a fast-growing, rootless stem plant with bristly, needle-like leaves that can float freely or be weighted down in the tank.$g$, $g$Hornwort grows so quickly that it soaks up nitrate and outcompetes algae, which makes it a great helper in new or heavily stocked tanks. It tolerates cool and warm water, low light and hard water, so it suits beginners, goldfish tanks and fry-rearing setups.$g$, $g$Hornwort Care: Growing, Planting and Stopping Needle Shedding$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Light and water", "text": "Hornwort (Ceratophyllum demersum) does well in low to moderate light and a wide temperature range of about 59 to 86°F. It prefers neutral to hard water, roughly pH 6.5 to 8. It needs no CO2 or special substrate because it absorbs nutrients through its leaves. Strong light gives denser, brighter growth."}, {"heading": "How to grow it", "text": "Since it has no true roots, let it float, tie it loosely to decor, or anchor the base under a rock or with a plant weight. Avoid burying stems deeply in substrate, where they tend to rot. Leave room at the surface, since it can reach several feet long and will shade other plants if it forms a mat."}, {"heading": "Propagation", "text": "Snip off a section of any length and it becomes a new plant. Trim regularly to keep it in check, and remove extra stems rather than letting them pile up. Excess growth makes useful cover for fry in breeding tanks or a nutrient sponge in another tank."}, {"heading": "Problems to watch for", "text": "- Needle shedding: common after moving tanks or sudden changes in temperature or water; usually settles in a week or two\n- Mess: dropped needles clog filter intakes, so a prefilter sponge helps\n- Browning at the base: low light reaching the lower stems; trim and replant tips\n- Some liquid carbon products can make it melt, so skip them in tanks with hornwort"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Why is my hornwort shedding needles?", "a": "Hornwort sheds when conditions change quickly, such as a new tank, temperature swing or water change. It usually stabilizes and regrows once settled."}, {"q": "Should hornwort float or be planted?", "a": "Either works. Floating gives it the most light, while anchoring under a rock gives a neater look. Avoid burying the stems deep in substrate."}, {"q": "Can hornwort live with goldfish?", "a": "Yes. It tolerates cool water and grows fast enough to survive some nibbling, which makes it one of the better plants for goldfish tanks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$java-fern$g$, $g$Java Fern$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Java fern is a hardy, slow-growing epiphyte fern with long green leaves that grows from a rhizome attached to wood or rock instead of being buried.$g$, $g$It thrives in low light, needs no CO2 and is not eaten by most fish, including many cichlids and goldfish. That makes it one of the best first plants for beginners and a safe choice for tanks where other plants get destroyed.$g$, $g$Java Fern Care: How to Plant, Attach and Propagate It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Light and water", "text": "Java fern (Microsorum pteropus, now often listed as Leptochilus pteropus) grows in low to moderate light and temperatures from about 68 to 82°F. It adapts to soft or hard water, roughly pH 6 to 7.5. Strong direct light can cause brown or see-through patches, so shadier spots are ideal. Varieties include narrow leaf, needle leaf, trident and windelov."}, {"heading": "How to attach it", "text": "Never bury the rhizome, the thick horizontal stem the leaves grow from, or it will rot. Tie it to driftwood or rock with cotton thread or fishing line, or use a dab of gel superglue on the rhizome. Roots grip the surface within a few weeks. You can also wedge it between stones."}, {"heading": "Propagation", "text": "Java fern makes baby plantlets on the leaves, often on older or darker leaves. Once a plantlet has a few leaves and roots, pull it off and attach it elsewhere. You can also cut the rhizome into pieces with several leaves each, and each piece will grow."}, {"heading": "Common problems", "text": "- Black or brown spots: these are often spore or plantlet sites, but spreading mushy brown areas mean poor nutrients or too much light\n- Melting leaves: usually from burying the rhizome or a sudden change in conditions\n- Slow growth is normal; a monthly dose of fertilizer helps in bare tanks\n- Algae on old leaves: trim them, since slow leaves collect algae"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Can I plant Java fern in gravel?", "a": "You can wedge the roots into gravel, but keep the rhizome above the substrate. Burying it will make the plant rot."}, {"q": "Why is my Java fern turning brown?", "a": "Small dark spots are often normal spores, but spreading brown patches usually mean too much light, too few nutrients, or a buried rhizome. Trim damaged leaves and adjust."}, {"q": "Do fish eat Java fern?", "a": "Very few fish eat it because of its tough, bitter-tasting leaves. That's why it's a favorite for cichlid and goldfish tanks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$java-moss$g$, $g$Java Moss$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Java moss is a hardy, low-light aquarium moss that grows as a dense green mat on wood, rock, mesh or floating freely in the tank.$g$, $g$It is nearly impossible to kill, grows in almost any conditions and gives shrimp and fry somewhere to graze and hide. It is also the go-to plant for moss walls, carpets and soft natural-looking driftwood, though it can trap debris if not trimmed.$g$, $g$Java Moss Care: How to Attach, Grow and Trim It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "Light and water", "text": "Java moss (commonly sold as Taxiphyllum barbieri, though the exact species is debated) grows in low to high light and from about 59 to 86°F. It prefers cooler water, under about 78°F, for the best growth. Brighter light and some fertilizer make it denser and greener, while low light gives a looser, stringy look. Christmas moss, flame moss and weeping moss are close relatives with more structured shapes and slightly higher needs."}, {"heading": "How to attach it", "text": "Spread a thin layer over wood or rock and tie it down with cotton thread or fishing line, or use a little gel superglue. For walls and carpets, sandwich thin layers between two pieces of stainless or plastic mesh. Thin layers work better than thick clumps, since the bottom of a thick clump rots from lack of light."}, {"heading": "Trimming and propagation", "text": "Trim with sharp scissors every few weeks to keep it tidy and dense, and siphon up the clippings so they don't take root everywhere. Any piece will grow into a new patch, so propagation is as simple as moving a clipping."}, {"heading": "Problems to watch for", "text": "- Debris build-up: fine waste gets trapped, so gently swish or siphon over it during water changes\n- Algae tangled in the moss: hair algae is hard to remove, so quarantine new moss and keep light balanced\n- Browning: often from warm water, low flow or being buried under thicker growth\n- Clogged intakes: loose strands get sucked into filters"}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "Does Java moss need light?", "a": "It needs some light but very little. It grows in dim tanks, though more light gives a denser, greener moss."}, {"q": "Why is my Java moss turning brown?", "a": "Common causes are warm water, too little light reaching the lower layers, or debris smothering it. Thin it out, rinse it and trim the brown parts."}, {"q": "Is Java moss good for shrimp?", "a": "Yes. Shrimp graze biofilm on it constantly and baby shrimp hide in it, which is why it's a staple in shrimp tanks."}]$g$::jsonb),
  ($g$staghorn-algae$g$, $g$Staghorn Algae$g$, $g$Plants & Aquascaping$g$, $g$Staghorn algae is a gray-green, branching filamentous algae that grows in thin, forked strands resembling deer antlers on plants, decor and hardscape edges.$g$, $g$It is tough and wiry, clings to plant edges and equipment, and is often a sign of unstable CO2 or poor flow. Fixing the conditions behind it, rather than just pulling it out, is the key to getting rid of it.$g$, $g$Staghorn Algae: Causes, Removal and How to Prevent It$g$, $g$[{"heading": "How to recognize it", "text": "Staghorn algae, usually a Compsopogon species, grows as stiff gray-green or gray-brown strands that split into Y-shaped branches. It often attaches to leaf edges, slow plants, filter intakes and hardscape. It feels coarse rather than slimy and doesn't pull apart easily like soft hair algae. It is sometimes confused with black beard algae, which forms darker, shorter tufts."}, {"heading": "What causes it", "text": "- Unstable or low CO2 in planted tanks\n- Poor water circulation that leaves dead spots\n- Excess organic waste from overfeeding or an overdue clean\n- Ammonia spikes from a new or disturbed tank\n- Lighting that is too strong for the plants and CO2 available"}, {"heading": "How to remove it", "text": "Pull it off by hand where possible and trim affected leaves. Spot-treat stubborn growth with liquid carbon or a small dose of hydrogen peroxide using a syringe while the filter is off for a few minutes. Liquid carbon can melt Vallisneria and some mosses, and peroxide in larger amounts can harm shrimp, snails and delicate plants, so treat sparingly. Siamese algae eaters and Amano shrimp eat some, but rarely clear it alone."}, {"heading": "Preventing it", "text": "Clean filters regularly and improve flow so water reaches every corner. In CO2 tanks, keep levels stable throughout the photoperiod. Feed less, remove dead leaves, and keep up with weekly 30 to 50 percent water changes. Cutting light intensity or duration by an hour or two while you rebalance gives plants the upper hand."}]$g$::jsonb, $g$[{"q": "What causes staghorn algae?", "a": "Usually unstable CO2, poor circulation or excess organic waste. It's common in tanks that have recently had a change in lighting or maintenance."}, {"q": "What eats staghorn algae?", "a": "Siamese algae eaters and Amano shrimp nibble it, but they usually can't remove an established outbreak without fixing the cause."}, {"q": "Does liquid carbon kill staghorn algae?", "a": "Spot-dosing liquid carbon directly on the algae often kills it within a few days. Use caution near sensitive plants like Vallisneria."}]$g$::jsonb)
) as v(slug, term, category, definition, body, seo_title, sections, faq)
where not exists (select 1 from public.glossary_terms x where x.slug = v.slug);

-- Near-duplicates: the kept term now covers each of these in full.
delete from public.glossary_terms
where slug in ('quarantine-tank', 'acclimate', 'cycling-a-tank', 'hardscape-scape', 'nitrifying-bacteria', 'partial-water-change');

commit;

-- Check: every term should now have a full write-up.
select count(*) as terms,
       count(*) filter (where faq is not null and jsonb_array_length(faq::jsonb) = 3) as with_faq,
       count(*) filter (where sections is not null) as with_sections,
       count(*) filter (where seo_title is not null) as with_search_title
from public.glossary_terms;
